export const PACK = { id: "core-v3", json: "[{\"skill_code\":\"area_volume\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A rectangular cotton field is 120 meters long and 85 meters wide. What is the area, in square meters, of the field?\",\"option_a\":\"205\",\"option_b\":\"410\",\"option_c\":\"5,100\",\"option_d\":\"10,200\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The area of a rectangle is length times width: $A = 120 \\\\cdot 85 = 10{,}200$ square meters, so choice D is correct.\\n\\nChoice A (205) is $120 + 85$, the sum of the two dimensions. Choice B (410) is $2(120 + 85)$, the perimeter of the field, not its area. Choice C (5,100) is half of the correct area; the factor $\\\\frac{1}{2}$ belongs in the area of a triangle, not a rectangle.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"To‘g‘ri to‘rtburchakning yuzasi uzunlik bilan enining ko‘paytmasiga teng: $A = 120 \\\\cdot 85 = 10{,}200$ kvadrat metr, demak to‘g‘ri javob D.\\n\\nA (205) — bu $120 + 85$, ya’ni ikki o‘lchamning yig‘indisi. B (410) — bu $2(120 + 85)$, ya’ni maydonning perimetri, yuzasi emas. C (5,100) — to‘g‘ri yuzaning yarmi; $\\\\frac{1}{2}$ ko‘paytuvchi to‘g‘ri to‘rtburchak uchun emas, uchburchak yuzasi uchun ishlatiladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"area_volume\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A plot of land is shaped like a triangle. One side of the plot is 30 meters long, and the perpendicular distance from the opposite corner of the plot to the line containing that side is 18 meters. What is the area, in square meters, of the plot?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"270\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The 30-meter side is a base, and the 18-meter perpendicular distance is the matching height. The area of a triangle is $A = \\\\frac{1}{2}bh = \\\\frac{1}{2}(30)(18) = 270$ square meters.\\n\\nCommon mistake: $30 \\\\cdot 18 = 540$ forgets the factor $\\\\frac{1}{2}$; that would be the area of a rectangle with the same base and height.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"30 metrli tomon asos, 18 metrli perpendikulyar masofa esa unga mos balandlik. Uchburchak yuzasi $A = \\\\frac{1}{2}bh = \\\\frac{1}{2}(30)(18) = 270$ kvadrat metr.\\n\\nKo‘p uchraydigan xato: $30 \\\\cdot 18 = 540$ — bunda $\\\\frac{1}{2}$ ko‘paytuvchi unutilgan; bu asosi va balandligi xuddi shunday bo‘lgan to‘g‘ri to‘rtburchakning yuzasi bo‘lardi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"area_volume\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A water tank is shaped like a right circular cylinder with an inside radius of 5 meters and an inside height of 6 meters. What is the volume, in cubic meters, of the inside of the tank?\",\"option_a\":\"$30\\\\pi$\",\"option_b\":\"$60\\\\pi$\",\"option_c\":\"$150\\\\pi$\",\"option_d\":\"$600\\\\pi$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The volume of a cylinder is $V = \\\\pi r^2 h = \\\\pi (5)^2 (6) = 150\\\\pi$ cubic meters, so choice C is correct.\\n\\nChoice A ($30\\\\pi$) is $\\\\pi r h$; the radius was not squared. Choice B ($60\\\\pi$) is $2\\\\pi r h$, the area of the curved side of the tank, not its volume. Choice D ($600\\\\pi$) uses the diameter, 10, in place of the radius: $\\\\pi (10)^2 (6)$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Silindr hajmi $V = \\\\pi r^2 h = \\\\pi (5)^2 (6) = 150\\\\pi$ kub metr, demak to‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\nA ($30\\\\pi$) — bu $\\\\pi r h$; radius kvadratga oshirilmagan. B ($60\\\\pi$) — bu $2\\\\pi r h$, ya’ni bakning yon sirti yuzasi, hajmi emas. D ($600\\\\pi$) — radius o‘rniga diametr (10) olingan: $\\\\pi (10)^2 (6)$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":65,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"area_volume\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"The roof of a pavilion is a dome shaped like a hemisphere (half of a sphere) with a diameter of 12 meters. The curved outer surface of the dome, not including the flat circular base, will be covered with blue tiles. What is the area, in square meters, of the surface to be tiled?\",\"option_a\":\"$36\\\\pi$\",\"option_b\":\"$72\\\\pi$\",\"option_c\":\"$144\\\\pi$\",\"option_d\":\"$288\\\\pi$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The radius is half the diameter: $r = 6$ meters. A sphere has surface area $4\\\\pi r^2$, so the curved surface of a hemisphere is half of that: $2\\\\pi r^2 = 2\\\\pi (6)^2 = 72\\\\pi$ square meters. Choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice A ($36\\\\pi$) is $\\\\pi r^2$, the area of the flat circular base. Choice C ($144\\\\pi$) is $4\\\\pi r^2$, the surface area of a whole sphere. Choice D ($288\\\\pi$) uses the diameter, 12, as the radius in $2\\\\pi r^2$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Radius diametrning yarmiga teng: $r = 6$ metr. Sfera sirtining yuzasi $4\\\\pi r^2$, demak yarim sferaning egri sirti uning yarmi: $2\\\\pi r^2 = 2\\\\pi (6)^2 = 72\\\\pi$ kvadrat metr. To‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\nA ($36\\\\pi$) — bu $\\\\pi r^2$, ya’ni gumbazning tekis doiraviy asosi yuzasi. C ($144\\\\pi$) — bu $4\\\\pi r^2$, butun sferaning sirt yuzasi. D ($288\\\\pi$) — $2\\\\pi r^2$ formulasida radius o‘rniga diametr (12) olingan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"area_volume\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A solid concrete wheelchair ramp is shaped like a right triangular prism. Each of its two triangular ends is a right triangle with a horizontal leg of 6 meters along the ground and a vertical leg of 0.5 meter. The ramp is 1.5 meters wide, which is the distance between the two triangular ends. What is the volume, in cubic meters, of concrete in the ramp?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"2.25\",\"9/4\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The volume of a prism is the area of its base (here, the triangular end) times the distance between the two ends. The triangular end has area $\\\\frac{1}{2}(6)(0.5) = 1.5$ square meters, so the volume is $1.5 \\\\cdot 1.5 = 2.25$ cubic meters.\\n\\nCommon mistake: $6 \\\\cdot 0.5 \\\\cdot 1.5 = 4.5$ treats the ramp as a rectangular box and forgets the factor $\\\\frac{1}{2}$ for the triangle.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Prizma hajmi asos yuzasini (bu yerda uchburchak shaklidagi uchini) ikki uch orasidagi masofaga ko‘paytirishga teng. Uchburchak yuzasi $\\\\frac{1}{2}(6)(0.5) = 1.5$ kvadrat metr, demak hajm $1.5 \\\\cdot 1.5 = 2.25$ kub metr.\\n\\nKo‘p uchraydigan xato: $6 \\\\cdot 0.5 \\\\cdot 1.5 = 4.5$ — bunda pandus to‘g‘ri burchakli quti deb olingan va uchburchak uchun $\\\\frac{1}{2}$ ko‘paytuvchi unutilgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"area_volume\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A stone monument is shaped like a pyramid with a square base. Each side of the base is 9 meters long, and the height of the pyramid (the perpendicular distance from the top vertex to the base) is 12 meters. What is the volume, in cubic meters, of the monument?\",\"option_a\":\"324\",\"option_b\":\"432\",\"option_c\":\"648\",\"option_d\":\"972\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The volume of a pyramid is $V = \\\\frac{1}{3}Bh$, where $B$ is the area of the base. Here $B = 9^2 = 81$ square meters, so $V = \\\\frac{1}{3}(81)(12) = 324$ cubic meters. Choice A is correct.\\n\\nChoice B (432) is $\\\\frac{1}{3}(12^2)(9)$; it squares the height instead of the base side. Choice C (648) uses $\\\\frac{1}{2}$ instead of $\\\\frac{1}{3}$. Choice D (972) is $81 \\\\cdot 12$, the volume of a prism with the same base and height; it forgets the $\\\\frac{1}{3}$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Piramida hajmi $V = \\\\frac{1}{3}Bh$, bu yerda $B$ — asos yuzasi. Bu yerda $B = 9^2 = 81$ kvadrat metr, demak $V = \\\\frac{1}{3}(81)(12) = 324$ kub metr. To‘g‘ri javob A.\\n\\nB (432) — bu $\\\\frac{1}{3}(12^2)(9)$; asos tomoni o‘rniga balandlik kvadratga oshirilgan. C (648) — $\\\\frac{1}{3}$ o‘rniga $\\\\frac{1}{2}$ olingan. D (972) — bu $81 \\\\cdot 12$, ya’ni asosi va balandligi bir xil bo‘lgan prizmaning hajmi; $\\\\frac{1}{3}$ unutilgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"area_volume\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A grain silo is made of a right circular cylinder with a hemisphere (half of a sphere) on top. The cylinder and the hemisphere both have a radius of 3 meters, and the flat face of the hemisphere fits exactly on the top of the cylinder. The total height of the silo, from the ground to the highest point of the hemisphere, is 13 meters. What is the total volume, in cubic meters, of the silo?\",\"option_a\":\"$90\\\\pi$\",\"option_b\":\"$99\\\\pi$\",\"option_c\":\"$108\\\\pi$\",\"option_d\":\"$135\\\\pi$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The hemisphere is 3 meters tall (its radius), so the cylinder is $13 - 3 = 10$ meters tall. Cylinder: $\\\\pi (3)^2 (10) = 90\\\\pi$. Hemisphere: $\\\\frac{1}{2} \\\\cdot \\\\frac{4}{3}\\\\pi (3)^3 = 18\\\\pi$. Total: $90\\\\pi + 18\\\\pi = 108\\\\pi$ cubic meters, so choice C is correct.\\n\\nChoice A ($90\\\\pi$) is the cylinder alone. Choice B ($99\\\\pi$) treats the top as a cone of height 3: $\\\\frac{1}{3}\\\\pi(3)^2(3) = 9\\\\pi$. Choice D ($135\\\\pi$) uses the full 13 meters as the height of the cylinder: $117\\\\pi + 18\\\\pi$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Yarim sferaning balandligi uning radiusiga, ya’ni 3 metrga teng, shuning uchun silindrning balandligi $13 - 3 = 10$ metr. Silindr: $\\\\pi (3)^2 (10) = 90\\\\pi$. Yarim sfera: $\\\\frac{1}{2} \\\\cdot \\\\frac{4}{3}\\\\pi (3)^3 = 18\\\\pi$. Jami: $90\\\\pi + 18\\\\pi = 108\\\\pi$ kub metr, demak to‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\nA ($90\\\\pi$) — faqat silindr hajmi. B ($99\\\\pi$) — tepa qism balandligi 3 bo‘lgan konus deb olingan: $\\\\frac{1}{3}\\\\pi(3)^2(3) = 9\\\\pi$. D ($135\\\\pi$) — silindr balandligi sifatida butun 13 metr olingan: $117\\\\pi + 18\\\\pi$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"area_volume\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"On a map, 1 centimeter represents an actual distance of 200 meters. A farm plot is shown on the map as a region with an area of 7.5 square centimeters. If 1 hectare equals 10,000 square meters, what is the actual area, in hectares, of the farm plot?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"30\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Lengths scale by 200, so areas scale by $200^2$: 1 square centimeter on the map represents $200 \\\\cdot 200 = 40{,}000$ square meters. The actual area is $7.5 \\\\cdot 40{,}000 = 300{,}000$ square meters, which is $\\\\frac{300{,}000}{10{,}000} = 30$ hectares.\\n\\nCommon mistake: multiplying by 200 only once gives $7.5 \\\\cdot 200 = 1{,}500$ square meters, or 0.15 hectare. Areas must be scaled by the square of the length scale.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Uzunliklar 200 marta kattalashadi, demak yuzalar $200^2$ marta kattalashadi: xaritadagi 1 kvadrat santimetr $200 \\\\cdot 200 = 40{,}000$ kvadrat metrga mos keladi. Haqiqiy yuza $7.5 \\\\cdot 40{,}000 = 300{,}000$ kvadrat metr, ya’ni $\\\\frac{300{,}000}{10{,}000} = 30$ gektar.\\n\\nKo‘p uchraydigan xato: faqat bir marta 200 ga ko‘paytirish $7.5 \\\\cdot 200 = 1{,}500$ kvadrat metr, ya’ni 0.15 gektar beradi. Yuzalar uzunlik masshtabining kvadratiga ko‘paytirilishi kerak.\",\"estimated_seconds\":115,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The rings visible in a cross section of a tree ______ allow scientists to estimate the tree's age. In temperate climates, most trees add one new ring each year, with the newest ring forming just beneath the bark.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"trunk\",\"option_b\":\"trunk,\",\"option_c\":\"trunk;\",\"option_d\":\"trunk:\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The subject of the first sentence is “The rings visible in a cross section of a tree trunk,” and its verb is “allow.” No punctuation should separate a subject from its verb, so choice A is correct.\\n\\nChoice B places a comma between the subject and the verb. Choice C's semicolon should join two independent clauses, but “The rings visible in a cross section of a tree trunk” is not an independent clause. Choice D's colon must follow an independent clause, and it would also separate the subject from its verb.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi gapning egasi — “The rings visible in a cross section of a tree trunk”, kesimi — “allow”. Ega va kesim orasiga tinish belgisi qo‘yilmaydi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob A.\\n\\nB ega bilan kesim orasiga vergul qo‘yadi. C dagi nuqtali vergul ikki mustaqil gapni bog‘lashi kerak, lekin “The rings visible in a cross section of a tree trunk” mustaqil gap emas. D dagi ikki nuqta mustaqil gapdan keyin kelishi kerak, bundan tashqari u ham egani kesimdan ajratib qo‘yadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The Arctic fox changes its coat with the ______ winter, its thick white fur blends in with the snow, while in summer a thinner brownish-gray coat matches the rocks and plants of the tundra.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"seasons, in\",\"option_b\":\"seasons in\",\"option_c\":\"seasons. In\",\"option_d\":\"seasons, which in\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"“The Arctic fox changes its coat with the seasons” is an independent clause, and “In winter, its thick white fur blends in with the snow, while in summer…” is another. Choice C correctly separates them with a period, making two complete sentences.\\n\\nChoice A joins the two independent clauses with only a comma, creating a comma splice. Choice B joins them with no punctuation at all, creating a run-on sentence and making “in winter” seem to belong to the first clause. Choice D introduces “which,” but the words that follow do not form a grammatical clause, so the sentence becomes garbled.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“The Arctic fox changes its coat with the seasons” — mustaqil gap, “In winter, its thick white fur blends in with the snow, while in summer…” — yana bir mustaqil gap. C ularni nuqta bilan to‘g‘ri ajratib, ikkita to‘liq gap hosil qiladi.\\n\\nA ikki mustaqil gapni faqat vergul bilan bog‘laydi — bu “comma splice” xatosi. B ularni hech qanday belgisiz qo‘shadi, natijada qo‘shilib ketgan gap (run-on) hosil bo‘ladi va “in winter” birinchi gapga tegishlidek ko‘rinadi. D “which” so‘zini kiritadi, lekin undan keyingi so‘zlar grammatik jihatdan to‘g‘ri ergash gap hosil qilmaydi, gap buziladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Baikonur Cosmodrome, a spaceport on the steppe of Kazakhstan, was the launch site of two historic missions of the 1950s and ______ Sputnik 1 in 1957 and Vostok 1 in 1961. The second of these carried Yuri Gagarin, the first person to travel into space.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"1960s;\",\"option_b\":\"1960s:\",\"option_c\":\"1960s\",\"option_d\":\"1960s, these were\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first part of the sentence, ending with “two historic missions of the 1950s and 1960s,” is an independent clause, and what follows names those two missions. A colon is used after an independent clause to introduce a list or explanation of what was just mentioned, so choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice A's semicolon must join two independent clauses, but “Sputnik 1 in 1957 and Vostok 1 in 1961” is not an independent clause. Choice C has no punctuation, so the names of the missions run directly into “1960s.” Choice D joins two independent clauses with only a comma, creating a comma splice.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gapning “two historic missions of the 1950s and 1960s” bilan tugaydigan birinchi qismi mustaqil gap, undan keyin esa o‘sha ikki parvoz nomlanadi. Ikki nuqta mustaqil gapdan keyin qo‘yilib, hozirgina aytilgan narsaning ro‘yxati yoki izohini kiritadi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\nA dagi nuqtali vergul ikki mustaqil gapni bog‘lashi kerak, lekin “Sputnik 1 in 1957 and Vostok 1 in 1961” mustaqil gap emas. C da tinish belgisi yo‘q, parvozlar nomi “1960s” ga qo‘shilib ketadi. D ikki mustaqil gapni faqat vergul bilan bog‘laydi — bu “comma splice” xatosi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Many of the tulips grown in gardens today are descended from wild species native to the mountains of Central ______ the flowers were grown in Persia by the tenth century and reached Europe in the mid-sixteenth century.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"Asia,\",\"option_b\":\"Asia\",\"option_c\":\"Asia, later\",\"option_d\":\"Asia;\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"“Many of the tulips grown in gardens today are descended from wild species native to the mountains of Central Asia” and “the flowers were grown in Persia by the tenth century and reached Europe in the mid-sixteenth century” are both independent clauses. A semicolon can join two closely related independent clauses, so choice D is correct.\\n\\nChoice A joins the clauses with only a comma, creating a comma splice. Choice B uses no punctuation, creating a run-on sentence. Choice C adds “later,” but “later” is an adverb, not a conjunction, so the clauses are still joined by only a comma.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“Many of the tulips grown in gardens today are descended from wild species native to the mountains of Central Asia” va “the flowers were grown in Persia by the tenth century and reached Europe in the mid-sixteenth century” — ikkalasi ham mustaqil gap. Ma’nosi yaqin ikki mustaqil gapni nuqtali vergul bilan bog‘lash mumkin, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob D.\\n\\nA gaplarni faqat vergul bilan bog‘laydi — “comma splice” xatosi. B da tinish belgisi yo‘q, natijada qo‘shilib ketgan gap (run-on) hosil bo‘ladi. C “later” so‘zini qo‘shadi, lekin “later” bog‘lovchi emas, ravish, shuning uchun gaplar baribir faqat vergul bilan bog‘langan bo‘lib qoladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Ibn Sina—known in Europe by the Latin form of his name, ______ was born around 980 in Afshana, a village near Bukhara. His Canon of Medicine remained a standard medical textbook in European universities for centuries.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"Avicenna—\",\"option_b\":\"Avicenna,\",\"option_c\":\"Avicenna\",\"option_d\":\"Avicenna;\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The phrase “known in Europe by the Latin form of his name, Avicenna” is extra information about Ibn Sina that interrupts the sentence between the subject and the verb “was born.” It begins with a dash, so it must end with a matching dash. Choice A is correct.\\n\\nChoice B closes the interruption with a comma, which does not match the opening dash. Choice C leaves the interruption unclosed, so “Avicenna” runs directly into “was born.” Choice D's semicolon would separate the subject “Ibn Sina” from its verb “was born.”\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“known in Europe by the Latin form of his name, Avicenna” — Ibn Sino haqidagi qo‘shimcha ma’lumot bo‘lib, gapni ega bilan “was born” kesimi orasida bo‘lib turadi. U tire bilan boshlangan, demak u xuddi shunday tire bilan yopilishi kerak. To‘g‘ri javob A.\\n\\nB qo‘shimcha qismni vergul bilan yopadi — bu ochuvchi tirega mos emas. C qo‘shimcha qismni yopmaydi, natijada “Avicenna” to‘g‘ridan-to‘g‘ri “was born” ga qo‘shilib ketadi. D dagi nuqtali vergul “Ibn Sina” egasini “was born” kesimidan ajratib qo‘yadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Uzbek ______ Abdulla Qodiriy wrote O‘tkan kunlar (Bygone Days), a love story set in Tashkent and Margilan in the nineteenth century. Published in the 1920s, the book is widely considered the first full-length novel in the Uzbek language.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"novelist,\",\"option_b\":\"novelist—\",\"option_c\":\"novelist\",\"option_d\":\"novelist:\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"“Uzbek novelist” is a title that comes directly before the name “Abdulla Qodiriy” and identifies him. Because the title and the name work together as the subject of the sentence, no punctuation should separate them. Choice C is correct.\\n\\nChoices A, B, and D each insert punctuation—a comma, a dash, or a colon—between the title and the name it identifies, which incorrectly separates the parts of the subject.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“Uzbek novelist” — “Abdulla Qodiriy” ismidan oldin keladigan va uni tanishtiradigan unvon. Unvon va ism birgalikda gapning egasini tashkil qilgani uchun ular orasiga tinish belgisi qo‘yilmaydi. To‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\nA, B va D unvon bilan ism orasiga tinish belgisi — vergul, tire yoki ikki nuqta — qo‘yadi; bu eganing qismlarini noto‘g‘ri ajratib qo‘yadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The Kalyan Minaret in Bukhara, built in 1127 on the orders of the Karakhanid ruler Arslan ______ is said to have so impressed Genghis Khan that he ordered it spared when his army destroyed much of the city about a century later.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"Khan—\",\"option_b\":\"Khan;\",\"option_c\":\"Khan\",\"option_d\":\"Khan,\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The phrase “built in 1127 on the orders of the Karakhanid ruler Arslan Khan” describes the minaret and interrupts the sentence between the subject (“The Kalyan Minaret in Bukhara”) and the verb (“is said”). The phrase opens with a comma after “Bukhara,” so it must close with a comma as well. Choice D is correct.\\n\\nChoice A closes the phrase with a dash, which does not match the opening comma. Choice B's semicolon would separate the subject from its verb, and what comes before it is not an independent clause. Choice C leaves the phrase unclosed, so “Arslan Khan” runs directly into “is said.”\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“built in 1127 on the orders of the Karakhanid ruler Arslan Khan” iborasi minorani tasvirlaydi va gapni ega (“The Kalyan Minaret in Bukhara”) bilan kesim (“is said”) orasida bo‘lib turadi. Ibora “Bukhara” dan keyingi vergul bilan ochilgan, demak u vergul bilan yopilishi ham kerak. To‘g‘ri javob D.\\n\\nA iborani tire bilan yopadi — bu ochuvchi vergulga mos emas. B dagi nuqtali vergul egani kesimdan ajratib qo‘yadi, bundan tashqari undan oldingi qism mustaqil gap emas. C iborani yopmaydi, natijada “Arslan Khan” to‘g‘ridan-to‘g‘ri “is said” ga qo‘shilib ketadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"A phone screen produces every color it displays by combining tiny points of just three colors of ______ red, green, and blue—at different levels of brightness.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"light:\",\"option_b\":\"light—\",\"option_c\":\"light,\",\"option_d\":\"light;\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The list “red, green, and blue” interrupts the sentence to name the three colors, and it ends with a dash before “at different levels of brightness.” An interruption that ends with a dash must also begin with a dash, so choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice A's colon cannot pair with the closing dash; a colon introduces material that finishes the sentence, not an interruption that the sentence continues after. Choice C's comma does not match the closing dash and makes it unclear whether “red, green, and blue” names the three colors or continues a longer list. Choice D's semicolon must join two independent clauses, but “red, green, and blue—at different levels of brightness” is not an independent clause.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“red, green, and blue” ro‘yxati gapni bo‘lib, uchta rangni nomlaydi va “at different levels of brightness” dan oldin tire bilan tugaydi. Tire bilan tugaydigan qo‘shimcha qism tire bilan boshlanishi ham kerak, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\nA dagi ikki nuqta yopuvchi tire bilan juftlasha olmaydi: ikki nuqta gapni yakunlovchi qismni kiritadi, gap undan keyin davom etadigan qo‘shimcha qismni emas. C dagi vergul yopuvchi tirega mos emas va “red, green, and blue” uchta rangni nomlayaptimi yoki uzunroq ro‘yxatni davom ettiryaptimi, noaniq qoladi. D dagi nuqtali vergul ikki mustaqil gapni bog‘lashi kerak, lekin “red, green, and blue—at different levels of brightness” mustaqil gap emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from Charlotte Brontë's 1847 novel *Jane Eyre*. The narrator, Jane, is an orphan living with her aunt, Mrs. Reed, and Mrs. Reed's three children: Eliza, John, and Georgiana.\\n\\nThe said Eliza, John, and Georgiana were now clustered round their mama in the drawing-room: she lay reclined on a sofa by the fireside, and with her darlings about her (for the time neither quarrelling nor crying) looked perfectly happy. Me, she had dispensed from joining the group; saying, “She regretted to be under the necessity of keeping me at a distance; but that until she heard from Bessie, and could discover by her own observation, that I was endeavouring in good earnest to acquire a more sociable and childlike disposition, a more attractive and sprightly manner—something lighter, franker, more natural, as it were—she really must exclude me from privileges intended only for contented, happy, little children.”\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"Mrs. Reed keeps Jane apart from the family group until Jane shows a more cheerful and agreeable manner.\",\"option_b\":\"Jane refuses to join her cousins because they are quarrelling and crying.\",\"option_c\":\"Mrs. Reed asks Bessie to keep Jane company while the rest of the family rests by the fire.\",\"option_d\":\"Jane's cousins invite her to sit with them, but she prefers to be alone.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Mrs. Reed sits happily with her own children but has “dispensed” Jane from “joining the group.” She says she “must exclude” Jane until she sees that Jane is trying to acquire “a more sociable and childlike disposition.” Choice A states this main idea.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because the cousins are “neither quarrelling nor crying,” and it is Mrs. Reed, not Jane, who keeps Jane away. Choice C is wrong because Bessie is mentioned only as someone who will report on Jane's behavior, not as her companion. Choice D is wrong because no one invites Jane; she is excluded.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Rid xonim o‘z farzandlari bilan baxtiyor o‘tiradi, ammo Jeynni “davraga qo‘shilishdan ozod qilgan.” U Jeyn “ko‘proq dilkash va bolalarga xos fe’l-atvor” orttirishga harakat qilayotganini ko‘rmaguncha uni “chetlatishga majbur”ligini aytadi. A javob shu asosiy g‘oyani ifodalaydi.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: amakivachchalar “na janjallashyapti, na yig‘layapti,” Jeynni esa o‘zi emas, Rid xonim chetda ushlab turibdi. C noto‘g‘ri: Bessi faqat Jeynning xulqi haqida xabar beradigan odam sifatida tilga olinadi, uning hamrohi sifatida emas. D noto‘g‘ri: Jeynni hech kim taklif qilmaydi — u chetlatilgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from Willa Cather's 1913 novel *O Pioneers!* The characters are traveling home from a small town across the Nebraska prairie.\\n\\nThe little town behind them had vanished as if it had never been, had fallen behind the swell of the prairie, and the stern frozen country received them into its bosom. The homesteads were few and far apart; here and there a windmill gaunt against the sky, a sod house crouching in a hollow. But the great fact was the land itself, which seemed to overwhelm the little beginnings of human society that struggled in its sombre wastes. It was from facing this vast hardness that the boy’s mouth had become so bitter; because he felt that men were too weak to make any mark here, that the land wanted to be let alone, to preserve its own fierce strength, its peculiar, savage kind of beauty, its uninterrupted mournfulness.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"The boy is excited about building a farm that will transform the prairie.\",\"option_b\":\"So many homesteads have been built that the prairie is becoming crowded.\",\"option_c\":\"The vast, harsh prairie seems to overwhelm the settlers' small efforts, leaving the boy convinced that people cannot change it.\",\"option_d\":\"The boy thinks that because the prairie is beautiful, it will be easy to farm.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text calls the land “the great fact,” says it “seemed to overwhelm the little beginnings of human society,” and explains that the boy felt “men were too weak to make any mark here.” Choice C captures this main idea.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the boy is bitter, not excited, and believes people cannot change the land. Choice B is wrong because the homesteads are “few and far apart.” Choice D is wrong because, although the boy sees a “savage kind of beauty” in the land, he associates it with “fierce strength” and “vast hardness,” not with easy farming.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda yer “eng katta haqiqat” deb ataladi, u “inson jamiyatining kichik boshlanishlarini bosib ketayotgandek” tuyulgani va bola “bu yerda odamlar iz qoldirishga juda ojiz” deb his qilgani aytiladi. C javob shu asosiy g‘oyani ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: bola hayajonlangan emas, balki tushkun va odamlar bu yerni o‘zgartira olmaydi deb hisoblaydi. B noto‘g‘ri: fermer xo‘jaliklari “kam va bir-biridan uzoqda.” D noto‘g‘ri: bola yerda “yovvoyi go‘zallik”ni ko‘rsa ham, uni “shiddatli kuch” va “ulkan qattiqlik” bilan bog‘laydi, dehqonchilik osonligi bilan emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In 2019, volunteers in the town of Chorvoq started a monthly “repair café.” On the first Saturday of each month, people bring broken lamps, radios, bicycles, and clothing to the town library, where volunteers with tools fix them for free. Organizers estimate that the event has already kept hundreds of items out of the town dump. Just as important, they say, visitors are encouraged to watch and help with the repairs, so many now leave knowing how to fix simple problems on their own.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"The town library in Chorvoq has started selling tools to residents.\",\"option_b\":\"Chorvoq's repair café reduces waste and also teaches residents how to make repairs themselves.\",\"option_c\":\"Most of the items brought to Chorvoq's repair café cannot be fixed.\",\"option_d\":\"Volunteers in Chorvoq prefer repairing bicycles to repairing clothing.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text describes the repair café's two benefits: it has “kept hundreds of items out of the town dump,” and visitors often “leave knowing how to fix simple problems on their own.” Choice B combines both.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the library hosts the event; nothing suggests it sells tools. Choice C is wrong because the text indicates that many items are repaired and kept out of the dump. Choice D is wrong because the text lists several kinds of items without saying the volunteers prefer any of them.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda “ta’mirlash kafesi”ning ikki foydasi tasvirlanadi: u “yuzlab buyumlarning shahar axlatxonasiga tushishining oldini olgan,” tashrif buyuruvchilar esa ko‘pincha “oddiy nosozliklarni o‘zlari tuzatishni o‘rganib ketishadi.” B javob ikkalasini ham qamrab oladi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: kutubxona faqat tadbirga joy beradi, asbob sotishi haqida gap yo‘q. C noto‘g‘ri: matndan ko‘plab buyumlar tuzatilib, axlatxonaga tushmagani ma’lum. D noto‘g‘ri: matnda bir necha xil buyum sanab o‘tiladi, ammo ko‘ngillilar ulardan birini afzal ko‘rishi aytilmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from W. E. B. Du Bois's 1903 book *The Souls of Black Folk*.\\n\\nAfter the Egyptian and Indian, the Negro is a sort of seventh son, born with a veil, and gifted with second-sight in this American world,—a world which yields him no true self-consciousness, but only lets him see himself through the revelation of the other world. It is a peculiar sensation, this double-consciousness, this sense of always looking at one’s self through the eyes of others, of measuring one’s soul by the tape of a world that looks on in amused contempt and pity. One ever feels his twoness,—an American, a Negro; two souls, two thoughts, two unreconciled strivings; two warring ideals in one dark body, whose dogged strength alone keeps it from being torn asunder.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"Black Americans have fully reconciled their identities as Americans and as people of African descent.\",\"option_b\":\"Most white Americans regard Black Americans with admiration and respect.\",\"option_c\":\"The “second-sight” that Black Americans possess gives them an advantage that outweighs any hardship.\",\"option_d\":\"Being forced to see oneself through the scornful eyes of others leaves Black Americans with a painful sense of being divided between two identities.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Du Bois describes “double-consciousness” as “always looking at one’s self through the eyes of others,” a world that responds with “amused contempt and pity.” The result is “twoness”: “two unreconciled strivings,” “two warring ideals” that threaten to tear one apart. Choice D captures this main idea.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because Du Bois calls the two strivings “unreconciled” and “warring.” Choice B is wrong because the other world looks on with “contempt and pity,” not admiration. Choice C is wrong because, although Du Bois uses the phrase “gifted with second-sight,” he goes on to describe this vision as a source of strain, since it gives “no true self-consciousness”; he never says it outweighs the hardship.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Dyu Bua “ikki tomonlama ong”ni (double-consciousness) “o‘zingga doim boshqalarning ko‘zi bilan qarash” deb ta’riflaydi, bu dunyo esa “masxaraomuz nafrat va achinish” bilan qaraydi. Natijada “ikkilik” paydo bo‘ladi: “ikki murosasiz intilish,” insonni parchalab yuborishi mumkin bo‘lgan “ikki kurashuvchi ideal.” D javob shu asosiy g‘oyani ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: Dyu Bua bu ikki intilishni “murosasiz” va “bir-biri bilan kurashuvchi” deb ataydi. B noto‘g‘ri: boshqa dunyo hayrat bilan emas, “nafrat va achinish” bilan qaraydi. C noto‘g‘ri: Dyu Bua “ikkinchi ko‘rish qobiliyati bilan siylangan” iborasini ishlatsa ham, keyin bu ko‘rish “haqiqiy o‘zlikni anglash” bermasligini va og‘ir zo‘riqish manbai ekanini tasvirlaydi; u bu qiyinchilikdan ustun turadi demaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from Henry David Thoreau's 1862 essay “Walking.”\\n\\nIt is true, we are but faint-hearted crusaders, even the walkers, nowadays, who undertake no persevering, never-ending enterprises. Our expeditions are but tours, and come round again at evening to the old hearth-side from which we set out. Half the walk is but retracing our steps. We should go forth on the shortest walk, perchance, in the spirit of undying adventure, never to return,—prepared to send back our embalmed hearts only as relics to our desolate kingdoms. If you are ready to leave father and mother, and brother and sister, and wife and child and friends, and never see them again,—if you have paid your debts, and made your will, and settled all your affairs, and are a free man; then you are ready for a walk.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"Thoreau recommends that walkers plan their routes so that they can return home before evening.\",\"option_b\":\"Thoreau argues that true walking demands a spirit of total adventure and freedom that most walkers of his day lack.\",\"option_c\":\"Thoreau warns that walking is so dangerous that only people without families should attempt it.\",\"option_d\":\"Thoreau describes the history of the crusades and their influence on travel in his time.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Thoreau complains that modern walkers are “faint-hearted crusaders” whose walks are mere “tours” that return home. He says one should walk “in the spirit of undying adventure,” free of all ties. Choice B states this main idea.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because Thoreau criticizes walks that “come round again at evening” to the hearth. Choice C is wrong because it reads Thoreau's exaggeration too literally: he uses the image of leaving family and settling one's affairs to describe the complete freedom and commitment a walker should have, not to warn of physical danger. Choice D is wrong because “crusaders” is a metaphor for walkers; the text does not discuss the history of the crusades.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Toro hozirgi sayr qiluvchilarni “yuragi zaif salibchilar” deb ataydi: ularning sayrlari kechqurun uyga qaytadigan oddiy “sayohat”lardir. Uning fikricha, sayrga “so‘nmas sarguzasht ruhi” bilan, hech qanday bog‘liqliksiz chiqish kerak. B javob shu asosiy g‘oyani ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: Toro aynan kechqurun o‘choq boshiga qaytadigan sayrlarni tanqid qiladi. C noto‘g‘ri: bu Toroning mubolag‘asini so‘zma-so‘z tushunish; u oiladan ketish va ishlarni tartibga keltirish obrazidan sayr qiluvchida bo‘lishi kerak bo‘lgan to‘liq erkinlik va qat’iyatni tasvirlash uchun foydalanadi, jismoniy xavf haqida ogohlantirish uchun emas. D noto‘g‘ri: “salibchilar” — sayr qiluvchilar uchun metafora; matnda salib yurishlari tarixi muhokama qilinmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Earthquakes are usually detected by seismometers, sensitive instruments that are expensive to install and are often spaced many kilometers apart. Geophysicist Laylo Mirzaeva and her team tested whether the fiber-optic cables that already carry internet data beneath city streets could serve the same purpose. By sending laser pulses through a 20-kilometer cable and measuring tiny changes in the light that bounced back, the team detected several small tremors that the nearest seismometer, 15 kilometers away, had not recorded. Mirzaeva notes that such cables already run beneath most large cities.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"Seismometers are no longer needed because fiber-optic cables can detect every earthquake.\",\"option_b\":\"Laser pulses sent through fiber-optic cables can damage internet service during earthquakes.\",\"option_c\":\"Existing fiber-optic cables may offer a widespread and sensitive way to detect earthquakes.\",\"option_d\":\"Small tremors occur more often near large cities than in rural areas.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Mirzaeva's team used an existing internet cable to detect tremors that a seismometer missed, and she points out that such cables “already run beneath most large cities.” The main idea is that these cables could provide a widespread, sensitive means of detecting earthquakes, as choice C says.\\n\\nChoice A overstates the findings: one test detected tremors a single distant seismometer missed, which does not show that seismometers are unnecessary or that cables detect every earthquake. Choice B is wrong because the text never mentions damage to internet service. Choice D is wrong because the text does not compare how often tremors occur in different places.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Mirzayeva jamoasi mavjud internet kabelidan foydalanib, seysmometr qayd etmagan silkinishlarni aniqlagan. U bunday kabellar “aksariyat yirik shaharlar ostidan allaqachon o‘tganini” ta’kidlaydi. Asosiy g‘oya — bu kabellar zilzilalarni aniqlashning keng tarqalgan va sezgir vositasi bo‘lishi mumkin. To‘g‘ri javob — C.\\n\\nA natijalarni bo‘rttiradi: bitta sinovda uzoqdagi bitta seysmometr qayd etmagan silkinishlar aniqlangan, bu seysmometrlar keraksiz yoki kabellar har bir zilzilani aniqlaydi degani emas. B noto‘g‘ri: matnda internet xizmatiga zarar haqida gap yo‘q. D noto‘g‘ri: matnda turli joylarda silkinishlar qanchalik tez-tez bo‘lishi solishtirilmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from Jane Austen's 1813 novel *Pride and Prejudice*. Mr. Bingley has arrived at a ball with his sisters, his brother-in-law Mr. Hurst, and his friend Mr. Darcy.\\n\\nHis brother-in-law, Mr. Hurst, merely looked the gentleman; but his friend Mr. Darcy soon drew the attention of the room by his fine, tall person, handsome features, noble mien, and the report, which was in general circulation within five minutes after his entrance, of his having ten thousand a year. The gentlemen pronounced him to be a fine figure of a man, the ladies declared he was much handsomer than Mr. Bingley, and he was looked at with great admiration for about half the evening, till his manners gave a disgust which turned the tide of his popularity; for he was discovered to be proud, to be above his company, and above being pleased; and not all his large estate in Derbyshire could save him from having a most forbidding, disagreeable countenance, and being unworthy to be compared with his friend.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes how the narrator portrays the guests' judgment of Mr. Darcy?\",\"option_a\":\"As fair and carefully reasoned, since the guests wait until they know Mr. Darcy well before forming an opinion\",\"option_b\":\"As based entirely on his wealth, which the guests continue to admire throughout the evening\",\"option_c\":\"As consistently negative from the moment Mr. Darcy enters the room\",\"option_d\":\"As shifting from admiration to dislike so completely that even the guests' opinion of his looks is reversed\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"At first the guests admire Mr. Darcy's “fine, tall person” and “handsome features,” and the ladies find him “much handsomer than Mr. Bingley.” Once his manners make him seem proud, the “tide of his popularity” turns, and suddenly he has “a most forbidding, disagreeable countenance” (face) and is “unworthy to be compared with his friend.” The narrator shows the guests' judgment swinging so far that even their view of his appearance flips, as choice D says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the guests form opinions within minutes, based on rumor and first impressions. Choice B is wrong because the narrator says that “not all his large estate” could save his reputation, so wealth does not keep their admiration. Choice C is wrong because the guests look at him “with great admiration for about half the evening.”\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Avvaliga mehmonlar janob Darsining “kelishgan, baland qaddi” va “chiroyli yuz tuzilishi”ga mahliyo bo‘lishadi, xonimlar uni “janob Bingliydan ancha chiroyliroq” deb topishadi. Ammo xulq-atvori uni mag‘rur qilib ko‘rsatgach, “uning shuhrati oqimi teskari burilib ketadi” va birdan u “juda sovuq, yoqimsiz qiyofali” hamda “do‘sti bilan solishtirishga ham arzimaydigan” bo‘lib qoladi. Hikoyachi mehmonlar fikri shu darajada o‘zgarib ketganini, hatto uning tashqi ko‘rinishiga bahosi ham teskarisiga aylanganini ko‘rsatadi. To‘g‘ri javob — D.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: mehmonlar bir necha daqiqa ichida, mish-mishlar va birinchi taassurot asosida fikr bildirishadi. B noto‘g‘ri: hikoyachi “uning katta mulki ham” obro‘sini saqlab qola olmaganini aytadi, demak boylik hayratni saqlab qolmagan. C noto‘g‘ri: mehmonlar unga “kechaning yarmigacha katta hayrat bilan” qarashgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Linguists have often assumed that when two languages are spoken side by side, the language of the more powerful group gradually replaces the other. Studying three centuries of records from a river port where traders speaking two languages lived together, linguist Dilfuza Hamroyeva found a different pattern. Neither language disappeared; instead, each borrowed hundreds of words from the other, and many residents switched between the two depending on whether they were at the market or at home. Hamroyeva cautions, however, that the port may have been unusual, since neither group ever held clear political control over the other.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"Hamroyeva's study of one port suggests that languages in contact do not always replace one another, although conditions at that port may have been unusual.\",\"option_b\":\"Hamroyeva's study proves that languages in contact never replace one another.\",\"option_c\":\"At the river port, the language of the more powerful group gradually replaced the other language.\",\"option_d\":\"Residents of the river port preferred to speak one language at home and avoided it at the market.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text contrasts an assumption (the more powerful group's language replaces the other) with Hamroyeva's finding that, at one port, both languages survived and borrowed from each other. It ends with her caution that the port “may have been unusual.” Choice A captures both the finding and its limitation.\\n\\nChoice B overstates the conclusion: Hamroyeva studied one port and herself warns it may be unusual, so she does not claim replacement “never” happens. Choice C contradicts the text, which says “neither language disappeared.” Choice D distorts a detail: residents switched languages depending on the setting, but the text does not say they avoided either language anywhere, and this detail is not the main idea.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matn bir taxminni (kuchliroq guruhning tili boshqasining o‘rnini egallaydi) Hamroyevaning bir portdagi topilmasi bilan qarama-qarshi qo‘yadi: u yerda ikkala til ham saqlanib qolgan va bir-biridan so‘z olgan. Matn uning port “odatdagidan farqli bo‘lgan bo‘lishi mumkin” degan ehtiyotkor izohi bilan tugaydi. A javob ham topilmani, ham uning cheklovini qamrab oladi.\\n\\nB xulosani bo‘rttiradi: Hamroyeva faqat bitta portni o‘rgangan va o‘zi u odatiy bo‘lmasligi mumkinligini aytadi, demak u almashinuv “hech qachon” bo‘lmaydi demaydi. C matnga zid: unda “hech bir til yo‘qolmagan” deyilgan. D tafsilotni buzib ko‘rsatadi: aholi vaziyatga qarab tilni almashtirgan, lekin matnda ular biror joyda biror tildan qochgani aytilmagan; bundan tashqari, bu tafsilot asosiy g‘oya emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"circles\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A circular fountain has a diameter of 14 meters. A decorative stone border runs once around the outer edge of the fountain. What is the length, in meters, of the border?\",\"option_a\":\"$14\\\\pi$\",\"option_b\":\"$28\\\\pi$\",\"option_c\":\"$49\\\\pi$\",\"option_d\":\"$196\\\\pi$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The border is the circumference of the circle: $C = \\\\pi d = \\\\pi (14) = 14\\\\pi$ meters (equivalently, $2\\\\pi r$ with $r = 7$). Choice A is correct.\\n\\nChoice B ($28\\\\pi$) is $2\\\\pi(14)$; it uses the diameter as the radius. Choice C ($49\\\\pi$) is $\\\\pi(7)^2$, the area of the fountain, not the length around it. Choice D ($196\\\\pi$) is $\\\\pi(14)^2$, an area computed with the diameter in place of the radius.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Hoshiya — bu aylananing uzunligi: $C = \\\\pi d = \\\\pi (14) = 14\\\\pi$ metr (yoki $r = 7$ bilan $2\\\\pi r$). To‘g‘ri javob A.\\n\\nB ($28\\\\pi$) — bu $2\\\\pi(14)$; diametr radius sifatida olingan. C ($49\\\\pi$) — bu $\\\\pi(7)^2$, ya’ni favvoraning yuzasi, atrofining uzunligi emas. D ($196\\\\pi$) — bu $\\\\pi(14)^2$, radius o‘rniga diametr bilan hisoblangan yuza.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"circles\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"The minute hand of a wall clock is 15 centimeters long. In 20 minutes, the tip of the minute hand travels along an arc of length $k\\\\pi$ centimeters. What is the value of $k$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"10\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The minute hand makes a full turn ($360^\\\\circ$) in 60 minutes, so in 20 minutes it turns $\\\\frac{20}{60} = \\\\frac{1}{3}$ of a full turn. The full circle traced by the tip has circumference $2\\\\pi(15) = 30\\\\pi$ centimeters, so the arc is $\\\\frac{1}{3} \\\\cdot 30\\\\pi = 10\\\\pi$ centimeters, and $k = 10$.\\n\\nIf you got 75, you found the area of the sector, $\\\\frac{1}{3}\\\\pi(15)^2 = 75\\\\pi$, instead of the arc length. If you got 5, you used $\\\\pi r$ instead of $2\\\\pi r$ for the circumference.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Minut mili to‘liq aylanishni ($360^\\\\circ$) 60 daqiqada bajaradi, demak 20 daqiqada u to‘liq aylanishning $\\\\frac{20}{60} = \\\\frac{1}{3}$ qismiga buriladi. Mil uchi chizadigan aylananing uzunligi $2\\\\pi(15) = 30\\\\pi$ santimetr, shuning uchun yoy uzunligi $\\\\frac{1}{3} \\\\cdot 30\\\\pi = 10\\\\pi$ santimetr va $k = 10$.\\n\\nAgar 75 chiqqan bo‘lsa, siz yoy uzunligi o‘rniga sektor yuzasini, $\\\\frac{1}{3}\\\\pi(15)^2 = 75\\\\pi$ ni topgansiz. Agar 5 chiqqan bo‘lsa, aylana uzunligi uchun $2\\\\pi r$ o‘rniga $\\\\pi r$ olingan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":65,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"circles\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"In the $xy$-plane, the graph of $(x - 5)^2 + (y + 2)^2 = 49$ is a circle. Which of the following gives the center and the radius of the circle?\",\"option_a\":\"Center $(-5, 2)$, radius 7\",\"option_b\":\"Center $(-5, 2)$, radius 49\",\"option_c\":\"Center $(5, -2)$, radius 49\",\"option_d\":\"Center $(5, -2)$, radius 7\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"A circle with center $(h, k)$ and radius $r$ has equation $(x - h)^2 + (y - k)^2 = r^2$. Writing $(y + 2)$ as $(y - (-2))$ shows that $h = 5$ and $k = -2$, and $r^2 = 49$ gives $r = 7$. Choice D is correct.\\n\\nChoices A and B flip the signs of the center coordinates; the numbers inside the parentheses are subtracted from $x$ and $y$. Choices B and C give 49 as the radius, but 49 is $r^2$, not $r$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Markazi $(h, k)$ va radiusi $r$ bo‘lgan aylana tenglamasi $(x - h)^2 + (y - k)^2 = r^2$ ko‘rinishida bo‘ladi. $(y + 2)$ ni $(y - (-2))$ deb yozsak, $h = 5$ va $k = -2$ ekani ko‘rinadi, $r^2 = 49$ dan esa $r = 7$. To‘g‘ri javob D.\\n\\nA va B variantlarida markaz koordinatalarining ishoralari almashtirilgan; qavs ichidagi sonlar $x$ va $y$ dan ayiriladi. B va C variantlarida radius 49 deb olingan, lekin 49 — bu $r$ emas, $r^2$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"circles\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A rotating sprinkler waters a region of a lawn shaped like a sector of a circle. The radius of the sector is 12 meters, and its central angle measures $150^\\\\circ$. What is the area, in square meters, of the watered region?\",\"option_a\":\"$10\\\\pi$\",\"option_b\":\"$60\\\\pi$\",\"option_c\":\"$72\\\\pi$\",\"option_d\":\"$144\\\\pi$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The sector is $\\\\frac{150}{360} = \\\\frac{5}{12}$ of the whole circle. The whole circle has area $\\\\pi(12)^2 = 144\\\\pi$, so the sector has area $\\\\frac{5}{12} \\\\cdot 144\\\\pi = 60\\\\pi$ square meters. Choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice A ($10\\\\pi$) is $\\\\frac{5}{12} \\\\cdot 24\\\\pi$, the length of the arc, not the area. Choice C ($72\\\\pi$) is the area of a half circle, which would need a $180^\\\\circ$ angle. Choice D ($144\\\\pi$) is the area of the whole circle.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Sektor butun doiraning $\\\\frac{150}{360} = \\\\frac{5}{12}$ qismini tashkil qiladi. Butun doiraning yuzasi $\\\\pi(12)^2 = 144\\\\pi$, demak sektor yuzasi $\\\\frac{5}{12} \\\\cdot 144\\\\pi = 60\\\\pi$ kvadrat metr. To‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\nA ($10\\\\pi$) — bu $\\\\frac{5}{12} \\\\cdot 24\\\\pi$, ya’ni yoy uzunligi, yuza emas. C ($72\\\\pi$) — yarim doira yuzasi, buning uchun burchak $180^\\\\circ$ bo‘lishi kerak edi. D ($144\\\\pi$) — butun doiraning yuzasi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"circles\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"Points $A$, $B$, and $C$ lie on a circle, and $\\\\overline{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle. The measure of angle $CAB$ is $28^\\\\circ$. What is the measure, in degrees, of angle $ABC$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"62\"],\"explanation_en\":\"An angle inscribed in a semicircle is a right angle, so angle $ACB$, which intercepts diameter $\\\\overline{AB}$, measures $90^\\\\circ$. The angles of triangle $ABC$ add to $180^\\\\circ$, so angle $ABC$ measures $180^\\\\circ - 90^\\\\circ - 28^\\\\circ = 62^\\\\circ$.\\n\\nCommon mistakes: 152 comes from $180 - 28$ (forgetting the right angle at $C$), and 56 doubles the $28^\\\\circ$ angle as if it were a central angle.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Yarim aylanaga tiralgan ichki chizilgan burchak to‘g‘ri burchak bo‘ladi, shuning uchun $\\\\overline{AB}$ diametrga tiralgan $ACB$ burchak $90^\\\\circ$ ga teng. $ABC$ uchburchak burchaklarining yig‘indisi $180^\\\\circ$, demak $ABC$ burchak $180^\\\\circ - 90^\\\\circ - 28^\\\\circ = 62^\\\\circ$.\\n\\nKo‘p uchraydigan xatolar: 152 — bu $180 - 28$ ($C$ dagi to‘g‘ri burchak unutilgan), 56 esa $28^\\\\circ$ li burchakni markaziy burchak deb ikkilantirish natijasi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"circles\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A Ferris wheel has a diameter of 40 meters. As the wheel turns, a passenger cabin on its rim moves along an arc of length $10\\\\pi$ meters. Through what angle, in radians, does the wheel turn?\",\"option_a\":\"$\\\\frac{1}{2}$\",\"option_b\":\"$\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{4}$\",\"option_c\":\"$\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}$\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\pi$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The radius is $\\\\frac{40}{2} = 20$ meters. For an angle $\\\\theta$ in radians, arc length is $s = r\\\\theta$, so $\\\\theta = \\\\frac{s}{r} = \\\\frac{10\\\\pi}{20} = \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}$. Choice C is correct.\\n\\nChoice A ($\\\\frac{1}{2}$) drops the $\\\\pi$ from the arc length. Choice B ($\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{4}$) divides by the diameter, 40, instead of the radius. Choice D ($\\\\pi$) compares the arc with $\\\\pi r = 20\\\\pi$ instead of the full circumference $2\\\\pi r = 40\\\\pi$, so it doubles the fraction of a turn.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Radius $\\\\frac{40}{2} = 20$ metr. Radianlarda berilgan $\\\\theta$ burchak uchun yoy uzunligi $s = r\\\\theta$, demak $\\\\theta = \\\\frac{s}{r} = \\\\frac{10\\\\pi}{20} = \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}$. To‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\nA ($\\\\frac{1}{2}$) — yoy uzunligidagi $\\\\pi$ tushirib qoldirilgan. B ($\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{4}$) — radius o‘rniga diametrga (40 ga) bo‘lingan. D ($\\\\pi$) — yoy to‘liq aylana uzunligi $2\\\\pi r = 40\\\\pi$ bilan emas, $\\\\pi r = 20\\\\pi$ bilan solishtirilgan, shuning uchun aylanish ulushi ikki baravar oshib ketgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"circles\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"In the $xy$-plane, a circle has its center at the point $(6, k)$, where $k$ is a positive constant. The radius of the circle is 10, and the circle passes through the origin, $(0, 0)$. Which of the following is an equation of the circle?\",\"option_a\":\"$(x - 6)^2 + (y - 8)^2 = 100$\",\"option_b\":\"$(x + 6)^2 + (y + 8)^2 = 100$\",\"option_c\":\"$(x - 6)^2 + (y - 8)^2 = 10$\",\"option_d\":\"$(x - 8)^2 + (y - 6)^2 = 100$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The distance from the center $(6, k)$ to the origin is the radius, so $6^2 + k^2 = 10^2$, which gives $k^2 = 64$ and, since $k > 0$, $k = 8$. With center $(6, 8)$ and $r^2 = 100$, the equation is $(x - 6)^2 + (y - 8)^2 = 100$. Choice A is correct.\\n\\nChoice B has center $(-6, -8)$; the signs inside the parentheses are reversed. Choice C sets the right side equal to the radius, 10, instead of $r^2 = 100$. Choice D has center $(8, 6)$; that circle also passes through the origin, but its center does not have $x$-coordinate 6.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Markaz $(6, k)$ dan koordinata boshigacha bo‘lgan masofa radiusga teng, demak $6^2 + k^2 = 10^2$, bundan $k^2 = 64$ va $k > 0$ bo‘lgani uchun $k = 8$. Markazi $(6, 8)$ va $r^2 = 100$ bo‘lgani uchun tenglama $(x - 6)^2 + (y - 8)^2 = 100$. To‘g‘ri javob A.\\n\\nB variantda markaz $(-6, -8)$; qavs ichidagi ishoralar teskari olingan. C variantda o‘ng tomonga $r^2 = 100$ o‘rniga radiusning o‘zi, 10 yozilgan. D variantda markaz $(8, 6)$; bu aylana ham koordinata boshidan o‘tadi, lekin uning markazining $x$-koordinatasi 6 emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"circles\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"The entrance arch of a madrasa is shaped like an arc of a circle. The two ends of the arch are on the same horizontal floor and are 16 meters apart. The highest point of the arch is 4 meters above the floor, directly above the midpoint between the two ends. What is the radius, in meters, of the circle that contains the arch?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"10\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Let $r$ be the radius. The center of the circle lies on the vertical line through the highest point, $r$ meters below that point, so it is $r - 4$ meters below the floor. A segment from the center to one end of the arch is a radius, and it is the hypotenuse of a right triangle with legs 8 (half of 16) and $r - 4$. So $r^2 = 8^2 + (r - 4)^2 = 64 + r^2 - 8r + 16$, which gives $8r = 80$ and $r = 10$.\\n\\nCommon mistakes: 8 assumes the arch is a semicircle (half the span), but then its height would be 8, not 4; and $\\\\sqrt{8^2 + 4^2} = \\\\sqrt{80}$ treats the 4-meter height as a leg from the center.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Radiusni $r$ deb olaylik. Aylana markazi eng baland nuqtadan o‘tuvchi vertikal chiziqda, shu nuqtadan $r$ metr pastda yotadi, ya’ni poldan $r - 4$ metr pastda. Markazdan arkaning bir uchigacha bo‘lgan kesma radius bo‘lib, u katetlari 8 (16 ning yarmi) va $r - 4$ bo‘lgan to‘g‘ri burchakli uchburchakning gipotenuzasidir. Demak $r^2 = 8^2 + (r - 4)^2 = 64 + r^2 - 8r + 16$, bundan $8r = 80$ va $r = 10$.\\n\\nKo‘p uchraydigan xatolar: 8 — arkani yarim aylana deb hisoblash (oraliqning yarmi), lekin u holda arkaning balandligi 4 emas, 8 bo‘lardi; $\\\\sqrt{8^2 + 4^2} = \\\\sqrt{80}$ esa 4 metrli balandlikni markazdan chiquvchi katet deb olish natijasi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":130,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"A geography student compared the highest points of four Central Asian countries. She noticed that one country's highest point is far lower than the others: ______\\n\\n**Highest point of four Central Asian countries**\\n\\n| Country | Highest point | Elevation (m) |\\n| --- | --- | ---: |\\n| Kazakhstan | Khan Tengri | 7,010 |\\n| Kyrgyzstan | Jengish Chokusu | 7,439 |\\n| Tajikistan | Ismoil Somoni Peak | 7,495 |\\n| Turkmenistan | Aýrybaba | 3,139 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?\",\"option_a\":\"Tajikistan's Ismoil Somoni Peak rises to 7,495 meters.\",\"option_b\":\"Turkmenistan's Aýrybaba reaches only 3,139 meters, less than half the elevation of any of the other three peaks.\",\"option_c\":\"Kyrgyzstan's Jengish Chokusu reaches 7,439 meters.\",\"option_d\":\"Kazakhstan's Khan Tengri, at 7,010 meters, is the lowest of the four peaks.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The statement says that one country's highest point is far lower than the others. According to the table, Turkmenistan's Aýrybaba is 3,139 meters high, while the other three peaks are all above 7,000 meters; since half of the lowest of them (7,010 ÷ 2 = 3,505) is still greater than 3,139, Aýrybaba is less than half as high as each of them. Choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoices A and C give the elevations of the two highest peaks, which do not show that any peak is far lower than the others. Choice D is inaccurate: Khan Tengri is the lowest of the three tall peaks, but Aýrybaba, at 3,139 meters, is the lowest of the four.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gapda bir davlatning eng baland nuqtasi boshqalarnikidan ancha past ekanligi aytilgan. Jadvalga ko‘ra, Turkmanistondagi Ayribaba 3,139 metr, qolgan uch cho‘qqining har biri esa 7,000 metrdan baland. Ularning eng pastining yarmi ham (7,010 ÷ 2 = 3,505) 3,139 dan katta, demak Ayribaba ularning har biridan ikki baravardan ko‘proq past. To‘g‘ri javob — B.\\n\\nA va C eng baland ikki cho‘qqining balandligini beradi; ular biror cho‘qqi boshqalardan ancha past ekanini ko‘rsatmaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: Xon Tangri uchta baland cho‘qqining eng pasti, lekin to‘rttasi ichida eng pasti 3,139 metrli Ayribaba.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"For one month, students at a school in Andijan recorded how many minutes they spent each day reading for pleasure. Teacher Zarina Usmonova found that the students who read for pleasure the most were ______\\n\\n**Average daily time spent reading for pleasure**\\n\\n| Grade | Average time (minutes per day) |\\n| --- | ---: |\\n| 7 | 22 |\\n| 8 | 18 |\\n| 9 | 27 |\\n| 10 | 34 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?\",\"option_a\":\"seventh graders, who averaged 22 minutes a day.\",\"option_b\":\"eighth graders, who averaged 18 minutes a day.\",\"option_c\":\"ninth graders, who averaged 27 minutes a day.\",\"option_d\":\"tenth graders, who averaged 34 minutes a day.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The statement identifies the students who read for pleasure the most. In the table, tenth graders have the greatest average, 34 minutes per day, so choice D is correct.\\n\\nChoice A names seventh graders, whose average of 22 minutes is lower than that of both ninth and tenth graders. Choice B names eighth graders, who actually read the least (18 minutes). Choice C names ninth graders, whose 27 minutes is the second-highest average, not the highest.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gapda zavq uchun eng ko‘p kitob o‘qigan o‘quvchilar haqida so‘z boradi. Jadvalda eng katta o‘rtacha ko‘rsatkich — kuniga 34 daqiqa — 10-sinf o‘quvchilariga tegishli, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob D.\\n\\nA dagi 7-sinflarning o‘rtacha vaqti (22 daqiqa) 9- va 10-sinflarnikidan kam. B dagi 8-sinflar, aksincha, eng kam o‘qigan (18 daqiqa). C dagi 9-sinflarning 27 daqiqasi eng yuqori emas, ikkinchi o‘rindagi ko‘rsatkich.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In an experiment, biology student Bekzod Tursunov kept 50 mung bean seeds at each of four temperatures and counted how many had sprouted after three days. He reported that the seeds sprouted best at 30°C, far better than they did at the coldest temperature he tested: ______\\n\\n**Mung bean seeds sprouted after three days (out of 50)**\\n\\n| Temperature (°C) | Seeds sprouted |\\n| ---: | ---: |\\n| 10 | 4 |\\n| 20 | 31 |\\n| 30 | 47 |\\n| 40 | 22 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?\",\"option_a\":\"47 of the 50 seeds kept at 30°C sprouted, compared with only 4 of the 50 seeds kept at 10°C.\",\"option_b\":\"31 of the 50 seeds kept at 20°C sprouted.\",\"option_c\":\"22 of the 50 seeds kept at 40°C sprouted, compared with 31 of the 50 seeds kept at 20°C.\",\"option_d\":\"Fewer seeds sprouted at 40°C than at 20°C.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The statement compares sprouting at 30°C with sprouting at the coldest temperature tested, 10°C. Choice A makes exactly this comparison: 47 of 50 seeds sprouted at 30°C, but only 4 of 50 sprouted at 10°C.\\n\\nChoice B gives the result for 20°C only, which is neither 30°C nor the coldest temperature. Choices C and D compare 40°C with 20°C; they mention neither 30°C nor 10°C, so they do not support the statement.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gapda 30°C dagi unib chiqish sinovdagi eng past harorat — 10°C dagisi bilan solishtiriladi. A aynan shu solishtirishni qiladi: 30°C da 50 ta urug‘dan 47 tasi, 10°C da esa atigi 4 tasi unib chiqqan.\\n\\nB faqat 20°C natijasini beradi — bu na 30°C, na eng past harorat. C va D 40°C ni 20°C bilan solishtiradi; ularda 30°C ham, 10°C ham tilga olinmagan, shuning uchun ular gapni qo‘llab-quvvatlamaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Food scientist Madina Qosimova compared three ways of drying apricots: in the open sun, in the shade, and in a solar dryer, a covered box that traps the Sun's heat. Open-sun drying is fast, while shade drying is known to preserve more of the fruit's vitamin C. Qosimova claims that the solar dryer combines these two advantages.\\n\\n**Results of three apricot-drying methods**\\n\\n| Drying method | Drying time (days) | Vitamin C retained (%) |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Open sun | 4 | 18 |\\n| Shade | 9 | 41 |\\n| Solar dryer | 4 | 39 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support Qosimova's claim?\",\"option_a\":\"Apricots dried in the shade retained 41% of their vitamin C, the most of any method.\",\"option_b\":\"Apricots dried in the open sun took 4 days to dry, while those dried in the shade took 9 days.\",\"option_c\":\"Apricots in the solar dryer dried in 4 days, as quickly as those dried in the open sun, and retained 39% of their vitamin C, close to the 41% retained by those dried in the shade.\",\"option_d\":\"Apricots in the solar dryer retained 39% of their vitamin C, while those dried in the open sun retained only 18%.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Qosimova claims that the solar dryer combines the speed of open-sun drying with the vitamin C retention of shade drying. Choice C supports both parts: the solar dryer took 4 days, the same as the open sun, and it retained 39% of the vitamin C, nearly as much as the 41% retained in the shade.\\n\\nChoice A describes only shade drying and says nothing about the solar dryer. Choice B compares the two traditional methods, again without the solar dryer. Choice D shows that the solar dryer retained more vitamin C than open-sun drying, but it says nothing about drying speed, so it supports only part of the claim.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qosimova quyosh quritgichi ochiq quyoshda quritishning tezligini va soyada quritishning C vitaminini saqlab qolish afzalligini birlashtiradi, deb da’vo qiladi. C ikkala qismni ham qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi: quyosh quritgichida o‘rik ochiq quyoshdagidek 4 kunda qurigan va C vitaminining 39% ini saqlab qolgan — bu soyadagi 41% ga juda yaqin.\\n\\nA faqat soyada quritish haqida, quyosh quritgichi haqida hech narsa demaydi. B ham quyosh quritgichisiz ikki an’anaviy usulni solishtiradi. D quyosh quritgichi ochiq quyoshga qaraganda ko‘proq C vitamini saqlaganini ko‘rsatadi, lekin quritish tezligi haqida hech narsa demaydi, ya’ni da’voning faqat bir qismini qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Engineer Sardor Aliyev studied how dust affects rooftop solar panels in the city of Nukus. Over one summer, he compared identical panels that were cleaned every week, cleaned once a month, or never cleaned. Aliyev argues that dust buildup steadily reduces output: as the summer went on, the panels that were never cleaned fell further and further behind the panels that were cleaned every week.\\n\\n**Average daily output of solar panels (kilowatt-hours)**\\n\\n| Cleaning schedule | June | July | August |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Every week | 5.1 | 5.0 | 5.1 |\\n| Once a month | 4.8 | 4.6 | 4.7 |\\n| Never | 4.6 | 4.0 | 3.5 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support Aliyev's argument?\",\"option_a\":\"The gap in average daily output between the panels cleaned every week and the panels never cleaned grew from 0.5 kilowatt-hours in June to 1.0 in July and 1.6 in August.\",\"option_b\":\"The panels cleaned every week produced about the same average daily output in all three months: 5.1, 5.0, and 5.1 kilowatt-hours.\",\"option_c\":\"In June, the panels cleaned once a month produced 4.8 kilowatt-hours per day on average, more than the 4.6 produced by the panels never cleaned.\",\"option_d\":\"The panels cleaned once a month produced less in July (4.6 kilowatt-hours per day) than in June (4.8 kilowatt-hours per day).\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Aliyev argues that the never-cleaned panels fell further and further behind the weekly-cleaned panels as the summer went on. Choice A shows exactly this widening gap: 5.1 − 4.6 = 0.5 in June, 5.0 − 4.0 = 1.0 in July, and 5.1 − 3.5 = 1.6 in August.\\n\\nChoice B describes only the weekly-cleaned panels, so it shows no comparison. Choice C compares the monthly-cleaned and never-cleaned panels in a single month, which cannot show a change over the summer. Choice D describes a small drop for the monthly-cleaned panels between two months; it does not involve the never-cleaned panels or the weekly-cleaned panels.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Aliyevning fikricha, yoz davomida hech tozalanmagan panellar har hafta tozalangan panellardan tobora ko‘proq ortda qolgan. A aynan shu kengayib borayotgan farqni ko‘rsatadi: iyunda 5.1 − 4.6 = 0.5, iyulda 5.0 − 4.0 = 1.0, avgustda 5.1 − 3.5 = 1.6.\\n\\nB faqat har hafta tozalangan panellarni tasvirlaydi, unda solishtirish yo‘q. C oyda bir tozalangan va hech tozalanmagan panellarni faqat bitta oyda solishtiradi — bu yoz davomidagi o‘zgarishni ko‘rsata olmaydi. D oyda bir tozalangan panellarning ikki oy orasidagi kichik pasayishini tasvirlaydi; unda hech tozalanmagan yoki har hafta tozalangan panellar yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Agronomist Feruza Jurayeva grew barley, wheat, and rice in soils with low, medium, and high levels of salt and recorded each crop's yield as a percentage of its yield in salt-free soil. She concludes that barley is the most salt-tolerant of the three crops: even in the saltiest soil she tested, barley's yield fell much less than the yields of the other two crops did.\\n\\n**Crop yield in salty soil (% of yield in salt-free soil)**\\n\\n| Salt level | Barley | Wheat | Rice |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Low | 98 | 95 | 88 |\\n| Medium | 90 | 78 | 60 |\\n| High | 76 | 52 | 31 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support Jurayeva's conclusion?\",\"option_a\":\"In soil with a low salt level, barley produced 98% of its normal yield, while wheat produced 95%.\",\"option_b\":\"Rice's yield dropped from 88% of its normal yield at the low salt level to 31% at the high salt level.\",\"option_c\":\"In soil with a medium salt level, wheat produced 78% of its normal yield.\",\"option_d\":\"In soil with a high salt level, barley still produced 76% of its normal yield, compared with 52% for wheat and 31% for rice.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Jurayeva's conclusion focuses on the saltiest soil: there, barley's yield fell much less than the yields of wheat and rice. Choice D gives the high-salt results for all three crops—76% for barley, 52% for wheat, and 31% for rice—so it directly supports the conclusion.\\n\\nChoice A uses the low-salt results, where barley and wheat differ by only 3 percentage points, so it does not show that barley held up much better. Choice B describes rice alone and does not compare it with barley. Choice C gives a single figure for wheat at the medium salt level and mentions neither barley nor the saltiest soil.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Jurayevaning xulosasi eng sho‘r tuproqqa qaratilgan: u yerda arpaning hosili bug‘doy va sholinikiga qaraganda ancha kam kamaygan. D yuqori sho‘rlikdagi uchala ekin natijasini beradi — arpa 76%, bug‘doy 52%, sholi 31% — shuning uchun xulosani bevosita qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi.\\n\\nA past sho‘rlikdagi natijalardan foydalanadi; u yerda arpa va bug‘doy orasidagi farq atigi 3 foiz punkti, demak arpa ancha chidamliroq ekanini ko‘rsatmaydi. B faqat sholini tasvirlaydi va uni arpa bilan solishtirmaydi. C o‘rtacha sho‘rlikdagi bug‘doy uchun bitta raqamni beradi, unda na arpa, na eng sho‘r tuproq bor.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Irrigation researchers grew cotton on test plots with two types of soil, watering half of the plots on each soil with traditional furrow irrigation and half with drip irrigation, which delivers water directly to each plant's roots. Sandy soil drains quickly, so the researchers hypothesized that switching from furrow irrigation to drip irrigation would raise yields more on sandy soil than on loamy soil.\\n\\n**Average cotton yield (tonnes per hectare)**\\n\\n| Soil type | Furrow irrigation | Drip irrigation |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Sandy | 2.0 | 3.0 |\\n| Loamy | 4.0 | 4.6 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the researchers' hypothesis?\",\"option_a\":\"On loamy soil, drip irrigation produced an average yield of 4.6 tonnes per hectare, the highest yield in the study.\",\"option_b\":\"Switching from furrow to drip irrigation increased the average yield by 1.0 tonne per hectare on sandy soil but by only 0.6 tonne per hectare on loamy soil.\",\"option_c\":\"With furrow irrigation, the average yield on loamy soil was 4.0 tonnes per hectare, twice the 2.0 tonnes per hectare on sandy soil.\",\"option_d\":\"Drip-irrigated plots produced higher average yields than furrow-irrigated plots on both sandy soil and loamy soil.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The hypothesis is about the size of the improvement from switching irrigation methods on each soil: the gain should be larger on sandy soil. Choice B compares exactly those gains: 3.0 − 2.0 = 1.0 tonne per hectare on sandy soil versus 4.6 − 4.0 = 0.6 tonne per hectare on loamy soil.\\n\\nChoice A gives the highest single yield, which comes from loamy soil and says nothing about how much drip irrigation helped on each soil. Choice C compares the two soils under furrow irrigation only. Choice D is true but shows only that drip irrigation helped on both soils, not that it helped more on sandy soil.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gipoteza har bir tuproqda sug‘orish usulini almashtirishdan olingan o‘sish hajmi haqida: qumli tuproqda o‘sish kattaroq bo‘lishi kerak. B aynan shu o‘sishlarni solishtiradi: qumli tuproqda 3.0 − 2.0 = 1.0 tonna/gektar, qumoq tuproqda esa 4.6 − 4.0 = 0.6 tonna/gektar.\\n\\nA eng yuqori bitta hosilni beradi; u qumoq tuproqqa tegishli va tomchilatib sug‘orish har bir tuproqda qanchalik foyda berganini aytmaydi. C ikki tuproqni faqat egat usulida solishtiradi. D to‘g‘ri ma’lumot, lekin u tomchilatib sug‘orish ikkala tuproqda ham foyda berganini ko‘rsatadi xolos, qumli tuproqda ko‘proq foyda berganini emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"A survey asked students at four schools in Fergana whether they would join a new after-school gardening program. Education researcher Otabek Yuldashev cautions against judging the program's popularity by raw counts alone: the school with the greatest number of interested students was not the school where the program was most popular relative to the number of students surveyed.\\n\\n**Interest in an after-school gardening program**\\n\\n| School | Students surveyed | Students who would join |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| School 1 | 400 | 120 |\\n| School 2 | 250 | 100 |\\n| School 3 | 150 | 81 |\\n| School 4 | 600 | 150 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support Yuldashev's point?\",\"option_a\":\"School 4 had 150 students who said they would join the program, more than any other school.\",\"option_b\":\"At School 2, 100 of the 250 students surveyed, or 40 percent, said they would join the program.\",\"option_c\":\"School 4 had the greatest number of students who said they would join (150), but School 3 had the greatest proportion: 54 percent, compared with 25 percent at School 4.\",\"option_d\":\"School 3 had both the fewest students surveyed (150) and the fewest students who said they would join (81).\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Yuldashev's point has two parts: one school had the most interested students, but a different school had the highest proportion of interested students. Choice C supports both parts: School 4 had the most interested students (150), but at School 3, 81 of 150 students (54%) were interested, compared with 150 of 600 (25%) at School 4 and lower proportions at the other schools (30% at School 1 and 40% at School 2).\\n\\nChoice A gives only the raw count, which supports the first part of the point but does not show that another school had a higher proportion of interested students. Choice B gives one school's proportion, which is not the highest. Choice D uses raw counts to make School 3 look like the least interested school, which is the opposite of what Yuldashev's point requires.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Yuldashevning fikri ikki qismdan iborat: bir maktabda qiziqqan o‘quvchilar soni eng ko‘p, lekin qiziqqanlar ulushi eng yuqori bo‘lgan maktab boshqa. C ikkala qismni ham qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi: 4-maktabda qiziqqanlar eng ko‘p (150), ammo 3-maktabda 150 o‘quvchidan 81 tasi (54%) qiziqqan; 4-maktabda bu ko‘rsatkich 600 dan 150 (25%), boshqa maktablarda esa undan ham past (1-maktabda 30%, 2-maktabda 40%).\\n\\nA faqat sonni beradi: u fikrning birinchi qismini qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi, lekin boshqa maktabda qiziqqanlar ulushi yuqoriroq ekanini ko‘rsatmaydi. B bitta maktabning ulushini beradi, lekin u eng yuqori emas. D sonlardan foydalanib 3-maktabni eng kam qiziqqan maktabdek ko‘rsatadi — bu Yuldashevning fikriga zid.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"*Jane Eyre* is an 1847 novel by Charlotte Brontë. In the novel, the narrator, Jane, recalls that as a child she was fascinated by the pictures in a book about birds, even when she could not fully understand them.\",\"stem\":\"Which quotation from *Jane Eyre* most effectively illustrates the claim?\",\"option_a\":\"“At intervals, while turning over the leaves of my book, I studied the aspect of that winter afternoon.”\",\"option_b\":\"“I returned to my book—Bewick’s History of British Birds: the letterpress thereof I cared little for, generally speaking…”\",\"option_c\":\"“Each picture told a story; mysterious often to my undeveloped understanding and imperfect feelings, yet ever profoundly interesting…”\",\"option_d\":\"“A breakfast-room adjoined the drawing-room, I slipped in there.”\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The claim has two parts: Jane was fascinated by the pictures, and she did not always understand them. Choice C matches both: the pictures were “mysterious often to my undeveloped understanding” yet “ever profoundly interesting.”\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because it describes Jane looking at the winter weather, not at the pictures. Choice B is wrong because it concerns the “letterpress” (the printed words), which Jane says she “cared little for”; it says nothing about the pictures. Choice D is wrong because it only describes where Jane went.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Da’vo ikki qismdan iborat: Jeyn rasmlarga mahliyo bo‘lgan va ularni har doim ham tushunavermagan. C javob ikkalasiga ham mos: rasmlar “mening yetilmagan aqlim uchun ko‘pincha sirli,” ammo “doimo nihoyatda qiziqarli” bo‘lgan.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: unda Jeyn rasmlarga emas, qishki ob-havoga qaragani tasvirlangan. B noto‘g‘ri: u kitobning bosma matni (“letterpress”) haqida, Jeyn esa unga “unchalik qiziqmaganini” aytadi; rasmlar haqida hech narsa demaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: u faqat Jeyn qayerga borganini tasvirlaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"*Pride and Prejudice* is an 1813 novel by Jane Austen. In the novel, the narrator suggests that Mrs. Bennet is chiefly concerned with finding husbands for her daughters.\",\"stem\":\"Which quotation from *Pride and Prejudice* most effectively illustrates the claim?\",\"option_a\":\"“The business of her life was to get her daughters married: its solace was visiting and news.”\",\"option_b\":\"“When she was discontented, she fancied herself nervous.”\",\"option_c\":\"“Mr. Bingley was good-looking and gentlemanlike: he had a pleasant countenance, and easy, unaffected manners.”\",\"option_d\":\"“His sisters were fine women, with an air of decided fashion.”\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The claim is that Mrs. Bennet's main concern is getting her daughters married. Choice A states this directly: “The business of her life was to get her daughters married.”\\n\\nChoice B describes Mrs. Bennet, but it concerns her moods, not her daughters' marriages. Choice C describes Mr. Bingley's appearance and manners. Choice D describes Mr. Bingley's sisters. Neither C nor D mentions Mrs. Bennet's concerns.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Da’voga ko‘ra, Bennet xonimning asosiy tashvishi — qizlarini turmushga chiqarish. A javob buni to‘g‘ridan-to‘g‘ri aytadi: “Uning hayotdagi asosiy ishi qizlarini turmushga chiqarish edi.”\\n\\nB ham Bennet xonimni tasvirlaydi, lekin uning kayfiyati haqida, qizlarining turmushi haqida emas. C janob Binglining tashqi ko‘rinishi va xulqini tasvirlaydi. D janob Binglining opa-singillarini tasvirlaydi. C ham, D ham Bennet xonimning tashvishlari haqida gapirmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Along the Pacific coast of North America, sea urchins graze on kelp, a large seaweed that forms underwater forests. Marine biologist Feruza Alimova hypothesizes that sea otters help kelp forests survive by eating sea urchins and keeping their numbers low.\",\"stem\":\"Which finding, if true, would most directly support Alimova's hypothesis?\",\"option_a\":\"Sea otters often use rocks as tools to crack open the shells of their prey.\",\"option_b\":\"In coastal areas without sea otters, sea urchins were far more numerous and kelp was much sparser than in nearby areas with otters.\",\"option_c\":\"Kelp grows fastest in cold water that is rich in nutrients.\",\"option_d\":\"Some species of fish also feed on young sea urchins.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Alimova's hypothesis links three things: otters reduce urchins, and fewer urchins means healthier kelp. Choice B shows exactly this pattern: where otters were absent, urchins were more numerous and kelp was sparser.\\n\\nChoice A describes how otters eat but not whether they affect urchin numbers or kelp. Choice C concerns conditions for kelp growth and says nothing about otters. Choice D concerns fish, not otters, so it does not test the hypothesis.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Alimova gipotezasi uch narsani bog‘laydi: dengiz qunduzlari dengiz tipratikanlarini kamaytiradi, tipratikanlar kam bo‘lsa, kelp o‘rmonlari sog‘lomroq bo‘ladi. B javob aynan shu holatni ko‘rsatadi: qunduzlar yo‘q joylarda tipratikanlar ko‘p, kelp esa siyrak bo‘lgan.\\n\\nA qunduzlar qanday ovqatlanishini tasvirlaydi, ammo ularning tipratikanlar soni yoki kelpga ta’sirini emas. C kelp o‘sishi uchun sharoit haqida, qunduzlar haqida hech narsa demaydi. D qunduzlar emas, baliqlar haqida, shuning uchun gipotezani tekshirmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"*Narrative of the Life of Frederick Douglass, an American Slave* is an 1845 autobiography by Frederick Douglass. In the book, Douglass suggests that enslavers deliberately kept enslaved people ignorant of basic facts about their own lives.\",\"stem\":\"Which quotation from *Narrative of the Life of Frederick Douglass, an American Slave* most effectively illustrates the claim?\",\"option_a\":\"“I was born in Tuckahoe, near Hillsborough, and about twelve miles from Easton, in Talbot county, Maryland.”\",\"option_b\":\"“They seldom come nearer to it than planting-time, harvest-time, cherry-time, spring-time, or fall-time.”\",\"option_c\":\"“By far the larger part of the slaves know as little of their ages as horses know of theirs, and it is the wish of most masters within my knowledge to keep their slaves thus ignorant.”\",\"option_d\":\"“My mother was named Harriet Bailey.”\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The claim stresses that the ignorance was deliberate: enslavers intended it. Choice C states that most enslaved people did not know their ages and that “it is the wish of most masters … to keep their slaves thus ignorant,” which shows the ignorance was intentional.\\n\\nChoice B is tempting because it shows that enslaved people knew their birthdays only by season, but it does not say anyone caused this on purpose. Choice A gives Douglass's birthplace, which he does know. Choice D gives his mother's name; neither A nor D shows enslavers withholding information.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Da’voning asosiy jihati — bu jaholat ataylab qilingan, ya’ni quldorlar shuni xohlagan. C javobda qullarning aksariyati o‘z yoshini bilmasligi va “ko‘pchilik xo‘jayinlar qullarini aynan shunday nodon saqlashni xohlashi” aytiladi — bu jaholat qasddan ekanini ko‘rsatadi.\\n\\nB jozibali tuyuladi, chunki u qullar tug‘ilgan kunini faqat fasl bo‘yicha bilganini ko‘rsatadi, ammo bunga kimdir ataylab sabab bo‘lgani aytilmaydi. A Duglasning tug‘ilgan joyini keltiradi, buni u biladi. D onasining ismini keltiradi; A ham, D ham quldorlar ma’lumotni yashirganini ko‘rsatmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"*Life on the Mississippi* is an 1883 memoir by Mark Twain. In the memoir, Twain recalls that after one boy from his town found work on a steamboat, many other boys from the town followed his example.\",\"stem\":\"Which quotation from *Life on the Mississippi* most effectively illustrates the claim?\",\"option_a\":\"“Pilot was the grandest position of all.”\",\"option_b\":\"“He wore a leather belt and used no suspenders.”\",\"option_c\":\"“We could not get on the river--at least our parents would not let us.”\",\"option_d\":\"“Boy after boy managed to get on the river.”\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The claim is that many boys followed the first boy onto the river. Choice D, “Boy after boy managed to get on the river,” shows exactly that.\\n\\nChoice A ranks steamboat jobs but does not show boys following anyone's example. Choice B describes the first boy's clothing. Choice C is tempting because it concerns getting on the river, but it describes boys who failed to do so, which does not illustrate many boys following the example.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Da’voga ko‘ra, ko‘plab bolalar birinchi bolaning ortidan daryoga ishga ketgan. D javob — “Bola ketidan bola daryoga ishga joylashib oldi” — aynan shuni ko‘rsatadi.\\n\\nA paroxoddagi lavozimlarni baholaydi, lekin bolalar kimningdir o‘rnagiga ergashganini ko‘rsatmaydi. B birinchi bolaning kiyimini tasvirlaydi. C jozibali, chunki u ham daryoda ishlash haqida, ammo unda bunga erisha olmagan bolalar tasvirlangan — bu ko‘p bolalar o‘rnakka ergashganini ko‘rsatmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":65,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"On flat, open farmland, strong winds can carry away fertile topsoil. Agricultural scientist Sardor Yo‘ldoshev hypothesizes that planting rows of trees along the edges of fields reduces this loss by slowing the wind before it reaches the soil.\",\"stem\":\"Which finding, if true, would most directly support Yo‘ldoshev's hypothesis?\",\"option_a\":\"During windstorms, fields bordered by tree rows lost much less topsoil than otherwise similar fields without tree rows.\",\"option_b\":\"Fields bordered by tree rows produced higher crop yields than fields without tree rows.\",\"option_c\":\"Soil erosion by wind is more common in dry years than in wet years.\",\"option_d\":\"Tree rows along fields provide nesting sites for many species of birds.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The hypothesis is that tree rows reduce wind-driven topsoil loss. Choice A compares similar fields with and without tree rows during windstorms and finds much less soil loss where trees were planted, which directly supports the hypothesis.\\n\\nChoice B is tempting, but higher yields could result from many causes (shade, moisture, fewer pests), so it does not directly show reduced soil loss. Choice C concerns when erosion happens, not whether tree rows reduce it. Choice D describes a different benefit of tree rows that is unrelated to soil.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gipotezaga ko‘ra, daraxt qatorlari shamol sababli unumdor tuproq yo‘qolishini kamaytiradi. A javob shamolli bo‘ronlar paytida daraxt qatori bor va yo‘q bo‘lgan o‘xshash dalalarni solishtiradi va daraxt ekilgan joyda tuproq ancha kam yo‘qolganini aniqlaydi — bu gipotezani bevosita tasdiqlaydi.\\n\\nB jozibali, ammo yuqori hosil ko‘p sabablarga (soya, namlik, zararkunandalar kamligi) bog‘liq bo‘lishi mumkin, shuning uchun u tuproq yo‘qolishi kamayganini bevosita ko‘rsatmaydi. C eroziya qachon sodir bo‘lishi haqida, daraxtlar uni kamaytiradimi-yo‘qmi, bu haqda emas. D daraxt qatorlarining tuproqqa aloqasi yo‘q boshqa foydasini tasvirlaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":65,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"*The Souls of Black Folk* is a 1903 book by W. E. B. Du Bois. In the book, Du Bois argues that Black Americans do not wish to abandon either of the two identities he describes—American and Black—but rather to be able to hold both at once.\",\"stem\":\"Which quotation from *The Souls of Black Folk* most effectively illustrates the claim?\",\"option_a\":\"“All, nevertheless, flutter round it.”\",\"option_b\":\"“He simply wishes to make it possible for a man to be both a Negro and an American, without being cursed and spit upon by his fellows, without having the doors of Opportunity closed roughly in his face.”\",\"option_c\":\"“One ever feels his twoness,—an American, a Negro; two souls, two thoughts, two unreconciled strivings; two warring ideals in one dark body, whose dogged strength alone keeps it from being torn asunder.”\",\"option_d\":\"“It is a peculiar sensation, this double-consciousness, this sense of always looking at one’s self through the eyes of others, of measuring one’s soul by the tape of a world that looks on in amused contempt and pity.”\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The claim concerns a wish: to keep both identities rather than give either up. Choice B states that wish directly: “He simply wishes to make it possible for a man to be both a Negro and an American.”\\n\\nChoice C is tempting because it names the same two identities, but it describes them as “unreconciled” and “warring”—the painful conflict, not the wish to hold both. Choice D describes the experience of double-consciousness, seeing oneself through others' contemptuous eyes, not what Black Americans want. Choice A refers to people circling an unasked question and says nothing about identity.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Da’vo bir istak haqida: birorta o‘zlikdan voz kechmasdan, ikkalasini ham saqlab qolish. B javob bu istakni to‘g‘ridan-to‘g‘ri ifodalaydi: “U shunchaki insonning ham qora tanli, ham amerikalik bo‘la olishini ta’minlashni xohlaydi.”\\n\\nC jozibali, chunki unda ham o‘sha ikki o‘zlik tilga olinadi, ammo ular “murosasiz” va “bir-biri bilan kurashuvchi” deb tasvirlanadi — bu ikkalasini saqlash istagi emas, og‘riqli ziddiyat. D ikki tomonlama ong tajribasini — o‘zingga boshqalarning nafratli ko‘zi bilan qarashni tasvirlaydi, qora tanli amerikaliklar nimani xohlashini emas. A odamlarning so‘ralmagan savol atrofida aylanishi haqida va o‘zlik haqida hech narsa demaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Around 600 CE, the people of the ancient settlement of Qal’a Sabz abandoned their homes, and the site was never reoccupied. Archaeologist Behruz Nazarov hypothesizes that a prolonged drought dried up the springs that supplied the settlement's water, forcing the residents to leave.\",\"stem\":\"Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken Nazarov's hypothesis?\",\"option_a\":\"Pollen preserved in nearby lake sediments indicates that the region became drier around 600 CE.\",\"option_b\":\"The population of Qal’a Sabz grew steadily during the century before it was abandoned.\",\"option_c\":\"Several other settlements in the region were also abandoned between 550 and 650 CE.\",\"option_d\":\"Mineral deposits from the settlement's springs show that the springs continued to flow steadily for at least two centuries after the settlement was abandoned.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Nazarov's hypothesis depends on the springs drying up. Choice D shows that the springs kept flowing steadily for centuries after the residents left, so the residents did not leave because their water supply failed. This directly weakens the hypothesis.\\n\\nChoice A would support, not weaken, the hypothesis by showing that the region became drier at the right time. Choice B does not address the springs or a drought; a growing population is consistent with many explanations for later abandonment. Choice C is consistent with a regional drought and so, if anything, supports the hypothesis.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Nazarov gipotezasi buloqlar qurib qolganiga asoslanadi. D javob esa aholi ketganidan keyin ham buloqlar asrlar davomida barqaror oqqanini ko‘rsatadi, demak aholi suv ta’minoti tugagani uchun ketmagan. Bu gipotezani bevosita zaiflashtiradi.\\n\\nA gipotezani zaiflashtirmaydi, aksincha, mintaqa aynan o‘sha paytda qurg‘oqlashganini ko‘rsatib, uni qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi. B buloqlar yoki qurg‘oqchilik haqida emas; aholining o‘sishi keyingi tark etilishning ko‘p xil tushuntirishlariga mos keladi. C mintaqaviy qurg‘oqchilikka mos keladi, shuning uchun u ham gipotezani qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nThe following text is from Frederick Douglass's 1845 autobiography *Narrative of the Life of Frederick Douglass, an American Slave*. Douglass's enslaver, Mr. Auld, has just forbidden his wife to keep teaching the young Douglass to read.\\n\\nThe very decided manner with which he spoke, and strove to impress his wife with the evil consequences of giving me instruction, served to convince me that he was deeply sensible of the truths he was uttering. … What he most dreaded, that I most desired. What he most loved, that I most hated. That which to him was a great evil, to be carefully shunned, was to me a great good, to be diligently sought; and the argument which he so warmly urged, against my learning to read, only served to inspire me with a desire and determination to learn.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nIn the decades before the Civil War, several Southern states passed laws making it a crime to teach enslaved people to read or write. Historian Malika Yusupova argues that these laws reveal what enslavers themselves believed: that literacy would allow enslaved people to read antislavery writings, forge travel passes, and communicate with one another, making slavery harder to maintain.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Yusupova (Text 2) most likely view Mr. Auld's attitude as Douglass describes it in Text 1?\",\"option_a\":\"As an unusual opinion that few other enslavers shared\",\"option_b\":\"As typical of a widespread belief among enslavers that literacy threatened slavery\",\"option_c\":\"As evidence that enslavers secretly wanted enslaved people to learn to read\",\"option_d\":\"As proof that laws against teaching enslaved people to read were rarely passed\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Mr. Auld fears the “evil consequences” of teaching Douglass to read. Yusupova argues that laws in several states show enslavers generally believed literacy would make slavery “harder to maintain.” She would therefore see Mr. Auld's attitude as typical of that belief, as choice B says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because Yusupova describes this belief as widespread enough to produce laws in several states. Choice C contradicts both texts: Mr. Auld forbids reading lessons, and Text 2 describes laws banning them. Choice D contradicts Text 2, which says several states passed such laws.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Janob Old Duglasni o‘qishga o‘rgatishning “yomon oqibatlari”dan qo‘rqadi. Yusupova esa bir nechta shtatdagi qonunlar quldorlar umuman savodxonlik qullikni saqlab qolishni “qiyinlashtiradi” deb hisoblaganini ko‘rsatishini ta’kidlaydi. Demak, u janob Oldning munosabatini shu keng tarqalgan e’tiqodning odatiy namunasi deb biladi. To‘g‘ri javob — B.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: Yusupova bu e’tiqodni bir nechta shtatda qonun qabul qilinishiga olib kelgan darajada keng tarqalgan deb tasvirlaydi. C ikkala matnga ham zid: janob Old o‘qish darslarini taqiqlaydi, Text 2 da esa ularni taqiqlovchi qonunlar tasvirlanadi. D Text 2 ga zid: unda bir nechta shtat bunday qonunlarni qabul qilgani aytilgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nFor much of the twentieth century, textbooks and magazines often repeated the claim that the Great Wall of China is the only human-made structure that can be seen with the naked eye from space. The claim appeared in print decades before anyone had actually traveled into orbit.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nAstronauts orbiting Earth at about 400 kilometers have generally reported that they could not pick out the Great Wall without a camera lens. Although the wall is very long, it is only a few meters wide, and it is built of materials similar in color to the land around it. Meanwhile, astronauts easily see other human-made features from orbit, such as large airports and brightly lit cities at night.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim described in Text 1?\",\"option_a\":\"By rejecting it, since astronauts generally cannot see the wall without a lens but can see other human-made features\",\"option_b\":\"By accepting it, since the wall's great length makes it easy to see from orbit\",\"option_c\":\"By arguing that the claim is true only at night, when the wall is lit\",\"option_d\":\"By suggesting that the claim was based on careful observations made from orbit\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Text 1's claim has two parts: the wall can be seen with the naked eye from space, and it is the only human-made structure that can. Text 2 contradicts both parts: astronauts generally could not see the wall without a lens, and they easily see airports and lit cities. The author would reject the claim, as choice A says.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because Text 2 says the wall is hard to see despite its length, because it is narrow and similar in color to its surroundings. Choice C is wrong because Text 2 mentions lit cities, not a lit wall. Choice D is wrong because Text 1 says the claim appeared “decades before anyone had actually traveled into orbit,” so it could not have come from observations made there.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Text 1 dagi da’vo ikki qismdan iborat: devorni koinotdan oddiy ko‘z bilan ko‘rish mumkin va bu ko‘rinadigan yagona inson qurgan inshoot. Text 2 ikkala qismga ham zid: kosmonavtlar odatda devorni linzasiz ko‘ra olmagan, aeroportlar va yoritilgan shaharlarni esa bemalol ko‘rishadi. Demak, muallif bu da’voni rad etadi — to‘g‘ri javob A.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: Text 2 ga ko‘ra, devor uzun bo‘lishiga qaramay, tor va atrofdagi yer bilan bir xil rangda bo‘lgani uchun qiyin ko‘rinadi. C noto‘g‘ri: Text 2 da yoritilgan devor emas, yoritilgan shaharlar tilga olinadi. D noto‘g‘ri: Text 1 ga ko‘ra, da’vo “hali hech kim orbitaga uchmasdan o‘n yilliklar oldin” paydo bo‘lgan, demak u orbitadan qilingan kuzatuvlarga asoslana olmas edi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nSome educators argue that schools should stop teaching handwriting. Since students now do most of their writing on keyboards, they say, the hours spent practicing letters by hand would be better used for other subjects.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nEducational psychologist Zuhra Olimova asked two groups of university students to take notes on the same lecture, one group by hand and the other on laptops. A week later, the students who had written by hand answered more questions about the lecture's main concepts correctly. Olimova suggests that because writing by hand is slower, it forces students to summarize ideas in their own words rather than copying everything down.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Olimova (Text 2) most likely respond to the educators' argument in Text 1?\",\"option_a\":\"She would agree, since her study found that typing helps students remember more.\",\"option_b\":\"She would agree, since students rarely write by hand outside school.\",\"option_c\":\"She would caution that writing by hand may still help students learn, so it should not be dismissed as useless.\",\"option_d\":\"She would argue that handwriting is useful only for students who type slowly.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The educators in Text 1 claim handwriting practice is no longer worth the time. Olimova found that students who took notes by hand remembered main concepts better, possibly because writing by hand makes them summarize. She would therefore warn that handwriting may still have learning benefits, as choice C says.\\n\\nChoice A contradicts her results: the handwriting group did better. Choice B is wrong because her study gives her a reason to question, not support, the educators' view, and she says nothing about how often students write by hand. Choice D is wrong because her study compared handwriting with typing in general; it did not focus on students who type slowly.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Text 1 dagi pedagoglar qo‘lda yozishni mashq qilish endi vaqtga arzimaydi deb hisoblaydi. Olimova esa qo‘lda konspekt yozgan talabalar asosiy tushunchalarni yaxshiroq eslab qolganini aniqlagan; buning sababi qo‘lda yozish ularni fikrni qisqartirib yozishga majbur qilishi bo‘lishi mumkin. Demak, u qo‘lda yozish hali ham o‘rganishga yordam berishi mumkinligi haqida ogohlantiradi — to‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\nA uning natijalariga zid: qo‘lda yozgan guruh yaxshiroq natija ko‘rsatgan. B noto‘g‘ri: uning tadqiqoti pedagoglar fikrini qo‘llab-quvvatlash emas, balki shubha ostiga olish uchun asos beradi; u talabalar qanchalik tez-tez qo‘lda yozishi haqida hech narsa demaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: uning tadqiqoti umuman qo‘lda yozish va klaviaturada yozishni solishtirgan, sekin yozadigan talabalarga e’tibor qaratmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nThe following text is from the opening of Henry David Thoreau's 1862 essay “Walking.”\\n\\nI wish to speak a word for Nature, for absolute Freedom and Wildness, as contrasted with a freedom and culture merely civil,—to regard man as an inhabitant, or a part and parcel of Nature, rather than a member of society. I wish to make an extreme statement, if so I may make an emphatic one, for there are enough champions of civilization: the minister and the school committee and every one of you will take care of that.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nUrban ecologist Kamola Nurmatova studies the foxes, hawks, and wildflowers that thrive in the parks, rail yards, and empty lots of large cities. She argues that treating “nature” and “civilization” as opposites has led people to overlook the wildlife all around them. In her view, a city is not the absence of nature but one of the places where nature now exists, and protecting urban habitats deserves as much attention as protecting remote wilderness.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Nurmatova (Text 2) most likely respond to the way Thoreau frames his discussion in Text 1?\",\"option_a\":\"She would agree that champions of civilization have ignored wild places far from cities.\",\"option_b\":\"She would argue that people should be regarded as members of society rather than as part of Nature.\",\"option_c\":\"She would claim that Thoreau's statement is not extreme enough.\",\"option_d\":\"She would question his contrast between Nature and civilized society, since she sees cities themselves as places where nature exists.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Thoreau frames his essay as speaking for Nature “as contrasted with” civil society and civilization. Nurmatova argues that treating nature and civilization “as opposites” causes people to miss the wildlife in cities, which she sees as places “where nature now exists.” She would therefore question the contrast Thoreau draws, as choice D says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because Nurmatova's concern is that people overlook nature inside cities, not wild places far from them. Choice B is wrong because nothing in Text 2 suggests she would separate people from nature; her view blurs that line. Choice C is wrong because Text 2 gives no reason to think she wants Thoreau's opposition between nature and civilization made even sharper.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Toro o‘z inshosini fuqarolik jamiyati va sivilizatsiyaga “qarama-qarshi” bo‘lgan Tabiat nomidan so‘zlash sifatida quradi. Nurmatova esa tabiat va sivilizatsiyani “qarama-qarshi” deb bilish odamlarni shahardagi yovvoyi hayotni e’tiborsiz qoldirishga olib kelganini aytadi; uning fikricha, shaharlar “tabiat hozir mavjud bo‘lgan joylardan biri.” Demak, u Toro chizgan qarama-qarshilikka shubha bildiradi — to‘g‘ri javob D.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: Nurmatovani shahardan uzoqdagi yovvoyi joylar emas, aynan shahar ichidagi tabiatning e’tiborsiz qolayotgani tashvishlantiradi. B noto‘g‘ri: Text 2 da u odamlarni tabiatdan ajratishi haqida ishora yo‘q; uning qarashi aksincha, bu chegarani xiralashtiradi. C noto‘g‘ri: Text 2 da u Toroning tabiat va sivilizatsiya o‘rtasidagi qarama-qarshiligini yanada keskinlashtirishni xohlaydi deyishga asos yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nThe following text is from Mark Twain's 1883 memoir *Life on the Mississippi*. Twain describes a steamboat arriving at the small river town where he grew up.\\n\\nDrays, carts, men, boys, all go hurrying from many quarters to a common center, the wharf. Assembled there, the people fasten their eyes upon the coming boat as upon a wonder they are seeing for the first time. And the boat *is* rather a handsome sight, too. … great volumes of the blackest smoke are rolling and tumbling out of the chimneys--a husbanded grandeur created with a bit of pitch pine just before arriving at a town; …\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nHistorian Rustam Karimov notes that in the mid-1800s, steamboat companies competed fiercely for passengers and freight in the towns along the Mississippi. According to Karimov, captains often staged their arrivals—ringing bells, flying flags, and burning resin-rich wood to produce towering clouds of dark smoke—so that their boats would look grand and powerful to the townspeople watching from shore.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Karimov (Text 2) most likely interpret the “husbanded grandeur” described in Text 1?\",\"option_a\":\"As a deliberate display meant to impress the townspeople who might become customers\",\"option_b\":\"As an accident caused by burning poor-quality wood\",\"option_c\":\"As a sign that the boat's engines were dangerously overheated\",\"option_d\":\"As evidence that steamboat companies did not compete with one another\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Twain says the dramatic black smoke was “a husbanded grandeur” (a grand effect saved up for the right moment) “created with a bit of pitch pine just before arriving at a town.” Karimov explains that captains staged such displays, including burning resin-rich wood for dark smoke, to look impressive while competing for passengers and freight. He would see the smoke as a deliberate show for potential customers, as choice A says.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because both texts present the smoke as intentional, not accidental. Choice C is wrong because neither text links the smoke to engine trouble. Choice D contradicts Text 2, which says the companies “competed fiercely.”\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Tven ta’kidlashicha, qop-qora quyuq tutun “tejab saqlangan ulug‘vorlik” bo‘lib, u “shaharga yetib kelishdan sal oldin bir parcha smolali qarag‘ay yoqib hosil qilingan.” Karimov esa kapitanlar yo‘lovchi va yuk uchun raqobatlashib, o‘zlarini ulug‘vor ko‘rsatish maqsadida bunday tomoshalar uyushtirganini, jumladan qora tutun uchun smolali yog‘och yoqqanini tushuntiradi. Demak, u tutunni bo‘lajak mijozlar uchun ataylab qilingan namoyish deb biladi — to‘g‘ri javob A.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: ikkala matnda ham tutun tasodif emas, ataylab qilingan narsa sifatida ko‘rsatilgan. C noto‘g‘ri: hech bir matnda tutun dvigatel nosozligi bilan bog‘lanmaydi. D Text 2 ga zid: unda kompaniyalar “qattiq raqobatlashgani” aytilgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nWhy do zebras have stripes? A long-popular explanation holds that the stripes serve as camouflage against predators. When a herd of zebras runs, the theory goes, the mass of moving stripes makes it hard for a lion to pick out a single animal to chase.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nBiologist Nadia Ferro filmed horseflies approaching horses wearing either plain or striped coats. Far fewer flies managed to land on the striped coats; many seemed unable to slow down properly and flew past or bumped into them. Ferro also notes that lions hunt mostly at dusk and at night, when stripes are hard to see from any distance. She concludes that protection from biting insects is a more likely explanation for zebras' stripes than defense against predators.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Ferro (Text 2) most likely respond to the explanation described in Text 1?\",\"option_a\":\"She would accept it, since her experiment showed that stripes confuse lions.\",\"option_b\":\"She would argue that stripes help zebras attract mates rather than avoid predators.\",\"option_c\":\"She would doubt it, since lions usually hunt when stripes are hard to see and her experiment points to a different function.\",\"option_d\":\"She would claim that zebras without stripes are hunted less often by lions.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Text 1 describes the idea that stripes confuse predators such as lions. Ferro's experiment showed that stripes keep biting flies from landing, and she points out that lions hunt mostly when stripes are hard to see. She concludes that insect protection is “a more likely explanation.” She would therefore doubt the predator explanation, as choice C says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because her experiment involved flies, not lions. Choice B is wrong because Text 2 says nothing about attracting mates. Choice D is wrong because Text 2 gives no information about how often unstriped zebras are hunted.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Text 1 da chiziqlar sher kabi yirtqichlarni chalg‘itadi degan g‘oya tasvirlanadi. Ferroning tajribasi esa chiziqlar qon so‘ruvchi pashshalarning qo‘nishiga to‘sqinlik qilishini ko‘rsatgan; bundan tashqari, u sherlar asosan chiziqlar yaxshi ko‘rinmaydigan paytda ov qilishini ta’kidlaydi. U hasharotlardan himoya “ehtimoli kattaroq tushuntirish” degan xulosaga keladi. Demak, u yirtqichlar haqidagi tushuntirishga shubha bilan qaraydi — to‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: uning tajribasi sherlar bilan emas, pashshalar bilan o‘tkazilgan. B noto‘g‘ri: Text 2 da juft jalb qilish haqida gap yo‘q. D noto‘g‘ri: Text 2 da chiziqsiz zebralar qanchalik tez-tez ovlanishi haqida ma’lumot yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nThe following text is from the Introduction to the 1872 edition of Charles Darwin's *On the Origin of Species*.\\n\\nAlthough much remains obscure, and will long remain obscure, I can entertain no doubt, after the most deliberate study and dispassionate judgment of which I am capable, that the view which most naturalists until recently entertained, and which I formerly entertained—namely, that each species has been independently created—is erroneous. I am fully convinced that species are not immutable; but that those belonging to what are called the same genera are lineal descendants of some other and generally extinct species … Furthermore, I am convinced that natural selection has been the most important, but not the exclusive, means of modification.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nBiologists today recognize several processes besides natural selection that change populations over time. One of the most studied is genetic drift: random changes in how common a gene variant is, which occur simply because some individuals happen to leave more offspring than others for reasons unrelated to their traits. In very small populations, drift can cause a variant to spread or disappear entirely, regardless of whether it helps or harms the organisms that carry it.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to Darwin's final claim in Text 1?\",\"option_a\":\"By rejecting it, since modern biologists have shown that natural selection plays no role in changing populations\",\"option_b\":\"By arguing that Darwin underestimated how strongly natural selection controls small populations\",\"option_c\":\"By claiming that genetic drift shows that species are immutable after all\",\"option_d\":\"By noting that genetic drift is consistent with Darwin's view that natural selection is not the only means of modification\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Darwin's final claim is that natural selection is “the most important, but not the exclusive, means of modification.” Text 2 describes genetic drift as one of “several processes besides natural selection that change populations.” The author of Text 2 would see drift as an example of the kind of non-selective change Darwin allowed for, as choice D says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because Text 2 says other processes exist “besides natural selection,” which implies that selection still operates. Choice B is wrong because Text 2 says that in small populations drift can override whether a variant “helps or harms” its carriers, which suggests selection is weaker there, not stronger. Choice C is wrong because drift is described as a process that changes populations, which supports Darwin's view that species are not immutable.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Darvinning oxirgi da’vosi: tabiiy tanlanish o‘zgarishning “eng muhim, ammo yagona bo‘lmagan vositasi.” Text 2 da genetik dreyf “tabiiy tanlanishdan tashqari populyatsiyalarni o‘zgartiradigan bir nechta jarayon”dan biri sifatida tasvirlanadi. Demak, Text 2 muallifi dreyfni Darvin yo‘l qo‘ygan tanlanishga bog‘liq bo‘lmagan o‘zgarishning bir misoli deb biladi — to‘g‘ri javob D.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: Text 2 da boshqa jarayonlar “tabiiy tanlanishdan tashqari” mavjud deyilgan, bu tanlanish ham amal qilishini bildiradi. B noto‘g‘ri: Text 2 ga ko‘ra, kichik populyatsiyalarda dreyf variantning “foydali yoki zararli” ekanidan qat’i nazar ta’sir qiladi, ya’ni u yerda tanlanish kuchliroq emas, aksincha, zaifroq. C noto‘g‘ri: dreyf populyatsiyalarni o‘zgartiruvchi jarayon sifatida tasvirlangan, bu esa Darvinning turlar o‘zgarmas emas degan fikrini qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nThe following text is from W. E. B. Du Bois's 1903 book *The Souls of Black Folk*. Du Bois has been describing the “double-consciousness” of Black Americans, who feel torn between two identities.\\n\\nThe history of the American Negro is the history of this strife,—this longing to attain self-conscious manhood, to merge his double self into a better and truer self. In this merging he wishes neither of the older selves to be lost. He would not Africanize America, for America has too much to teach the world and Africa. He would not bleach his Negro soul in a flood of white Americanism, for he knows that Negro blood has a message for the world. He simply wishes to make it possible for a man to be both a Negro and an American, without being cursed and spit upon by his fellows, without having the doors of Opportunity closed roughly in his face.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nPsychologists who study people with two cultural backgrounds describe several ways of handling such identities. Some people give up one identity in favor of the other; some keep the two strictly separate, behaving one way at home and another in public. Others, whom researchers call “integrated,” come to see their two identities as compatible parts of a single self. Studies suggest, however, that reaching this integration depends heavily on whether the surrounding society treats both identities with respect.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would the researchers described in Text 2 most likely characterize the goal Du Bois describes in Text 1?\",\"option_a\":\"As an example of giving up one identity in favor of the other\",\"option_b\":\"As a form of integration whose achievement would depend on the kind of respectful treatment that Du Bois says is being denied\",\"option_c\":\"As a strategy of keeping two identities strictly separate in different settings\",\"option_d\":\"As a goal that no person with two cultural backgrounds could ever reach\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Du Bois describes a wish “to merge his double self into a better and truer self” while losing neither identity—to be “both a Negro and an American.” This matches what Text 2 calls integration: seeing two identities “as compatible parts of a single self.” Text 2 adds that integration depends on society respecting both identities, and Du Bois says this goal requires not being “cursed and spit upon” or having doors “closed roughly in his face.” Choice B captures both connections.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because Du Bois explicitly rejects giving up either identity (“neither of the older selves to be lost”). Choice C is wrong because Du Bois wants to merge the two selves, not keep them apart. Choice D is wrong because Text 2 describes integration as something people do reach under the right conditions.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Dyu Bua hech bir o‘zlikni yo‘qotmagan holda “ikki tomonlama o‘zligini yaxshiroq va haqiqiyroq bitta o‘zlikka birlashtirish” — “ham qora tanli, ham amerikalik” bo‘lish istagini tasvirlaydi. Bu Text 2 da “integratsiya” deb atalgan holatga — ikki o‘zlikni “yagona shaxsiyatning bir-biriga mos qismlari” deb bilishga mos keladi. Text 2 integratsiya jamiyat ikkala o‘zlikni hurmat qilishiga bog‘liqligini qo‘shimcha qiladi, Dyu Bua esa bu maqsad uchun “la’natlanmaslik, tupurilmaslik” va imkoniyat eshiklari “yuziga qo‘pollik bilan yopilmasligi” kerakligini aytadi. B javob ikkala bog‘liqlikni ham qamrab oladi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: Dyu Bua birorta o‘zlikdan voz kechishni ochiq rad etadi (“hech bir eski o‘zlik yo‘qolmasligi”). C noto‘g‘ri: Dyu Bua ikki o‘zlikni ajratib qo‘yishni emas, birlashtirishni xohlaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: Text 2 da integratsiya tegishli sharoitda odamlar haqiqatan erishadigan holat sifatida tasvirlangan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"equivalent_expressions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Which expression is equivalent to $4x^2y \\\\cdot 3xy^3$?\",\"option_a\":\"$7x^3y^4$\",\"option_b\":\"$12x^2y^3$\",\"option_c\":\"$12x^3y^4$\",\"option_d\":\"$12x^3y^3$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Multiply the coefficients and add the exponents of each variable: $4 \\\\cdot 3 = 12$, $x^2 \\\\cdot x = x^{2+1} = x^3$, and $y \\\\cdot y^3 = y^{1+3} = y^4$.\\n\\nSo the product is $12x^3y^4$.\\n\\nChoice A adds the coefficients ($4 + 3 = 7$) instead of multiplying them. Choice B ignores the unwritten exponent $1$ on $x$ and $y$ in the second and first factors. Choice D leaves out the $y$ in the first factor.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Koeffitsiyentlarni ko‘paytiramiz, har bir o‘zgaruvchining daraja ko‘rsatkichlarini qo‘shamiz: $4 \\\\cdot 3 = 12$, $x^2 \\\\cdot x = x^{2+1} = x^3$ va $y \\\\cdot y^3 = y^{1+3} = y^4$.\\n\\nDemak ko‘paytma $12x^3y^4$ ga teng.\\n\\nA variantda koeffitsiyentlar ko‘paytirilish o‘rniga qo‘shilgan ($4 + 3 = 7$). B variantda $x$ va $y$ ning yozilmagan $1$ ko‘rsatkichi hisobga olinmagan. D variantda birinchi ko‘paytuvchidagi $y$ tushib qolgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"equivalent_expressions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Which expression is equivalent to $9x^2 - 49$?\",\"option_a\":\"$(3x - 7)^2$\",\"option_b\":\"$(9x - 7)(x + 7)$\",\"option_c\":\"$(3x + 7)^2$\",\"option_d\":\"$(3x - 7)(3x + 7)$\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Both terms are perfect squares: $9x^2 = (3x)^2$ and $49 = 7^2$. A difference of squares factors as $a^2 - b^2 = (a - b)(a + b)$, so $9x^2 - 49 = (3x - 7)(3x + 7)$.\\n\\nCheck: $(3x - 7)(3x + 7) = 9x^2 + 21x - 21x - 49 = 9x^2 - 49$.\\n\\nChoice A expands to $9x^2 - 42x + 49$ and choice C to $9x^2 + 42x + 49$; both have a middle term and a $+49$. Choice B expands to $9x^2 + 56x - 49$, because $9x$ and $x$ are not the square roots of $9x^2$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkala had ham to‘liq kvadrat: $9x^2 = (3x)^2$ va $49 = 7^2$. Kvadratlar ayirmasi $a^2 - b^2 = (a - b)(a + b)$ ko‘rinishida ko‘paytuvchilarga ajraladi, shuning uchun $9x^2 - 49 = (3x - 7)(3x + 7)$.\\n\\nTekshiramiz: $(3x - 7)(3x + 7) = 9x^2 + 21x - 21x - 49 = 9x^2 - 49$.\\n\\nA variant $9x^2 - 42x + 49$ ga, C variant esa $9x^2 + 42x + 49$ ga teng; ikkalasida o‘rta had va $+49$ bor. B variant $9x^2 + 56x - 49$ ga teng, chunki $9x$ va $x$ lar $9x^2$ ning kvadrat ildizi emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"equivalent_expressions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"The expression $(5x^2 + 3x) - 3(x^2 - 4x + 2)$ is equivalent to $ax^2 + bx + c$, where $a$, $b$, and $c$ are constants. What is the value of $a + b + c$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"11\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Distribute $-3$ to every term in the parentheses: $-3(x^2 - 4x + 2) = -3x^2 + 12x - 6$.\\n\\nCombine like terms: $5x^2 + 3x - 3x^2 + 12x - 6 = 2x^2 + 15x - 6$.\\n\\nSo $a = 2$, $b = 15$, $c = -6$, and $a + b + c = 2 + 15 - 6 = 11$.\\n\\nIf you multiply $-3$ by $-4x$ and get $-12x$, you get $2x^2 - 9x - 6$ and the wrong sum $-13$. If you forget to multiply the $2$ by $-3$, you get $c = -2$ and the wrong sum $15$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$-3$ ni qavs ichidagi har bir hadga ko‘paytiramiz: $-3(x^2 - 4x + 2) = -3x^2 + 12x - 6$.\\n\\nO‘xshash hadlarni birlashtiramiz: $5x^2 + 3x - 3x^2 + 12x - 6 = 2x^2 + 15x - 6$.\\n\\nDemak $a = 2$, $b = 15$, $c = -6$ va $a + b + c = 2 + 15 - 6 = 11$.\\n\\nAgar $-3$ ni $-4x$ ga ko‘paytirib $-12x$ olinsa, $2x^2 - 9x - 6$ va noto‘g‘ri yig‘indi $-13$ chiqadi. $2$ ni $-3$ ga ko‘paytirish unutilsa, $c = -2$ va noto‘g‘ri yig‘indi $15$ hosil bo‘ladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"equivalent_expressions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Which of the following is equivalent to $\\\\dfrac{2}{x - 3} - \\\\dfrac{1}{x + 3}$ for $x > 3$?\",\"option_a\":\"$\\\\dfrac{x + 3}{x^2 - 9}$\",\"option_b\":\"$\\\\dfrac{x + 9}{x^2 - 9}$\",\"option_c\":\"$\\\\dfrac{3x + 3}{x^2 - 9}$\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\dfrac{1}{x^2 - 9}$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The common denominator is $(x - 3)(x + 3) = x^2 - 9$.\\n\\nRewrite each fraction: $\\\\frac{2(x + 3)}{x^2 - 9} - \\\\frac{x - 3}{x^2 - 9} = \\\\frac{2x + 6 - x + 3}{x^2 - 9} = \\\\frac{x + 9}{x^2 - 9}$.\\n\\nChoice A subtracts only $x$ and not the whole numerator $x - 3$, so it gets $2x + 6 - x - 3 = x + 3$. Choice C adds the fractions instead of subtracting: $2x + 6 + x - 3 = 3x + 3$. Choice D subtracts the original numerators $2 - 1$ without rewriting them over the common denominator.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Umumiy maxraj $(x - 3)(x + 3) = x^2 - 9$.\\n\\nHar bir kasrni shu maxrajga keltiramiz: $\\\\frac{2(x + 3)}{x^2 - 9} - \\\\frac{x - 3}{x^2 - 9} = \\\\frac{2x + 6 - x + 3}{x^2 - 9} = \\\\frac{x + 9}{x^2 - 9}$.\\n\\nA variantda butun surat $x - 3$ emas, faqat $x$ ayirilgan: $2x + 6 - x - 3 = x + 3$. C variantda kasrlar ayirilish o‘rniga qo‘shilgan: $2x + 6 + x - 3 = 3x + 3$. D variantda dastlabki suratlar $2 - 1$ umumiy maxrajga keltirilmasdan ayirilgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"equivalent_expressions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Dilnoza deposits $2{,}000{,}000$ soums in a savings account that pays $18\\\\%$ annual interest compounded monthly. The balance, in soums, after $t$ years is given by the expression $2{,}000{,}000(1.015)^{12t}$. Which of the following expressions is equivalent to the given expression?\",\"option_a\":\"$2{,}000{,}000\\\\left(1.015^{12}\\\\right)^{t}$\",\"option_b\":\"$2{,}000{,}000(1.18)^{t}$\",\"option_c\":\"$24{,}000{,}000(1.015)^{t}$\",\"option_d\":\"$2{,}000{,}000(1.015)^{t + 12}$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"By the power-of-a-power rule, $a^{mn} = (a^m)^n$. So $(1.015)^{12t} = \\\\left(1.015^{12}\\\\right)^{t}$, and the balance is $2{,}000{,}000\\\\left(1.015^{12}\\\\right)^{t}$.\\n\\nChoice B assumes $1.015^{12} = 1.18$, but $1.015^{12} \\\\approx 1.1956$: monthly compounding earns more than $18\\\\%$ per year, so the two expressions are not equal. Choice C multiplies the $12$ into the coefficient instead of keeping it in the exponent. Choice D adds $12$ to the exponent instead of multiplying, since $a^{t + 12} = a^t \\\\cdot a^{12}$, not $a^{12t}$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Darajani darajaga ko‘tarish qoidasiga ko‘ra $a^{mn} = (a^m)^n$. Demak $(1.015)^{12t} = \\\\left(1.015^{12}\\\\right)^{t}$ va hisobdagi pul $2{,}000{,}000\\\\left(1.015^{12}\\\\right)^{t}$ ga teng.\\n\\nB variant $1.015^{12} = 1.18$ deb hisoblaydi, lekin $1.015^{12} \\\\approx 1.1956$: oylik murakkab foiz yiliga $18\\\\%$ dan ko‘proq daromad beradi, shuning uchun bu ifodalar teng emas. C variantda $12$ daraja ko‘rsatkichida qolish o‘rniga koeffitsiyentga ko‘paytirilgan. D variantda $12$ ko‘rsatkichga ko‘paytirilish o‘rniga qo‘shilgan, holbuki $a^{t + 12} = a^t \\\\cdot a^{12}$, $a^{12t}$ emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"equivalent_expressions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"For $x \\\\ne 3$, the expression $\\\\dfrac{2x^2 - 5x + 4}{x - 3}$ is equivalent to $2x + 1 + \\\\dfrac{r}{x - 3}$, where $r$ is a constant. What is the value of $r$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"7\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Multiply both sides by $x - 3$: $2x^2 - 5x + 4 = (2x + 1)(x - 3) + r$.\\n\\nExpand: $(2x + 1)(x - 3) = 2x^2 - 6x + x - 3 = 2x^2 - 5x - 3$.\\n\\nSo $2x^2 - 5x + 4 = 2x^2 - 5x - 3 + r$, which gives $r = 4 - (-3) = 7$.\\n\\nA quick check: substitute $x = 3$ into the numerator, $2(9) - 15 + 4 = 7$, which is the remainder. A common error is entering $4$, the constant term of the numerator, or $1$, the constant term of the quotient.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkala tomonni $x - 3$ ga ko‘paytiramiz: $2x^2 - 5x + 4 = (2x + 1)(x - 3) + r$.\\n\\nQavslarni ochamiz: $(2x + 1)(x - 3) = 2x^2 - 6x + x - 3 = 2x^2 - 5x - 3$.\\n\\nDemak $2x^2 - 5x + 4 = 2x^2 - 5x - 3 + r$, bundan $r = 4 - (-3) = 7$.\\n\\nTez tekshirish: suratga $x = 3$ ni qo‘yamiz, $2(9) - 15 + 4 = 7$ — bu qoldiq. Ko‘p uchraydigan xato — suratning ozod hadi $4$ ni yoki bo‘linmaning ozod hadi $1$ ni kiritish.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"equivalent_expressions\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"If $x - y = 6$ and $xy = 8$, what is the value of $x^2 + y^2$?\",\"option_a\":\"$20$\",\"option_b\":\"$28$\",\"option_c\":\"$52$\",\"option_d\":\"$68$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Square the first equation: $(x - y)^2 = x^2 - 2xy + y^2 = 36$.\\n\\nSo $x^2 + y^2 = 36 + 2xy = 36 + 2(8) = 52$. There is no need to find $x$ and $y$ themselves.\\n\\nChoice A subtracts $2xy$ instead of adding it: $36 - 16 = 20$. Choice B uses $(x - y)^2 = x^2 - xy + y^2$ with the wrong sign as well: $36 - 8 = 28$. Choice D is $(x + y)^2 = x^2 + y^2 + 2xy = 52 + 16 = 68$, not $x^2 + y^2$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi tenglikni kvadratga ko‘taramiz: $(x - y)^2 = x^2 - 2xy + y^2 = 36$.\\n\\nDemak $x^2 + y^2 = 36 + 2xy = 36 + 2(8) = 52$. $x$ va $y$ ning o‘zini topish shart emas.\\n\\nA variantda $2xy$ qo‘shilish o‘rniga ayirilgan: $36 - 16 = 20$. B variantda $(x - y)^2 = x^2 - xy + y^2$ deb olinib, ishora ham xato qilingan: $36 - 8 = 28$. D variant — bu $(x + y)^2 = x^2 + y^2 + 2xy = 52 + 16 = 68$, $x^2 + y^2$ emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"equivalent_expressions\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"For all positive values of $x$ and $y$, the expression $\\\\dfrac{(x^3y^{-2})^4}{x^{-2}y^5}$ is equivalent to $x^ay^b$, where $a$ and $b$ are constants. What is the value of $a - b$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"27\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Raise each factor to the fourth power: $(x^3y^{-2})^4 = x^{12}y^{-8}$.\\n\\nDivide by subtracting exponents: $x^{12 - (-2)}y^{-8 - 5} = x^{14}y^{-13}$.\\n\\nSo $a = 14$, $b = -13$, and $a - b = 14 - (-13) = 27$.\\n\\nCommon errors: adding the exponents $3 + 4$ instead of multiplying them; computing $12 - 2 = 10$ for $x$ (ignoring the negative exponent in the denominator); or giving $a + b = 1$ instead of $a - b$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Har bir ko‘paytuvchini to‘rtinchi darajaga ko‘taramiz: $(x^3y^{-2})^4 = x^{12}y^{-8}$.\\n\\nBo‘lishda ko‘rsatkichlar ayiriladi: $x^{12 - (-2)}y^{-8 - 5} = x^{14}y^{-13}$.\\n\\nDemak $a = 14$, $b = -13$ va $a - b = 14 - (-13) = 27$.\\n\\nKo‘p uchraydigan xatolar: ko‘rsatkichlarni ko‘paytirish o‘rniga qo‘shish ($3 + 4$); $x$ uchun $12 - 2 = 10$ deb hisoblash (maxrajdagi manfiy ko‘rsatkichni e’tiborsiz qoldirish); yoki $a - b$ o‘rniga $a + b = 1$ ni kiritish.\",\"estimated_seconds\":110,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"evaluating_statistical_claims\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Tashkent city has about 3.1 million residents. A radio station in Tashkent asked its listeners to send a text message saying whether they support building a new metro line. Of the 2,400 listeners who sent a message, 78% said they support it. The station then claimed that about 78% of all Tashkent residents support the new metro line. Which of the following is the best reason the station's claim may not be valid?\",\"option_a\":\"A sample of 2,400 people is too small to give any information about 3.1 million residents.\",\"option_b\":\"The percentage of 78% is too high to be accurate.\",\"option_c\":\"The people in the sample chose whether to respond and were not selected at random from all Tashkent residents.\",\"option_d\":\"Some of the listeners may live outside Tashkent city.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The sample is made of radio listeners who **chose** to respond. People who feel strongly, and people who listen to this station, may differ from typical residents, so the sample may not represent all Tashkent residents. A random sample from all residents would avoid this bias. Choice A is wrong: a random sample of 2,400 people could estimate a city's opinion quite well; the problem is how the sample was chosen, not its size. Choice B is wrong because a high percentage is not, by itself, a sign of error. Choice D describes a possible problem, but the main flaw is that the respondents selected themselves.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Tanlanma javob berishni o‘zi **tanlagan** radio tinglovchilaridan iborat. Masalaga qattiq qiziqqan odamlar va aynan shu radioni tinglaydiganlar oddiy aholidan farq qilishi mumkin, shuning uchun tanlanma Toshkentning barcha aholisini ifodalamasligi mumkin. Barcha aholidan tasodifiy tanlanma bu xatolikning oldini olardi. A javob noto‘g‘ri: 2,400 kishilik tasodifiy tanlanma shahar fikrini ancha yaxshi baholashi mumkin; muammo tanlanmaning hajmida emas, qanday tanlanganida. B javob noto‘g‘ri, chunki yuqori foizning o‘zi xato belgisi emas. D javob mumkin bo‘lgan muammoni ko‘rsatadi, lekin asosiy kamchilik — javob beruvchilarning o‘zlarini o‘zlari tanlagani.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"evaluating_statistical_claims\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"An agricultural researcher selected a random sample of 300 cotton farms from a list of all cotton farms in the Bukhara region of Uzbekistan. The farms in the sample harvested an average of 3.4 metric tons of cotton per hectare in 2025. To which of the following groups can the results of this study most appropriately be generalized?\",\"option_a\":\"All cotton farms in the Bukhara region\",\"option_b\":\"All cotton farms in Uzbekistan\",\"option_c\":\"All farms of any kind in the Bukhara region\",\"option_d\":\"Only the 300 cotton farms in the sample\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The sample was selected at random from all cotton farms in the Bukhara region, so the results can be generalized to that population, and only to that population. Choice B is too broad: farms in other regions, with different soil and water conditions, had no chance of being selected. Choice C is also too broad, because farms that do not grow cotton were not in the population sampled. Choice D is too narrow: random sampling is what allows a conclusion about the whole population.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Tanlanma Buxoro viloyatidagi barcha paxta xo‘jaliklari orasidan tasodifiy tanlangan, shuning uchun natijalarni aynan shu to‘plamga va faqat shu to‘plamga umumlashtirish mumkin. B javob juda keng: boshqa viloyatlardagi, tuprog‘i va suv sharoiti boshqacha bo‘lgan xo‘jaliklarning tanlanishga imkoni bo‘lmagan. C javob ham juda keng, chunki paxta yetishtirmaydigan xo‘jaliklar tanlov olingan to‘plamga kirmagan. D javob juda tor: aynan tasodifiy tanlash butun to‘plam haqida xulosa chiqarishga imkon beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"evaluating_statistical_claims\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A university has 9,000 students. A random sample of 250 of these students was surveyed, and 70 of the students in the sample said they visit the university library every day. Based on the sample, what is the best estimate of the number of students at the university who visit the library every day?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"2520\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The sample was selected at random, so the proportion in the sample estimates the proportion of all students: $\\\\frac{70}{250} = 0.28$. Then $0.28 \\\\times 9{,}000 = 2{,}520$ students. Answering 70 counts only the students in the sample. Enter 2520 (without a comma).\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Tanlanma tasodifiy olingan, shuning uchun tanlanmadagi ulush barcha talabalar ulushini baholaydi: $\\\\frac{70}{250} = 0.28$. Demak $0.28 \\\\times 9{,}000 = 2{,}520$ nafar talaba. 70 deb javob berish faqat tanlanmadagi talabalarni sanaydi. Javob maydoniga vergulsiz 2520 deb yozing.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"evaluating_statistical_claims\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Using World Bank data for 150 countries, a student found that countries with a higher percentage of people who use the internet tend to have a higher average life expectancy. Which of the following conclusions is best supported by this information?\",\"option_a\":\"Increasing internet use in a country will cause the country's average life expectancy to increase.\",\"option_b\":\"For the 150 countries, there is a positive association between internet use and life expectancy, but the data do not show that one causes the other.\",\"option_c\":\"People who use the internet live longer than people who do not use the internet.\",\"option_d\":\"Increasing life expectancy in a country will cause more people in the country to use the internet.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"These are observational data: no one assigned internet use to countries. The data show a positive association, but other factors, such as a country's wealth, may raise both internet use and life expectancy, so a cause-and-effect conclusion is not supported. Choices A and D each claim causation (in opposite directions), which requires a randomized experiment. Choice C draws a conclusion about individual people from country-level averages; the data say nothing about whether individual internet users live longer.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Bu kuzatuv ma’lumotlari: hech kim davlatlarga internetdan foydalanishni tayinlamagan. Ma’lumotlar musbat bog‘liqlikni ko‘rsatadi, ammo boshqa omillar, masalan, davlatning boyligi, ham internetdan foydalanishni, ham umr ko‘rish davomiyligini oshirishi mumkin, shuning uchun sabab-oqibat xulosasi asoslanmagan. A va D javoblar sabab-oqibat bog‘liqligini (qarama-qarshi yo‘nalishlarda) da’vo qiladi — buning uchun tasodifiy tayinlashli tajriba kerak. C javob davlatlar bo‘yicha o‘rtacha qiymatlardan alohida odamlar haqida xulosa chiqargan; ma’lumotlar internetdan foydalanuvchi alohida odamlar uzoqroq yashashi haqida hech narsa demaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"evaluating_statistical_claims\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A researcher recruited 120 farmers in the Fergana region who volunteered to take part in a study. Each farmer's apricot orchard was randomly assigned to use either drip irrigation or the farmer's usual watering method for one season. The orchards that used drip irrigation produced significantly more apricots, on average, than the other orchards. Which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion?\",\"option_a\":\"Drip irrigation increases apricot production for all orchards in Uzbekistan.\",\"option_b\":\"Drip irrigation increases apricot production for all orchards in the Fergana region.\",\"option_c\":\"No conclusion about cause and effect can be made, because the farmers were volunteers.\",\"option_d\":\"Drip irrigation is likely to increase apricot production for orchards similar to those in the study.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Because the watering methods were **randomly assigned**, the difference in production can be attributed to drip irrigation, so a cause-and-effect conclusion is reasonable. However, the farmers were volunteers, not a random sample, so the result cannot be generalized to all orchards in the region or the country, only to orchards like those in the study. Choices A and B generalize too far. Choice C is wrong: random assignment, not random sampling, is what supports a causal conclusion.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Sug‘orish usullari **tasodifiy tayinlangani** uchun hosildagi farqni tomchilatib sug‘orishga bog‘lash mumkin, ya’ni sabab-oqibat xulosasi o‘rinli. Biroq fermerlar tasodifiy tanlanma emas, ko‘ngillilar edi, shuning uchun natijani viloyat yoki mamlakatdagi barcha bog‘larga emas, faqat tadqiqotdagilarga o‘xshash bog‘larga umumlashtirish mumkin. A va B javoblar haddan tashqari keng umumlashtirgan. C javob noto‘g‘ri: sabab-oqibat xulosasini tasodifiy tanlash emas, tasodifiy tayinlash asoslaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"evaluating_statistical_claims\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"The principal of a school with 1,200 students wants to estimate the average number of hours per week that the school's students spend reading for pleasure. Which of the following methods of choosing 100 students to survey is most likely to give an accurate estimate?\",\"option_a\":\"Randomly select 100 students from a list of all 1,200 students.\",\"option_b\":\"Survey the first 100 students who arrive at school on Monday.\",\"option_c\":\"Survey 100 students who are in the school library during lunch.\",\"option_d\":\"Randomly select 100 students from the members of the school's book club.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Selecting students at random from a list of **all** students gives every student the same chance of being chosen, so the sample is likely to represent the whole school. Choice C surveys students in the library, who probably read more than typical students. Choice D samples only book club members, who are also likely to read more; random selection within a biased group does not remove the bias. Choice B is a convenience sample: students who arrive first may differ from others, for example, in how far they live from school.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"**Barcha** o‘quvchilar ro‘yxatidan tasodifiy tanlash har bir o‘quvchiga tanlanish uchun teng imkoniyat beradi, shuning uchun tanlanma butun maktabni ifodalashi ehtimoli yuqori. C javob kutubxonadagi o‘quvchilarni so‘raydi — ular odatdagi o‘quvchilardan ko‘proq kitob o‘qishi ehtimoli bor. D javob faqat kitobxonlar klubi a’zolaridan tanlaydi, ular ham ko‘proq o‘qiydi; noxolis guruh ichida tasodifiy tanlash noxolislikni yo‘qotmaydi. B javob — qulaylik bo‘yicha tanlanma: birinchi kelgan o‘quvchilar boshqalardan farq qilishi mumkin, masalan, maktabdan qancha uzoqda yashashi bo‘yicha.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"evaluating_statistical_claims\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A traffic agency selected a random sample of 600 drivers from a list of all registered drivers in the Samarkand region. The agency randomly assigned 300 of the selected drivers to receive weekly text-message reminders about speed limits for six months; the other 300 received no reminders. During the six months, the drivers who received the reminders got significantly fewer speeding fines, on average, than the drivers who did not. Which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion?\",\"option_a\":\"The reminders caused a decrease in speeding fines, but this conclusion applies only to the 600 drivers in the study.\",\"option_b\":\"There is an association between the reminders and speeding fines, but no conclusion about cause and effect can be made.\",\"option_c\":\"Sending such reminders would likely reduce speeding fines among registered drivers in the Samarkand region.\",\"option_d\":\"Sending such reminders would likely reduce speeding fines among all drivers in Uzbekistan.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Two features of the design matter. **Random assignment** to the reminder and no-reminder groups supports a cause-and-effect conclusion. **Random sampling** from all registered drivers in the Samarkand region allows the conclusion to be generalized to that population. So it is reasonable to conclude that the reminders would likely reduce speeding fines among registered drivers in the Samarkand region. Choice A is too narrow because the sample was random. Choice B ignores the random assignment. Choice D is too broad: drivers outside the Samarkand region were not part of the population sampled.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Tadqiqot dizaynining ikki jihati muhim. Eslatma oladigan va olmaydigan guruhlarga **tasodifiy tayinlash** sabab-oqibat xulosasini asoslaydi. Samarqand viloyatidagi barcha ro‘yxatdagi haydovchilardan **tasodifiy tanlash** esa xulosani shu to‘plamga umumlashtirishga imkon beradi. Demak, eslatmalar Samarqand viloyatidagi ro‘yxatdagi haydovchilar orasida tezlikni oshirish uchun jarimalarni kamaytirishi ehtimoli bor degan xulosa o‘rinli. A javob juda tor, chunki tanlanma tasodifiy edi. B javob tasodifiy tayinlashni e’tiborsiz qoldirgan. D javob juda keng: Samarqand viloyatidan tashqaridagi haydovchilar tanlov olingan to‘plamga kirmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":110,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"evaluating_statistical_claims\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"An energy company selected a random sample of 400 households from all of its customers in a city. Of these households, 240 were randomly assigned to receive a monthly report comparing their electricity use with that of their neighbors, and the other 160 households did not receive the report. After one year, 156 of the households that received the report and 72 of the households that did not receive the report had reduced their electricity use. Based on this experiment, the percentage of households that reduce their electricity use is estimated to be how many percentage points greater when households receive the report than when they do not?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"20\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Compare percentages, not counts, because the groups have different sizes. With the report: $\\\\frac{156}{240} = 0.65$, or 65%. Without the report: $\\\\frac{72}{160} = 0.45$, or 45%. The difference is $65 - 45 = 20$ percentage points. Comparing the counts ($156 - 72 = 84$) or dividing by all 400 households ($\\\\frac{156}{400} = 39\\\\%$ and $\\\\frac{72}{400} = 18\\\\%$, a difference of 21) gives a wrong answer. Enter 20.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Guruhlar hajmi har xil bo‘lgani uchun sonlarni emas, foizlarni taqqoslaymiz. Hisobot olganlar: $\\\\frac{156}{240} = 0.65$, ya’ni 65%. Hisobot olmaganlar: $\\\\frac{72}{160} = 0.45$, ya’ni 45%. Farq $65 - 45 = 20$ foiz punkti. Sonlarni taqqoslash ($156 - 72 = 84$) yoki barcha 400 ta xonadonga bo‘lish ($\\\\frac{156}{400} = 39\\\\%$ va $\\\\frac{72}{400} = 18\\\\%$, farq 21) noto‘g‘ri javob beradi. Javob: 20.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In January 1610, Galileo Galilei pointed his telescope at Jupiter and spotted four small points of light near the planet. Over several nights, he tracked the four ______ changing positions and concluded that they were moons orbiting Jupiter.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"object's\",\"option_b\":\"objects\",\"option_c\":\"objects'\",\"option_d\":\"objects's\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The blank needs a plural possessive noun: the changing positions belong to the four objects. A plural noun that ends in -s forms its possessive by adding only an apostrophe, so “objects'” (choice C) is correct.\\n\\nChoice A, “object's,” is singular possessive, but there are four objects. Choice B, “objects,” is plural but not possessive. Choice D, “objects's,” adds an extra s that is not used for plural nouns ending in -s.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Bo‘sh joyga ko‘plikdagi qaratqich shakl kerak: o‘zgarib turgan joylashuvlar to‘rtta jismga tegishli. -s bilan tugaydigan ko‘plikdagi otga faqat apostrof qo‘shiladi, shuning uchun “objects'” (C) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA — “object's” birlikdagi qaratqich shakl, jismlar esa to‘rtta. B — “objects” ko‘plikda, lekin qaratqich shaklda emas. D — “objects's” da -s bilan tugaydigan ko‘plikdagi otlarda ishlatilmaydigan ortiqcha s bor.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In 1869, Russian chemist Dmitri Mendeleev ______ a table that arranged the known chemical elements in order of increasing atomic weight. He left gaps in the table for elements that had not yet been discovered.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"published\",\"option_b\":\"publishes\",\"option_c\":\"will publish\",\"option_d\":\"is publishing\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The sentence describes an event that happened at a specific time in the past, 1869, and the next sentence also uses the past tense (“left”). The simple past tense “published” is therefore correct, choice A.\\n\\nChoice B (“publishes”) and choice D (“is publishing”) are present-tense forms, and choice C (“will publish”) is a future form. None of them fits an action completed in 1869.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gapda o‘tmishdagi aniq vaqtda — 1869-yilda sodir bo‘lgan voqea tasvirlangan, keyingi gapda ham o‘tgan zamon (“left”) ishlatilgan. Shuning uchun oddiy o‘tgan zamondagi “published” to‘g‘ri, javob A.\\n\\nB (“publishes”) va D (“is publishing”) hozirgi zamon shakllari, C (“will publish”) esa kelasi zamon shakli. Ularning hech biri 1869-yilda tugallangan harakatga mos kelmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":40,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"During the Antarctic winter, male emperor penguins huddle together in large groups to survive bitter cold and wind. By continually shuffling between the cold outer edge of the huddle and its warm center, ______ take turns sheltering from the wind.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"it\",\"option_b\":\"he\",\"option_c\":\"one\",\"option_d\":\"they\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The pronoun in the blank refers to “male emperor penguins,” a plural noun, and it must agree with the plural verb “take.” The plural pronoun “they” does both, so choice D is correct.\\n\\nChoices A (“it”), B (“he”), and C (“one”) are singular pronouns. They do not agree with the plural antecedent “penguins” or with the plural verb “take.”\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Bo‘sh joydagi olmosh “male emperor penguins” ga, ya’ni ko‘plikdagi otga ishora qiladi va ko‘plikdagi “take” kesimiga mos kelishi kerak. Ko‘plikdagi “they” olmoshi ikkalasiga ham mos, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob D.\\n\\nA (“it”), B (“he”) va C (“one”) birlikdagi olmoshlar. Ular ko‘plikdagi “penguins” so‘ziga ham, ko‘plikdagi “take” kesimiga ham mos kelmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The Shah-i-Zinda necropolis in Samarkand, a narrow street lined with more than twenty mausoleums and other buildings, ______ built over roughly eight centuries, from the eleventh century to the nineteenth.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"were\",\"option_b\":\"was\",\"option_c\":\"have been\",\"option_d\":\"are being\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The subject of the sentence is the singular noun “necropolis” (“The Shah-i-Zinda necropolis in Samarkand”). The long phrase that follows, “a narrow street lined with more than twenty mausoleums and other buildings,” only describes the necropolis and does not change the subject's number. A singular verb is needed, and because the building took place in the past, “was” (choice B) is correct.\\n\\nChoices A (“were”), C (“have been”), and D (“are being”) are plural verbs. They might seem to agree with the nearby plural nouns “mausoleums” and “buildings,” but those nouns are not the subject.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gapning egasi — birlikdagi “necropolis” (“The Shah-i-Zinda necropolis in Samarkand”). Undan keyingi uzun ibora — “a narrow street lined with more than twenty mausoleums and other buildings” — faqat nekropolni tasvirlaydi va eganing sonini o‘zgartirmaydi. Birlikdagi fe’l kerak, qurilish esa o‘tmishda bo‘lgani uchun “was” (B) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA (“were”), C (“have been”) va D (“are being”) ko‘plikdagi fe’llar. Ular yaqin turgan ko‘plikdagi “mausoleums” va “buildings” so‘zlariga mosdek ko‘rinishi mumkin, lekin bu so‘zlar ega emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"For decades, coelacanths were known to scientists only from fossils, and researchers believed that these large fish had died out tens of millions of years ago. Caught by a fishing boat off the coast of South Africa in 1938, ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"a living coelacanth astonished scientists who had assumed that the group was extinct.\",\"option_b\":\"scientists who had assumed that the group was extinct were astonished by a living coelacanth.\",\"option_c\":\"the discovery of a living coelacanth astonished scientists who had assumed that the group was extinct.\",\"option_d\":\"the news of a living coelacanth astonished scientists who had assumed that the group was extinct.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The sentence begins with the modifier “Caught by a fishing boat off the coast of South Africa in 1938.” A modifier like this must be followed immediately by the noun it describes. The thing that was caught is the coelacanth, so the main clause must begin with “a living coelacanth.” Choice A is correct.\\n\\nChoice B places “scientists” right after the modifier, illogically saying that the scientists were caught by a fishing boat. Choices C and D place “the discovery” and “the news” after the modifier, but neither a discovery nor news can be caught by a fishing boat.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gap “Caught by a fishing boat off the coast of South Africa in 1938” aniqlovchi iborasi bilan boshlanadi. Bunday iboradan keyin darhol u tasvirlayotgan ot kelishi kerak. Qayiq tutgan narsa — selakant, shuning uchun bosh gap “a living coelacanth” bilan boshlanishi kerak. To‘g‘ri javob A.\\n\\nB iboradan keyin “scientists” ni qo‘yadi — natijada olimlarni baliqchi qayiq tutgan degan mantiqsiz ma’no chiqadi. C va D iboradan keyin “the discovery” va “the news” ni qo‘yadi, lekin kashfiyotni ham, xabarni ham qayiq tuta olmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":65,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The Indian mathematician and astronomer Brahmagupta, in a work that he completed in 628 CE, ______ rules for calculating with zero, which he defined as the result of subtracting a number from itself.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"giving\",\"option_b\":\"having given\",\"option_c\":\"gave\",\"option_d\":\"to give\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The subject of the sentence is “Brahmagupta,” but without the blank the sentence has no main verb: “in a work that he completed in 628 CE” is a phrase, and “completed” belongs to that phrase. The blank must supply a finite verb for the subject. “Gave” is a finite past-tense verb, so choice C is correct.\\n\\nChoices A (“giving”), B (“having given”), and D (“to give”) are nonfinite forms—a participle, a perfect participle, and an infinitive. None of them can serve as the main verb of a sentence, so each choice leaves the sentence incomplete.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gapning egasi — “Brahmagupta”, lekin bo‘sh joysiz gapda asosiy kesim yo‘q: “in a work that he completed in 628 CE” — ibora, “completed” esa shu iboraga tegishli. Bo‘sh joyga egaga mos shaxsli fe’l kerak. “Gave” — o‘tgan zamondagi shaxsli fe’l, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\nA (“giving”), B (“having given”) va D (“to give”) — shaxssiz shakllar: sifatdosh, tugallangan sifatdosh va infinitiv. Ularning hech biri gapning asosiy kesimi bo‘la olmaydi, shuning uchun gap tugallanmay qoladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":65,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Voyager 1 was launched from Cape Canaveral, Florida, in 1977 to study Jupiter and Saturn. Since then, the spacecraft ______ farther from Earth than any other human-made object, and in 2012 it became the first spacecraft to enter interstellar space.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"traveled\",\"option_b\":\"travels\",\"option_c\":\"had traveled\",\"option_d\":\"has traveled\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The phrase “Since then” describes an action that began in the past (1977) and continues up to the present. The present perfect tense is used for such actions, so “has traveled” (choice D) is correct.\\n\\nChoice A (“traveled”) is the simple past tense, which describes a finished action and does not fit with “since then.” Choice B (“travels”) is the simple present tense, which does not connect the action to its starting point in the past. Choice C (“had traveled”) is the past perfect tense, which describes an action completed before another past event; no such later past event is given.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“Since then” iborasi o‘tmishda (1977-yilda) boshlanib, hozirgacha davom etayotgan harakatni bildiradi. Bunday harakatlar uchun “present perfect” zamoni ishlatiladi, shuning uchun “has traveled” (D) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA (“traveled”) oddiy o‘tgan zamon — u tugallangan harakatni bildiradi va “since then” ga mos kelmaydi. B (“travels”) oddiy hozirgi zamon — u harakatni o‘tmishdagi boshlanish nuqtasi bilan bog‘lamaydi. C (“had traveled”) “past perfect” zamoni — u o‘tmishdagi boshqa voqeadan oldin tugagan harakatni bildiradi, matnda esa bunday keyingi o‘tmish voqeasi yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Emmanuelle Charpentier and Jennifer Doudna, whose 2012 research showed how a defense system that bacteria use against viruses could be turned into a precise tool for editing DNA, ______ awarded the 2020 Nobel Prize in Chemistry.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"was\",\"option_b\":\"were\",\"option_c\":\"is\",\"option_d\":\"has been\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The subject of the sentence is “Emmanuelle Charpentier and Jennifer Doudna,” two people joined by “and,” so it requires a plural verb. The long clause beginning with “whose” separates the subject from the verb but does not change its number. Because the prize was awarded in the past (2020), the plural past-tense verb “were” (choice B) is correct.\\n\\nChoices A (“was”), C (“is”), and D (“has been”) are singular verbs. They may seem to fit because the singular nouns “system,” “tool,” and “DNA” appear just before the blank, but those words are part of the interrupting clause, not the subject.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gapning egasi — “Emmanuelle Charpentier and Jennifer Doudna”, ya’ni “and” bilan bog‘langan ikki kishi, shuning uchun ko‘plikdagi fe’l kerak. “whose” bilan boshlanadigan uzun ergash gap egani kesimdan ajratib turadi, lekin eganing sonini o‘zgartirmaydi. Mukofot o‘tmishda (2020-yilda) berilgani uchun ko‘plikdagi o‘tgan zamon fe’li “were” (B) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA (“was”), C (“is”) va D (“has been”) birlikdagi fe’llar. Bo‘sh joydan oldin birlikdagi “system”, “tool” va “DNA” so‘zlari kelgani uchun ular to‘g‘ridek ko‘rinishi mumkin, lekin bu so‘zlar ega emas, oraliq ergash gapning qismi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inference_margin_of_error\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A random sample of households in Tashkent was surveyed. Based on the sample, it is estimated that 58% of all households in Tashkent have an air conditioner, with a margin of error of 3.5 percentage points. Which of the following is a plausible value for the percentage of all households in Tashkent that have an air conditioner?\",\"option_a\":\"52%\",\"option_b\":\"54%\",\"option_c\":\"60%\",\"option_d\":\"63%\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The plausible values are the estimate plus or minus the margin of error: from $58 - 3.5 = 54.5\\\\%$ to $58 + 3.5 = 61.5\\\\%$. Only 60% is in this interval. Choice B, 54%, is just below the lower endpoint, and choice D, 63%, is above the upper endpoint. Choice A, 52%, is even farther below the interval; it would be plausible only if the margin of error were at least 6 percentage points.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ehtimoliy qiymatlar — baho plyus-minus xatolik chegarasi: $58 - 3.5 = 54.5\\\\%$ dan $58 + 3.5 = 61.5\\\\%$ gacha. Bu oraliqda faqat 60% bor. B javob (54%) quyi chegaradan sal pastda, D javob (63%) esa yuqori chegaradan yuqorida. A javob (52%) oraliqdan yanada pastda; u faqat xatolik chegarasi kamida 6 foiz punkti bo‘lganda ehtimoliy bo‘lardi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inference_margin_of_error\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"An apricot orchard in the Fergana Valley has 2,400 trees. A random sample of 160 of the trees was inspected, and 12 of the sampled trees had a leaf disease. Based on the sample, what is the best estimate of the number of trees in the entire orchard that have the leaf disease?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"180\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The proportion of diseased trees in the random sample is $\\\\frac{12}{160} = 0.075$. Applying it to the whole orchard: $0.075 \\\\times 2{,}400 = 180$ trees. Answering 12 counts only the sampled trees. Enter 180.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Tasodifiy tanlanmadagi kasal daraxtlar ulushi $\\\\frac{12}{160} = 0.075$. Uni butun bog‘ga qo‘llaymiz: $0.075 \\\\times 2{,}400 = 180$ ta daraxt. 12 deb javob berish faqat tekshirilgan daraxtlarni sanaydi. Javob: 180.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inference_margin_of_error\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A random sample of people who ride the Tashkent metro to work was surveyed. Based on the sample, the mean one-way commute time of all people who ride the Tashkent metro to work is estimated to be 34 minutes, with an associated margin of error of 2.5 minutes. Which of the following gives the plausible values for the mean commute time of all people who ride the Tashkent metro to work?\",\"option_a\":\"From 29 minutes to 39 minutes\",\"option_b\":\"From 31.5 minutes to 36.5 minutes\",\"option_c\":\"From 34 minutes to 36.5 minutes\",\"option_d\":\"From 33.15 minutes to 34.85 minutes\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Plausible values are the estimate minus the margin of error to the estimate plus the margin of error: $34 - 2.5 = 31.5$ to $34 + 2.5 = 36.5$ minutes. Choice A uses twice the margin of error on each side. Choice C adds the margin of error but never subtracts it. Choice D treats the margin of error as 2.5% of 34 ($0.85$ minute) instead of 2.5 minutes.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ehtimoliy qiymatlar: bahodan xatolik chegarasini ayirgandan bahoga xatolik chegarasini qo‘shgangacha, ya’ni $34 - 2.5 = 31.5$ dan $34 + 2.5 = 36.5$ daqiqagacha. A javob har ikki tomonga xatolik chegarasini ikki marta olgan. C javob xatolik chegarasini faqat qo‘shgan, ayirmagan. D javob xatolik chegarasini 2.5 daqiqa emas, 34 ning 2.5% i ($0.85$ daqiqa) deb olgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inference_margin_of_error\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Tashkent city has about 3,110,000 residents. Based on a random sample of the city's residents, it is estimated that 24% of all residents ride the metro at least once a week, with a margin of error of 3 percentage points. Based on this estimate, which of the following gives the plausible values for the number of Tashkent residents who ride the metro at least once a week?\",\"option_a\":\"From 653,100 to 746,400\",\"option_b\":\"From 724,008 to 768,792\",\"option_c\":\"From 746,400 to 839,700\",\"option_d\":\"From 653,100 to 839,700\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The plausible percentages are $24 - 3 = 21\\\\%$ to $24 + 3 = 27\\\\%$. Applying them to the population: $0.21 \\\\times 3{,}110{,}000 = 653{,}100$ and $0.27 \\\\times 3{,}110{,}000 = 839{,}700$. Choices A and C each give only one half of the interval, from an endpoint to the estimate ($0.24 \\\\times 3{,}110{,}000 = 746{,}400$). Choice B treats the margin of error as 3% **of** 746,400 instead of 3 percentage points of the population.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ehtimoliy foizlar: $24 - 3 = 21\\\\%$ dan $24 + 3 = 27\\\\%$ gacha. Ularni aholiga qo‘llaymiz: $0.21 \\\\times 3{,}110{,}000 = 653{,}100$ va $0.27 \\\\times 3{,}110{,}000 = 839{,}700$. A va C javoblar oraliqning faqat yarmini — chegaradan bahogacha ($0.24 \\\\times 3{,}110{,}000 = 746{,}400$) bo‘lgan qismini beradi. B javob xatolik chegarasini aholining 3 foiz punkti emas, 746,400 ning 3% i deb olgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inference_margin_of_error\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A random sample of students at a university in Samarkand was asked whether they use public transportation to get to classes. Based on the sample, the plausible values for the percentage of all students at the university who use public transportation to get to classes are from 61% to 69%. What is the margin of error, in percentage points, associated with this estimate?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"4\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The interval is centered at the estimate, $\\\\frac{61 + 69}{2} = 65\\\\%$, and extends the margin of error on each side. So the margin of error is half the width of the interval: $\\\\frac{69 - 61}{2} = 4$ percentage points. Answering 8 gives the full width of the interval, which is twice the margin of error. Enter 4.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Oraliq markazi — baho: $\\\\frac{61 + 69}{2} = 65\\\\%$; oraliq har ikki tomonga xatolik chegarasicha cho‘ziladi. Demak xatolik chegarasi oraliq kengligining yarmi: $\\\\frac{69 - 61}{2} = 4$ foiz punkti. 8 deb javob berish oraliqning to‘liq kengligini beradi — bu xatolik chegarasining ikki baravari. Javob: 4.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inference_margin_of_error\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A random sample of adults in a city was asked whether they support a plan to plant 10,000 new trees. Based on the sample, it is estimated that 53% of all adults in the city support the plan, with a margin of error of 4 percentage points. A city council member claims that the survey proves that a majority (more than 50%) of the city's adults support the plan. Which of the following best evaluates this claim?\",\"option_a\":\"The claim is not supported, because plausible values range from 49% to 57%, and values of 50% or less are in this interval.\",\"option_b\":\"The claim is supported, because the estimate of 53% is greater than 50%.\",\"option_c\":\"The claim is supported, because 53% plus the margin of error is 57%, which is greater than 50%.\",\"option_d\":\"The survey shows that less than half of the city's adults support the plan, because 49% is a plausible value.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The plausible values are $53 - 4 = 49\\\\%$ to $53 + 4 = 57\\\\%$. Since this interval includes values at or below 50%, the survey does not prove that more than half of the city's adults support the plan; a majority is plausible but not certain. Choice B ignores the margin of error. Choice C uses only the upper end of the interval. Choice D makes the opposite mistake: 49% is plausible, but so are values above 50%, so the survey does not show that less than half support the plan either.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ehtimoliy qiymatlar: $53 - 4 = 49\\\\%$ dan $53 + 4 = 57\\\\%$ gacha. Bu oraliqda 50% va undan kichik qiymatlar ham bor, shuning uchun so‘rovnoma shahar kattalarining yarmidan ko‘pi rejani qo‘llab-quvvatlashini isbotlamaydi; ko‘pchilik qo‘llashi mumkin, lekin aniq emas. B javob xatolik chegarasini e’tiborsiz qoldirgan. C javob oraliqning faqat yuqori chegarasidan foydalangan. D javob teskari xatoga yo‘l qo‘ygan: 49% ehtimoliy qiymat, lekin 50% dan yuqori qiymatlar ham ehtimoliy, shuning uchun so‘rovnoma yarmidan kami qo‘llashini ham ko‘rsatmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inference_margin_of_error\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"To estimate the mean wheat yield per hectare in two regions, a researcher selected a random sample of 200 wheat fields in Region X and a random sample of 200 wheat fields in Region Y. The yields of the sampled fields in Region X had a standard deviation of 0.9 metric ton per hectare, and the yields of the sampled fields in Region Y had a standard deviation of 0.4 metric ton per hectare. The same method was used to calculate a margin of error for each estimate. Which of the following is most likely true?\",\"option_a\":\"The estimate for Region X has the smaller margin of error, because its yields vary more.\",\"option_b\":\"The estimate for Region Y has the smaller margin of error, because its yields vary less.\",\"option_c\":\"The two margins of error are equal, because the two samples are the same size.\",\"option_d\":\"The margins of error cannot be compared unless the two sample means are equal.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"For samples of the same size, a margin of error depends on how much the data vary: the more the values vary, the less precisely the sample mean estimates the population mean, and the greater the margin of error. Region Y's yields have a much smaller standard deviation (0.4 compared with 0.9), so its estimate most likely has the smaller margin of error. Choice A reverses this relationship. Choice C is wrong because sample size is not the only factor; variability also matters. Choice D is wrong because the margin of error does not depend on the value of the mean.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Hajmi bir xil tanlanmalarda xatolik chegarasi ma’lumotlarning qanchalik tarqoqligiga bog‘liq: qiymatlar qancha ko‘p o‘zgarsa, tanlanma o‘rtachasi to‘plam o‘rtachasini shuncha kam aniqlikda baholaydi va xatolik chegarasi shuncha katta bo‘ladi. Y viloyatidagi hosildorlikning standart chetlanishi ancha kichik (0.9 ga nisbatan 0.4), shuning uchun uning bahosining xatolik chegarasi kichikroq bo‘lishi ehtimoli katta. A javob bu bog‘liqlikni teskari qilgan. C javob noto‘g‘ri, chunki tanlanma hajmi yagona omil emas — tarqoqlik ham muhim. D javob noto‘g‘ri, chunki xatolik chegarasi o‘rtachaning qiymatiga bog‘liq emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":110,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inference_margin_of_error\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A district has 12,000 households. Based on a random sample of the district's households, the percentage of all households in the district that have a home garden was estimated, along with a margin of error. When the estimate and the margin of error were applied to all 12,000 households, the plausible numbers of households with a home garden were from 2,940 to 3,660. What is the margin of error, in percentage points?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"3\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Convert the endpoints to percentages of 12,000: $\\\\frac{2{,}940}{12{,}000} = 0.245 = 24.5\\\\%$ and $\\\\frac{3{,}660}{12{,}000} = 0.305 = 30.5\\\\%$. The estimate is the center, $\\\\frac{24.5 + 30.5}{2} = 27.5\\\\%$, and the margin of error is half the width: $\\\\frac{30.5 - 24.5}{2} = 3$ percentage points. Equivalently, half the width in households is $\\\\frac{3{,}660 - 2{,}940}{2} = 360$, and $\\\\frac{360}{12{,}000} = 3\\\\%$. Answering 360 gives the margin of error in households, not percentage points, and answering 6 gives the full width. Enter 3.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Chegaralarni 12,000 ga nisbatan foizga aylantiramiz: $\\\\frac{2{,}940}{12{,}000} = 0.245 = 24.5\\\\%$ va $\\\\frac{3{,}660}{12{,}000} = 0.305 = 30.5\\\\%$. Baho — markaz: $\\\\frac{24.5 + 30.5}{2} = 27.5\\\\%$, xatolik chegarasi esa kenglikning yarmi: $\\\\frac{30.5 - 24.5}{2} = 3$ foiz punkti. Boshqacha aytganda, xonadonlarda kenglikning yarmi $\\\\frac{3{,}660 - 2{,}940}{2} = 360$, va $\\\\frac{360}{12{,}000} = 3\\\\%$. 360 deb javob berish xatolik chegarasini foiz punktida emas, xonadonlar sonida beradi; 6 esa to‘liq kenglik. Javob: 3.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from Jane Austen's 1813 novel *Pride and Prejudice*. Mr. Bingley and his friend Mr. Darcy are attending a ball. Mrs. Bennet and her daughters are also guests.\\n\\nMr. Bingley had soon made himself acquainted with all the principal people in the room: he was lively and unreserved, danced every dance, was angry that the ball closed so early, and talked of giving one himself at Netherfield. Such amiable qualities must speak for themselves. What a contrast between him and his friend! Mr. Darcy danced only once with Mrs. Hurst and once with Miss Bingley, declined being introduced to any other lady, and spent the rest of the evening in walking about the room, speaking occasionally to one of his own party. … Amongst the most violent against him was Mrs. Bennet, whose dislike of his general behaviour was sharpened into particular resentment by his having slighted one of her daughters.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the text, what can most reasonably be inferred about Mrs. Bennet's opinion of Mr. Darcy?\",\"option_a\":\"It is based partly on how he treated a member of her own family.\",\"option_b\":\"It is more favorable than the opinion held by most of the other guests.\",\"option_c\":\"It improved after Mr. Darcy danced with one of her daughters.\",\"option_d\":\"It was formed before she had ever seen Mr. Darcy.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Mrs. Bennet disliked Mr. Darcy's “general behaviour,” but her dislike was “sharpened into particular resentment by his having slighted one of her daughters.” Her opinion therefore rests partly on how he treated her own daughter, as choice A says.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because she is “amongst the most violent against him,” so her opinion is not more favorable than others'. Choice C is wrong because Mr. Darcy “slighted” her daughter and danced only with Mrs. Hurst and Miss Bingley. Choice D is wrong because her resentment came from his behavior at the ball, which she observed.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Bennet xonimga janob Darsining “umumiy xulq-atvori” yoqmagan, ammo bu norozilik u “qizlaridan birini mensimagani” sababli “alohida adovatga aylangan.” Demak, uning fikri qisman Darsi uning qiziga qanday munosabatda bo‘lganiga asoslangan — to‘g‘ri javob A.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: u “Darsiga eng qattiq qarshi bo‘lganlar orasida,” demak uning fikri boshqalarnikidan ijobiyroq emas. C noto‘g‘ri: janob Darsi uning qizini “mensimagan” va faqat Xerst xonim hamda miss Bingli bilan raqsga tushgan. D noto‘g‘ri: uning adovati Darsining bal paytidagi xatti-harakatidan kelib chiqqan, buni esa u o‘z ko‘zi bilan ko‘rgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from Willa Cather's 1913 novel *O Pioneers!*\\n\\nOn the sidewalk in front of one of the stores sat a little Swede boy, crying bitterly. He was about five years old. … He cried quietly, and the few people who hurried by did not notice him. He was afraid to stop any one, afraid to go into the store and ask for help, so he sat wringing his long sleeves and looking up a telegraph pole beside him, whimpering, “My kitten, oh, my kitten! Her will fweeze!” … He was a little country boy, and this village was to him a very strange and perplexing place, where people wore fine clothes and had hard hearts. He always felt shy and awkward here, and wanted to hide behind things for fear some one might laugh at him.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the text, what can most reasonably be inferred about why the boy does not ask anyone for help?\",\"option_a\":\"He believes that his kitten will soon climb down on its own.\",\"option_b\":\"He feels out of place in the town and fears how the people there will treat him.\",\"option_c\":\"He has been told by his parents never to speak to strangers.\",\"option_d\":\"He thinks that no one in the town is strong enough to reach the kitten.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The boy is “afraid to stop any one,” and the text explains that the village seems “strange and perplexing” to him, a place where people “had hard hearts,” and that he fears someone “might laugh at him.” It is reasonable to infer that he stays silent because he feels like an outsider and fears the townspeople's reaction, as choice B says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because he is crying and worried that the kitten “will fweeze” (freeze), which shows he does not expect it to come down safely. Choice C is wrong because the text never mentions any instructions from his parents. Choice D is wrong because nothing suggests he doubts anyone's strength; his fear concerns how people will treat him.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Bola “hech kimni to‘xtatishga qo‘rqadi”; matnda shaharcha unga “g‘alati va tushunarsiz,” odamlari “tosh yurak” joy bo‘lib tuyulgani va kimdir “ustidan kulishidan” qo‘rqishi aytiladi. Demak, u o‘zini begona his qilgani va shahar aholisining munosabatidan qo‘rqqani uchun indamay o‘tiradi, degan xulosa mantiqiy — to‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: u yig‘layapti va mushukcha “muzlab qoladi” deb xavotirda, ya’ni u mushukcha o‘zi eson-omon tushadi deb o‘ylamaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: matnda ota-onasining biror ko‘rsatmasi tilga olinmaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: u birovning kuchiga shubha qilayotgani haqida ishora yo‘q; uning qo‘rquvi odamlar unga qanday munosabatda bo‘lishi bilan bog‘liq.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Over one summer, ecologist Bakhrom Tursunov counted the bees visiting two neighboring sunflower fields. The fields were planted on the same day with the same seed, but one was bordered by a strip of wildflowers and the other was not. Tursunov counted nearly twice as many bees in the field with the wildflower strip, and that field also produced noticeably more sunflower seeds. Because sunflowers produce more seeds when more of their flowers are pollinated, these results suggest that ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"sunflowers produce more seeds when they are planted later in the season.\",\"option_b\":\"bees generally avoid fields that are bordered by wildflowers.\",\"option_c\":\"the wildflower strip may have drawn more bees to the area, leading to more pollination of the sunflowers.\",\"option_d\":\"the field without the wildflower strip received more rain during the summer.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The field with the wildflower strip had nearly twice as many bees and more seeds, and the text notes that more pollination leads to more seeds. The most logical conclusion is that the wildflowers attracted bees that then pollinated the sunflowers, as choice C says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because both fields were planted on the same day. Choice B contradicts the results: the field bordered by wildflowers had more bees. Choice D is unsupported: the text gives no information about rainfall.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Yovvoyi gullar chizig‘i bo‘lgan dalada asalarilar deyarli ikki baravar ko‘p va urug‘ ham ko‘proq bo‘lgan; matnda ko‘proq changlanish ko‘proq urug‘ berishi ham aytilgan. Eng mantiqiy xulosa — yovvoyi gullar asalarilarni jalb qilgan, ular esa kungaboqarlarni changlatgan. To‘g‘ri javob — C.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: ikkala dala ham bir kunda ekilgan. B natijalarga zid: yovvoyi gullar bilan o‘ralgan dalada asalarilar ko‘proq bo‘lgan. D asossiz: matnda yog‘ingarchilik haqida ma’lumot yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from Zitkala-Ša's 1900 memoir “Impressions of an Indian Childhood.” The narrator, a young Dakota girl, is walking home from the river with her mother, who carries water for the family.\\n\\nSo I said: “Mother, when I am tall as my cousin Warca-Ziwin, you shall not have to come for water. I will do it for you.” With a strange tremor in her voice which I could not understand, she answered, “If the paleface does not take away from us the river we drink.” … Setting the pail of water on the ground, my mother stooped, and stretching her left hand out on the level with my eyes, she placed her other arm about me; she pointed to the hill where my uncle and my only sister lay buried. “There is what the paleface has done! Since then your father too has been buried in a hill nearer the rising sun. We were once very happy. But the paleface has stolen our lands and driven us hither.”\",\"stem\":\"Based on the text, what can most reasonably be inferred about the mother's response to her daughter's promise?\",\"option_a\":\"The mother is annoyed that her daughter admires her cousin so much.\",\"option_b\":\"The mother doubts that her daughter will ever grow as tall as her cousin.\",\"option_c\":\"The mother expects the family to return soon to the land where they once lived happily.\",\"option_d\":\"The mother fears that the family may lose even the river they now depend on, just as they lost their land before.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The daughter promises to carry water when she is older, and the mother answers with “a strange tremor,” adding, “If the paleface does not take away from us the river we drink.” She then explains that the family's land was “stolen” and they were “driven” to this place. Her reply suggests she fears losing the river too, as choice D says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the mother never reacts to the cousin; her concern is the river. Choice B is wrong because her condition (“If the paleface does not take away…”) concerns the river, not her daughter's height. Choice C is wrong because she says the family was “driven” away and expresses no expectation of returning.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qizcha katta bo‘lganida suvni o‘zi tashishini va’da qiladi, onasi esa “g‘alati titroq ovoz” bilan javob beradi: “Agar oq yuzli bizdan ichadigan daryomizni tortib olmasa.” So‘ng u oilaning yerlari “o‘g‘irlangani” va ular bu yerga “haydab kelingani”ni tushuntiradi. Uning javobi daryodan ham ayrilib qolishdan qo‘rqayotganini ko‘rsatadi — to‘g‘ri javob D.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: ona jiyani haqida hech qanday munosabat bildirmaydi, uning tashvishi — daryo. B noto‘g‘ri: uning sharti (“Agar oq yuzli tortib olmasa…”) qizining bo‘yi haqida emas, daryo haqida. C noto‘g‘ri: u oila “haydab chiqarilgan”ini aytadi va qaytib borishni kutayotganini bildirmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from the Introduction to the 1872 edition of Charles Darwin's *On the Origin of Species*. Darwin is outlining the chapters of the book.\\n\\nThis fundamental subject of natural selection will be treated at some length in the fourth chapter; and we shall then see how natural selection almost inevitably causes much extinction of the less improved forms of life, and leads to what I have called divergence of character. In the next chapter I shall discuss the complex and little known laws of variation. In the five succeeding chapters, the most apparent and gravest difficulties in accepting the theory will be given: namely, first, the difficulties of transitions … secondly the subject of instinct, or the mental powers of animals; thirdly, hybridism … and fourthly, the imperfection of the geological record.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the text, what can most reasonably be inferred about Darwin's approach in the book?\",\"option_a\":\"He believes that his theory faces no serious difficulties.\",\"option_b\":\"He considers the laws of variation to be fully understood.\",\"option_c\":\"He intends to devote a large part of the book to addressing objections that readers might raise against his theory.\",\"option_d\":\"He plans to discuss the geological record before he discusses natural selection.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Darwin says that “five succeeding chapters” will present “the most apparent and gravest difficulties in accepting the theory.” Giving several whole chapters to these difficulties suggests that he plans to take objections seriously and address them at length, as choice C says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because he calls some difficulties “gravest,” acknowledging that they are serious. Choice B is wrong because he calls the laws of variation “complex and little known.” Choice D is wrong because natural selection comes in the fourth chapter, while the geological record is part of the difficulties discussed in later chapters.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Darvin “keyingi beshta bob”da “nazariyani qabul qilishdagi eng ko‘zga tashlanadigan va eng jiddiy qiyinchiliklar” keltirilishini aytadi. Bu qiyinchiliklarga bir nechta to‘liq bob ajratilishi uning e’tirozlarga jiddiy qarashini va ularni batafsil ko‘rib chiqmoqchi ekanini ko‘rsatadi — to‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: u ba’zi qiyinchiliklarni “eng jiddiy” deb ataydi, ya’ni ularning jiddiyligini tan oladi. B noto‘g‘ri: u o‘zgaruvchanlik qonunlarini “murakkab va kam o‘rganilgan” deb ataydi. D noto‘g‘ri: tabiiy tanlanish to‘rtinchi bobda keladi, geologik yilnoma esa keyingi boblarda muhokama qilinadigan qiyinchiliklar qatorida.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Astronomers monitoring a distant star noticed that its brightness dips by the same small amount every 41 days, with each dip lasting about six hours. When they examined older telescope images of the star taken over the previous decade, they found dips of the same size occurring on the same 41-day schedule throughout. Because a small, brief dip in a star's brightness can occur when a planet passes between the star and observers on Earth, the regularity of these dips suggests that ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"the star's brightness has steadily declined over the past decade.\",\"option_b\":\"the dips began only recently and are likely to stop soon.\",\"option_c\":\"the telescopes used in the older images were not working properly.\",\"option_d\":\"the dips may be caused by a planet that orbits the star once every 41 days.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The dips repeat every 41 days with the same size and duration, and they have done so for a decade. Since a planet passing in front of a star can cause such a dip, a dip that recurs on a fixed schedule suggests an object, such as a planet, orbiting the star every 41 days. Choice D follows logically.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the text describes repeated brief dips, not a steady decline. Choice B contradicts the older images, which show the dips occurring throughout the past decade. Choice C is unsupported: the older images match the newer observations, which gives no reason to think the telescopes were faulty.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Yorqinlikning pasayishlari har 41 kunda bir xil kattalik va davomiylikda takrorlanadi va bu o‘n yildan beri davom etmoqda. Sayyora yulduz oldidan o‘tganda shunday pasayish yuz berishi mumkinligi sababli, qat’iy jadval bo‘yicha takrorlanadigan pasayish yulduz atrofida har 41 kunda aylanadigan jism, masalan, sayyora borligini ko‘rsatadi. D javob mantiqan kelib chiqadi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: matnda doimiy pasayish emas, takroriy qisqa pasayishlar tasvirlangan. B eski tasvirlarga zid: ular pasayishlar so‘nggi o‘n yil davomida bo‘lganini ko‘rsatadi. C asossiz: eski tasvirlar yangi kuzatuvlarga mos keladi, demak teleskoplar nosoz bo‘lgan deyishga asos yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":65,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from Mark Twain's 1883 memoir *Life on the Mississippi*. As a boy, Twain longed to work on a steamboat. Here he describes another boy from his town who had found such a job.\\n\\nThat boy had been notoriously worldly, and I just the reverse; yet he was exalted to this eminence, and I left in obscurity and misery. There was nothing generous about this fellow in his greatness. He would always manage to have a rusty bolt to scrub while his boat tarried at our town, and he would sit on the inside guard and scrub it, where we could all see him and envy him and loathe him. … When his boat blew up at last, it diffused a tranquil contentment among us such as we had not known for months. But when he came home the next week, alive, renowned, and appeared in church all battered up and bandaged, a shining hero, stared at and wondered over by everybody, it seemed to us that the partiality of Providence for an undeserving reptile had reached a point where it was open to criticism.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the text, what can most reasonably be inferred about the narrator's attitude toward the boy?\",\"option_a\":\"The narrator believed the boy deserved his success because he worked harder than the other boys.\",\"option_b\":\"The narrator's dislike of the boy was driven largely by envy of the attention and admiration the boy received.\",\"option_c\":\"The narrator admired the boy for being modest about his job on the steamboat.\",\"option_d\":\"The narrator was relieved and grateful when the boy returned home safely after the accident.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The narrator admits that the boys would “envy him and loathe him,” and he complains that this “undeserving” boy got the job while he himself was left “in obscurity and misery.” Even the boy's survival is resented because he came home “a shining hero, stared at and wondered over by everybody.” The humorous exaggeration shows that the narrator's dislike grew mainly from envy of the boy's glory, as choice B says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the narrator calls the boy “undeserving” and says nothing about hard work; the rusty bolt is scrubbed for show, “where we could all see him.” Choice C is wrong because the boy shows off, so he is not modest. Choice D is wrong because the boys felt “tranquil contentment” when the boat blew up and treated his return as unfair luck, not as a relief.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Hikoyachi bolalar unga “havas qilib, undan nafratlanganini” tan oladi va bu “noloyiq” bola ishga joylashgani, o‘zi esa “nomsiz va baxtsiz” qolganidan nolinadi. Hatto bolaning omon qolgani ham alam qiladi, chunki u “hamma tikilib, hayratlanib qaraydigan porloq qahramon” bo‘lib qaytgan. Bu kulgili mubolag‘a hikoyachining nafrati asosan bolaning shon-shuhratiga bo‘lgan hasaddan kelib chiqqanini ko‘rsatadi — to‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: hikoyachi bolani “noloyiq” deb ataydi va mehnatsevarligi haqida hech narsa demaydi; zanglagan boltni u ko‘z-ko‘z qilish uchun, “hammamiz ko‘radigan joyda” tozalaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: bola maqtanadi, demak u kamtar emas. D noto‘g‘ri: kema portlaganda bolalar “osuda mamnuniyat” his qilgan va uning qaytishini adolatsiz omad deb bilgan, yengil tortish deb emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Scholars have long assumed that a collection of undated letters by the merchant Hasan Bekov was written during the 1840s, when records show he was living in the city of Marvan. Historian Gulnora Ismoilova points out, however, that several of the letters mention goods arriving “by the new railway,” and that the railway to Marvan was not completed until 1879. Records also show that Bekov was still alive and trading in 1885. Taken together, these facts suggest that ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"at least some of the letters were probably written after 1879, decades later than scholars have assumed.\",\"option_b\":\"none of the letters in the collection could have been written in the 1840s.\",\"option_c\":\"Bekov had moved away from Marvan before the railway was completed.\",\"option_d\":\"the records showing when the railway to Marvan was completed are inaccurate.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Several letters mention “the new railway,” which reached Marvan only in 1879, and Bekov was still alive and trading in 1885. Those letters were therefore most likely written after 1879, which conflicts with the assumption that the collection dates from the 1840s. Choice A states this conclusion with appropriate caution.\\n\\nChoice B goes too far: only “several” letters mention the railway, so the others could still date from the 1840s. Choice C is unsupported; nothing in the text indicates where Bekov lived after the 1840s, and letters mentioning the railway's arrival suggest a connection to Marvan, not a move away. Choice D is unsupported: Ismoilova relies on the railway date, and nothing suggests it is wrong.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Bir nechta maktubda “yangi temir yo‘l” tilga olinadi, temir yo‘l esa Marvanga faqat 1879-yilda yetib kelgan; Bekov 1885-yilda ham tirik bo‘lib, savdo bilan shug‘ullangan. Demak, o‘sha maktublar katta ehtimol bilan 1879-yildan keyin yozilgan, bu esa to‘plam 1840-yillarga tegishli degan taxminga zid. A javob bu xulosani kerakli ehtiyotkorlik bilan ifodalaydi.\\n\\nB haddan oshib ketadi: temir yo‘lni faqat “bir nechta” maktub tilga oladi, qolganlari hali ham 1840-yillarda yozilgan bo‘lishi mumkin. C asossiz: matnda Bekov 1840-yillardan keyin qayerda yashagani aytilmagan, temir yo‘l orqali yuk kelganini eslatuvchi maktublar esa Marvandan ketganini emas, u bilan aloqani ko‘rsatadi. D asossiz: Ismoilova temir yo‘l sanasiga tayanadi va uning noto‘g‘ri ekaniga hech qanday ishora yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_one_variable\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"What value of $x$ is the solution to the equation $9 - 2(x - 4) = 3x + 2$?\",\"option_a\":\"$-\\\\frac{1}{5}$\",\"option_b\":\"$\\\\frac{3}{5}$\",\"option_c\":\"$3$\",\"option_d\":\"$15$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Distribute the $-2$: $9 - 2x + 8 = 3x + 2$, so $17 - 2x = 3x + 2$.\\n\\nAdd $2x$ to both sides and subtract $2$: $15 = 5x$, so $x = 3$. Check: $9 - 2(3 - 4) = 9 + 2 = 11$ and $3(3) + 2 = 11$.\\n\\nChoice A comes from writing $-2(x - 4)$ as $-2x - 8$ (the sign of the $8$ is wrong). Choice B comes from multiplying only $x$ by $-2$, writing $-2x - 4$. Choice D is $5x$, not $x$: the last division by $5$ was skipped.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$-2$ ni qavsga ko‘paytiramiz: $9 - 2x + 8 = 3x + 2$, ya’ni $17 - 2x = 3x + 2$.\\n\\nIkkala tomonga $2x$ ni qo‘shamiz va $2$ ni ayiramiz: $15 = 5x$, demak $x = 3$. Tekshiruv: $9 - 2(3 - 4) = 9 + 2 = 11$ va $3(3) + 2 = 11$.\\n\\nA variant $-2(x - 4)$ ni $-2x - 8$ deb yozishdan chiqadi ($8$ ning ishorasi xato). B variant faqat $x$ ni $-2$ ga ko‘paytirib, $-2x - 4$ deb yozishdan kelib chiqadi. D variant — bu $5x$ ning qiymati: oxirida $5$ ga bo‘lish unutilgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_one_variable\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"The Afrosiyob high-speed train runs on the $344$-kilometer rail line from Tashkent to Samarkand. On one trip, after traveling at an average speed of $160$ kilometers per hour for $t$ hours, the train still had $104$ kilometers left to travel. The equation $344 - 160t = 104$ represents this situation. What is the value of $t$?\",\"option_a\":\"$0.65$\",\"option_b\":\"$1.5$\",\"option_c\":\"$2.15$\",\"option_d\":\"$2.8$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Subtract $344$ from both sides: $-160t = 104 - 344 = -240$. Divide by $-160$: $t = 1.5$.\\n\\nSo the train had been traveling for $1.5$ hours. Check: $160(1.5) = 240$ km traveled, and $344 - 240 = 104$ km remain.\\n\\nChoice A is $104 \\\\div 160$, the time needed for the remaining distance, not the time already spent. Choice C is $344 \\\\div 160$, the time for the whole trip. Choice D comes from adding instead of subtracting: $(344 + 104) \\\\div 160 = 2.8$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkala tomondan $344$ ni ayiramiz: $-160t = 104 - 344 = -240$. $-160$ ga bo‘lamiz: $t = 1.5$.\\n\\nDemak, poyezd $1.5$ soat yo‘l yurgan. Tekshiruv: $160(1.5) = 240$ km bosib o‘tilgan va $344 - 240 = 104$ km qolgan.\\n\\nA variant — $104 \\\\div 160$, ya’ni qolgan masofa uchun kerak bo‘ladigan vaqt, o‘tgan vaqt emas. C variant — $344 \\\\div 160$, butun yo‘l uchun vaqt. D variant ayirish o‘rniga qo‘shishdan chiqadi: $(344 + 104) \\\\div 160 = 2.8$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_one_variable\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"The temperature $F$, in degrees Fahrenheit, and the temperature $C$, in degrees Celsius, are related by the equation $F = 1.8C + 32$. On a January morning in Nukus, a thermometer showed a temperature of $14$ degrees Fahrenheit. What was this temperature, in degrees Celsius?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"-10\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Substitute $F = 14$: $14 = 1.8C + 32$.\\n\\nSubtract $32$ from both sides: $1.8C = -18$. Divide by $1.8$: $C = -10$.\\n\\nCheck: $1.8(-10) + 32 = -18 + 32 = 14$. A common error is to divide first and then subtract, $\\\\frac{14}{1.8} - 32 \\\\approx -24.2$; the $32$ must be removed before dividing by $1.8$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$F = 14$ ni qo‘yamiz: $14 = 1.8C + 32$.\\n\\nIkkala tomondan $32$ ni ayiramiz: $1.8C = -18$. $1.8$ ga bo‘lamiz: $C = -10$.\\n\\nTekshiruv: $1.8(-10) + 32 = -18 + 32 = 14$. Ko‘p uchraydigan xato — avval bo‘lib, keyin ayirish: $\\\\frac{14}{1.8} - 32 \\\\approx -24.2$. $1.8$ ga bo‘lishdan oldin $32$ ni ayirish kerak.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_one_variable\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"What is the solution to the equation $\\\\dfrac{3x + 2}{4} - \\\\dfrac{x - 1}{6} = 2$?\",\"option_a\":\"$-1$\",\"option_b\":\"$-\\\\frac{6}{7}$\",\"option_c\":\"$\\\\frac{20}{7}$\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\frac{16}{7}$\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Multiply every term by the least common denominator, $12$: $3(3x + 2) - 2(x - 1) = 24$.\\n\\nDistribute: $9x + 6 - 2x + 2 = 24$, so $7x + 8 = 24$. Then $7x = 16$ and $x = \\\\frac{16}{7}$.\\n\\nChoice C comes from writing $-2(x - 1)$ as $-2x - 2$, which gives $7x + 4 = 24$. Choice B comes from multiplying the left side by $12$ but leaving the right side as $2$. Choice A makes the same mistake with the denominator $24$: $6(3x + 2) - 4(x - 1) = 2$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Har bir hadni eng kichik umumiy maxraj $12$ ga ko‘paytiramiz: $3(3x + 2) - 2(x - 1) = 24$.\\n\\nQavslarni ochamiz: $9x + 6 - 2x + 2 = 24$, ya’ni $7x + 8 = 24$. U holda $7x = 16$ va $x = \\\\frac{16}{7}$.\\n\\nC variant $-2(x - 1)$ ni $-2x - 2$ deb yozishdan chiqadi, bunda $7x + 4 = 24$ hosil bo‘ladi. B variant chap tomonni $12$ ga ko‘paytirib, o‘ng tomondagi $2$ ni ko‘paytirmay qoldirishdan chiqadi. A variant ham xuddi shu xato, faqat maxraj $24$ olingan: $6(3x + 2) - 4(x - 1) = 2$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_one_variable\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"On January 1, 2026, the population of Uzbekistan was about $38.2$ million. A planner uses the equation $38.2 + 0.68t = 45$ to estimate the number of years $t$ after January 1, 2026, at which the population will reach $45$ million. According to this model, in which year will the population reach $45$ million?\",\"option_a\":\"$2036$\",\"option_b\":\"$2033$\",\"option_c\":\"$2016$\",\"option_d\":\"$2092$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Subtract $38.2$ from both sides: $0.68t = 6.8$. Divide by $0.68$: $t = 10$.\\n\\nThe population reaches $45$ million $10$ years after 2026, that is, in $2026 + 10 = 2036$.\\n\\nChoice B treats the increase $6.8$ million as a number of years ($2026 + 6.8 \\\\approx 2033$). Choice C subtracts $10$ years instead of adding them. Choice D divides $45$ by $0.68$ ($\\\\approx 66$ years) without first subtracting the starting population.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkala tomondan $38.2$ ni ayiramiz: $0.68t = 6.8$. $0.68$ ga bo‘lamiz: $t = 10$.\\n\\nAholi soni 2026-yildan $10$ yil o‘tib $45$ millionga yetadi, ya’ni $2026 + 10 = 2036$-yilda.\\n\\nB variant $6.8$ million o‘sishni yillar soni deb oladi ($2026 + 6.8 \\\\approx 2033$). C variant $10$ yilni qo‘shish o‘rniga ayiradi. D variant boshlang‘ich aholi sonini ayirmasdan $45$ ni $0.68$ ga bo‘ladi ($\\\\approx 66$ yil).\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_one_variable\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"$$\\\\frac{5}{6}x - \\\\frac{1}{3} = \\\\frac{1}{2}(x + 3)$$\\n\\nWhat is the solution to the given equation?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"11/2\",\"5.5\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Multiply both sides by $6$: $5x - 2 = 3(x + 3) = 3x + 9$.\\n\\nSubtract $3x$ and add $2$: $2x = 11$, so $x = \\\\frac{11}{2}$. Enter 11/2 or 5.5.\\n\\nCheck: $\\\\frac{5}{6}(5.5) - \\\\frac{1}{3} = \\\\frac{55}{12} - \\\\frac{4}{12} = \\\\frac{51}{12} = 4.25$ and $\\\\frac{1}{2}(8.5) = 4.25$. A common error is to multiply only the $x + 3$ by $6$ on the right and forget the factor $\\\\frac{1}{2}$, which gives $5x - 2 = 6x + 18$ and $x = -20$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkala tomonni $6$ ga ko‘paytiramiz: $5x - 2 = 3(x + 3) = 3x + 9$.\\n\\n$3x$ ni ayiramiz va $2$ ni qo‘shamiz: $2x = 11$, demak $x = \\\\frac{11}{2}$. Javobni 11/2 yoki 5.5 shaklida kiriting.\\n\\nTekshiruv: $\\\\frac{5}{6}(5.5) - \\\\frac{1}{3} = \\\\frac{55}{12} - \\\\frac{4}{12} = \\\\frac{51}{12} = 4.25$ va $\\\\frac{1}{2}(8.5) = 4.25$. Ko‘p uchraydigan xato — o‘ng tomonda $\\\\frac{1}{2}$ ko‘paytuvchini unutib, $x + 3$ ni to‘g‘ridan-to‘g‘ri $6$ ga ko‘paytirish: $5x - 2 = 6x + 18$ va $x = -20$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_one_variable\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"$$k(2x + 3) - 4 = 6x + 2k$$\\n\\nIn the given equation, $k$ is a constant. For what value of $k$ does the equation have no solution?\",\"option_a\":\"$-3$\",\"option_b\":\"$3$\",\"option_c\":\"$4$\",\"option_d\":\"$6$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Expand the left side: $2kx + 3k - 4 = 6x + 2k$. A linear equation has no solution when the $x$-coefficients on the two sides are equal but the constant terms are different.\\n\\nEqual coefficients: $2k = 6$, so $k = 3$. Then the constants are $3(3) - 4 = 5$ on the left and $2(3) = 6$ on the right. The equation becomes $6x + 5 = 6x + 6$, which is never true, so there is no solution.\\n\\nChoice C makes the constants equal ($3k - 4 = 2k$ gives $k = 4$), but then $8x + 8 = 6x + 8$ has exactly one solution, $x = 0$. Choice D sets $k$ equal to the coefficient $6$ instead of $2k = 6$. Choice A has the wrong sign; with $k = -3$ the equation $-6x - 13 = 6x - 6$ has one solution.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Chap tomonni ochamiz: $2kx + 3k - 4 = 6x + 2k$. Chiziqli tenglama ikkala tomondagi $x$ koeffitsiyentlari teng, ozod hadlari esa har xil bo‘lganda yechimga ega bo‘lmaydi.\\n\\nKoeffitsiyentlar teng: $2k = 6$, demak $k = 3$. Bunda chapdagi ozod had $3(3) - 4 = 5$, o‘ngdagisi $2(3) = 6$. Tenglama $6x + 5 = 6x + 6$ ko‘rinishiga keladi — bu hech qachon to‘g‘ri emas, demak yechim yo‘q.\\n\\nC variant ozod hadlarni tenglashtiradi ($3k - 4 = 2k$ dan $k = 4$), lekin unda $8x + 8 = 6x + 8$ tenglama aynan bitta yechimga ($x = 0$) ega. D variant $2k = 6$ o‘rniga $k$ ni $6$ ga tenglaydi. A variantda ishora xato: $k = -3$ bo‘lsa, $-6x - 13 = 6x - 6$ bitta yechimga ega.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_one_variable\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"$$\\\\frac{a}{3}(6x - 9) + 4 = 10x + b$$\\n\\nIn the given equation, $a$ and $b$ are constants. If the equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $a - b$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"16\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Expand the left side: $\\\\frac{a}{3} \\\\cdot 6x - \\\\frac{a}{3} \\\\cdot 9 + 4 = 2ax - 3a + 4$.\\n\\nFor infinitely many solutions, both sides must be the same expression: $2a = 10$ and $-3a + 4 = b$. So $a = 5$ and $b = -15 + 4 = -11$.\\n\\nTherefore $a - b = 5 - (-11) = 16$. A common error is to compute $5 - 11 = -6$, ignoring that $b$ is negative; another is to use $a = 10$ by forgetting the factor $\\\\frac{6}{3} = 2$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Chap tomonni ochamiz: $\\\\frac{a}{3} \\\\cdot 6x - \\\\frac{a}{3} \\\\cdot 9 + 4 = 2ax - 3a + 4$.\\n\\nCheksiz ko‘p yechim bo‘lishi uchun ikkala tomon bir xil ifoda bo‘lishi kerak: $2a = 10$ va $-3a + 4 = b$. Demak $a = 5$ va $b = -15 + 4 = -11$.\\n\\nShunday qilib, $a - b = 5 - (-11) = 16$. Ko‘p uchraydigan xato — $b$ manfiy ekanini e’tiborsiz qoldirib, $5 - 11 = -6$ deb hisoblash; yana bir xato — $\\\\frac{6}{3} = 2$ ko‘paytuvchini unutib, $a = 10$ deb olish.\",\"estimated_seconds\":130,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_two_variables\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Which point $(x, y)$ lies on the graph of $5x + 4y = 20$ in the $xy$-plane?\",\"option_a\":\"$(2, 3)$\",\"option_b\":\"$(10, -4)$\",\"option_c\":\"$(4, 5)$\",\"option_d\":\"$(-4, 10)$\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Substitute each point into $5x + 4y$ and look for a value of $20$.\\n\\nFor $(-4, 10)$: $5(-4) + 4(10) = -20 + 40 = 20$. This point lies on the graph.\\n\\nChoice A gives $10 + 12 = 22$. Choice B swaps the coordinates of the correct point and gives $50 - 16 = 34$. Choice C combines the $x$-intercept $4$ and the $y$-intercept $5$ into one point, but $(4, 5)$ gives $20 + 20 = 40$; the intercepts are $(4, 0)$ and $(0, 5)$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Har bir nuqtani $5x + 4y$ ga qo‘yib, natija $20$ bo‘ladiganini qidiramiz.\\n\\n$(-4, 10)$ uchun: $5(-4) + 4(10) = -20 + 40 = 20$. Bu nuqta grafikda yotadi.\\n\\nA variant $10 + 12 = 22$ beradi. B variant to‘g‘ri nuqtaning koordinatalarini almashtirib qo‘yadi: $50 - 16 = 34$. C variant $x$ o‘qi bilan kesishish ($4$) va $y$ o‘qi bilan kesishish ($5$) qiymatlarini bitta nuqtaga birlashtiradi, lekin $(4, 5)$ uchun $20 + 20 = 40$; aslida kesishish nuqtalari $(4, 0)$ va $(0, 5)$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_two_variables\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"At Chorsu Bazaar in Tashkent, dried apricots cost $60{,}000$ soums per kilogram and raisins cost $45{,}000$ soums per kilogram. A customer spends exactly $300{,}000$ soums on $a$ kilograms of dried apricots and $r$ kilograms of raisins, so $60{,}000a + 45{,}000r = 300{,}000$. If the customer buys $2$ kilograms of dried apricots, how many kilograms of raisins does the customer buy?\",\"option_a\":\"$3$\",\"option_b\":\"$4$\",\"option_c\":\"$5$\",\"option_d\":\"$6\\\\frac{2}{3}$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Substitute $a = 2$: $120{,}000 + 45{,}000r = 300{,}000$.\\n\\nSubtract $120{,}000$: $45{,}000r = 180{,}000$. Divide by $45{,}000$: $r = 4$.\\n\\nChoice A divides the remaining $180{,}000$ soums by the apricot price $60{,}000$ instead of the raisin price. Choice D divides the whole $300{,}000$ by $45{,}000$, ignoring the money spent on apricots. Choice C is $300{,}000 \\\\div 60{,}000$, the amount of apricots alone that the money would buy.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$a = 2$ ni qo‘yamiz: $120{,}000 + 45{,}000r = 300{,}000$.\\n\\n$120{,}000$ ni ayiramiz: $45{,}000r = 180{,}000$. $45{,}000$ ga bo‘lamiz: $r = 4$.\\n\\nA variant qolgan $180{,}000$ so‘mni mayiz narxiga emas, o‘rik narxi $60{,}000$ ga bo‘ladi. D variant o‘rikka sarflangan pulni hisobga olmay, butun $300{,}000$ ni $45{,}000$ ga bo‘ladi. C variant — $300{,}000 \\\\div 60{,}000$, ya’ni butun pulga faqat o‘rik olinsa qancha bo‘lishi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_two_variables\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"In the $xy$-plane, the graph of $2x + 5y = k$, where $k$ is a constant, passes through the point $(3, -4)$. What is the value of $k$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"-14\"],\"explanation_en\":\"A point lies on a graph when its coordinates satisfy the equation. Substitute $x = 3$ and $y = -4$: $k = 2(3) + 5(-4) = 6 - 20 = -14$.\\n\\nA common error is to swap the coordinates and compute $2(-4) + 5(3) = 7$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Nuqta grafikda yotishi uchun uning koordinatalari tenglamani qanoatlantirishi kerak. $x = 3$ va $y = -4$ ni qo‘yamiz: $k = 2(3) + 5(-4) = 6 - 20 = -14$.\\n\\nKo‘p uchraydigan xato — koordinatalarni almashtirib, $2(-4) + 5(3) = 7$ deb hisoblash.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_two_variables\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Javlon has $90{,}000$ soums to spend on his mobile plan. Calls cost $150$ soums per minute and mobile internet costs $3{,}000$ soums per gigabyte. The equation $150m + 3{,}000g = 90{,}000$ represents the situation in which Javlon spends all of the money on $m$ minutes of calls and $g$ gigabytes of internet. What is the best interpretation of the fact that $m = 0$, $g = 30$ is a solution to the equation?\",\"option_a\":\"If Javlon makes no calls, he can buy $30$ gigabytes of internet.\",\"option_b\":\"If Javlon buys no internet, he can make $30$ minutes of calls.\",\"option_c\":\"One gigabyte of internet costs $30$ soums.\",\"option_d\":\"Javlon uses $30$ gigabytes of internet each day.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The solution $m = 0$, $g = 30$ says: $0$ minutes of calls and $30$ gigabytes of internet together cost exactly $90{,}000$ soums. Check: $150(0) + 3{,}000(30) = 90{,}000$.\\n\\nSo if Javlon makes no calls, all of his money buys $30$ gigabytes.\\n\\nChoice B mixes up the variables: with no internet ($g = 0$), he could make $90{,}000 \\\\div 150 = 600$ minutes of calls. Choice C confuses a quantity with a price (one gigabyte costs $3{,}000$ soums). Choice D adds a time period that the equation does not contain.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$m = 0$, $g = 30$ yechimi shuni bildiradi: $0$ daqiqa qo‘ng‘iroq va $30$ gigabayt internet birgalikda aynan $90{,}000$ so‘m turadi. Tekshiruv: $150(0) + 3{,}000(30) = 90{,}000$.\\n\\nDemak, Javlon umuman qo‘ng‘iroq qilmasa, butun puliga $30$ gigabayt internet sotib oladi.\\n\\nB variant o‘zgaruvchilarni chalkashtiradi: internet olinmasa ($g = 0$), u $90{,}000 \\\\div 150 = 600$ daqiqa gaplasha oladi. C variant miqdorni narx bilan adashtiradi (bir gigabayt $3{,}000$ so‘m turadi). D variant tenglamada yo‘q bo‘lgan vaqt davrini qo‘shadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_two_variables\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A line in the $xy$-plane passes through the points $(-2, 7)$ and $(4, -2)$. What is the $x$-coordinate of the $x$-intercept of the line?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"8/3\",\"2.666\",\"2.667\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Slope: $\\\\frac{-2 - 7}{4 - (-2)} = \\\\frac{-9}{6} = -\\\\frac{3}{2}$.\\n\\nAt the $x$-intercept, $y = 0$. Starting from $(4, -2)$, $y$ must increase by $2$. Since $y$ changes by $-\\\\frac{3}{2}$ for each $1$ unit increase in $x$, $x$ must change by $\\\\frac{2}{-3/2} = -\\\\frac{4}{3}$. So $x = 4 - \\\\frac{4}{3} = \\\\frac{8}{3}$.\\n\\nEquation check: $y - 7 = -\\\\frac{3}{2}(x + 2)$; with $y = 0$: $x + 2 = \\\\frac{14}{3}$, so $x = \\\\frac{8}{3}$. Enter 8/3, 2.666, or 2.667. A common error is to give the $y$-intercept, $4$, instead.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qiyalik: $\\\\frac{-2 - 7}{4 - (-2)} = \\\\frac{-9}{6} = -\\\\frac{3}{2}$.\\n\\n$x$ o‘qi bilan kesishish nuqtasida $y = 0$. $(4, -2)$ nuqtadan boshlasak, $y$ $2$ ga oshishi kerak. $x$ har $1$ ga oshganda $y$ $-\\\\frac{3}{2}$ ga o‘zgaradi, demak $x$ $\\\\frac{2}{-3/2} = -\\\\frac{4}{3}$ ga o‘zgarishi kerak. Shunday qilib, $x = 4 - \\\\frac{4}{3} = \\\\frac{8}{3}$.\\n\\nTenglama bilan tekshiruv: $y - 7 = -\\\\frac{3}{2}(x + 2)$; $y = 0$ bo‘lsa, $x + 2 = \\\\frac{14}{3}$, ya’ni $x = \\\\frac{8}{3}$. Javobni 8/3, 2.666 yoki 2.667 shaklida kiriting. Ko‘p uchraydigan xato — o‘rniga $y$ o‘qi bilan kesishish qiymati $4$ ni yozish.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_two_variables\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"In the $xy$-plane, line $\\\\ell$ passes through the point $(0, -3)$ and has the same $x$-intercept as the graph of $3x - 4y = 18$. What is the slope of line $\\\\ell$?\",\"option_a\":\"$-\\\\frac{1}{2}$\",\"option_b\":\"$\\\\frac{3}{4}$\",\"option_c\":\"$\\\\frac{1}{2}$\",\"option_d\":\"$2$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The $x$-intercept of $3x - 4y = 18$: set $y = 0$, so $3x = 18$ and $x = 6$. Line $\\\\ell$ passes through $(6, 0)$ and $(0, -3)$.\\n\\nSlope of $\\\\ell$: $\\\\frac{0 - (-3)}{6 - 0} = \\\\frac{3}{6} = \\\\frac{1}{2}$.\\n\\nChoice B is the slope of the given line $3x - 4y = 18$, not of $\\\\ell$. Choice D inverts the slope ($\\\\frac{6}{3}$). Choice A comes from a sign error in $0 - (-3)$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$3x - 4y = 18$ ning $x$ o‘qi bilan kesishish nuqtasi: $y = 0$ desak, $3x = 18$ va $x = 6$. Demak $\\\\ell$ to‘g‘ri chiziq $(6, 0)$ va $(0, -3)$ nuqtalardan o‘tadi.\\n\\n$\\\\ell$ ning qiyaligi: $\\\\frac{0 - (-3)}{6 - 0} = \\\\frac{3}{6} = \\\\frac{1}{2}$.\\n\\nB variant — berilgan $3x - 4y = 18$ chiziqning qiyaligi, $\\\\ell$ niki emas. D variant qiyalikni teskari oladi ($\\\\frac{6}{3}$). A variant $0 - (-3)$ dagi ishora xatosidan chiqadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_two_variables\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A temperature of $C$ degrees Celsius is equal to $F$ degrees Fahrenheit, where $9C - 5F = -160$. At what temperature, in degrees Celsius, is the Fahrenheit reading exactly twice the Celsius reading?\",\"option_a\":\"$-40$\",\"option_b\":\"$80$\",\"option_c\":\"$320$\",\"option_d\":\"$160$\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The Fahrenheit reading is twice the Celsius reading, so $F = 2C$. Substitute: $9C - 5(2C) = -160$, so $-C = -160$ and $C = 160$.\\n\\nCheck: at $160$°C, $F = \\\\frac{9(160) + 160}{5} = \\\\frac{1600}{5} = 320$, and $320 = 2(160)$.\\n\\nChoice A is the temperature at which the two readings are equal ($F = C$), not where one is twice the other. Choice C is the Fahrenheit reading, $320$, but the question asks for the Celsius temperature. Choice B is half of the correct temperature: at $80$°C, $F = \\\\frac{9(80) + 160}{5} = 176$, which is not $2(80) = 160$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Farengeyt ko‘rsatkichi Selsiy ko‘rsatkichidan ikki marta katta, ya’ni $F = 2C$. Qo‘yamiz: $9C - 5(2C) = -160$, demak $-C = -160$ va $C = 160$.\\n\\nTekshiruv: $160$°C da $F = \\\\frac{9(160) + 160}{5} = \\\\frac{1600}{5} = 320$ va $320 = 2(160)$.\\n\\nA variant — ikki ko‘rsatkich teng bo‘ladigan harorat ($F = C$), biri ikkinchisidan ikki marta katta bo‘ladigani emas. C variant — Farengeyt ko‘rsatkichi $320$, lekin savol Selsiy bo‘yicha haroratni so‘rayapti. B variant — to‘g‘ri javobning yarmi: $80$°C da $F = \\\\frac{9(80) + 160}{5} = 176$, bu $2(80) = 160$ emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_two_variables\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"In the $xy$-plane, the graph of $(k - 1)x + 3y = 6$, where $k$ is a constant and $k \\\\ne 1$, has an $x$-intercept that is twice its $y$-intercept. What is the value of $k$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"5/2\",\"2.5\"],\"explanation_en\":\"$y$-intercept: set $x = 0$, so $3y = 6$ and $y = 2$. The $x$-intercept must therefore be $2(2) = 4$.\\n\\nSet $y = 0$ and $x = 4$: $(k - 1)(4) = 6$, so $k - 1 = \\\\frac{3}{2}$ and $k = \\\\frac{5}{2}$. Enter 5/2 or 2.5.\\n\\nCheck: the line $1.5x + 3y = 6$ crosses the axes at $(4, 0)$ and $(0, 2)$. A common error is to stop at $k - 1 = \\\\frac{3}{2}$ and enter $1.5$, or to make the $y$-intercept twice the $x$-intercept, which gives $k - 1 = 6$ and $k = 7$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$y$ o‘qi bilan kesishish: $x = 0$ desak, $3y = 6$ va $y = 2$. Demak $x$ o‘qi bilan kesishish $2(2) = 4$ bo‘lishi kerak.\\n\\n$y = 0$ va $x = 4$ ni qo‘yamiz: $(k - 1)(4) = 6$, demak $k - 1 = \\\\frac{3}{2}$ va $k = \\\\frac{5}{2}$. Javobni 5/2 yoki 2.5 shaklida kiriting.\\n\\nTekshiruv: $1.5x + 3y = 6$ chiziq o‘qlarni $(4, 0)$ va $(0, 2)$ nuqtalarda kesadi. Ko‘p uchraydigan xato — $k - 1 = \\\\frac{3}{2}$ da to‘xtab, $1.5$ ni kiritish yoki aksincha $y$ o‘qidagi kesishishni ikki marta katta deb olish: unda $k - 1 = 6$ va $k = 7$ chiqadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":130,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"The function $f$ is defined by $f(x) = -2x + 11$. What is the value of $f(-4)$?\",\"option_a\":\"$-19$\",\"option_b\":\"$3$\",\"option_c\":\"$19$\",\"option_d\":\"$7.5$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Substitute $x = -4$: $f(-4) = -2(-4) + 11 = 8 + 11 = 19$.\\n\\nChoice B comes from writing $-2(-4)$ as $-8$, which gives $-8 + 11 = 3$. Choice A has the sign of the whole answer flipped. Choice D solves $-2x + 11 = -4$, the $x$-value where the output is $-4$, instead of finding the output when the input is $-4$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$x = -4$ ni qo‘yamiz: $f(-4) = -2(-4) + 11 = 8 + 11 = 19$.\\n\\nB variant $-2(-4)$ ni $-8$ deb yozishdan chiqadi: $-8 + 11 = 3$. A variantda butun javobning ishorasi teskari. D variant $-2x + 11 = -4$ tenglamani yechadi, ya’ni qiymati $-4$ bo‘ladigan $x$ ni topadi, kirish qiymati $-4$ bo‘lgandagi natijani emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"In the lower atmosphere, air temperature drops by about $6.5$°C for each kilometer of altitude. On a summer afternoon, the temperature $T$, in degrees Celsius, at an altitude of $h$ kilometers above the Tashkent region is modeled by $T(h) = 34 - 6.5h$. What is the best interpretation of $6.5$ in this context?\",\"option_a\":\"The temperature decreases by $6.5$°C for each $1$-kilometer increase in altitude.\",\"option_b\":\"The temperature at ground level is $6.5$°C.\",\"option_c\":\"The altitude increases by $6.5$ kilometers for each $1$°C drop in temperature.\",\"option_d\":\"The temperature is $6.5$°C at an altitude of $1$ kilometer.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"In $T(h) = 34 - 6.5h$, the number $-6.5$ is the slope: each time $h$ increases by $1$, $T$ changes by $-6.5$. So the temperature falls by $6.5$°C for every additional kilometer of altitude.\\n\\nChoice B describes $T(0) = 34$, the ground-level temperature, which is $34$°C, not $6.5$°C. Choice C reverses the roles of the variables. Choice D is wrong because $T(1) = 34 - 6.5 = 27.5$°C.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$T(h) = 34 - 6.5h$ da $-6.5$ — qiyalik: $h$ har safar $1$ ga oshganda $T$ $-6.5$ ga o‘zgaradi. Demak, balandlik har $1$ kilometrga oshganda harorat $6.5$°C ga pasayadi.\\n\\nB variant $T(0) = 34$, ya’ni yer sathidagi haroratni tasvirlaydi — u $34$°C, $6.5$°C emas. C variant o‘zgaruvchilarning rolini almashtiradi. D variant noto‘g‘ri, chunki $T(1) = 34 - 6.5 = 27.5$°C.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"The speed of sound in dry air depends on the air temperature. The function $v(T) = 331 + 0.6T$ gives the approximate speed of sound $v$, in meters per second, when the air temperature is $T$ degrees Celsius. What is the approximate speed of sound, in meters per second, when the air temperature is $25$°C?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"346\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Substitute $T = 25$: $v(25) = 331 + 0.6(25) = 331 + 15 = 346$ meters per second.\\n\\nA common error is to add $0.6$ and $25$ instead of multiplying, which gives $356.6$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$T = 25$ ni qo‘yamiz: $v(25) = 331 + 0.6(25) = 331 + 15 = 346$ metr/sekund.\\n\\nKo‘p uchraydigan xato — $0.6$ va $25$ ni ko‘paytirish o‘rniga qo‘shish: unda $356.6$ chiqadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"For the linear function $g$, $g(-2) = 13$, $g(1) = 4$, and $g(5) = -8$. Which equation defines $g$?\",\"option_a\":\"$g(x) = -x + 11$\",\"option_b\":\"$g(x) = 3x + 1$\",\"option_c\":\"$g(x) = -3x + 13$\",\"option_d\":\"$g(x) = -3x + 7$\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Slope: $\\\\frac{4 - 13}{1 - (-2)} = \\\\frac{-9}{3} = -3$. Using $(1, 4)$: $4 = -3(1) + b$, so $b = 7$ and $g(x) = -3x + 7$.\\n\\nCheck all three points: $-3(-2) + 7 = 13$, $-3(1) + 7 = 4$, $-3(5) + 7 = -8$.\\n\\nChoice A fits only $(-2, 13)$; at $x = 1$ it gives $10$. Choice B fits only $(1, 4)$ and has the wrong sign of slope. Choice C has the correct slope but uses $13$ as the $y$-intercept, although $13$ is the value at $x = -2$, not at $x = 0$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qiyalik: $\\\\frac{4 - 13}{1 - (-2)} = \\\\frac{-9}{3} = -3$. $(1, 4)$ nuqtadan: $4 = -3(1) + b$, demak $b = 7$ va $g(x) = -3x + 7$.\\n\\nUchala nuqtani tekshiramiz: $-3(-2) + 7 = 13$, $-3(1) + 7 = 4$, $-3(5) + 7 = -8$.\\n\\nA variant faqat $(-2, 13)$ ga mos keladi; $x = 1$ da $10$ beradi. B variant faqat $(1, 4)$ ga mos va qiyalik ishorasi xato. C variantda qiyalik to‘g‘ri, lekin $13$ ozod had deb olingan, holbuki $13$ — $x = 0$ dagi emas, $x = -2$ dagi qiymat.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"The population of Uzbekistan was about $37.5$ million on January 1, 2025, and about $38.2$ million on January 1, 2026. Suppose the population $P$, in millions, $t$ years after January 1, 2025, is modeled by a linear function that fits these two values. According to the model, how many years after January 1, 2025, will the population be $42.4$ million?\",\"option_a\":\"$4.9$\",\"option_b\":\"$6$\",\"option_c\":\"$7$\",\"option_d\":\"$61$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The population grew by $38.2 - 37.5 = 0.7$ million in one year, so $P(t) = 37.5 + 0.7t$.\\n\\nSolve $37.5 + 0.7t = 42.4$: $0.7t = 4.9$, so $t = 7$ years after January 1, 2025 (that is, in 2032).\\n\\nChoice A is the total growth $4.9$ million, not a number of years. Choice B counts years after 2026 ($\\\\frac{42.4 - 38.2}{0.7} = 6$) but the question measures from 2025. Choice D divides $42.4$ by $0.7$ without subtracting the starting population.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Aholi bir yilda $38.2 - 37.5 = 0.7$ millionga o‘sgan, demak $P(t) = 37.5 + 0.7t$.\\n\\n$37.5 + 0.7t = 42.4$ ni yechamiz: $0.7t = 4.9$, ya’ni 2025-yil 1-yanvardan $t = 7$ yil o‘tib (2032-yilda).\\n\\nA variant — umumiy o‘sish $4.9$ million, yillar soni emas. B variant yillarni 2026-yildan sanaydi ($\\\\frac{42.4 - 38.2}{0.7} = 6$), savol esa 2025-yildan boshlab so‘raydi. D variant boshlang‘ich aholini ayirmasdan $42.4$ ni $0.7$ ga bo‘ladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"For the linear function $f$, $f(-1) = 8$ and $f(3) = -4$. For what value of $x$ is $f(x) = -16$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"7\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Slope: $\\\\frac{-4 - 8}{3 - (-1)} = \\\\frac{-12}{4} = -3$. Using $(-1, 8)$: $8 = -3(-1) + b$, so $b = 5$ and $f(x) = -3x + 5$.\\n\\nSolve $-3x + 5 = -16$: $-3x = -21$, so $x = 7$. Check: $f(7) = -21 + 5 = -16$.\\n\\nA common error is to answer $f(-16) = 53$, which reverses input and output.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qiyalik: $\\\\frac{-4 - 8}{3 - (-1)} = \\\\frac{-12}{4} = -3$. $(-1, 8)$ nuqtadan: $8 = -3(-1) + b$, demak $b = 5$ va $f(x) = -3x + 5$.\\n\\n$-3x + 5 = -16$ ni yechamiz: $-3x = -21$, demak $x = 7$. Tekshiruv: $f(7) = -21 + 5 = -16$.\\n\\nKo‘p uchraydigan xato — kirish va chiqishni almashtirib, $f(-16) = 53$ ni javob deb berish.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"The linear function $f$ is defined by $f(x) = ax + b$, where $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $f(2x + 1) = 6x + 7$ for all values of $x$, what is the value of $f(4)$?\",\"option_a\":\"$13$\",\"option_b\":\"$16$\",\"option_c\":\"$19$\",\"option_d\":\"$31$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"$f(2x + 1) = a(2x + 1) + b = 2ax + a + b$. Matching with $6x + 7$: $2a = 6$, so $a = 3$, and $a + b = 7$, so $b = 4$. Then $f(x) = 3x + 4$ and $f(4) = 16$.\\n\\nFaster: $2x + 1 = 4$ when $x = 1.5$, so $f(4) = 6(1.5) + 7 = 16$.\\n\\nChoice D substitutes $4$ directly into $6x + 7$, treating $6x + 7$ as $f(x)$. Choice C uses $a = 3$ but takes $b = 7$. Choice A uses $x = 1$, which gives $f(3) = 13$, not $f(4)$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$f(2x + 1) = a(2x + 1) + b = 2ax + a + b$. Buni $6x + 7$ bilan solishtiramiz: $2a = 6$, demak $a = 3$, va $a + b = 7$, demak $b = 4$. U holda $f(x) = 3x + 4$ va $f(4) = 16$.\\n\\nQisqa yo‘l: $2x + 1 = 4$ bo‘lishi uchun $x = 1.5$, demak $f(4) = 6(1.5) + 7 = 16$.\\n\\nD variant $4$ ni to‘g‘ridan-to‘g‘ri $6x + 7$ ga qo‘yadi, ya’ni $6x + 7$ ni $f(x)$ deb oladi. C variant $a = 3$ ni to‘g‘ri topadi, lekin $b = 7$ deb oladi. A variant $x = 1$ ni qo‘yadi — bu $f(3) = 13$ beradi, $f(4)$ ni emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"The function $f$ is defined by $f(x) = 3x - 2$. The graph of the linear function $g$ in the $xy$-plane has a $y$-intercept of $(0, 22)$ and intersects the graph of $f$ at the point where $x = 4$. What is the value of $g(10)$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"-8\"],\"explanation_en\":\"At $x = 4$ the graphs meet, so $g(4) = f(4) = 3(4) - 2 = 10$.\\n\\nThe graph of $g$ passes through $(0, 22)$ and $(4, 10)$, so its slope is $\\\\frac{10 - 22}{4 - 0} = -3$ and $g(x) = -3x + 22$.\\n\\nThen $g(10) = -30 + 22 = -8$. A common error is to use the slope of $f$ for $g$, which gives $3(10) + 22 = 52$; another is to compute $f(10) = 28$ instead of $g(10)$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$x = 4$ da grafiklar kesishadi, demak $g(4) = f(4) = 3(4) - 2 = 10$.\\n\\n$g$ ning grafigi $(0, 22)$ va $(4, 10)$ nuqtalardan o‘tadi, uning qiyaligi $\\\\frac{10 - 22}{4 - 0} = -3$ va $g(x) = -3x + 22$.\\n\\nU holda $g(10) = -30 + 22 = -8$. Ko‘p uchraydigan xato — $g$ uchun $f$ ning qiyaligini olish: $3(10) + 22 = 52$; yana biri — $g(10)$ o‘rniga $f(10) = 28$ ni hisoblash.\",\"estimated_seconds\":130,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_inequalities\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Which of the following values of $x$ satisfies the inequality $\\\\dfrac{x}{2} - 3 > 4$?\",\"option_a\":\"$16$\",\"option_b\":\"$14$\",\"option_c\":\"$10$\",\"option_d\":\"$7$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Add $3$ to both sides: $\\\\frac{x}{2} > 7$. Multiply by $2$: $x > 14$. Of the choices, only $16$ is greater than $14$.\\n\\nChoice B is the boundary value: at $x = 14$, $\\\\frac{14}{2} - 3 = 4$, and $4 > 4$ is false. Choice D comes from stopping at $\\\\frac{x}{2} > 7$ and forgetting to multiply by $2$. Choice C gives $5 - 3 = 2$, which is not greater than $4$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkala tomonga $3$ ni qo‘shamiz: $\\\\frac{x}{2} > 7$. $2$ ga ko‘paytiramiz: $x > 14$. Variantlardan faqat $16$ $14$ dan katta.\\n\\nB variant — chegaraviy qiymat: $x = 14$ da $\\\\frac{14}{2} - 3 = 4$, $4 > 4$ esa noto‘g‘ri. D variant $\\\\frac{x}{2} > 7$ da to‘xtab, $2$ ga ko‘paytirishni unutishdan chiqadi. C variant $5 - 3 = 2$ beradi, bu $4$ dan katta emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_inequalities\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A small bridge over an irrigation canal in Khorezm has a posted weight limit of $20$ tonnes. A truck weighs $7.5$ tonnes when empty, and it is loaded with $n$ bales of cotton that weigh $0.25$ tonne each. Which inequality represents the numbers of bales $n$ for which the loaded truck does not exceed the bridge's weight limit?\",\"option_a\":\"$7.5 + 0.25n \\\\ge 20$\",\"option_b\":\"$7.5 + 0.25n \\\\le 20$\",\"option_c\":\"$0.25 + 7.5n \\\\le 20$\",\"option_d\":\"$7.5n + 0.25n \\\\le 20$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The loaded truck weighs $7.5$ tonnes plus $0.25$ tonne for each of the $n$ bales: $7.5 + 0.25n$. “Does not exceed” means less than or equal to, so $7.5 + 0.25n \\\\le 20$.\\n\\nChoice A uses the wrong direction (it describes trucks that are at least $20$ tonnes). Choice C swaps the fixed weight and the weight per bale. Choice D multiplies the truck's empty weight by $n$, as if the truck were counted once per bale.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Yuklangan yuk mashinasi $7.5$ tonna va $n$ ta toyning har biri uchun $0.25$ tonna og‘irlikka ega: $7.5 + 0.25n$. “Oshmaydi” — kichik yoki teng degani, demak $7.5 + 0.25n \\\\le 20$.\\n\\nA variantda tengsizlik yo‘nalishi xato (u kamida $20$ tonna bo‘lgan holatni tasvirlaydi). C variant mashinaning o‘z og‘irligi bilan bitta toy og‘irligini almashtirib qo‘yadi. D variant bo‘sh mashina og‘irligini $n$ ga ko‘paytiradi, go‘yo mashina har bir toy uchun qayta sanalgandek.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_inequalities\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"What is the greatest integer $x$ that satisfies the inequality $2(x + 3) < 15$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"4\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Divide both sides by $2$: $x + 3 < 7.5$. Subtract $3$: $x < 4.5$.\\n\\nThe greatest integer less than $4.5$ is $4$. Check: $2(4 + 3) = 14 < 15$, but $2(5 + 3) = 16$, which is not less than $15$.\\n\\nA common error is to distribute as $2x + 3 < 15$, which gives $x < 6$ and the wrong answer $5$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkala tomonni $2$ ga bo‘lamiz: $x + 3 < 7.5$. $3$ ni ayiramiz: $x < 4.5$.\\n\\n$4.5$ dan kichik eng katta butun son — $4$. Tekshiruv: $2(4 + 3) = 14 < 15$, lekin $2(5 + 3) = 16$, bu $15$ dan kichik emas.\\n\\nKo‘p uchraydigan xato — qavsni $2x + 3 < 15$ deb ochish: unda $x < 6$ va noto‘g‘ri javob $5$ chiqadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_inequalities\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"$$\\\\begin{aligned} y &< -2x + 5 \\\\\\\\ y &\\\\ge x - 4 \\\\end{aligned}$$\\n\\nWhich point $(x, y)$ is a solution to the given system of inequalities?\",\"option_a\":\"$(3, 0)$\",\"option_b\":\"$(1, -4)$\",\"option_c\":\"$(2, 0)$\",\"option_d\":\"$(1, 3)$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Test each point in both inequalities.\\n\\n$(2, 0)$: $0 < -2(2) + 5 = 1$ is true, and $0 \\\\ge 2 - 4 = -2$ is true. So $(2, 0)$ is a solution.\\n\\n$(3, 0)$: $0 < -1$ is false. $(1, -4)$: $-4 < 3$ is true, but $-4 \\\\ge -3$ is false. $(1, 3)$: $3 < 3$ is false; this point lies on the boundary line $y = -2x + 5$, which is not included because the inequality is strict.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Har bir nuqtani ikkala tengsizlikka qo‘yib tekshiramiz.\\n\\n$(2, 0)$: $0 < -2(2) + 5 = 1$ to‘g‘ri va $0 \\\\ge 2 - 4 = -2$ to‘g‘ri. Demak $(2, 0)$ — yechim.\\n\\n$(3, 0)$: $0 < -1$ noto‘g‘ri. $(1, -4)$: $-4 < 3$ to‘g‘ri, lekin $-4 \\\\ge -3$ noto‘g‘ri. $(1, 3)$: $3 < 3$ noto‘g‘ri; bu nuqta $y = -2x + 5$ chegara chizig‘ida yotadi, tengsizlik qat’iy bo‘lgani uchun esa chegara kirmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_inequalities\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"At standard atmospheric pressure, pure water is a liquid when its temperature $C$, in degrees Celsius, satisfies $0 < C < 100$. The temperature $F$, in degrees Fahrenheit, is related to $C$ by $F = 1.8C + 32$. Which inequality describes the Fahrenheit temperatures at which pure water is a liquid at standard atmospheric pressure?\",\"option_a\":\"$0 < F < 100$\",\"option_b\":\"$32 < F < 212$\",\"option_c\":\"$32 < F < 180$\",\"option_d\":\"$-17.8 < F < 37.8$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Multiply each part of $0 < C < 100$ by $1.8$: $0 < 1.8C < 180$. Add $32$ to each part: $32 < 1.8C + 32 < 212$, that is, $32 < F < 212$.\\n\\nChoice A keeps the Celsius bounds unchanged. Choice C multiplies by $1.8$ but adds $32$ only to the lower bound. Choice D applies the reverse conversion, $\\\\frac{F - 32}{1.8}$, to the numbers $0$ and $100$, which turns Fahrenheit into Celsius rather than Celsius into Fahrenheit.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$0 < C < 100$ ning har bir qismini $1.8$ ga ko‘paytiramiz: $0 < 1.8C < 180$. Har bir qismga $32$ ni qo‘shamiz: $32 < 1.8C + 32 < 212$, ya’ni $32 < F < 212$.\\n\\nA variant Selsiy chegaralarini o‘zgarishsiz qoldiradi. C variant $1.8$ ga ko‘paytiradi, lekin $32$ ni faqat quyi chegaraga qo‘shadi. D variant $0$ va $100$ sonlariga teskari formulani, $\\\\frac{F - 32}{1.8}$ ni qo‘llaydi — bu Selsiyni Farengeytga emas, Farengeytni Selsiyga o‘tkazadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_inequalities\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"Nilufar's final grade is the average of five test scores, and she needs an average of at least $85$. Her scores on the first four tests were $78$, $92$, $81$, and $88$. Each test is scored out of $100$ points, and scores are whole numbers. What is the least score she can get on the fifth test to reach her goal?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"86\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Let $s$ be the fifth score. The average must satisfy $\\\\frac{78 + 92 + 81 + 88 + s}{5} \\\\ge 85$.\\n\\nThe first four scores add to $339$. Multiply by $5$: $339 + s \\\\ge 425$, so $s \\\\ge 86$. The least score is $86$.\\n\\nCheck: $\\\\frac{339 + 86}{5} = 85$ exactly. A common error is to average the four known scores ($84.75$) and conclude that any score above $85$ works, or to forget to multiply by $5$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$s$ — beshinchi test bali bo‘lsin. O‘rtacha qiymat quyidagini qanoatlantirishi kerak: $\\\\frac{78 + 92 + 81 + 88 + s}{5} \\\\ge 85$.\\n\\nDastlabki to‘rt ball yig‘indisi $339$. $5$ ga ko‘paytiramiz: $339 + s \\\\ge 425$, demak $s \\\\ge 86$. Eng kichik ball — $86$.\\n\\nTekshiruv: $\\\\frac{339 + 86}{5} = 85$ — aynan kerakli qiymat. Ko‘p uchraydigan xato — ma’lum to‘rt ballning o‘rtachasini ($84.75$) olib, $85$ dan yuqori har qanday ball yetadi deb o‘ylash yoki $5$ ga ko‘paytirishni unutish.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_inequalities\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A workshop in Samarkand makes embroidered suzani pillow covers and wall hangings. Each pillow cover takes $3$ hours to make and sells for $150{,}000$ soums. Each wall hanging takes $8$ hours to make and sells for $600{,}000$ soums. This month, the workshop has at most $120$ hours of work time and must earn at least $4{,}000{,}000$ soums. If the workshop makes exactly $5$ wall hangings this month, which of the following describes all possible numbers $p$ of pillow covers it can make?\",\"option_a\":\"$6 \\\\le p \\\\le 26$\",\"option_b\":\"$7 \\\\le p \\\\le 40$\",\"option_c\":\"$0 \\\\le p \\\\le 26$\",\"option_d\":\"$7 \\\\le p \\\\le 26$\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Time: $3p + 8(5) \\\\le 120$, so $3p \\\\le 80$ and $p \\\\le 26.67$. Since $p$ is a whole number, $p \\\\le 26$.\\n\\nMoney: $150{,}000p + 600{,}000(5) \\\\ge 4{,}000{,}000$, so $150{,}000p \\\\ge 1{,}000{,}000$ and $p \\\\ge 6.67$. Since $p$ is a whole number, $p \\\\ge 7$. Together: $7 \\\\le p \\\\le 26$.\\n\\nChoice A rounds $6.67$ down to $6$, but $6$ covers earn only $900{,}000 + 3{,}000{,}000 = 3{,}900{,}000$ soums. Choice B ignores the $40$ hours spent on wall hangings ($120 \\\\div 3 = 40$). Choice C ignores the earnings requirement.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Vaqt: $3p + 8(5) \\\\le 120$, demak $3p \\\\le 80$ va $p \\\\le 26.67$. $p$ butun son bo‘lgani uchun $p \\\\le 26$.\\n\\nDaromad: $150{,}000p + 600{,}000(5) \\\\ge 4{,}000{,}000$, demak $150{,}000p \\\\ge 1{,}000{,}000$ va $p \\\\ge 6.67$. $p$ butun son, shuning uchun $p \\\\ge 7$. Birgalikda: $7 \\\\le p \\\\le 26$.\\n\\nA variant $6.67$ ni pastga, $6$ ga yaxlitlaydi, lekin $6$ ta yostiq jildi bilan daromad atigi $900{,}000 + 3{,}000{,}000 = 3{,}900{,}000$ so‘m bo‘ladi. B variant devoriy so‘zanalarga ketadigan $40$ soatni hisobga olmaydi ($120 \\\\div 3 = 40$). C variant daromad shartini e’tiborsiz qoldiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":150,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_inequalities\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"$$\\\\begin{aligned} y &> 2x - 6 \\\\\\\\ y &< -x + 9 \\\\end{aligned}$$\\n\\nThe point $(x, y)$ is a solution to the given system of inequalities. If $x$ is an integer, what is the greatest possible value of $x$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"4\"],\"explanation_en\":\"A value $y$ exists exactly when $2x - 6 < y < -x + 9$ has room for $y$, that is, when $2x - 6 < -x + 9$. This gives $3x < 15$, so $x < 5$.\\n\\nThe greatest integer less than $5$ is $4$. For $x = 4$ we need $2 < y < 5$; for example, $(4, 3)$ satisfies both inequalities.\\n\\nA common error is to answer $5$, the $x$-coordinate where the two boundary lines intersect. At $x = 5$ the conditions become $y > 4$ and $y < 4$, which no $y$ satisfies.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$y$ mavjud bo‘lishi uchun $2x - 6 < y < -x + 9$ oralig‘i bo‘sh bo‘lmasligi, ya’ni $2x - 6 < -x + 9$ bo‘lishi kerak. Bundan $3x < 15$, demak $x < 5$.\\n\\n$5$ dan kichik eng katta butun son — $4$. $x = 4$ da $2 < y < 5$ kerak; masalan, $(4, 3)$ ikkala tengsizlikni qanoatlantiradi.\\n\\nKo‘p uchraydigan xato — ikki chegara chizig‘i kesishgan nuqtaning $x$ koordinatasi $5$ ni javob deb berish. $x = 5$ da shartlar $y > 4$ va $y < 4$ ko‘rinishiga keladi, bunday $y$ yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"lines_angles_triangles\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A wooden roof truss is shaped like an isosceles triangle. The two angles at the base of the truss each measure $35^\\\\circ$. What is the measure of the angle at the top of the truss?\",\"option_a\":\"$35^\\\\circ$\",\"option_b\":\"$55^\\\\circ$\",\"option_c\":\"$70^\\\\circ$\",\"option_d\":\"$110^\\\\circ$\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The three angles of a triangle add to $180^\\\\circ$, so the top angle measures $180^\\\\circ - 35^\\\\circ - 35^\\\\circ = 110^\\\\circ$. Choice D is correct.\\n\\nChoice A ($35^\\\\circ$) assumes all three angles are equal, but an isosceles triangle has only two equal angles here. Choice B ($55^\\\\circ$) is $90^\\\\circ - 35^\\\\circ$, which would apply only to a right triangle. Choice C ($70^\\\\circ$) is the sum of the two base angles, not the remaining angle.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Uchburchak uchta burchagining yig‘indisi $180^\\\\circ$, demak tepadagi burchak $180^\\\\circ - 35^\\\\circ - 35^\\\\circ = 110^\\\\circ$. To‘g‘ri javob D.\\n\\nA ($35^\\\\circ$) — uchala burchak teng deb olingan, lekin bu teng yonli uchburchakda faqat ikkita burchak teng. B ($55^\\\\circ$) — bu $90^\\\\circ - 35^\\\\circ$, bu faqat to‘g‘ri burchakli uchburchak uchun to‘g‘ri bo‘lardi. C ($70^\\\\circ$) — asosdagi ikki burchakning yig‘indisi, qolgan burchak emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"lines_angles_triangles\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"Two straight, parallel streets are crossed by a straight avenue. At the first street, one of the angles between the street and the avenue that lies between the two streets measures $64^\\\\circ$. At the second street, consider the angle between the street and the avenue that also lies between the two streets and is on the same side of the avenue as the $64^\\\\circ$ angle. What is the measure, in degrees, of that angle?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"116\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The avenue is a transversal of the two parallel streets. The two angles described are same-side interior angles (both between the parallel lines, on the same side of the transversal), and such angles are supplementary. So the angle measures $180^\\\\circ - 64^\\\\circ = 116^\\\\circ$.\\n\\nCommon mistake: answering 64 treats the angles as alternate interior or corresponding angles, which are congruent; same-side interior angles add to $180^\\\\circ$ instead.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Avenyu ikki parallel ko‘chani kesib o‘tuvchi kesuvchidir. Tasvirlangan ikki burchak bir tomonli ichki burchaklar (ikkalasi ham parallel chiziqlar orasida, kesuvchining bir tomonida), bunday burchaklarning yig‘indisi $180^\\\\circ$. Demak burchak $180^\\\\circ - 64^\\\\circ = 116^\\\\circ$.\\n\\nKo‘p uchraydigan xato: 64 javobi burchaklarni ichki almashinuvchi yoki mos burchaklar deb hisoblashdan kelib chiqadi, ular teng bo‘ladi; bir tomonli ichki burchaklarning esa yig‘indisi $180^\\\\circ$ ga teng.\",\"estimated_seconds\":65,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"lines_angles_triangles\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"In triangle $PQR$, the measure of angle $P$ is $48^\\\\circ$ and the measure of angle $Q$ is $67^\\\\circ$. Side $\\\\overline{QR}$ is extended beyond $R$ to point $S$. What is the measure of angle $PRS$?\",\"option_a\":\"$65^\\\\circ$\",\"option_b\":\"$113^\\\\circ$\",\"option_c\":\"$115^\\\\circ$\",\"option_d\":\"$132^\\\\circ$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Angle $PRS$ is an exterior angle of the triangle at $R$. An exterior angle equals the sum of the two remote interior angles: $48^\\\\circ + 67^\\\\circ = 115^\\\\circ$. (Check: angle $PRQ = 180^\\\\circ - 115^\\\\circ = 65^\\\\circ$, and $65^\\\\circ + 115^\\\\circ = 180^\\\\circ$.) Choice C is correct.\\n\\nChoice A ($65^\\\\circ$) is the interior angle $PRQ$, not the exterior angle. Choice B ($113^\\\\circ$) is $180^\\\\circ - 67^\\\\circ$, and choice D ($132^\\\\circ$) is $180^\\\\circ - 48^\\\\circ$; each uses only one of the two remote interior angles.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$PRS$ burchak uchburchakning $R$ uchidagi tashqi burchagidir. Tashqi burchak unga qo‘shni bo‘lmagan ikki ichki burchakning yig‘indisiga teng: $48^\\\\circ + 67^\\\\circ = 115^\\\\circ$. (Tekshirish: $PRQ = 180^\\\\circ - 115^\\\\circ = 65^\\\\circ$ va $65^\\\\circ + 115^\\\\circ = 180^\\\\circ$.) To‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\nA ($65^\\\\circ$) — bu tashqi emas, ichki $PRQ$ burchak. B ($113^\\\\circ$) — bu $180^\\\\circ - 67^\\\\circ$, D ($132^\\\\circ$) esa $180^\\\\circ - 48^\\\\circ$; ikkalasida ham qo‘shni bo‘lmagan ikki ichki burchakdan faqat bittasi olingan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"lines_angles_triangles\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A traditional geometric tile pattern contains tiles shaped like regular octagons (8-sided polygons with all sides equal and all angles equal). What is the measure of each interior angle of one of these octagons?\",\"option_a\":\"$45^\\\\circ$\",\"option_b\":\"$135^\\\\circ$\",\"option_c\":\"$144^\\\\circ$\",\"option_d\":\"$1{,}080^\\\\circ$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The interior angles of an $n$-sided polygon add to $(n - 2) \\\\cdot 180^\\\\circ$. For $n = 8$: $6 \\\\cdot 180^\\\\circ = 1{,}080^\\\\circ$. All 8 angles are equal, so each measures $\\\\frac{1{,}080^\\\\circ}{8} = 135^\\\\circ$. Choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice A ($45^\\\\circ$) is $\\\\frac{360^\\\\circ}{8}$, the exterior angle. Choice C ($144^\\\\circ$) is the interior angle of a regular 10-sided polygon. Choice D ($1{,}080^\\\\circ$) is the sum of all 8 interior angles, not one of them.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$n$ burchakli ko‘pburchak ichki burchaklarining yig‘indisi $(n - 2) \\\\cdot 180^\\\\circ$. $n = 8$ uchun: $6 \\\\cdot 180^\\\\circ = 1{,}080^\\\\circ$. Barcha 8 ta burchak teng, demak har biri $\\\\frac{1{,}080^\\\\circ}{8} = 135^\\\\circ$. To‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\nA ($45^\\\\circ$) — bu $\\\\frac{360^\\\\circ}{8}$, ya’ni tashqi burchak. C ($144^\\\\circ$) — muntazam o‘nburchakning ichki burchagi. D ($1{,}080^\\\\circ$) — 8 ta ichki burchakning yig‘indisi, bittasi emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"lines_angles_triangles\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"To estimate the height of a minaret, a student places a small flat mirror on level ground 32 meters from the base of the minaret. She then stands 2 meters from the mirror, on the other side of it from the minaret, so that she sees the top of the minaret in the mirror. Her eyes are 1.5 meters above the ground. The minaret and the student are both vertical, and the angle at which light hits the mirror equals the angle at which it leaves. What is the height, in meters, of the minaret?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"24\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The equal angles at the mirror, together with the right angles at the base of the minaret and at the student's feet, make the two right triangles similar (AA). Corresponding sides are proportional: $\\\\frac{h}{32} = \\\\frac{1.5}{2}$, so $h = 32 \\\\cdot 0.75 = 24$ meters.\\n\\nCommon mistake: setting up $\\\\frac{h}{1.5} = \\\\frac{2}{32}$ pairs the sides incorrectly and gives a height of less than 1 meter, which is not reasonable for a minaret.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ko‘zgudagi teng burchaklar hamda minora asosi va o‘quvchi oyog‘i yonidagi to‘g‘ri burchaklar ikki to‘g‘ri burchakli uchburchakni o‘xshash qiladi (ikki burchak bo‘yicha). Mos tomonlar proporsional: $\\\\frac{h}{32} = \\\\frac{1.5}{2}$, demak $h = 32 \\\\cdot 0.75 = 24$ metr.\\n\\nKo‘p uchraydigan xato: $\\\\frac{h}{1.5} = \\\\frac{2}{32}$ proporsiyasi tomonlarni noto‘g‘ri juftlaydi va 1 metrdan kichik balandlik beradi, bu minora uchun mantiqqa to‘g‘ri kelmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"lines_angles_triangles\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"The three angles of a triangle measure $(2x + 10)^\\\\circ$, $(3x - 5)^\\\\circ$, and $(x + 25)^\\\\circ$. What is the measure of the largest angle of the triangle?\",\"option_a\":\"$25^\\\\circ$\",\"option_b\":\"$50^\\\\circ$\",\"option_c\":\"$60^\\\\circ$\",\"option_d\":\"$70^\\\\circ$\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The angles add to $180^\\\\circ$: $(2x + 10) + (3x - 5) + (x + 25) = 6x + 30 = 180$, so $6x = 150$ and $x = 25$. The angles are $2(25) + 10 = 60^\\\\circ$, $3(25) - 5 = 70^\\\\circ$, and $25 + 25 = 50^\\\\circ$. The largest is $70^\\\\circ$, choice D.\\n\\nChoice A (25) is the value of $x$, not an angle measure. Choice B ($50^\\\\circ$) is the smallest angle, and choice C ($60^\\\\circ$) is the middle one.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Burchaklar yig‘indisi $180^\\\\circ$: $(2x + 10) + (3x - 5) + (x + 25) = 6x + 30 = 180$, demak $6x = 150$ va $x = 25$. Burchaklar: $2(25) + 10 = 60^\\\\circ$, $3(25) - 5 = 70^\\\\circ$ va $25 + 25 = 50^\\\\circ$. Eng kattasi $70^\\\\circ$, ya’ni D.\\n\\nA (25) — bu burchak emas, $x$ ning qiymati. B ($50^\\\\circ$) — eng kichik burchak, C ($60^\\\\circ$) esa o‘rtachasi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"lines_angles_triangles\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Lines $\\\\ell$ and $m$ are parallel. Point $P$ lies between the two lines. Point $A$ is on line $\\\\ell$, point $B$ is on line $m$, and both $A$ and $B$ are to the right of $P$. Segment $\\\\overline{PA}$ makes an acute angle of $38^\\\\circ$ with line $\\\\ell$ at $A$, and segment $\\\\overline{PB}$ makes an acute angle of $47^\\\\circ$ with line $m$ at $B$. What is the measure of angle $APB$?\",\"option_a\":\"$85^\\\\circ$\",\"option_b\":\"$95^\\\\circ$\",\"option_c\":\"$133^\\\\circ$\",\"option_d\":\"$275^\\\\circ$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Draw the line through $P$ parallel to $\\\\ell$ (and therefore also parallel to $m$). This line splits angle $APB$ into two parts. The part toward $A$ and the $38^\\\\circ$ angle at $A$ are alternate interior angles for the parallel lines and transversal $\\\\overline{PA}$, so that part is $38^\\\\circ$. In the same way, the part toward $B$ is $47^\\\\circ$. So angle $APB = 38^\\\\circ + 47^\\\\circ = 85^\\\\circ$. Choice A is correct.\\n\\nChoice B ($95^\\\\circ$) is $180^\\\\circ - 85^\\\\circ$, the supplement of the correct angle. Choice C ($133^\\\\circ$) is $180^\\\\circ - 47^\\\\circ$, which uses only one of the given angles. Choice D ($275^\\\\circ$) is $360^\\\\circ - 85^\\\\circ$, the reflex angle on the other side of $P$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$P$ nuqta orqali $\\\\ell$ ga parallel (demak $m$ ga ham parallel) to‘g‘ri chiziq o‘tkazamiz. Bu chiziq $APB$ burchakni ikki qismga ajratadi. $A$ tomondagi qism va $A$ dagi $38^\\\\circ$ li burchak parallel chiziqlar va $\\\\overline{PA}$ kesuvchi uchun ichki almashinuvchi burchaklardir, shuning uchun bu qism $38^\\\\circ$. Xuddi shunday, $B$ tomondagi qism $47^\\\\circ$. Demak $APB = 38^\\\\circ + 47^\\\\circ = 85^\\\\circ$. To‘g‘ri javob A.\\n\\nB ($95^\\\\circ$) — bu $180^\\\\circ - 85^\\\\circ$, to‘g‘ri burchakka qo‘shni burchak. C ($133^\\\\circ$) — bu $180^\\\\circ - 47^\\\\circ$, berilgan burchaklardan faqat bittasi ishlatilgan. D ($275^\\\\circ$) — bu $360^\\\\circ - 85^\\\\circ$, $P$ ning narigi tomonidagi botiq burchak.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"lines_angles_triangles\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"In triangle $ABC$, point $D$ lies on side $\\\\overline{AB}$ and point $E$ lies on side $\\\\overline{AC}$. Angle $ADE$ is congruent to angle $ACB$. If $AD = 4$, $AE = 5$, and $EC = 7$, what is the length of $\\\\overline{DB}$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"11\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Triangles $ADE$ and $ACB$ share angle $A$, and angle $ADE$ is congruent to angle $ACB$, so the triangles are similar with $D$ corresponding to $C$ and $E$ corresponding to $B$. Then $\\\\frac{AD}{AC} = \\\\frac{AE}{AB}$. Since $AC = 5 + 7 = 12$, this gives $\\\\frac{4}{12} = \\\\frac{5}{AB}$, so $AB = 15$ and $DB = 15 - 4 = 11$.\\n\\nCommon mistake: matching $D$ with $B$ (as if $\\\\overline{DE}$ were parallel to $\\\\overline{BC}$) gives $\\\\frac{4}{AB} = \\\\frac{5}{12}$, so $AB = 9.6$ and $DB = 5.6$. The congruent angles show that $D$ corresponds to $C$, not to $B$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$ADE$ va $ACB$ uchburchaklarning $A$ burchagi umumiy, $ADE$ burchak esa $ACB$ burchakka teng, shuning uchun uchburchaklar o‘xshash, bunda $D$ ga $C$, $E$ ga esa $B$ mos keladi. U holda $\\\\frac{AD}{AC} = \\\\frac{AE}{AB}$. $AC = 5 + 7 = 12$ bo‘lgani uchun $\\\\frac{4}{12} = \\\\frac{5}{AB}$, demak $AB = 15$ va $DB = 15 - 4 = 11$.\\n\\nKo‘p uchraydigan xato: $D$ ni $B$ ga mos qo‘yish (go‘yo $\\\\overline{DE}$ kesma $\\\\overline{BC}$ ga parallel bo‘lgandek) $\\\\frac{4}{AB} = \\\\frac{5}{12}$ ni beradi, bundan $AB = 9.6$ va $DB = 5.6$. Teng burchaklar $D$ ga $B$ emas, $C$ mos kelishini ko‘rsatadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":130,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_equations_systems\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"What are the solutions to the equation $x^2 + 5x - 36 = 0$?\",\"option_a\":\"$-9$ and $4$\",\"option_b\":\"$-4$ and $9$\",\"option_c\":\"$-9$ and $-4$\",\"option_d\":\"$-6$ and $6$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Find two numbers whose product is $-36$ and whose sum is $5$: they are $9$ and $-4$. So $x^2 + 5x - 36 = (x + 9)(x - 4) = 0$.\\n\\nSetting each factor equal to $0$ gives $x = -9$ and $x = 4$. Check: $(-9)^2 + 5(-9) - 36 = 81 - 45 - 36 = 0$ and $16 + 20 - 36 = 0$.\\n\\nChoice B uses the numbers from the factors without changing their signs. Choice C gives two numbers whose product is $+36$, not $-36$. Choice D comes from ignoring the $5x$ term and solving $x^2 = 36$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ko‘paytmasi $-36$, yig‘indisi $5$ bo‘lgan ikki sonni topamiz: bular $9$ va $-4$. Demak $x^2 + 5x - 36 = (x + 9)(x - 4) = 0$.\\n\\nHar bir ko‘paytuvchini $0$ ga tenglab, $x = -9$ va $x = 4$ ni olamiz. Tekshirish: $(-9)^2 + 5(-9) - 36 = 81 - 45 - 36 = 0$ va $16 + 20 - 36 = 0$.\\n\\nB variantda ko‘paytuvchilardagi sonlar ishorasi o‘zgartirilmasdan olingan. C variantdagi sonlarning ko‘paytmasi $-36$ emas, $+36$. D variant $5x$ hadini tashlab, $x^2 = 36$ ni yechishdan kelib chiqqan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_equations_systems\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A stone is dropped from a bridge. The height $h$, in meters, of the stone above the water $t$ seconds after it is dropped is modeled by the equation $h = 45 - 5t^2$. According to the model, how many seconds after the stone is dropped does it hit the water?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"3\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The stone hits the water when its height is $0$: $45 - 5t^2 = 0$.\\n\\nThen $5t^2 = 45$, so $t^2 = 9$ and $t = 3$ or $t = -3$.\\n\\nTime after the drop cannot be negative, so the stone hits the water after $3$ seconds. A common error is entering $9$, the value of $t^2$, or $45$, the starting height.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Tosh suvga tushganda uning balandligi $0$ bo‘ladi: $45 - 5t^2 = 0$.\\n\\nU holda $5t^2 = 45$, ya’ni $t^2 = 9$ va $t = 3$ yoki $t = -3$.\\n\\nTosh tashlangandan keyingi vaqt manfiy bo‘lishi mumkin emas, demak tosh $3$ soniyadan keyin suvga tushadi. Ko‘p uchraydigan xato — $t^2$ ning qiymati $9$ ni yoki boshlang‘ich balandlik $45$ ni kiritish.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_equations_systems\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Iodine-131, which is used in medicine, has a half-life of about $8$ days. The mass $M$, in milligrams, of iodine-131 remaining $t$ days after a $200$-milligram sample is prepared is modeled by the equation $M = 200\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{1}{2}\\\\right)^{t/8}$. According to the model, after how many days will $25$ milligrams of the sample remain?\",\"option_a\":\"$3$\",\"option_b\":\"$8$\",\"option_c\":\"$16$\",\"option_d\":\"$24$\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Set $M = 25$: $200\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\right)^{t/8} = 25$, so $\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\right)^{t/8} = \\\\frac{25}{200} = \\\\frac{1}{8} = \\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\right)^3$.\\n\\nTherefore $\\\\frac{t}{8} = 3$ and $t = 24$ days. This makes sense: $200 \\\\to 100 \\\\to 50 \\\\to 25$ takes three half-lives of $8$ days each.\\n\\nChoice A is the number of half-lives, not the number of days. Choice B is one half-life, when $100$ milligrams remain. Choice C is two half-lives, when $50$ milligrams remain.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$M = 25$ deb olamiz: $200\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\right)^{t/8} = 25$, bundan $\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\right)^{t/8} = \\\\frac{25}{200} = \\\\frac{1}{8} = \\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\right)^3$.\\n\\nDemak $\\\\frac{t}{8} = 3$ va $t = 24$ kun. Bu mantiqan to‘g‘ri: $200 \\\\to 100 \\\\to 50 \\\\to 25$ uchun har biri $8$ kunlik uchta yarim yemirilish davri kerak.\\n\\nA variant — kunlar soni emas, yarim yemirilish davrlari soni. B variant — bitta davr, bunda $100$ milligramm qoladi. C variant — ikkita davr, bunda $50$ milligramm qoladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_equations_systems\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A ball is thrown upward from a balcony. The height $h$, in meters, of the ball above the ground $t$ seconds after it is thrown is modeled by the equation $h = -4.9t^2 + 19.6t + 1.5$. According to the model, at what times, in seconds, is the ball $16.2$ meters above the ground?\",\"option_a\":\"$1$ only\",\"option_b\":\"$1$ and $3$\",\"option_c\":\"$2$ only\",\"option_d\":\"$3$ only\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Set $h = 16.2$: $-4.9t^2 + 19.6t + 1.5 = 16.2$, so $-4.9t^2 + 19.6t - 14.7 = 0$.\\n\\nDivide every term by $-4.9$: $t^2 - 4t + 3 = 0$, which factors as $(t - 1)(t - 3) = 0$. So $t = 1$ or $t = 3$.\\n\\nBoth times are positive, so both are valid: the ball passes $16.2$ meters on the way up at $t = 1$ and on the way down at $t = 3$.\\n\\nChoices A and D keep only one of the two valid times. Choice C is the time of the maximum height; at $t = 2$ the height is $21.1$ meters.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$h = 16.2$ deb olamiz: $-4.9t^2 + 19.6t + 1.5 = 16.2$, ya’ni $-4.9t^2 + 19.6t - 14.7 = 0$.\\n\\nHar bir hadni $-4.9$ ga bo‘lamiz: $t^2 - 4t + 3 = 0$, bu $(t - 1)(t - 3) = 0$ ko‘rinishida ajraladi. Demak $t = 1$ yoki $t = 3$.\\n\\nIkkala vaqt ham musbat, demak ikkalasi ham to‘g‘ri: to‘p $16.2$ metrdan yuqoriga ko‘tarilayotganda $t = 1$ da, pastga tushayotganda $t = 3$ da o‘tadi.\\n\\nA va D variantlarda ikkita to‘g‘ri vaqtdan faqat bittasi olingan. C variant — eng yuqori nuqtaga yetish vaqti; $t = 2$ da balandlik $21.1$ metr.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_equations_systems\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"What are all the solutions to the equation $x - \\\\sqrt{x} = 12$?\",\"option_a\":\"$9$ and $16$\",\"option_b\":\"$9$\",\"option_c\":\"$16$\",\"option_d\":\"$4$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Let $u = \\\\sqrt{x}$, so $x = u^2$ and $u \\\\ge 0$. The equation becomes $u^2 - u - 12 = 0$, which factors as $(u - 4)(u + 3) = 0$.\\n\\nSo $u = 4$ or $u = -3$. A square root cannot be negative, so $u = -3$ is rejected. Then $\\\\sqrt{x} = 4$ and $x = 16$. Check: $16 - \\\\sqrt{16} = 16 - 4 = 12$.\\n\\nChoices A and B square the rejected value $u = -3$ to get $x = 9$, but $9 - \\\\sqrt{9} = 6$, not $12$, so $9$ is extraneous. Choice D is the value of $u = \\\\sqrt{x}$, not $x$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$u = \\\\sqrt{x}$ deb belgilaymiz, u holda $x = u^2$ va $u \\\\ge 0$. Tenglama $u^2 - u - 12 = 0$ ko‘rinishiga keladi va $(u - 4)(u + 3) = 0$ ga ajraladi.\\n\\nDemak $u = 4$ yoki $u = -3$. Kvadrat ildiz manfiy bo‘lmaydi, shuning uchun $u = -3$ tashlanadi. U holda $\\\\sqrt{x} = 4$ va $x = 16$. Tekshirish: $16 - \\\\sqrt{16} = 16 - 4 = 12$.\\n\\nA va B variantlarda tashlangan $u = -3$ kvadratga ko‘tarilib, $x = 9$ olingan, lekin $9 - \\\\sqrt{9} = 6$, $12$ emas, demak $9$ — chet ildiz. D variant — $x$ emas, $u = \\\\sqrt{x}$ ning qiymati.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_equations_systems\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"$$\\\\begin{aligned} y &= x^2 + 3 \\\\\\\\ y &= 4x \\\\end{aligned}$$\\n\\nIf $(x, y)$ is a solution to the given system of equations and $x > 1$, what is the value of $y$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"12\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Substitute $y = 4x$ into the first equation: $4x = x^2 + 3$, so $x^2 - 4x + 3 = 0$ and $(x - 1)(x - 3) = 0$.\\n\\nThe solutions are $x = 1$ and $x = 3$. Since $x > 1$, $x = 3$, and $y = 4(3) = 12$. Check in the first equation: $3^2 + 3 = 12$.\\n\\nA common error is entering $3$, the value of $x$, or $4$, the $y$-value of the other solution $(1, 4)$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$y = 4x$ ni birinchi tenglamaga qo‘yamiz: $4x = x^2 + 3$, ya’ni $x^2 - 4x + 3 = 0$ va $(x - 1)(x - 3) = 0$.\\n\\nYechimlar $x = 1$ va $x = 3$. $x > 1$ bo‘lgani uchun $x = 3$ va $y = 4(3) = 12$. Birinchi tenglamada tekshiramiz: $3^2 + 3 = 12$.\\n\\nKo‘p uchraydigan xato — $x$ ning qiymati $3$ ni yoki boshqa yechim $(1, 4)$ dagi $y$ qiymati $4$ ni kiritish.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_equations_systems\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"$$\\\\begin{aligned} y &= 2x^2 - 3x + k \\\\\\\\ y &= x + 1 \\\\end{aligned}$$\\n\\nIn the given system of equations, $k$ is a constant. If the system has no real solutions, which of the following could be the value of $k$?\",\"option_a\":\"$1$\",\"option_b\":\"$2$\",\"option_c\":\"$3$\",\"option_d\":\"$4$\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Set the expressions for $y$ equal: $2x^2 - 3x + k = x + 1$, so $2x^2 - 4x + (k - 1) = 0$.\\n\\nThe system has no real solutions when this quadratic has a negative discriminant: $(-4)^2 - 4(2)(k - 1) < 0$, so $16 - 8k + 8 < 0$, which gives $24 < 8k$ and $k > 3$. Of the choices, only $k = 4$ works.\\n\\nChoice C, $k = 3$, makes the discriminant exactly $0$, so the line touches the parabola at one point, $(1, 2)$. Choices A and B make the discriminant positive, giving two intersection points.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$y$ uchun ifodalarni tenglashtiramiz: $2x^2 - 3x + k = x + 1$, ya’ni $2x^2 - 4x + (k - 1) = 0$.\\n\\nBu kvadrat tenglamaning diskriminanti manfiy bo‘lsa, sistema haqiqiy yechimga ega emas: $(-4)^2 - 4(2)(k - 1) < 0$, ya’ni $16 - 8k + 8 < 0$, bundan $24 < 8k$ va $k > 3$. Variantlardan faqat $k = 4$ mos keladi.\\n\\nC variantda, $k = 3$ da, diskriminant aynan $0$ ga teng, ya’ni to‘g‘ri chiziq parabolaga bitta nuqtada, $(1, 2)$ da urinadi. A va B variantlarda diskriminant musbat, ikkita kesishish nuqtasi bor.\",\"estimated_seconds\":110,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_equations_systems\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"$$\\\\begin{aligned} y &= x^2 - 6x + 11 \\\\\\\\ y &= -x^2 + 2x + 5 \\\\end{aligned}$$\\n\\nWhat is the sum of the $y$-coordinates of all the solutions $(x, y)$ to the given system of equations?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"8\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Set the expressions for $y$ equal: $x^2 - 6x + 11 = -x^2 + 2x + 5$, so $2x^2 - 8x + 6 = 0$. Dividing by $2$ gives $x^2 - 4x + 3 = 0$, or $(x - 1)(x - 3) = 0$.\\n\\nFor $x = 1$: $y = 1 - 6 + 11 = 6$. For $x = 3$: $y = 9 - 18 + 11 = 2$. (Both values check in the second equation: $-1 + 2 + 5 = 6$ and $-9 + 6 + 5 = 2$.)\\n\\nThe sum of the $y$-coordinates is $6 + 2 = 8$. A common error is entering $4$, the sum of the $x$-coordinates.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$y$ uchun ifodalarni tenglashtiramiz: $x^2 - 6x + 11 = -x^2 + 2x + 5$, ya’ni $2x^2 - 8x + 6 = 0$. $2$ ga bo‘lsak, $x^2 - 4x + 3 = 0$, yoki $(x - 1)(x - 3) = 0$.\\n\\n$x = 1$ da: $y = 1 - 6 + 11 = 6$. $x = 3$ da: $y = 9 - 18 + 11 = 2$. (Ikkala qiymat ikkinchi tenglamani ham qanoatlantiradi: $-1 + 2 + 5 = 6$ va $-9 + 6 + 5 = 2$.)\\n\\n$y$ koordinatalar yig‘indisi $6 + 2 = 8$. Ko‘p uchraydigan xato — $x$ koordinatalar yig‘indisi $4$ ni kiritish.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A ball is kicked from a platform. The height, in meters, of the ball above the ground $t$ seconds after it is kicked is modeled by the function $h(t) = -5t^2 + 20t + 2$. What is the best interpretation of $2$ in this context?\",\"option_a\":\"The maximum height of the ball is $2$ meters.\",\"option_b\":\"The ball was $2$ meters above the ground when it was kicked.\",\"option_c\":\"The ball hits the ground $2$ seconds after it is kicked.\",\"option_d\":\"The height of the ball increases by $2$ meters each second.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The constant term is the value of the function when $t = 0$: $h(0) = -5(0)^2 + 20(0) + 2 = 2$. At $t = 0$ the ball is just being kicked, so $2$ is its starting height above the ground, in meters.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the maximum occurs at $t = 2$ and equals $h(2) = 22$ meters. Choice C is wrong because $h(2) = 22$, not $0$. Choice D describes a linear model; in a quadratic model the height does not change by a constant amount each second.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ozod had — bu $t = 0$ dagi funksiya qiymati: $h(0) = -5(0)^2 + 20(0) + 2 = 2$. $t = 0$ da to‘p endigina tepilgan, demak $2$ — uning yerdan boshlang‘ich balandligi (metrda).\\n\\nA variant noto‘g‘ri, chunki eng katta balandlik $t = 2$ da bo‘ladi va $h(2) = 22$ metrga teng. C variant noto‘g‘ri, chunki $h(2) = 22$, $0$ emas. D variant chiziqli modelni tasvirlaydi; kvadratik modelda balandlik har soniyada bir xil miqdorga o‘zgarmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"The population of a town $t$ years after 2025 is modeled by the function $P(t) = 52{,}000(1.024)^t$. According to the model, which of the following is closest to the population of the town in 2027?\",\"option_a\":\"$53{,}248$\",\"option_b\":\"$54{,}496$\",\"option_c\":\"$54{,}526$\",\"option_d\":\"$106{,}496$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The year 2027 is $2$ years after 2025, so $t = 2$: $P(2) = 52{,}000(1.024)^2 = 52{,}000(1.048576) \\\\approx 54{,}526$.\\n\\nChoice A uses $t = 1$, the population in 2026. Choice B adds the first year's increase of $1{,}248$ twice, treating the growth as linear and ignoring that the second year's $2.4\\\\%$ is taken of a larger population. Choice D multiplies $52{,}000$ by $2(1.024)$ instead of raising $1.024$ to the power $2$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"2027-yil 2025-yildan $2$ yil keyin, demak $t = 2$: $P(2) = 52{,}000(1.024)^2 = 52{,}000(1.048576) \\\\approx 54{,}526$.\\n\\nA variantda $t = 1$ olingan, bu 2026-yildagi aholi. B variantda birinchi yildagi o‘sish $1{,}248$ ikki marta qo‘shilgan, ya’ni o‘sish chiziqli deb olingan va ikkinchi yilgi $2.4\\\\%$ kattaroq aholidan hisoblanishi e’tiborsiz qoldirilgan. D variantda $1.024$ ni ikkinchi darajaga ko‘tarish o‘rniga $52{,}000$ soni $2(1.024)$ ga ko‘paytirilgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":65,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"The function $f$ is defined by $f(x) = \\\\dfrac{x^2 + 8}{x - 1}$. What is the value of $f(3)$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"17/2\",\"8.5\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Substitute $x = 3$: $f(3) = \\\\frac{3^2 + 8}{3 - 1} = \\\\frac{9 + 8}{2} = \\\\frac{17}{2}$, or $8.5$.\\n\\nCommon errors include computing $3^2$ as $6$, which gives $\\\\frac{14}{2} = 7$, and dividing only the $8$ by $2$, which gives $9 + 4 = 13$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$x = 3$ ni qo‘yamiz: $f(3) = \\\\frac{3^2 + 8}{3 - 1} = \\\\frac{9 + 8}{2} = \\\\frac{17}{2}$, ya’ni $8.5$.\\n\\nKo‘p uchraydigan xatolar: $3^2$ ni $6$ deb hisoblash, bu $\\\\frac{14}{2} = 7$ ni beradi, va faqat $8$ ni $2$ ga bo‘lish, bu $9 + 4 = 13$ ni beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Cesium-137 has a half-life of about $30$ years. A sample initially contains $120$ grams of cesium-137. Which of the following functions best models the mass $M$, in grams, of cesium-137 remaining in the sample $t$ years later?\",\"option_a\":\"$M(t) = 120\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{1}{2}\\\\right)^{t/30}$\",\"option_b\":\"$M(t) = 120\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{1}{2}\\\\right)^{30t}$\",\"option_c\":\"$M(t) = 60\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{1}{2}\\\\right)^{t/30}$\",\"option_d\":\"$M(t) = 120 - 2t$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Radioactive decay is exponential: the mass is multiplied by $\\\\frac{1}{2}$ once every $30$ years. After $t$ years, $\\\\frac{t}{30}$ half-lives have passed, so $M(t) = 120\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\right)^{t/30}$. Check: $M(0) = 120$ and $M(30) = 60$.\\n\\nChoice B halves the mass $30$ times every year, so $M(1)$ would be almost $0$. Choice C starts with $60$ grams instead of $120$. Choice D is linear: it matches $M(30) = 60$ but predicts $0$ grams after $60$ years, while the correct model gives $30$ grams.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Radioaktiv yemirilish ko‘rsatkichli funksiya bilan ifodalanadi: massa har $30$ yilda bir marta $\\\\frac{1}{2}$ ga ko‘paytiriladi. $t$ yilda $\\\\frac{t}{30}$ ta yarim yemirilish davri o‘tadi, demak $M(t) = 120\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\right)^{t/30}$. Tekshirish: $M(0) = 120$ va $M(30) = 60$.\\n\\nB variantda massa har yili $30$ marta ikkiga bo‘linadi, shuning uchun $M(1)$ deyarli $0$ bo‘lardi. C variant $120$ emas, $60$ grammdan boshlanadi. D variant chiziqli: u $M(30) = 60$ ga mos keladi, lekin $60$ yildan keyin $0$ gramm qoladi deydi, to‘g‘ri model esa $30$ grammni beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"The function $f$ is defined by $f(x) = 3(2)^x - 12$. In the $xy$-plane, what is the $x$-intercept of the graph of $y = f(x)$?\",\"option_a\":\"$(0, -9)$\",\"option_b\":\"$(-12, 0)$\",\"option_c\":\"$(4, 0)$\",\"option_d\":\"$(2, 0)$\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"At the $x$-intercept, $f(x) = 0$: $3(2)^x - 12 = 0$, so $3(2)^x = 12$, $2^x = 4 = 2^2$, and $x = 2$. The $x$-intercept is $(2, 0)$.\\n\\nChoice A is the $y$-intercept: $f(0) = 3(1) - 12 = -9$. Choice B uses the constant $-12$ as the intercept. Choice C stops at $2^x = 4$ and takes $4$ as the value of $x$; but $f(4) = 3(16) - 12 = 36$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$x$ o‘qini kesish nuqtasida $f(x) = 0$: $3(2)^x - 12 = 0$, ya’ni $3(2)^x = 12$, $2^x = 4 = 2^2$ va $x = 2$. Kesish nuqtasi $(2, 0)$.\\n\\nA variant — $y$ o‘qini kesish nuqtasi: $f(0) = 3(1) - 12 = -9$. B variantda ozod had $-12$ kesish nuqtasi deb olingan. C variantda $2^x = 4$ da to‘xtab, $x$ ning qiymati $4$ deb olingan; lekin $f(4) = 3(16) - 12 = 36$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"The balance, in soums, of a bank deposit $t$ years after it was opened is modeled by the function $B(t) = 4{,}000{,}000(1.25)^t$. According to the model, what is the smallest whole number of years $t$ for which the balance is greater than $9{,}000{,}000$ soums?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"4\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Compute the balance year by year (each year it is multiplied by $1.25$):\\n\\n$B(1) = 5{,}000{,}000$, $B(2) = 6{,}250{,}000$, $B(3) = 7{,}812{,}500$, $B(4) = 9{,}765{,}625$.\\n\\nThe balance first exceeds $9{,}000{,}000$ soums at $t = 4$.\\n\\nA common error is treating the growth as linear: adding $1{,}000{,}000$ soums per year would give $B(5) = 9{,}000{,}000$, which is not greater than $9{,}000{,}000$, and leads to $6$. Another error is entering $3$, because $B(3)$ is still below $9{,}000{,}000$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Hisobdagi pulni yilma-yil hisoblaymiz (har yili $1.25$ ga ko‘paytiriladi):\\n\\n$B(1) = 5{,}000{,}000$, $B(2) = 6{,}250{,}000$, $B(3) = 7{,}812{,}500$, $B(4) = 9{,}765{,}625$.\\n\\nHisobdagi pul birinchi marta $t = 4$ da $9{,}000{,}000$ so‘mdan oshadi.\\n\\nKo‘p uchraydigan xato — o‘sishni chiziqli deb olish: yiliga $1{,}000{,}000$ so‘m qo‘shilsa, $B(5) = 9{,}000{,}000$ bo‘ladi, bu $9{,}000{,}000$ dan katta emas, va $6$ javobiga olib keladi. Yana bir xato — $3$ ni kiritish, chunki $B(3)$ hali $9{,}000{,}000$ dan kichik.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"For the exponential function $f$, $f(x + 1) = 1.5f(x)$ for all values of $x$, and $f(2) = 36$. Which of the following defines $f$?\",\"option_a\":\"$f(x) = 12(1.5)^x$\",\"option_b\":\"$f(x) = 16(1.5)^x$\",\"option_c\":\"$f(x) = 24(1.5)^x$\",\"option_d\":\"$f(x) = 36(1.5)^x$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Since $f(x + 1) = 1.5f(x)$, each time $x$ increases by $1$ the value is multiplied by $1.5$, so $f(x) = a(1.5)^x$ with $a = f(0)$.\\n\\nWork backward from $f(2) = 36$: $f(1) = \\\\frac{36}{1.5} = 24$ and $f(0) = \\\\frac{24}{1.5} = 16$. So $f(x) = 16(1.5)^x$. Check: $16(1.5)^2 = 16(2.25) = 36$.\\n\\nChoice A divides $36$ by $1.5 \\\\cdot 2 = 3$ instead of by $(1.5)^2 = 2.25$. Choice C uses $f(1) = 24$ as the initial value. Choice D uses $f(2) = 36$ as the initial value $f(0)$; it gives $f(2) = 81$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$f(x + 1) = 1.5f(x)$ bo‘lgani uchun $x$ har safar $1$ ga oshganda qiymat $1.5$ ga ko‘paytiriladi, demak $f(x) = a(1.5)^x$, bunda $a = f(0)$.\\n\\n$f(2) = 36$ dan orqaga hisoblaymiz: $f(1) = \\\\frac{36}{1.5} = 24$ va $f(0) = \\\\frac{24}{1.5} = 16$. Demak $f(x) = 16(1.5)^x$. Tekshirish: $16(1.5)^2 = 16(2.25) = 36$.\\n\\nA variantda $36$ soni $(1.5)^2 = 2.25$ ga emas, $1.5 \\\\cdot 2 = 3$ ga bo‘lingan. C variantda boshlang‘ich qiymat sifatida $f(1) = 24$ olingan. D variantda $f(2) = 36$ boshlang‘ich qiymat $f(0)$ deb olingan; bu $f(2) = 81$ ni beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":105,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A ball is thrown upward. The height, in meters, of the ball above the ground $t$ seconds after it is thrown is modeled by the function $h(t) = -5t^2 + bt + 1.6$, where $b$ is a constant. According to the model, the ball reaches its maximum height $1.8$ seconds after it is thrown. What is the maximum height of the ball, in meters?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"17.8\",\"89/5\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The maximum of $h(t) = at^2 + bt + c$ occurs at $t = -\\\\frac{b}{2a}$. Here $-\\\\frac{b}{2(-5)} = \\\\frac{b}{10} = 1.8$, so $b = 18$.\\n\\nThen $h(1.8) = -5(1.8)^2 + 18(1.8) + 1.6 = -5(3.24) + 32.4 + 1.6 = -16.2 + 32.4 + 1.6 = 17.8$ meters.\\n\\nCommon errors: entering $b = 18$ instead of the height; using $t = -\\\\frac{b}{a}$, which gives $b = 9$ and a height of only $1.6$; or forgetting the starting height $1.6$, which gives $16.2$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$h(t) = at^2 + bt + c$ ning eng katta qiymati $t = -\\\\frac{b}{2a}$ da bo‘ladi. Bu yerda $-\\\\frac{b}{2(-5)} = \\\\frac{b}{10} = 1.8$, demak $b = 18$.\\n\\nU holda $h(1.8) = -5(1.8)^2 + 18(1.8) + 1.6 = -5(3.24) + 32.4 + 1.6 = -16.2 + 32.4 + 1.6 = 17.8$ metr.\\n\\nKo‘p uchraydigan xatolar: balandlik o‘rniga $b = 18$ ni kiritish; $t = -\\\\frac{b}{a}$ formulasidan foydalanish, bu $b = 9$ va atigi $1.6$ balandlikni beradi; yoki boshlang‘ich balandlik $1.6$ ni unutish, bu $16.2$ ni beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"one_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"What is the range of the 12 monthly mean temperatures shown in the table?\",\"passage\":\"The table shows the mean temperature in Tashkent, Uzbekistan, for each month, based on climate data for 1991–2020.\\n\\n| Month | Mean temperature (°C) |\\n| --- | ---: |\\n| January | 3.0 |\\n| February | 4.9 |\\n| March | 10.8 |\\n| April | 16.2 |\\n| May | 21.2 |\\n| June | 26.0 |\\n| July | 28.0 |\\n| August | 26.6 |\\n| September | 21.5 |\\n| October | 15.2 |\\n| November | 9.0 |\\n| December | 4.3 |\",\"option_a\":\"3.0°C\",\"option_b\":\"15.6°C\",\"option_c\":\"25.0°C\",\"option_d\":\"28.0°C\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The range is the greatest value minus the least value. The greatest monthly mean is 28.0°C (July), and the least is 3.0°C (January), so the range is $28.0 - 3.0 = 25.0$°C. Choice D is only the greatest value, and choice A is only the least value. Choice B, about 15.6°C, is the mean of the 12 values, which measures center, not spread.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Oraliq (range) — eng katta qiymatdan eng kichik qiymatni ayirish. Eng katta oylik o‘rtacha harorat 28.0°C (iyul), eng kichigi 3.0°C (yanvar), demak oraliq $28.0 - 3.0 = 25.0$°C. D javob — faqat eng katta qiymat, A javob — faqat eng kichik qiymat. B javob (taxminan 15.6°C) — 12 ta qiymatning o‘rtachasi; u tarqoqlikni emas, markazni o‘lchaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"one_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"The list shows the number of gold medals won by athletes from Uzbekistan at each of the 8 Summer Olympics from 1996 to 2024, in order.\\n\\n0, 1, 2, 0, 0, 4, 3, 8\\n\\nWhat is the median number of gold medals won per Summer Olympics?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"1.5\",\"3/2\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Order the values: 0, 0, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 8. There are 8 values, an even number, so the median is the mean of the 4th and 5th values: $\\\\frac{1 + 2}{2} = 1.5$. Taking the middle values of the unordered list (0 and 0) gives 0, which is wrong; the list must be ordered first. Enter 1.5 or 3/2.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qiymatlarni tartiblaymiz: 0, 0, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 8. Qiymatlar soni 8 ta (juft), shuning uchun mediana 4- va 5-qiymatlarning o‘rtachasi: $\\\\frac{1 + 2}{2} = 1.5$. Tartiblanmagan ro‘yxatning o‘rtadagi qiymatlarini (0 va 0) olish 0 ni beradi — bu xato; avval ro‘yxatni tartiblash kerak. Javob maydoniga 1.5 yoki 3/2 deb yozing.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"one_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"The table shows the total number of medals won by each of the 5 Central Asian countries at the 2024 Summer Olympics in Paris. What are the mean and the median of these 5 totals?\",\"passage\":\"| Country | Total medals |\\n| --- | ---: |\\n| Kazakhstan | 7 |\\n| Kyrgyzstan | 6 |\\n| Tajikistan | 3 |\\n| Turkmenistan | 0 |\\n| Uzbekistan | 13 |\",\"option_a\":\"Mean: 5.8; median: 3\",\"option_b\":\"Mean: 5.8; median: 6\",\"option_c\":\"Mean: 6; median: 5.8\",\"option_d\":\"Mean: 7.25; median: 6.5\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The sum of the totals is $7 + 6 + 3 + 0 + 13 = 29$, so the mean is $\\\\frac{29}{5} = 5.8$. In order, the totals are 0, 3, 6, 7, 13, and the middle (3rd) value is 6, so the median is 6. Choice A takes the middle row of the table (Tajikistan, 3) without ordering the values. Choice C switches the mean and the median. Choice D leaves out Turkmenistan's total of 0; a value of 0 is still a data value and must be included.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Jami medallar yig‘indisi $7 + 6 + 3 + 0 + 13 = 29$, demak o‘rtacha $\\\\frac{29}{5} = 5.8$. Tartiblangan qiymatlar: 0, 3, 6, 7, 13; o‘rtadagi (3-) qiymat 6, demak mediana 6. A javob qiymatlarni tartiblamasdan jadvalning o‘rta qatorini (Tojikiston, 3) olgan. C javob o‘rtacha va medianani almashtirib qo‘ygan. D javob Turkmanistonning 0 natijasini tashlab yuborgan; 0 ham ma’lumot qiymati, uni hisobga olish shart.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"one_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Which of the following is true about the standard deviations of the two data sets?\",\"passage\":\"The table shows the mean temperature, in degrees Celsius, for each month in Tashkent, Uzbekistan, and in Singapore, based on long-term climate records.\\n\\n| Month | Tashkent | Singapore |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Jan | 3.0 | 26.8 |\\n| Feb | 4.9 | 27.3 |\\n| Mar | 10.8 | 27.9 |\\n| Apr | 16.2 | 28.2 |\\n| May | 21.2 | 28.6 |\\n| Jun | 26.0 | 28.5 |\\n| Jul | 28.0 | 28.2 |\\n| Aug | 26.6 | 28.1 |\\n| Sep | 21.5 | 28.0 |\\n| Oct | 15.2 | 27.9 |\\n| Nov | 9.0 | 27.2 |\\n| Dec | 4.3 | 26.8 |\",\"option_a\":\"The standard deviation of the Singapore data is greater, because Singapore's monthly mean temperatures are higher.\",\"option_b\":\"The two standard deviations are equal, because each data set has 12 values.\",\"option_c\":\"The standard deviation of the Singapore data is greater, because its mean is greater than the mean of the Tashkent data.\",\"option_d\":\"The standard deviation of the Tashkent data is greater, because Tashkent's monthly mean temperatures are spread farther from their mean.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Standard deviation measures how far the values tend to be from their mean, not how large the values are. Tashkent's values range from 3.0 to 28.0 (range 25.0), while Singapore's range from 26.8 to 28.6 (range 1.8). Tashkent's values are much more spread out, so its standard deviation is greater (about 8.7°C compared with about 0.6°C). Choices A and C confuse large values or a large mean with large spread. Choice B is wrong because having the same number of values says nothing about spread.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Standart chetlanish qiymatlarning o‘z o‘rtachasidan qanchalik uzoqligini o‘lchaydi, qiymatlarning kattaligini emas. Toshkent qiymatlari 3.0 dan 28.0 gacha (oraliq 25.0), Singapurniki esa 26.8 dan 28.6 gacha (oraliq 1.8). Toshkent qiymatlari ancha tarqoq, shuning uchun uning standart chetlanishi katta (taxminan 8.7°C, Singapurda esa taxminan 0.6°C). A va C javoblar katta qiymatlar yoki katta o‘rtachani katta tarqoqlik bilan adashtirgan. B javob noto‘g‘ri: qiymatlar soni bir xil bo‘lishi tarqoqlik haqida hech narsa demaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"one_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"What is the median of the numbers of births, in thousands, shown in the table?\",\"passage\":\"The table shows the number of registered births in Uzbekistan, in thousands, for each year from 2015 to 2024.\\n\\n| Year | Births (thousands) |\\n| --- | ---: |\\n| 2015 | 734.1 |\\n| 2016 | 726.2 |\\n| 2017 | 715.5 |\\n| 2018 | 768.5 |\\n| 2019 | 815.0 |\\n| 2020 | 841.8 |\\n| 2021 | 905.2 |\\n| 2022 | 932.2 |\\n| 2023 | 962.0 |\\n| 2024 | 926.4 |\",\"spr_answers\":[\"828.4\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Order the 10 values: 715.5, 726.2, 734.1, 768.5, **815.0**, **841.8**, 905.2, 926.4, 932.2, 962.0. With an even number of values, the median is the mean of the two middle (5th and 6th) values: $\\\\frac{815.0 + 841.8}{2} = \\\\frac{1{,}656.8}{2} = 828.4$. The mean of the 10 values, about 832.7, is a different measure. Enter 828.4.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"10 ta qiymatni tartiblaymiz: 715.5, 726.2, 734.1, 768.5, **815.0**, **841.8**, 905.2, 926.4, 932.2, 962.0. Qiymatlar soni juft, shuning uchun mediana o‘rtadagi ikki (5- va 6-) qiymatning o‘rtachasi: $\\\\frac{815.0 + 841.8}{2} = \\\\frac{1{,}656.8}{2} = 828.4$. 10 ta qiymatning o‘rtachasi (taxminan 832.7) — boshqa ko‘rsatkich. Javob: 828.4.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"one_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"The table shows the diameter, in kilometers, of each of the 8 planets in the solar system. Which of the following gives the mean and the median of these 8 diameters, to the nearest kilometer?\",\"passage\":\"According to NASA, the diameters of the planets are as shown.\\n\\n| Planet | Diameter (km) |\\n| --- | ---: |\\n| Mercury | 4,879 |\\n| Venus | 12,104 |\\n| Earth | 12,756 |\\n| Mars | 6,792 |\\n| Jupiter | 142,984 |\\n| Saturn | 120,536 |\\n| Uranus | 51,118 |\\n| Neptune | 49,528 |\",\"option_a\":\"Mean: 50,087; median: 31,142\",\"option_b\":\"Mean: 50,087; median: 74,888\",\"option_c\":\"Mean: 31,142; median: 50,087\",\"option_d\":\"Mean: 74,888; median: 31,142\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The sum of the diameters is 400,697, so the mean is $\\\\frac{400{,}697}{8} \\\\approx 50{,}087$ km. Ordered, the diameters are 4,879; 6,792; 12,104; 12,756; 49,528; 51,118; 120,536; 142,984. The median is the mean of the 4th and 5th values: $\\\\frac{12{,}756 + 49{,}528}{2} = 31{,}142$ km. The mean is much greater than the median because of the two very large values (Jupiter and Saturn). Choice B averages the middle two rows of the **unordered** table (Mars and Jupiter). Choice C switches the two measures, and choice D also uses the unordered middle values.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Diametrlar yig‘indisi 400,697, demak o‘rtacha $\\\\frac{400{,}697}{8} \\\\approx 50{,}087$ km. Tartiblangan diametrlar: 4,879; 6,792; 12,104; 12,756; 49,528; 51,118; 120,536; 142,984. Mediana — 4- va 5-qiymatlarning o‘rtachasi: $\\\\frac{12{,}756 + 49{,}528}{2} = 31{,}142$ km. Ikki juda katta qiymat (Yupiter va Saturn) tufayli o‘rtacha medianadan ancha katta. B javob **tartiblanmagan** jadvalning o‘rtadagi ikki qatorini (Mars va Yupiter) o‘rtachalagan. C javob ikki ko‘rsatkichni almashtirgan, D javob ham tartiblanmagan o‘rtadagi qiymatlardan foydalangan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":110,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"one_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"The 3 months with the least precipitation are removed from the data. Compared with the original 12 values, how do the mean and the median of the remaining 9 values change?\",\"passage\":\"The table shows the average monthly precipitation, in millimeters, in Tashkent, Uzbekistan, based on climate data for 1991–2020.\\n\\n| Month | Precipitation (mm) |\\n| --- | ---: |\\n| January | 55 |\\n| February | 72 |\\n| March | 66 |\\n| April | 63 |\\n| May | 41 |\\n| June | 17 |\\n| July | 3 |\\n| August | 2 |\\n| September | 5 |\\n| October | 24 |\\n| November | 51 |\\n| December | 58 |\",\"option_a\":\"The mean increases, and the median does not change.\",\"option_b\":\"Both increase, and the median increases by more than the mean.\",\"option_c\":\"Both increase, and the mean increases by more than the median.\",\"option_d\":\"The mean increases, and the median decreases.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The original sum is 457 mm, so the mean is $\\\\frac{457}{12} \\\\approx 38.1$ mm. Ordered, the values are 2, 3, 5, 17, 24, 41, 51, 55, 58, 63, 66, 72, so the median is $\\\\frac{41 + 51}{2} = 46$ mm. Removing the 3 least values, 2, 3, and 5 (August, July, and September), leaves 9 values with sum $457 - 10 = 447$, so the new mean is $\\\\frac{447}{9} \\\\approx 49.7$ mm. In order, the remaining values are 17, 24, 41, 51, 55, 58, 63, 66, 72, so the new median is the 5th value, 55 mm. The mean increases by about $49.7 - 38.1 = 11.6$ mm, and the median increases by $55 - 46 = 9$ mm, so the mean increases more. Choice A wrongly assumes that removing values at one end never changes the median; here the middle position shifts to greater values. Choice B reverses the comparison. Choice D is wrong because removing the least values cannot lower the median.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Dastlabki yig‘indi 457 mm, demak o‘rtacha $\\\\frac{457}{12} \\\\approx 38.1$ mm. Tartiblangan qiymatlar: 2, 3, 5, 17, 24, 41, 51, 55, 58, 63, 66, 72, shuning uchun mediana $\\\\frac{41 + 51}{2} = 46$ mm. Eng kichik 3 ta qiymat — 2, 3 va 5 (avgust, iyul va sentabr) olib tashlansa, yig‘indisi $457 - 10 = 447$ bo‘lgan 9 ta qiymat qoladi, yangi o‘rtacha $\\\\frac{447}{9} \\\\approx 49.7$ mm. Qolgan qiymatlar tartibda: 17, 24, 41, 51, 55, 58, 63, 66, 72, demak yangi mediana 5-qiymat, ya’ni 55 mm. O‘rtacha taxminan $49.7 - 38.1 = 11.6$ mm ga, mediana esa $55 - 46 = 9$ mm ga oshadi — o‘rtacha ko‘proq oshadi. A javob bir chetdan qiymatlarni olib tashlash medianani o‘zgartirmaydi deb noto‘g‘ri hisoblagan; aslida o‘rta o‘rin kattaroq qiymatlar tomon siljiydi. B javob taqqoslashni teskari qilgan. D javob noto‘g‘ri, chunki eng kichik qiymatlarni olib tashlash medianani kamaytira olmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":150,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"one_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"The list shows the total number of medals won by athletes from Uzbekistan at each of the 8 Summer Olympics from 1996 to 2024, in order.\\n\\n2, 4, 5, 4, 3, 13, 5, 13\\n\\nSuppose Uzbekistan's athletes win $x$ medals at the 2028 Summer Olympics, and the mean number of medals for all 9 Summer Olympics from 1996 to 2028 is 7. What is the median number of medals for these 9 Summer Olympics?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"5\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The sum of the 8 given totals is 49. For the mean of 9 values to be 7, their sum must be $9 \\\\times 7 = 63$, so $x = 63 - 49 = 14$. Ordered, the 9 values are 2, 3, 4, 4, 5, 5, 13, 13, 14, and the median is the 5th value, 5. Answering 14 gives the value of $x$, not the median. Using the old median of the 8 values, $\\\\frac{4 + 5}{2} = 4.5$, ignores the new value. Enter 5.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Berilgan 8 ta natija yig‘indisi 49. 9 ta qiymatning o‘rtachasi 7 bo‘lishi uchun ularning yig‘indisi $9 \\\\times 7 = 63$ bo‘lishi kerak, demak $x = 63 - 49 = 14$. Tartiblangan 9 ta qiymat: 2, 3, 4, 4, 5, 5, 13, 13, 14; mediana — 5-qiymat, ya’ni 5. 14 deb javob berish $x$ ning qiymatini beradi, medianani emas. 8 ta qiymatning eski medianasi $\\\\frac{4 + 5}{2} = 4.5$ ni olish yangi qiymatni hisobga olmaydi. Javob: 5.\",\"estimated_seconds\":130,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"percentages\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"At the 2024 Summer Olympics in Paris, athletes from Uzbekistan won 13 medals, and 8 of these medals were gold. To the nearest whole percent, what percent of Uzbekistan's medals were gold?\",\"option_a\":\"8%\",\"option_b\":\"38%\",\"option_c\":\"62%\",\"option_d\":\"163%\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Divide the part by the whole: $\\\\frac{8}{13} \\\\approx 0.615$, which is about 62%. Choice B, 38%, is the percent of the medals that were **not** gold ($\\\\frac{5}{13}$). Choice A treats the number of gold medals as a percent. Choice D divides the whole by the part ($\\\\frac{13}{8} = 1.625$).\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qismni butunga bo‘lamiz: $\\\\frac{8}{13} \\\\approx 0.615$, ya’ni taxminan 62%. B javob (38%) — oltin **bo‘lmagan** medallar ulushi ($\\\\frac{5}{13}$). A javob oltin medallar sonini foiz deb olgan. D javob esa butunni qismga bo‘lgan ($\\\\frac{13}{8} = 1.625$).\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"percentages\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"According to NASA, the gravity at the surface of Mars is about 38% of the gravity at the surface of Earth. A robotic rover weighs 9,000 newtons on Earth. Based on this percentage, how many newtons would the rover weigh on Mars?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"3420\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The rover's weight on Mars is 38% of its weight on Earth: $0.38 \\\\times 9{,}000 = 3{,}420$ newtons. Subtracting instead ($9{,}000 - 0.38 \\\\times 9{,}000 = 5{,}580$) gives the amount by which the weight decreases, not the weight on Mars. Enter 3420 (without a comma).\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Marsdagi og‘irlik Yerdagi og‘irlikning 38% iga teng: $0.38 \\\\times 9{,}000 = 3{,}420$ nyuton. Ayirib hisoblash ($9{,}000 - 0.38 \\\\times 9{,}000 = 5{,}580$) og‘irlik qanchaga kamayishini beradi, Marsdagi og‘irlikni emas. Javob maydoniga vergulsiz 3420 deb yozing.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"percentages\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"On January 1, 2025, the permanent population of Uzbekistan was about 37.5 million people, and about 18.4 million of them lived in rural areas. Which of the following is closest to the percent of Uzbekistan's population that lived in rural areas on that date?\",\"option_a\":\"0.49%\",\"option_b\":\"49%\",\"option_c\":\"51%\",\"option_d\":\"204%\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Percent $= \\\\frac{\\\\text{part}}{\\\\text{whole}} \\\\times 100\\\\%$: $\\\\frac{18.4}{37.5} \\\\approx 0.49$, or about 49%. Choice C, 51%, is the percent that lived in **urban** areas ($\\\\frac{37.5 - 18.4}{37.5}$). Choice A is the decimal 0.49 with a percent sign added, which is 100 times too small. Choice D divides the whole population by the rural population.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Foiz $= \\\\frac{\\\\text{qism}}{\\\\text{butun}} \\\\times 100\\\\%$: $\\\\frac{18.4}{37.5} \\\\approx 0.49$, ya’ni taxminan 49%. C javob (51%) — **shaharda** yashovchilar ulushi ($\\\\frac{37.5 - 18.4}{37.5}$). A javob 0.49 o‘nli kasrga shunchaki foiz belgisini qo‘shgan — bu 100 marta kichik. D javob butun aholini qishloq aholisiga bo‘lgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"percentages\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Athletes from Uzbekistan won 5 medals at the 2020 Summer Olympics in Tokyo and 13 medals at the 2024 Summer Olympics in Paris. To the nearest whole percent, the number of medals won in Tokyo was what percent less than the number won in Paris?\",\"option_a\":\"62%\",\"option_b\":\"38%\",\"option_c\":\"260%\",\"option_d\":\"160%\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"“Less than the number won in Paris” means Paris is the base: $\\\\frac{13 - 5}{13} = \\\\frac{8}{13} \\\\approx 0.615$, or about 62%. Choice D, 160%, uses Tokyo as the base ($\\\\frac{8}{5}$); it answers the different question “Paris was what percent greater than Tokyo?” Choice B, 38%, is Tokyo's total as a percent **of** Paris's total ($\\\\frac{5}{13}$). Choice C is Paris's total as a percent of Tokyo's ($\\\\frac{13}{5}$).\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“Parijdagidan necha foiz kam” degani — asos Parij: $\\\\frac{13 - 5}{13} = \\\\frac{8}{13} \\\\approx 0.615$, ya’ni taxminan 62%. D javob (160%) asos sifatida Tokioni olgan ($\\\\frac{8}{5}$) — bu “Parij Tokiodan necha foiz ko‘p?” degan boshqa savolning javobi. B javob (38%) — Tokiodagi medallar Parijdagilarning necha foizini **tashkil etishi** ($\\\\frac{5}{13}$). C javob — Parijdagi medallar Tokiodagilarning necha foizi ekanligi ($\\\\frac{13}{5}$).\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"percentages\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"According to NASA, the diameter of Earth is 12,756 kilometers and the diameter of Mars is 6,792 kilometers. The diameter of Mars is what percent less than the diameter of Earth? (Round your answer to the nearest whole percent.)\",\"spr_answers\":[\"47\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The base of the comparison is Earth's diameter: $\\\\frac{12{,}756 - 6{,}792}{12{,}756} = \\\\frac{5{,}964}{12{,}756} \\\\approx 0.4675$, which rounds to 47%. Dividing the difference by Mars's diameter instead ($\\\\frac{5{,}964}{6{,}792} \\\\approx 0.88$) answers “Earth's diameter is what percent greater than Mars's?” and gives 88. Enter 47.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Taqqoslash asosi — Yerning diametri: $\\\\frac{12{,}756 - 6{,}792}{12{,}756} = \\\\frac{5{,}964}{12{,}756} \\\\approx 0.4675$, yaxlitlansa 47%. Farqni Marsning diametriga bo‘lish ($\\\\frac{5{,}964}{6{,}792} \\\\approx 0.88$) “Yerning diametri Marsnikidan necha foiz katta?” degan savolga javob beradi va 88 ni beradi. Javob: 47.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"percentages\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"On January 1, 2025, the population of Tashkent city was about 3.11 million, which was about 21% greater than the city's population on January 1, 2020. Which of the following is closest to the population of Tashkent city on January 1, 2020?\",\"option_a\":\"2.46 million\",\"option_b\":\"2.57 million\",\"option_c\":\"2.90 million\",\"option_d\":\"3.76 million\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Let $p$ be the 2020 population. A 21% increase multiplies it by 1.21, so $1.21p = 3.11$ and $p = \\\\frac{3.11}{1.21} \\\\approx 2.57$ million. Choice A decreases 3.11 million by 21% ($0.79 \\\\times 3.11 \\\\approx 2.46$), but the 21% was calculated from the 2020 population, not the 2025 population. Choice C subtracts 0.21 million, treating the percent as a number of people. Choice D increases the 2025 population by another 21%. Check: $2.57 \\\\times 1.21 \\\\approx 3.11$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"2020 yilgi aholini $p$ deb olamiz. 21% ga oshish uni 1.21 ga ko‘paytiradi: $1.21p = 3.11$, demak $p = \\\\frac{3.11}{1.21} \\\\approx 2.57$ million. A javob 3.11 millionni 21% ga kamaytirgan ($0.79 \\\\times 3.11 \\\\approx 2.46$), ammo 21% 2025 yilgi emas, 2020 yilgi aholidan hisoblangan. C javob foizni odamlar soni deb olib, 0.21 millionni ayirgan. D javob 2025 yilgi aholini yana 21% ga oshirgan. Tekshiruv: $2.57 \\\\times 1.21 \\\\approx 3.11$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"percentages\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Usain Bolt's world record in the men's 100-meter race is 9.58 seconds. Noah Lyles won the men's 100-meter race at the 2024 Summer Olympics with a time of 9.79 seconds. Bolt's average speed in his world-record race was approximately what percent greater than Lyles's average speed in his Olympic race?\",\"option_a\":\"0.21%\",\"option_b\":\"2.1%\",\"option_c\":\"2.2%\",\"option_d\":\"97.9%\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Both runners covered 100 meters, so each average speed is $\\\\frac{100}{\\\\text{time}}$. The ratio of the speeds is $\\\\frac{100/9.58}{100/9.79} = \\\\frac{9.79}{9.58} \\\\approx 1.0219$, so Bolt's speed was about 2.2% greater. Choice B, 2.1%, is the percent by which Bolt's **time** was less than Lyles's time ($\\\\frac{0.21}{9.79} \\\\approx 0.0215$); speed and time are inversely related, so these percents are not equal. Choice A treats the 0.21-second difference as a percent. Choice D, $\\\\frac{9.58}{9.79} \\\\approx 97.9\\\\%$, compares the times, not the speeds.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkala yuguruvchi ham 100 metr yugurgan, shuning uchun har birining o‘rtacha tezligi $\\\\frac{100}{\\\\text{vaqt}}$. Tezliklar nisbati: $\\\\frac{100/9.58}{100/9.79} = \\\\frac{9.79}{9.58} \\\\approx 1.0219$, demak Boltning tezligi taxminan 2.2% katta. B javob (2.1%) — Boltning **vaqti** Laylsnikidan necha foiz kamligi ($\\\\frac{0.21}{9.79} \\\\approx 0.0215$); tezlik va vaqt teskari proporsional, shuning uchun bu foizlar teng emas. A javob 0.21 soniyalik farqni foiz deb olgan. D javob ($\\\\frac{9.58}{9.79} \\\\approx 97.9\\\\%$) tezliklarni emas, vaqtlarni taqqoslagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"percentages\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"From January 1, 2024, to January 1, 2025, the permanent population of Uzbekistan increased by about 2%. Suppose the population increases by exactly 2% each year for 3 years, starting on January 1, 2025. By what percent will the population increase in total over these 3 years? (Round your answer to the nearest tenth of a percent.)\",\"spr_answers\":[\"6.1\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Each 2% increase multiplies the population by 1.02, so after 3 years the population is multiplied by $1.02^3 = 1.061208$. That is a total increase of about 6.1208%, or 6.1% to the nearest tenth. Adding the percents ($3 \\\\times 2\\\\% = 6\\\\%$) is a common error: each year's 2% is calculated from a larger population than the year before, so the total increase is more than 6%. Enter 6.1.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Har bir 2% lik o‘sish aholini 1.02 ga ko‘paytiradi, demak 3 yildan keyin aholi $1.02^3 = 1.061208$ ga ko‘payadi. Bu jami taxminan 6.1208% o‘sish, o‘ndan birgacha yaxlitlansa 6.1%. Foizlarni qo‘shish ($3 \\\\times 2\\\\% = 6\\\\%$) — keng tarqalgan xato: har yilgi 2% oldingi yildagidan kattaroq aholidan hisoblanadi, shuning uchun jami o‘sish 6% dan ko‘p. Javob: 6.1.\",\"estimated_seconds\":110,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"probability_conditional\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"If one of these 29 medals is selected at random, what is the probability that it was won by an athlete from Kyrgyzstan?\",\"passage\":\"The table shows the numbers of medals won at the 2024 Summer Olympics in Paris by athletes from the Central Asian countries that won at least one medal.\\n\\n| Country | Gold | Silver | Bronze | Total |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Kazakhstan | 1 | 3 | 3 | 7 |\\n| Kyrgyzstan | 0 | 2 | 4 | 6 |\\n| Tajikistan | 0 | 0 | 3 | 3 |\\n| Uzbekistan | 8 | 2 | 3 | 13 |\\n| Total | 9 | 7 | 13 | 29 |\",\"option_a\":\"$\\\\frac{6}{23}$\",\"option_b\":\"$\\\\frac{6}{29}$\",\"option_c\":\"$\\\\frac{2}{29}$\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\frac{1}{4}$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Kyrgyzstan's athletes won $0 + 2 + 4 = 6$ of the 29 medals, so the probability is $\\\\frac{6}{29}$. Choice A divides by the 23 medals **not** won by Kyrgyzstan. Choice C uses only Kyrgyzstan's 2 silver medals. Choice D treats each of the 4 countries as equally likely, but the countries won different numbers of medals.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qirg‘iziston sportchilari 29 ta medaldan $0 + 2 + 4 = 6$ tasini qo‘lga kiritgan, demak ehtimollik $\\\\frac{6}{29}$. A javob Qirg‘iziston **yutmagan** 23 ta medalga bo‘lgan. C javob faqat Qirg‘izistonning 2 ta kumush medalini olgan. D javob 4 ta davlatni teng imkoniyatli deb hisoblagan, holbuki davlatlar turli sondagi medal yutgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"probability_conditional\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"If one of these 29 medals is selected at random, what is the probability that it is a bronze medal?\",\"passage\":\"The table shows the numbers of medals won at the 2024 Summer Olympics in Paris by athletes from the Central Asian countries that won at least one medal.\\n\\n| Country | Gold | Silver | Bronze | Total |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Kazakhstan | 1 | 3 | 3 | 7 |\\n| Kyrgyzstan | 0 | 2 | 4 | 6 |\\n| Tajikistan | 0 | 0 | 3 | 3 |\\n| Uzbekistan | 8 | 2 | 3 | 13 |\\n| Total | 9 | 7 | 13 | 29 |\",\"spr_answers\":[\"13/29\",\".4482\",\".4483\",\"0.448\"],\"explanation_en\":\"There are 13 bronze medals among the 29 medals, so the probability is $\\\\frac{13}{29} \\\\approx 0.4483$. Using only one country's bronze medals, or dividing by the number of gold and silver medals ($\\\\frac{13}{16}$), gives a wrong denominator. Enter 13/29, .4482, .4483, or 0.448.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"29 ta medal orasida 13 tasi bronza, demak ehtimollik $\\\\frac{13}{29} \\\\approx 0.4483$. Faqat bitta davlatning bronza medallarini olish yoki oltin va kumush medallar soniga bo‘lish ($\\\\frac{13}{16}$) noto‘g‘ri natija beradi. Javob maydoniga 13/29, .4482, .4483 yoki 0.448 deb yozing.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"probability_conditional\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Based on climate data for 1991–2020, Tashkent, Uzbekistan, has an average of 59 days with rain in a 365-day year. Based on this average, what is the probability that a day selected at random from such a year is a day with rain?\",\"option_a\":\"$\\\\frac{1}{59}$\",\"option_b\":\"$\\\\frac{59}{306}$\",\"option_c\":\"$\\\\frac{306}{365}$\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\frac{59}{365}$\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Probability $= \\\\frac{\\\\text{favorable outcomes}}{\\\\text{total outcomes}} = \\\\frac{59}{365}$. Choice C, $\\\\frac{306}{365}$, is the probability of a day **without** rain ($365 - 59 = 306$). Choice B compares rainy days with dry days instead of with all days. Choice A treats the 59 rainy days as a single outcome.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ehtimollik $= \\\\frac{\\\\text{qulay natijalar}}{\\\\text{barcha natijalar}} = \\\\frac{59}{365}$. C javob ($\\\\frac{306}{365}$) — yomg‘ir **yog‘magan** kunning ehtimolligi ($365 - 59 = 306$). B javob yomg‘irli kunlarni barcha kunlar bilan emas, quruq kunlar bilan taqqoslagan. A javob 59 ta yomg‘irli kunni bitta natija deb olgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"probability_conditional\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"If one of the gold medals in the table is selected at random, what is the probability that it was won by an athlete from Uzbekistan?\",\"passage\":\"The table shows the numbers of medals won at the 2024 Summer Olympics in Paris by athletes from the Central Asian countries that won at least one medal.\\n\\n| Country | Gold | Silver | Bronze | Total |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Kazakhstan | 1 | 3 | 3 | 7 |\\n| Kyrgyzstan | 0 | 2 | 4 | 6 |\\n| Tajikistan | 0 | 0 | 3 | 3 |\\n| Uzbekistan | 8 | 2 | 3 | 13 |\\n| Total | 9 | 7 | 13 | 29 |\",\"option_a\":\"$\\\\frac{8}{9}$\",\"option_b\":\"$\\\\frac{8}{13}$\",\"option_c\":\"$\\\\frac{8}{29}$\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\frac{9}{29}$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The selection is made only from the gold medals, so the denominator is the 9 gold medals. Of these, 8 were won by Uzbekistan, so the probability is $\\\\frac{8}{9}$. Choice B reverses the condition: $\\\\frac{8}{13}$ is the probability that one of **Uzbekistan's** medals is gold. Choice C divides by all 29 medals, and choice D is the probability that a randomly selected medal from the table is gold.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Tanlov faqat oltin medallar orasidan amalga oshiriladi, shuning uchun maxraj — 9 ta oltin medal. Ulardan 8 tasini O‘zbekiston yutgan, demak ehtimollik $\\\\frac{8}{9}$. B javobda shart teskari olingan: $\\\\frac{8}{13}$ — **O‘zbekiston** medallaridan biri oltin bo‘lish ehtimolligi. C javob barcha 29 ta medalga bo‘lgan, D javob esa jadvaldagi tasodifiy medalning oltin bo‘lish ehtimolligi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"probability_conditional\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"One of the medals won by athletes from Kazakhstan or Kyrgyzstan is selected at random. What is the probability that the selected medal is a silver medal?\",\"passage\":\"The table shows the numbers of medals won at the 2024 Summer Olympics in Paris by athletes from the Central Asian countries that won at least one medal.\\n\\n| Country | Gold | Silver | Bronze | Total |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Kazakhstan | 1 | 3 | 3 | 7 |\\n| Kyrgyzstan | 0 | 2 | 4 | 6 |\\n| Tajikistan | 0 | 0 | 3 | 3 |\\n| Uzbekistan | 8 | 2 | 3 | 13 |\\n| Total | 9 | 7 | 13 | 29 |\",\"spr_answers\":[\"5/13\",\".3846\",\".3847\",\"0.384\",\"0.385\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Kazakhstan and Kyrgyzstan won $7 + 6 = 13$ medals together, and $3 + 2 = 5$ of them are silver. The probability is $\\\\frac{5}{13} \\\\approx 0.3846$. Dividing by all 7 silver medals ($\\\\frac{5}{7}$) reverses the condition, and dividing by all 29 medals ($\\\\frac{5}{29}$) ignores it. Enter 5/13, .3846, .3847, 0.384, or 0.385.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qozog‘iston va Qirg‘iziston birgalikda $7 + 6 = 13$ ta medal yutgan, ulardan $3 + 2 = 5$ tasi kumush. Ehtimollik $\\\\frac{5}{13} \\\\approx 0.3846$. Barcha 7 ta kumush medalga bo‘lish ($\\\\frac{5}{7}$) shartni teskari qiladi, barcha 29 ta medalga bo‘lish ($\\\\frac{5}{29}$) esa shartni e’tiborsiz qoldiradi. Javob maydoniga 5/13, .3846, .3847, 0.384 yoki 0.385 deb yozing.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"probability_conditional\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"The table shows the diameter and the mean temperature of each of the 8 planets, according to NASA. If one of the planets with a mean temperature below 0°C is selected at random, what is the probability that its diameter is greater than Earth's diameter?\",\"passage\":\"| Planet | Diameter (km) | Mean temperature (°C) |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Mercury | 4,879 | 167 |\\n| Venus | 12,104 | 464 |\\n| Earth | 12,756 | 15 |\\n| Mars | 6,792 | -65 |\\n| Jupiter | 142,984 | -110 |\\n| Saturn | 120,536 | -140 |\\n| Uranus | 51,118 | -195 |\\n| Neptune | 49,528 | -200 |\",\"option_a\":\"$\\\\frac{1}{2}$\",\"option_b\":\"$\\\\frac{5}{8}$\",\"option_c\":\"$\\\\frac{4}{5}$\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\frac{1}{5}$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Five planets have a mean temperature below 0°C: Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, and Neptune. Of these, all but Mars have a diameter greater than 12,756 km, so 4 of the 5 qualify and the probability is $\\\\frac{4}{5}$. Choice A, $\\\\frac{4}{8}$, divides by all 8 planets, ignoring the condition. Choice B is the probability that a randomly selected planet has a mean temperature below 0°C. Choice D is the probability of selecting Mars, the cold planet that is smaller than Earth.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Beshta sayyoraning o‘rtacha harorati 0°C dan past: Mars, Yupiter, Saturn, Uran va Neptun. Ulardan Marsdan boshqa hammasining diametri 12,756 km dan katta, demak 5 tadan 4 tasi mos va ehtimollik $\\\\frac{4}{5}$. A javob ($\\\\frac{4}{8}$) shartni e’tiborsiz qoldirib, barcha 8 ta sayyoraga bo‘lgan. B javob — tasodifiy sayyoraning o‘rtacha harorati 0°C dan past bo‘lish ehtimolligi. D javob — Yerdan kichik bo‘lgan sovuq sayyora, ya’ni Marsni tanlash ehtimolligi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"probability_conditional\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"At a large school, 60% of the students study in classes taught in Uzbek, and the other 40% study in classes taught in Russian. Of the students in Uzbek-language classes, 25% are members of the robotics club. Of the students in Russian-language classes, 35% are members of the robotics club. If a member of the robotics club is selected at random, what is the probability that the student studies in a Russian-language class?\",\"option_a\":\"$\\\\frac{7}{50}$\",\"option_b\":\"$\\\\frac{7}{20}$\",\"option_c\":\"$\\\\frac{2}{5}$\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\frac{14}{29}$\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Imagine 1,000 students. Then 600 are in Uzbek-language classes, and $0.25 \\\\times 600 = 150$ of them are in the club; 400 are in Russian-language classes, and $0.35 \\\\times 400 = 140$ of them are in the club. The club has $150 + 140 = 290$ members, so the probability is $\\\\frac{140}{290} = \\\\frac{14}{29}$. Choice B, $\\\\frac{7}{20} = 35\\\\%$, reverses the condition: it is the probability that a student in a Russian-language class is in the club. Choice C is the fraction of **all** students in Russian-language classes. Choice A, $\\\\frac{140}{1{,}000}$, divides by all students instead of only club members.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"1,000 ta o‘quvchini tasavvur qilamiz. Ulardan 600 tasi o‘zbek tilidagi sinflarda, ulardan $0.25 \\\\times 600 = 150$ tasi to‘garakda; 400 tasi rus tilidagi sinflarda, ulardan $0.35 \\\\times 400 = 140$ tasi to‘garakda. To‘garakda $150 + 140 = 290$ a’zo bor, demak ehtimollik $\\\\frac{140}{290} = \\\\frac{14}{29}$. B javob ($\\\\frac{7}{20} = 35\\\\%$) shartni teskari olgan: bu rus tilidagi sinf o‘quvchisining to‘garakda bo‘lish ehtimolligi. C javob — rus tilidagi sinflardagi o‘quvchilarning **barcha** o‘quvchilar ichidagi ulushi. A javob ($\\\\frac{140}{1{,}000}$) faqat to‘garak a’zolariga emas, barcha o‘quvchilarga bo‘lgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":130,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"probability_conditional\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"At the 2024 Summer Olympics in Paris, athletes from Uzbekistan won 13 medals: 8 gold, 2 silver, and 3 bronze. For a museum display, 2 of these 13 medals will be selected at random, one after the other, without replacement. What is the probability that both selected medals are gold?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"14/39\",\".3589\",\".3590\",\"0.358\",\"0.359\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The probability that the first medal is gold is $\\\\frac{8}{13}$. After a gold medal is removed, 7 of the remaining 12 medals are gold, so the probability that the second is also gold is $\\\\frac{7}{12}$. Multiply: $\\\\frac{8}{13} \\\\times \\\\frac{7}{12} = \\\\frac{56}{156} = \\\\frac{14}{39} \\\\approx 0.3590$. A common error is to use $\\\\frac{8}{13}$ twice, $\\\\left(\\\\frac{8}{13}\\\\right)^2 = \\\\frac{64}{169}$, which treats the selections as if the first medal were put back. Enter 14/39, .3589, .3590, 0.358, or 0.359.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi medalning oltin bo‘lish ehtimolligi $\\\\frac{8}{13}$. Bitta oltin medal olingach, qolgan 12 ta medaldan 7 tasi oltin, demak ikkinchisining ham oltin bo‘lish ehtimolligi $\\\\frac{7}{12}$. Ko‘paytiramiz: $\\\\frac{8}{13} \\\\times \\\\frac{7}{12} = \\\\frac{56}{156} = \\\\frac{14}{39} \\\\approx 0.3590$. Ko‘p uchraydigan xato — $\\\\frac{8}{13}$ ni ikki marta olish: $\\\\left(\\\\frac{8}{13}\\\\right)^2 = \\\\frac{64}{169}$; bu birinchi medal qaytarib qo‘yilgandek hisoblaydi. Javob maydoniga 14/39, .3589, .3590, 0.358 yoki 0.359 deb yozing.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"ratios_rates_proportions_units\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"On January 1, 2025, the population of Uzbekistan was about 37.5 million people, and the population density was 83.6 people per square kilometer. Based on these values, which of the following is closest to the area of Uzbekistan, in square kilometers?\",\"option_a\":\"4,490\",\"option_b\":\"44,900\",\"option_c\":\"449,000\",\"option_d\":\"3,140,000,000\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Density is people per square kilometer, so area $= \\\\frac{\\\\text{people}}{\\\\text{density}} = \\\\frac{37{,}500{,}000}{83.6} \\\\approx 448{,}600$, which is closest to 449,000 square kilometers. Choices A and B have the right digits but are off by a factor of 100 and 10, which usually comes from writing 37.5 million with too few zeros. Choice D multiplies the population by the density instead of dividing; its unit would be people² per square kilometer, not square kilometers.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Zichlik — bir kvadrat kilometrga to‘g‘ri keladigan odamlar soni, shuning uchun maydon $= \\\\frac{\\\\text{odamlar}}{\\\\text{zichlik}} = \\\\frac{37{,}500{,}000}{83.6} \\\\approx 448{,}600$, bu 449,000 kvadrat kilometrga eng yaqin. A va B javoblarda raqamlar to‘g‘ri, lekin qiymat 100 va 10 marta kichik — odatda 37.5 millionni kam nol bilan yozishdan kelib chiqadi. D javob aholini zichlikka bo‘lish o‘rniga ko‘paytirgan; uning birligi kvadrat kilometr emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"ratios_rates_proportions_units\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"Usain Bolt set the world record in the men's 100-meter race with a time of 9.58 seconds. What was his average speed during this race, in meters per second? (Round your answer to the nearest tenth.)\",\"spr_answers\":[\"10.4\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Average speed $= \\\\frac{\\\\text{distance}}{\\\\text{time}} = \\\\frac{100 \\\\text{ m}}{9.58 \\\\text{ s}} \\\\approx 10.44$ meters per second, which rounds to 10.4. Dividing the time by the distance ($\\\\frac{9.58}{100} \\\\approx 0.096$) gives seconds per meter, not meters per second. Enter 10.4.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"O‘rtacha tezlik $= \\\\frac{\\\\text{masofa}}{\\\\text{vaqt}} = \\\\frac{100 \\\\text{ m}}{9.58 \\\\text{ s}} \\\\approx 10.44$ metr/soniya, o‘ndan birgacha yaxlitlansa 10.4. Vaqtni masofaga bo‘lish ($\\\\frac{9.58}{100} \\\\approx 0.096$) metr/soniyani emas, soniya/metrni beradi. Javob: 10.4.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"ratios_rates_proportions_units\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"In 2024, the birth rate in Uzbekistan was 24.9 births per 1,000 people. Tashkent city had a population of about 3.11 million people. If the birth rate in Tashkent city were the same as the national rate, which of the following is closest to the number of births in Tashkent city in 2024?\",\"option_a\":\"7,700\",\"option_b\":\"77,000\",\"option_c\":\"125,000\",\"option_d\":\"774,000\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Set up a proportion: $\\\\frac{24.9}{1{,}000} = \\\\frac{x}{3{,}110{,}000}$, so $x = 24.9 \\\\times 3{,}110 \\\\approx 77{,}400$, which is closest to 77,000. Choice C divides the population by 24.9, ignoring the “per 1,000.” Choices A and D are off by a factor of 10, which comes from using “per 10,000” or “per 100” instead of “per 1,000.”\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Proporsiya tuzamiz: $\\\\frac{24.9}{1{,}000} = \\\\frac{x}{3{,}110{,}000}$, demak $x = 24.9 \\\\times 3{,}110 \\\\approx 77{,}400$, bu 77,000 ga eng yaqin. C javob “har 1,000 kishiga” ekanini e’tiborsiz qoldirib, aholini 24.9 ga bo‘lgan. A va D javoblar 10 marta xato — “har 1,000 kishiga” o‘rniga “har 10,000” yoki “har 100 kishiga” deb olingan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"ratios_rates_proportions_units\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"The average distance from Earth to the Sun is 149.6 million kilometers, and light travels at about 299,792 kilometers per second. Which of the following is closest to the time, in minutes, that it takes light to travel from the Sun to Earth?\",\"option_a\":\"0.14 minute\",\"option_b\":\"0.5 minute\",\"option_c\":\"5.0 minutes\",\"option_d\":\"8.3 minutes\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Time $= \\\\frac{\\\\text{distance}}{\\\\text{speed}} = \\\\frac{149{,}600{,}000 \\\\text{ km}}{299{,}792 \\\\text{ km/s}} \\\\approx 499$ seconds. Since 1 minute = 60 seconds, $\\\\frac{499}{60} \\\\approx 8.3$ minutes. Choice A divides 499 by 3,600, which converts seconds to **hours**, not minutes. Choice B divides 149.6 by 299.792, mixing millions with thousands. Choice C divides 499 by 100, as if a minute had 100 seconds.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Vaqt $= \\\\frac{\\\\text{masofa}}{\\\\text{tezlik}} = \\\\frac{149{,}600{,}000 \\\\text{ km}}{299{,}792 \\\\text{ km/s}} \\\\approx 499$ soniya. 1 daqiqa = 60 soniya bo‘lgani uchun $\\\\frac{499}{60} \\\\approx 8.3$ daqiqa. A javob 499 ni 3,600 ga bo‘lgan — bu soniyani daqiqaga emas, **soatga** aylantiradi. B javob 149.6 ni 299.792 ga bo‘lgan, ya’ni millionlar bilan minglarni aralashtirgan. C javob 499 ni 100 ga bo‘lgan, go‘yo bir daqiqada 100 soniya bordek.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"ratios_rates_proportions_units\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"The men's marathon world record of 2 hours, 0 minutes, 35 seconds was set by Kelvin Kiptum in 2023. A marathon is 42.195 kilometers long. What was Kiptum's average speed during this race, in kilometers per hour? (Round your answer to the nearest whole number.)\",\"spr_answers\":[\"21\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Convert the time to hours: 35 seconds is $\\\\frac{35}{3{,}600} \\\\approx 0.0097$ hour, so the time is about 2.0097 hours (the 0 minutes add nothing). Then speed $= \\\\frac{42.195}{2.0097} \\\\approx 20.995$ kilometers per hour, which rounds to 21. Writing the time as 2.35 hours (reading “35 seconds” as a decimal part of an hour) gives about 18 km/h, which is wrong. Enter 21.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Vaqtni soatga aylantiramiz: 35 soniya $\\\\frac{35}{3{,}600} \\\\approx 0.0097$ soat, demak vaqt taxminan 2.0097 soat (0 daqiqa hech narsa qo‘shmaydi). Tezlik $= \\\\frac{42.195}{2.0097} \\\\approx 20.995$ km/soat, butun songacha yaxlitlansa 21. Vaqtni 2.35 soat deb yozish (“35 soniya”ni soatning o‘nli qismi deb o‘qish) taxminan 18 km/soat beradi — bu xato. Javob: 21.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"ratios_rates_proportions_units\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"On January 1, 2025, the population density of Uzbekistan was 83.6 people per square kilometer. A hectare is a unit of area equal to 10,000 square meters. What was the population density of Uzbekistan on that date, in people per hectare? (1 kilometer = 1,000 meters)\",\"option_a\":\"0.836\",\"option_b\":\"8.36\",\"option_c\":\"836\",\"option_d\":\"8,360\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"A square kilometer is 1,000 m by 1,000 m, so $1 \\\\text{ km}^2 = 1{,}000{,}000 \\\\text{ m}^2 = \\\\frac{1{,}000{,}000}{10{,}000} = 100$ hectares. The 83.6 people in 1 km² are spread over 100 hectares: $\\\\frac{83.6}{100} = 0.836$ person per hectare. Choice D multiplies by 100 instead of dividing; a hectare is smaller than a square kilometer, so it must hold fewer people. Choice B uses $1 \\\\text{ km}^2 = 10$ hectares, and choice C multiplies by 10.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Bir kvadrat kilometr — 1,000 m ga 1,000 m, demak $1 \\\\text{ km}^2 = 1{,}000{,}000 \\\\text{ m}^2 = \\\\frac{1{,}000{,}000}{10{,}000} = 100$ gektar. 1 km² dagi 83.6 kishi 100 gektarga taqsimlanadi: $\\\\frac{83.6}{100} = 0.836$ kishi/gektar. D javob 100 ga bo‘lish o‘rniga ko‘paytirgan; gektar kvadrat kilometrdan kichik, shuning uchun unga kamroq odam to‘g‘ri kelishi kerak. B javob $1 \\\\text{ km}^2 = 10$ gektar deb olgan, C javob esa 10 ga ko‘paytirgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"ratios_rates_proportions_units\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"According to NASA, Proxima Centauri, the star nearest to the Sun, is about 4.24 light-years away. A light-year is the distance light travels in one year. Light travels about $3.00 \\\\times 10^5$ kilometers per second. Using 1 year = 365.25 days, which of the following is closest to the distance to Proxima Centauri, in kilometers?\",\"option_a\":\"$1.27 \\\\times 10^6$\",\"option_b\":\"$4.58 \\\\times 10^9$\",\"option_c\":\"$4.01 \\\\times 10^{13}$\",\"option_d\":\"$4.01 \\\\times 10^{16}$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Seconds in one year: $365.25 \\\\times 24 \\\\times 60 \\\\times 60 = 31{,}557{,}600 \\\\approx 3.156 \\\\times 10^7$. One light-year is $3.00 \\\\times 10^5 \\\\times 3.156 \\\\times 10^7 \\\\approx 9.47 \\\\times 10^{12}$ km, so 4.24 light-years is $4.24 \\\\times 9.47 \\\\times 10^{12} \\\\approx 4.01 \\\\times 10^{13}$ km. Choice A multiplies only 4.24 by the speed, as if light traveled for 4.24 seconds. Choice B multiplies by 3,600, the number of seconds in one **hour**. Choice D is the distance in meters, not kilometers.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Bir yildagi soniyalar: $365.25 \\\\times 24 \\\\times 60 \\\\times 60 = 31{,}557{,}600 \\\\approx 3.156 \\\\times 10^7$. Bir yorug‘lik yili $3.00 \\\\times 10^5 \\\\times 3.156 \\\\times 10^7 \\\\approx 9.47 \\\\times 10^{12}$ km, demak 4.24 yorug‘lik yili $4.24 \\\\times 9.47 \\\\times 10^{12} \\\\approx 4.01 \\\\times 10^{13}$ km. A javob 4.24 ni faqat tezlikka ko‘paytirgan — go‘yo yorug‘lik 4.24 soniya yurgandek. B javob bir **soatdagi** soniyalar soniga (3,600) ko‘paytirgan. D javob — kilometrda emas, metrdagi masofa.\",\"estimated_seconds\":130,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"ratios_rates_proportions_units\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"According to NASA, Earth's diameter at the equator is 12,756 kilometers. Assume that Earth rotates once every 24 hours and that the equator is a circle. A point on the equator travels once around this circle each rotation. What is the speed of this point, in **meters per second**? (Round your answer to the nearest whole number.)\",\"spr_answers\":[\"464\"],\"explanation_en\":\"In one rotation the point travels the circumference, $\\\\pi d = \\\\pi \\\\times 12{,}756 \\\\approx 40{,}074$ km, which is $40{,}074{,}000$ m. The time is $24 \\\\times 60 \\\\times 60 = 86{,}400$ seconds. Speed $= \\\\frac{40{,}074{,}000}{86{,}400} \\\\approx 463.8$ m/s, which rounds to 464. (Using 3.14 for $\\\\pi$ also gives 464.) Common errors: using the diameter instead of the circumference (about 148), or stopping at km/h ($\\\\frac{40{,}074}{24} \\\\approx 1{,}670$). Enter 464.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Bir aylanishda nuqta aylana uzunligini bosib o‘tadi: $\\\\pi d = \\\\pi \\\\times 12{,}756 \\\\approx 40{,}074$ km, ya’ni $40{,}074{,}000$ m. Vaqt: $24 \\\\times 60 \\\\times 60 = 86{,}400$ soniya. Tezlik $= \\\\frac{40{,}074{,}000}{86{,}400} \\\\approx 463.8$ m/s, butun songacha yaxlitlansa 464. ($\\\\pi$ o‘rniga 3.14 olinsa ham 464 chiqadi.) Ko‘p uchraydigan xatolar: aylana uzunligi o‘rniga diametrni olish (taxminan 148) yoki km/soatda to‘xtab qolish ($\\\\frac{40{,}074}{24} \\\\approx 1{,}670$). Javob: 464.\",\"estimated_seconds\":130,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Muhammad ibn Musa al-Khwarizmi was a mathematician and astronomer born around 780.\\n- His name links him to Khwarazm, a region along the lower Amu Darya in present-day Uzbekistan and Turkmenistan.\\n- He worked at the House of Wisdom, a center of learning in Baghdad.\\n- One of his books has the Arabic word al-jabr in its title; the English word “algebra” comes from al-jabr.\\n- In a Latin translation of another of his books, his name was written “Algoritmi”; the English word “algorithm” comes from this Latin form of his name.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to explain the origin of the word “algorithm”. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"The English word “algebra” comes from al-jabr, an Arabic word in the title of one of al-Khwarizmi's books.\",\"option_b\":\"Al-Khwarizmi's name links him to Khwarazm, a region in present-day Uzbekistan and Turkmenistan.\",\"option_c\":\"The word “algorithm” comes from “Algoritmi,” the Latin form of al-Khwarizmi's name used in a translation of one of his books.\",\"option_d\":\"Al-Khwarizmi, a mathematician born around 780, worked at the House of Wisdom in Baghdad.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to explain where the word “algorithm” comes from. Choice C does this, using the note that the word comes from “Algoritmi,” the Latin form of al-Khwarizmi's name in a translation of his book.\\n\\nChoice A explains the origin of a different word, “algebra.” Choice B explains where al-Khwarizmi's own name comes from, not the word “algorithm.” Choice D gives facts about his life and work but does not mention the word at all.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — “algorithm” so‘zi qayerdan kelib chiqqanini tushuntirish. C buni bajaradi: u so‘z al-Xorazmiy kitobining tarjimasida ishlatilgan ismining lotincha shakli “Algoritmi” dan kelib chiqqani haqidagi eslatmadan foydalanadi.\\n\\nA boshqa so‘z — “algebra” ning kelib chiqishini tushuntiradi. B “algorithm” so‘zini emas, al-Xorazmiyning o‘z ismi qayerdan kelganini tushuntiradi. D uning hayoti va faoliyati haqida ma’lumot beradi, lekin bu so‘zni umuman tilga olmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- The Rosetta Stone is a stone slab carved with a decree issued in Egypt in 196 BCE.\\n- French soldiers found it in 1799 near the town of Rosetta (Rashid) in northern Egypt.\\n- The same decree is written on the stone in three scripts: Egyptian hieroglyphs, Demotic, and ancient Greek.\\n- Scholars could already read ancient Greek, but no one could read hieroglyphs.\\n- By comparing the texts, Jean-François Champollion was able to announce in 1822 that he had deciphered hieroglyphs.\\n- The stone has been displayed at the British Museum in London since 1802.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to explain why the Rosetta Stone was useful for deciphering hieroglyphs. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Because the stone repeats the same decree in hieroglyphs and in ancient Greek, a language scholars could already read, they could compare the texts to work out what the hieroglyphs meant.\",\"option_b\":\"The Rosetta Stone, which French soldiers found in 1799, has been displayed at the British Museum since 1802.\",\"option_c\":\"The Rosetta Stone is carved with a decree that was issued in Egypt in 196 BCE.\",\"option_d\":\"In 1822, Jean-François Champollion announced that he had deciphered Egyptian hieroglyphs.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to explain why the stone helped scholars decipher hieroglyphs. Choice A gives the reason: the stone contains the same text in hieroglyphs and in ancient Greek, which scholars could already read, so the two versions could be compared.\\n\\nChoice B tells when the stone was found and where it is displayed, not why it was useful. Choice C tells what the inscription is and when it was issued. Choice D states that hieroglyphs were deciphered but does not explain how the stone made this possible.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — tosh nima uchun iyerogliflarni o‘qishga yordam berganini tushuntirish. A sababini aytadi: toshda bir xil matn iyerogliflarda ham, olimlar allaqachon o‘qiy oladigan qadimgi yunon tilida ham yozilgan, shuning uchun ikki variantni solishtirish mumkin bo‘lgan.\\n\\nB tosh qachon topilgani va qayerda saqlanayotganini aytadi, nima uchun foydali bo‘lganini emas. C yozuv nima ekanini va qachon chiqarilganini aytadi. D iyerogliflar o‘qilganini ta’kidlaydi, lekin tosh buni qanday imkon berganini tushuntirmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Shahrisabz is a city in southern Uzbekistan.\\n- Its name means “green city” in Persian.\\n- In earlier centuries, the city was called Kesh.\\n- Timur, the founder of the Timurid Empire, was born near Kesh in the fourteenth century.\\n- In 1380, Timur began building a grand palace in the city called Ak-Saray (“White Palace”).\\n- In 2000, the historic center of Shahrisabz was added to UNESCO's World Heritage List.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to emphasize the city's connection to Timur. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Shahrisabz, a city in southern Uzbekistan, has a name that means “green city” in Persian.\",\"option_b\":\"In 2000, the historic center of Shahrisabz was added to UNESCO's World Heritage List.\",\"option_c\":\"Shahrisabz, a city in southern Uzbekistan, was once called Kesh.\",\"option_d\":\"Timur, the founder of the Timurid Empire, was born near Shahrisabz (then called Kesh) and later began building his Ak-Saray palace there in 1380.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to emphasize the city's connection to Timur. Choice D does this by stating that Timur was born near the city and later began building his Ak-Saray palace there.\\n\\nChoices A, B, and C each give a true fact about Shahrisabz—the meaning of its name, its UNESCO listing, and its former name—but none of them mentions Timur.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — shaharning Temur bilan bog‘liqligini ta’kidlash. D buni bajaradi: Temur shu shahar yaqinida tug‘ilgani va keyinchalik u yerda Oqsaroy saroyini qura boshlagani aytilgan.\\n\\nA, B va C Shahrisabz haqida to‘g‘ri ma’lumot beradi — nomining ma’nosi, UNESCO ro‘yxatiga kiritilgani va avvalgi nomi — lekin ularning hech birida Temur tilga olinmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- The Tashkent Metro is the subway system of Tashkent, the capital of Uzbekistan.\\n- Its first line opened on November 6, 1977, with nine stations.\\n- It was the first metro system in Central Asia.\\n- In 1966, a powerful earthquake struck Tashkent.\\n- Planning for the metro began in 1968, and its tunnels were designed to withstand strong earthquakes.\\n- Each station has its own decorative theme, with interiors made from materials such as marble, granite, and ceramics.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to emphasize that the Tashkent Metro was built with earthquakes in mind. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"The Tashkent Metro, which opened in 1977 with nine stations, was the first metro system in Central Asia.\",\"option_b\":\"Planned soon after a powerful earthquake struck the city in 1966, the Tashkent Metro has tunnels that were designed to withstand strong earthquakes.\",\"option_c\":\"Each station of the Tashkent Metro has its own decorative theme, with interiors made from marble, granite, and ceramics.\",\"option_d\":\"In 1966, a powerful earthquake struck Tashkent, two years before planning for the city's metro began.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to emphasize that the metro was built with earthquakes in mind. Choice B does this by stating that the metro's tunnels were designed to withstand strong earthquakes and by linking the metro's planning to the 1966 earthquake.\\n\\nChoice A emphasizes that the metro was the first in Central Asia. Choice C describes the stations' decoration. Choice D mentions the earthquake, but it says only that the earthquake came before planning began; it does not say that the metro was designed to withstand earthquakes.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — metro zilzilalarni hisobga olib qurilganini ta’kidlash. B buni bajaradi: unda metro tunnellari kuchli zilzilalarga bardosh beradigan qilib loyihalangani aytilgan va metroni rejalashtirish 1966-yilgi zilzila bilan bog‘langan.\\n\\nA metro Markaziy Osiyodagi birinchi metro ekanini ta’kidlaydi. C bekatlarning bezagini tasvirlaydi. D zilzilani eslatadi, lekin faqat u rejalashtirishdan oldin bo‘lganini aytadi; metro zilzilaga bardoshli qilib loyihalangani haqida hech narsa demaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":65,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- The Caspian Sea is bordered by Kazakhstan, Turkmenistan, Iran, Azerbaijan, and Russia.\\n- It is the largest inland body of water on Earth by surface area, covering about 371,000 square kilometers.\\n- Its water is slightly salty.\\n- At its deepest point, the Caspian Sea is about 1,025 meters deep.\\n- Lake Baikal, in southern Siberia, Russia, is the deepest lake in the world, more than 1,600 meters deep at its deepest point.\\n- Baikal is a freshwater lake that holds about 20 percent of Earth's fresh surface water.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to contrast the records held by the Caspian Sea and Lake Baikal. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"The Caspian Sea covers about 371,000 square kilometers and is about 1,025 meters deep at its deepest point.\",\"option_b\":\"Lake Baikal, which is more than 1,600 meters deep, holds about 20 percent of Earth's fresh surface water.\",\"option_c\":\"The Caspian Sea and Lake Baikal are both inland bodies of water, and both are more than 1,000 meters deep at their deepest points.\",\"option_d\":\"While the Caspian Sea is the largest inland body of water on Earth by surface area, Lake Baikal is the deepest lake in the world.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to contrast the records the two bodies of water hold. Choice D names both records—the Caspian Sea is the largest inland body of water by surface area, and Lake Baikal is the deepest lake—and uses “While” to set them against each other.\\n\\nChoice A describes only the Caspian Sea, and Choice B describes only Lake Baikal, so neither makes a contrast. Choice C points out similarities between the two rather than a contrast, and it mentions neither record.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — ikki suv havzasiga tegishli rekordlarni bir-biriga qarama-qarshi qo‘yish. D ikkala rekordni ham aytadi — Kaspiy dengizi maydoni bo‘yicha eng katta ichki suv havzasi, Baykal esa eng chuqur ko‘l — va “While” orqali ularni bir-biriga qarshi qo‘yadi.\\n\\nA faqat Kaspiy dengizini, B esa faqat Baykal ko‘lini tasvirlaydi, shuning uchun ularda qarama-qarshilik yo‘q. C farqni emas, o‘xshashliklarni ko‘rsatadi va rekordlarning hech birini tilga olmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":65,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- In 1928, Scottish scientist Alexander Fleming noticed that mold had contaminated a dish of bacteria in his laboratory.\\n- The bacteria near the mold had been killed.\\n- Fleming called the bacteria-killing substance produced by the mold penicillin.\\n- Fleming lacked the chemistry expertise to turn penicillin into a stable, usable drug.\\n- In 1940, Oxford scientists Howard Florey and Ernst Chain made the chemical advances needed to develop penicillin into a medicine.\\n- In 1945, Fleming, Florey, and Chain shared the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to emphasize that turning Fleming's discovery into a medicine depended on the work of other scientists. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Although Fleming discovered penicillin in 1928, he could not turn it into a usable drug; that step depended on Howard Florey and Ernst Chain, who made the necessary chemical advances in 1940.\",\"option_b\":\"In 1945, Alexander Fleming, Howard Florey, and Ernst Chain shared the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine.\",\"option_c\":\"In 1928, Fleming noticed that bacteria near a patch of mold in his laboratory had been killed.\",\"option_d\":\"Fleming called the bacteria-killing substance produced by the mold he found in 1928 penicillin.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to emphasize that making penicillin into a medicine depended on scientists other than Fleming. Choice A does this: it states that Fleming could not turn penicillin into a usable drug and that Florey and Chain made the advances that did.\\n\\nChoice B shows that the three scientists shared a prize, but it does not explain what Florey and Chain contributed or that the medicine depended on their work. Choices C and D describe only Fleming's own discovery.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — penitsillinni dori holiga keltirish Flemingdan boshqa olimlarning ishiga bog‘liq bo‘lganini ta’kidlash. A buni bajaradi: Fleming penitsillinni foydalanish mumkin bo‘lgan dori qila olmagani, buni Flori va Cheyn amalga oshirgani aytilgan.\\n\\nB uch olim mukofotni bo‘lishganini ko‘rsatadi, lekin Flori va Cheyn nima qilganini yoki dori ularning ishiga bog‘liq bo‘lganini tushuntirmaydi. C va D faqat Flemingning o‘z kashfiyotini tasvirlaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Biologists Peter and Rosemary Grant studied medium ground finches on Daphne Major, a small island in the Galápagos.\\n- In 1977, a severe drought struck the island, and plants produced few seeds.\\n- The finches quickly ate the small, soft seeds, leaving mainly large seeds with tough husks.\\n- Only finches with large, deep beaks could crack these tough seeds.\\n- The island's population of medium ground finches fell from about 1,400 birds to a few hundred.\\n- Before the drought, the finches' average beak depth was 9.2 millimeters; among finches born after the drought, in 1978, it was 9.7 millimeters.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to explain why the average beak depth of the island's medium ground finches increased after the drought. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"After a severe drought in 1977, the average beak depth of medium ground finches on Daphne Major increased from 9.2 to 9.7 millimeters.\",\"option_b\":\"During the 1977 drought on Daphne Major, the population of medium ground finches fell from about 1,400 birds to a few hundred.\",\"option_c\":\"Because the drought left mainly large, tough seeds that only deep-beaked finches could crack, those finches were the ones that survived, and the average beak depth rose from 9.2 millimeters before the drought to 9.7 millimeters among finches born in 1978.\",\"option_d\":\"Peter and Rosemary Grant studied how a 1977 drought affected the medium ground finches of Daphne Major, a small island in the Galápagos.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to explain why the average beak depth increased. Choice C gives the cause: the drought left mainly tough seeds that only deep-beaked finches could crack, so those finches survived, and the next generation had deeper beaks on average.\\n\\nChoice A reports the increase in beak depth but does not explain why it happened. Choice B describes the drop in population without connecting it to beak size. Choice D describes the study's focus without giving any of its findings.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — tumshuqning o‘rtacha chuqurligi nima uchun oshganini tushuntirish. C sababini aytadi: qurg‘oqchilikdan keyin asosan faqat chuqur tumshuqli vyuroklar chaqa oladigan qattiq urug‘lar qolgan, shuning uchun aynan o‘sha qushlar omon qolgan va keyingi avlodning tumshug‘i o‘rtacha chuqurroq bo‘lgan.\\n\\nA tumshuq chuqurligi oshganini aytadi, lekin nima uchun oshganini tushuntirmaydi. B populyatsiya kamayganini tasvirlaydi, ammo buni tumshuq kattaligi bilan bog‘lamaydi. D tadqiqot nimaga bag‘ishlanganini aytadi, lekin uning natijalaridan hech birini keltirmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- The platypus lives in rivers and streams in eastern Australia, including Tasmania.\\n- It has a flat, duck-like bill and hunts for small animals underwater.\\n- The short-beaked echidna lives throughout Australia and is covered with sharp spines.\\n- It uses a long, sticky tongue to eat ants and termites.\\n- Platypuses and echidnas are monotremes, the only mammals that lay eggs.\\n- Like other mammals, monotremes feed their young milk.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the platypus and the short-beaked echidna. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"The platypus has a flat, duck-like bill, while the short-beaked echidna is covered with sharp spines.\",\"option_b\":\"Although the platypus hunts underwater and the short-beaked echidna eats ants and termites, both animals are monotremes: mammals that lay eggs.\",\"option_c\":\"Monotremes are the only mammals that lay eggs, but like other mammals, they feed their young milk.\",\"option_d\":\"The platypus lives in rivers and streams in eastern Australia, while the short-beaked echidna lives throughout Australia.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to emphasize a similarity between the platypus and the short-beaked echidna. Choice B names both animals and states what they have in common: both are monotremes, mammals that lay eggs. Its opening clause mentions their differences only to highlight that shared trait.\\n\\nChoices A and D each name both animals but emphasize differences—in appearance and in where they live. Choice C does describe a similarity, but it is a similarity between monotremes and other mammals, and it does not mention the platypus or the echidna.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — o‘rdakburun va qisqa tumshuqli exidna orasidagi o‘xshashlikni ta’kidlash. B ikkala hayvonni ham nomlaydi va ularning umumiy jihatini aytadi: ikkalasi ham monotremlar, ya’ni tuxum qo‘yadigan sutemizuvchilar. Gapning birinchi qismidagi farqlar faqat shu umumiy belgini ajratib ko‘rsatish uchun keltirilgan.\\n\\nA va D ikkala hayvonni nomlaydi, lekin farqlarni — tashqi ko‘rinish va yashash joyidagi farqni — ta’kidlaydi. C haqiqatan ham o‘xshashlikni tasvirlaydi, ammo bu monotremlar bilan boshqa sutemizuvchilar orasidagi o‘xshashlik; unda o‘rdakburun ham, exidna ham tilga olinmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"right_triangles_trigonometry\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A ladder 6.5 meters long leans against a vertical wall. The foot of the ladder is on level ground, 2.5 meters from the base of the wall. How high up the wall, in meters, does the top of the ladder reach?\",\"option_a\":\"4\",\"option_b\":\"6\",\"option_c\":\"9\",\"option_d\":\"36\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The wall, the ground, and the ladder form a right triangle with the ladder as the hypotenuse. By the Pythagorean theorem, $h^2 + 2.5^2 = 6.5^2$, so $h^2 = 42.25 - 6.25 = 36$ and $h = 6$ meters. Choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice A (4) is $6.5 - 2.5$; side lengths of a right triangle cannot simply be subtracted. Choice C (9) is $6.5 + 2.5$. Choice D (36) is $h^2$; the square root was not taken.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Devor, yer va narvon to‘g‘ri burchakli uchburchak hosil qiladi, narvon — gipotenuza. Pifagor teoremasiga ko‘ra $h^2 + 2.5^2 = 6.5^2$, demak $h^2 = 42.25 - 6.25 = 36$ va $h = 6$ metr. To‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\nA (4) — bu $6.5 - 2.5$; to‘g‘ri burchakli uchburchak tomonlarini oddiygina ayirib bo‘lmaydi. C (9) — bu $6.5 + 2.5$. D (36) — bu $h^2$; kvadrat ildiz chiqarilmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"right_triangles_trigonometry\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"In a right triangle, $A$ is one of the acute angles and $\\\\cos A = 0.6$. What is the value of $\\\\sin A$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"0.8\",\".8\",\"4/5\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Since $\\\\cos A = 0.6 = \\\\frac{3}{5}$, the side adjacent to $A$ and the hypotenuse are in the ratio $3 : 5$. Using the 3-4-5 right triangle, the side opposite $A$ is 4, so $\\\\sin A = \\\\frac{4}{5} = 0.8$. (Equivalently, $\\\\sin^2 A = 1 - 0.36 = 0.64$.)\\n\\nCommon mistake: $1 - 0.6 = 0.4$ is not correct; it is $\\\\sin^2 A + \\\\cos^2 A$ that equals 1, not $\\\\sin A + \\\\cos A$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$\\\\cos A = 0.6 = \\\\frac{3}{5}$ bo‘lgani uchun $A$ ga yopishgan katet va gipotenuza $3 : 5$ nisbatda. 3-4-5 to‘g‘ri burchakli uchburchakdan $A$ ga qarama-qarshi katet 4 ga teng, demak $\\\\sin A = \\\\frac{4}{5} = 0.8$. (Boshqacha: $\\\\sin^2 A = 1 - 0.36 = 0.64$.)\\n\\nKo‘p uchraydigan xato: $1 - 0.6 = 0.4$ noto‘g‘ri; 1 ga $\\\\sin A + \\\\cos A$ emas, $\\\\sin^2 A + \\\\cos^2 A$ teng.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"right_triangles_trigonometry\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A straight ramp rises 0.7 meter over a horizontal distance of 2.4 meters. What is the length, in meters, of the sloped surface of the ramp?\",\"option_a\":\"2.5\",\"option_b\":\"3.1\",\"option_c\":\"6.25\",\"option_d\":\"9.61\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The rise and the horizontal distance are the legs of a right triangle, and the ramp is the hypotenuse: $\\\\sqrt{0.7^2 + 2.4^2} = \\\\sqrt{0.49 + 5.76} = \\\\sqrt{6.25} = 2.5$ meters. Choice A is correct.\\n\\nChoice B (3.1) is $0.7 + 2.4$, the sum of the legs. Choice C (6.25) is the square of the length; the square root was not taken. Choice D (9.61) is $(0.7 + 2.4)^2$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ko‘tarilish balandligi va gorizontal masofa to‘g‘ri burchakli uchburchakning katetlari, pandus esa gipotenuza: $\\\\sqrt{0.7^2 + 2.4^2} = \\\\sqrt{0.49 + 5.76} = \\\\sqrt{6.25} = 2.5$ metr. To‘g‘ri javob A.\\n\\nB (3.1) — bu $0.7 + 2.4$, katetlar yig‘indisi. C (6.25) — uzunlikning kvadrati; ildiz chiqarilmagan. D (9.61) — bu $(0.7 + 2.4)^2$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"right_triangles_trigonometry\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A surveyor stands on level ground 24 meters from the base of a vertical minaret. From ground level at that spot, the angle of elevation to the top of the minaret is $60^\\\\circ$. What is the height, in meters, of the minaret?\",\"option_a\":\"$8\\\\sqrt{3}$\",\"option_b\":\"12\",\"option_c\":\"$24\\\\sqrt{3}$\",\"option_d\":\"48\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The height is the side opposite the $60^\\\\circ$ angle, and the 24-meter distance is the adjacent side, so $\\\\tan 60^\\\\circ = \\\\frac{h}{24}$. Since $\\\\tan 60^\\\\circ = \\\\sqrt{3}$, $h = 24\\\\sqrt{3}$ meters (about 41.6 meters). Choice C is correct.\\n\\nChoice A ($8\\\\sqrt{3}$) uses $\\\\tan 30^\\\\circ = \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ instead of $\\\\tan 60^\\\\circ$. Choice B (12) is $24 \\\\cos 60^\\\\circ$, which mixes up the ratio. Choice D (48) is $\\\\frac{24}{\\\\cos 60^\\\\circ}$, the length of the line of sight (the hypotenuse), not the height.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Balandlik $60^\\\\circ$ li burchakka qarama-qarshi katet, 24 metrli masofa esa yopishgan katet, demak $\\\\tan 60^\\\\circ = \\\\frac{h}{24}$. $\\\\tan 60^\\\\circ = \\\\sqrt{3}$ bo‘lgani uchun $h = 24\\\\sqrt{3}$ metr (taxminan 41.6 metr). To‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\nA ($8\\\\sqrt{3}$) — $\\\\tan 60^\\\\circ$ o‘rniga $\\\\tan 30^\\\\circ = \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ olingan. B (12) — bu $24 \\\\cos 60^\\\\circ$, nisbat chalkashtirilgan. D (48) — bu $\\\\frac{24}{\\\\cos 60^\\\\circ}$, ya’ni balandlik emas, qarash chizig‘ining uzunligi (gipotenuza).\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"right_triangles_trigonometry\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"In right triangle $ABC$, angle $C$ is the right angle, $\\\\sin A = \\\\frac{12}{13}$, and $AC = 15$. What is the length of $\\\\overline{BC}$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"36\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Side $\\\\overline{BC}$ is opposite angle $A$ and $\\\\overline{AC}$ is adjacent to it, so $\\\\tan A = \\\\frac{BC}{AC}$. From $\\\\sin A = \\\\frac{12}{13}$, the sides are in the ratio of a 5-12-13 triangle: opposite 12, adjacent 5, hypotenuse 13. So $\\\\tan A = \\\\frac{12}{5}$ and $BC = 15 \\\\cdot \\\\frac{12}{5} = 36$. (Check: $AB = 39$ and $15^2 + 36^2 = 39^2$.)\\n\\nCommon mistake: $15 \\\\cdot \\\\frac{12}{13} = \\\\frac{180}{13} \\\\approx 13.8$ treats $AC$ as the hypotenuse. In fact, $AC$ is a leg, adjacent to angle $A$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$\\\\overline{BC}$ tomon $A$ burchakka qarama-qarshi, $\\\\overline{AC}$ esa unga yopishgan, demak $\\\\tan A = \\\\frac{BC}{AC}$. $\\\\sin A = \\\\frac{12}{13}$ dan tomonlar 5-12-13 uchburchak nisbatida: qarama-qarshi katet 12, yopishgan katet 5, gipotenuza 13. Demak $\\\\tan A = \\\\frac{12}{5}$ va $BC = 15 \\\\cdot \\\\frac{12}{5} = 36$. (Tekshirish: $AB = 39$ va $15^2 + 36^2 = 39^2$.)\\n\\nKo‘p uchraydigan xato: $15 \\\\cdot \\\\frac{12}{13} = \\\\frac{180}{13} \\\\approx 13.8$ — bunda $AC$ gipotenuza deb olingan. Aslida $AC$ — $A$ burchakka yopishgan katet.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"right_triangles_trigonometry\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"In right triangle $ABC$, angle $C$ is the right angle and $\\\\tan A = \\\\frac{8}{15}$. What is the value of $\\\\cos B$?\",\"option_a\":\"$\\\\frac{15}{17}$\",\"option_b\":\"$\\\\frac{8}{15}$\",\"option_c\":\"$\\\\frac{15}{8}$\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\frac{8}{17}$\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"From $\\\\tan A = \\\\frac{8}{15}$, take $BC = 8$ (opposite $A$) and $AC = 15$ (adjacent to $A$). The hypotenuse is $AB = \\\\sqrt{8^2 + 15^2} = 17$. For angle $B$, the adjacent leg is $BC = 8$, so $\\\\cos B = \\\\frac{8}{17}$. (This also follows from $\\\\cos B = \\\\sin A$, since $A$ and $B$ are complementary.) Choice D is correct.\\n\\nChoice A ($\\\\frac{15}{17}$) is $\\\\cos A$ (equal to $\\\\sin B$), not $\\\\cos B$. Choice B ($\\\\frac{8}{15}$) is $\\\\tan A$ itself. Choice C ($\\\\frac{15}{8}$) is $\\\\tan B$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$\\\\tan A = \\\\frac{8}{15}$ dan $BC = 8$ ($A$ ga qarama-qarshi) va $AC = 15$ ($A$ ga yopishgan) deb olamiz. Gipotenuza $AB = \\\\sqrt{8^2 + 15^2} = 17$. $B$ burchak uchun yopishgan katet $BC = 8$, demak $\\\\cos B = \\\\frac{8}{17}$. ($A$ va $B$ burchaklar yig‘indisi $90^\\\\circ$ bo‘lgani uchun bu $\\\\cos B = \\\\sin A$ dan ham kelib chiqadi.) To‘g‘ri javob D.\\n\\nA ($\\\\frac{15}{17}$) — bu $\\\\cos B$ emas, $\\\\cos A$ ($\\\\sin B$ ga teng). B ($\\\\frac{8}{15}$) — bu $\\\\tan A$ ning o‘zi. C ($\\\\frac{15}{8}$) — bu $\\\\tan B$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"right_triangles_trigonometry\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"From a point on level ground, the angle of elevation to the top of a vertical clock tower is $45^\\\\circ$. After walking 20 meters straight toward the tower on the same level ground, the angle of elevation to the top of the tower is $60^\\\\circ$. Both angles are measured from ground level. What is the height, in meters, of the tower?\",\"option_a\":\"20\",\"option_b\":\"$10 + 10\\\\sqrt{3}$\",\"option_c\":\"$20\\\\sqrt{3}$\",\"option_d\":\"$30 + 10\\\\sqrt{3}$\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Let $h$ be the height. From the first point, $\\\\tan 45^\\\\circ = \\\\frac{h}{d_1}$, so the distance to the base is $d_1 = h$. From the second point, $\\\\tan 60^\\\\circ = \\\\frac{h}{d_2}$, so $d_2 = \\\\frac{h}{\\\\sqrt{3}}$. The points are 20 meters apart, so $h - \\\\frac{h}{\\\\sqrt{3}} = 20$. Multiply by $\\\\sqrt{3}$: $h(\\\\sqrt{3} - 1) = 20\\\\sqrt{3}$, so $h = \\\\frac{20\\\\sqrt{3}}{\\\\sqrt{3} - 1} = \\\\frac{20\\\\sqrt{3}(\\\\sqrt{3} + 1)}{2} = 30 + 10\\\\sqrt{3}$ meters (about 47.3 meters). Choice D is correct.\\n\\nChoice A (20) assumes the first point is 20 meters from the tower. Choice B ($10 + 10\\\\sqrt{3}$) is the distance $d_2$ from the second point to the base of the tower; it also results from using $\\\\tan\\\\theta = \\\\frac{\\\\text{adjacent}}{\\\\text{opposite}}$. Choice C ($20\\\\sqrt{3}$) assumes the second point is 20 meters from the tower.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Balandlikni $h$ deb olaylik. Birinchi nuqtadan $\\\\tan 45^\\\\circ = \\\\frac{h}{d_1}$, demak minoraning asosigacha masofa $d_1 = h$. Ikkinchi nuqtadan $\\\\tan 60^\\\\circ = \\\\frac{h}{d_2}$, demak $d_2 = \\\\frac{h}{\\\\sqrt{3}}$. Nuqtalar orasidagi masofa 20 metr, shuning uchun $h - \\\\frac{h}{\\\\sqrt{3}} = 20$. $\\\\sqrt{3}$ ga ko‘paytiramiz: $h(\\\\sqrt{3} - 1) = 20\\\\sqrt{3}$, demak $h = \\\\frac{20\\\\sqrt{3}}{\\\\sqrt{3} - 1} = \\\\frac{20\\\\sqrt{3}(\\\\sqrt{3} + 1)}{2} = 30 + 10\\\\sqrt{3}$ metr (taxminan 47.3 metr). To‘g‘ri javob D.\\n\\nA (20) — birinchi nuqta minoradan 20 metr uzoqlikda deb olingan. B ($10 + 10\\\\sqrt{3}$) — ikkinchi nuqtadan minora asosigacha bo‘lgan $d_2$ masofa; bu natija $\\\\tan\\\\theta = \\\\frac{\\\\text{yopishgan}}{\\\\text{qarama-qarshi}}$ deb olinganda ham chiqadi. C ($20\\\\sqrt{3}$) — ikkinchi nuqta minoradan 20 metr uzoqlikda deb olingan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":130,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"right_triangles_trigonometry\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A straight loading ramp makes an angle $\\\\theta$ with the level ground, where $\\\\sin\\\\theta = \\\\frac{7}{25}$. The top of the ramp is 1.4 meters above the ground. What is the horizontal distance, in meters, from the bottom of the ramp to the point on the ground directly below the top of the ramp?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"4.8\",\"24/5\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The rise is the side opposite $\\\\theta$ and the ramp is the hypotenuse, so $\\\\sin\\\\theta = \\\\frac{1.4}{L} = \\\\frac{7}{25}$, which gives $L = 1.4 \\\\cdot \\\\frac{25}{7} = 5$ meters. The horizontal distance is the adjacent leg: $\\\\sqrt{5^2 - 1.4^2} = \\\\sqrt{25 - 1.96} = \\\\sqrt{23.04} = 4.8$ meters. (Or: $\\\\cos\\\\theta = \\\\frac{24}{25}$ from the 7-24-25 triangle, and $5 \\\\cdot \\\\frac{24}{25} = 4.8$.)\\n\\nCommon mistake: 5 is the length of the ramp itself (the hypotenuse), not the horizontal distance; $1.4 \\\\cdot \\\\frac{25}{7}$ must be followed by one more step.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ko‘tarilish balandligi $\\\\theta$ ga qarama-qarshi katet, pandus esa gipotenuza, demak $\\\\sin\\\\theta = \\\\frac{1.4}{L} = \\\\frac{7}{25}$, bundan $L = 1.4 \\\\cdot \\\\frac{25}{7} = 5$ metr. Gorizontal masofa yopishgan katet: $\\\\sqrt{5^2 - 1.4^2} = \\\\sqrt{25 - 1.96} = \\\\sqrt{23.04} = 4.8$ metr. (Yoki: 7-24-25 uchburchakdan $\\\\cos\\\\theta = \\\\frac{24}{25}$ va $5 \\\\cdot \\\\frac{24}{25} = 4.8$.)\\n\\nKo‘p uchraydigan xato: 5 — bu gorizontal masofa emas, pandusning o‘z uzunligi (gipotenuza); $1.4 \\\\cdot \\\\frac{25}{7}$ hisobidan keyin yana bir qadam kerak.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"systems_linear_equations\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"$$\\\\begin{aligned} y &= 4x - 7 \\\\\\\\ y &= x + 5 \\\\end{aligned}$$\\n\\nWhat is the solution $(x, y)$ to the given system of equations?\",\"option_a\":\"$(2, 7)$\",\"option_b\":\"$(9, 4)$\",\"option_c\":\"$(4, 9)$\",\"option_d\":\"$(-4, 1)$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Set the two expressions for $y$ equal: $4x - 7 = x + 5$. Then $3x = 12$, so $x = 4$, and $y = 4 + 5 = 9$.\\n\\nCheck in the first equation: $4(4) - 7 = 9$.\\n\\nChoice B swaps $x$ and $y$. Choice A satisfies only the second equation ($2 + 5 = 7$, but $4(2) - 7 = 1$). Choice D also satisfies only the second equation.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$y$ uchun ikki ifodani tenglaymiz: $4x - 7 = x + 5$. U holda $3x = 12$, demak $x = 4$ va $y = 4 + 5 = 9$.\\n\\nBirinchi tenglamada tekshiramiz: $4(4) - 7 = 9$.\\n\\nB variant $x$ va $y$ ni almashtirib qo‘yadi. A variant faqat ikkinchi tenglamani qanoatlantiradi ($2 + 5 = 7$, lekin $4(2) - 7 = 1$). D variant ham faqat ikkinchi tenglamani qanoatlantiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"systems_linear_equations\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A farmer in the Fergana Valley packed $52$ crates of fruit. Each crate held either $12$ kilograms of apricots or $15$ kilograms of peaches. The total mass of fruit in the crates was $699$ kilograms. How many crates of peaches did the farmer pack?\",\"option_a\":\"$25$\",\"option_b\":\"$27$\",\"option_c\":\"$47$\",\"option_d\":\"$58$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Let $a$ be the number of apricot crates and $p$ the number of peach crates: $a + p = 52$ and $12a + 15p = 699$.\\n\\nSubstitute $a = 52 - p$: $12(52 - p) + 15p = 699$, so $624 + 3p = 699$, $3p = 75$, and $p = 25$.\\n\\nCheck: $a = 27$ and $12(27) + 15(25) = 324 + 375 = 699$. Choice B is the number of apricot crates. Choice C is about $699 \\\\div 15$ and Choice D is about $699 \\\\div 12$; each assumes all the fruit is one kind.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$a$ — o‘rik yashiklari soni, $p$ — shaftoli yashiklari soni bo‘lsin: $a + p = 52$ va $12a + 15p = 699$.\\n\\n$a = 52 - p$ ni qo‘yamiz: $12(52 - p) + 15p = 699$, ya’ni $624 + 3p = 699$, $3p = 75$ va $p = 25$.\\n\\nTekshiruv: $a = 27$ va $12(27) + 15(25) = 324 + 375 = 699$. B variant — o‘rik yashiklari soni. C variant taxminan $699 \\\\div 15$, D variant esa taxminan $699 \\\\div 12$; ikkalasi ham barcha meva bir xil deb hisoblaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"systems_linear_equations\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"$$\\\\begin{aligned} 3x + 2y &= 7 \\\\\\\\ -3x + 5y &= 28 \\\\end{aligned}$$\\n\\nIf $(x, y)$ is the solution to the given system of equations, what is the value of $x$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"-1\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Add the equations to eliminate $x$: $7y = 35$, so $y = 5$.\\n\\nSubstitute into the first equation: $3x + 10 = 7$, so $3x = -3$ and $x = -1$. Check: $-3(-1) + 5(5) = 3 + 25 = 28$.\\n\\nBe careful to enter $x$; entering $y = 5$ is a common error.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$x$ ni yo‘qotish uchun tenglamalarni qo‘shamiz: $7y = 35$, demak $y = 5$.\\n\\nBirinchi tenglamaga qo‘yamiz: $3x + 10 = 7$, demak $3x = -3$ va $x = -1$. Tekshiruv: $-3(-1) + 5(5) = 3 + 25 = 28$.\\n\\nE’tibor bering: savol $x$ ni so‘rayapti; $y = 5$ ni kiritish — ko‘p uchraydigan xato.\",\"estimated_seconds\":65,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"systems_linear_equations\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"According to official statistics, the permanent population of Uzbekistan was about $38.2$ million on January 1, 2026, and there were about $0.4$ million more men than women. Based on these figures, about how many million women lived in Uzbekistan?\",\"option_a\":\"$18.7$\",\"option_b\":\"$18.9$\",\"option_c\":\"$19.3$\",\"option_d\":\"$37.8$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Let $m$ and $w$ be the numbers of men and women, in millions: $m + w = 38.2$ and $m - w = 0.4$.\\n\\nSubtract the second equation from the first: $2w = 37.8$, so $w = 18.9$. Then $m = 19.3$, and $19.3 - 18.9 = 0.4$.\\n\\nChoice C is the number of men. Choice D is $2w$, the result before dividing by $2$. Choice A halves the total and then subtracts the whole difference ($19.1 - 0.4$); only half of the difference should be subtracted.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$m$ va $w$ — erkaklar va ayollar soni (million) bo‘lsin: $m + w = 38.2$ va $m - w = 0.4$.\\n\\nBirinchi tenglamadan ikkinchisini ayiramiz: $2w = 37.8$, demak $w = 18.9$. U holda $m = 19.3$ va $19.3 - 18.9 = 0.4$.\\n\\nC variant — erkaklar soni. D variant — $2w$, ya’ni $2$ ga bo‘lishdan oldingi natija. A variant jami sonning yarmini olib, butun farqni ayiradi ($19.1 - 0.4$); aslida farqning yarmini ayirish kerak.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"systems_linear_equations\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"$$\\\\begin{aligned} 2x + 3y &= 12 \\\\\\\\ 5x - 2y &= 11 \\\\end{aligned}$$\\n\\nIf $(x, y)$ is the solution to the given system of equations, what is the value of $7x + y$?\",\"option_a\":\"$1$\",\"option_b\":\"$5$\",\"option_c\":\"$21$\",\"option_d\":\"$23$\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Adding the two equations gives $(2x + 5x) + (3y - 2y) = 12 + 11$, that is, $7x + y = 23$, without solving for $x$ and $y$.\\n\\n(Solving fully gives $x = 3$ and $y = 2$, and $7(3) + 2 = 23$.)\\n\\nChoice B is $x + y$ and Choice A is $x - y$. Choice C is $7x$; the value of $y$ was left out.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkala tenglamani qo‘shsak, $(2x + 5x) + (3y - 2y) = 12 + 11$, ya’ni $7x + y = 23$ — $x$ va $y$ ni alohida topmasdan.\\n\\n(To‘liq yechsak, $x = 3$ va $y = 2$, hamda $7(3) + 2 = 23$.)\\n\\nB variant — $x + y$, A variant — $x - y$. C variant — $7x$; $y$ ning qiymati qo‘shilmay qolgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"systems_linear_equations\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"The rail distance between Tashkent and Samarkand is $344$ kilometers. At noon, a freight train leaves Tashkent for Samarkand at an average speed of $90$ kilometers per hour, and at the same time another freight train leaves Samarkand for Tashkent at an average speed of $82$ kilometers per hour on a parallel track. How far from Tashkent, in kilometers, are the trains when they pass each other?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"180\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Let $d$ be the distance from Tashkent where they pass and $t$ the time in hours: $d = 90t$ and $344 - d = 82t$.\\n\\nAdding: $344 = 172t$, so $t = 2$ hours. Then $d = 90(2) = 180$ kilometers.\\n\\nCheck: the second train travels $82(2) = 164$ km, and $180 + 164 = 344$. A common error is to enter $164$, the distance from Samarkand, or $2$, the time.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$d$ — poyezdlar uchrashadigan joyning Toshkentdan masofasi, $t$ — soatlardagi vaqt bo‘lsin: $d = 90t$ va $344 - d = 82t$.\\n\\nQo‘shamiz: $344 = 172t$, demak $t = 2$ soat. U holda $d = 90(2) = 180$ kilometr.\\n\\nTekshiruv: ikkinchi poyezd $82(2) = 164$ km yuradi va $180 + 164 = 344$. Ko‘p uchraydigan xato — Samarqanddan masofa $164$ ni yoki vaqt $2$ ni kiritish.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"systems_linear_equations\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"$$\\\\begin{aligned} ax + 3y &= 9 \\\\\\\\ 4x + (a + 1)y &= 12 \\\\end{aligned}$$\\n\\nIn the given system of equations, $a$ is a constant. For which value of $a$ does the system have infinitely many solutions?\",\"option_a\":\"$-4$\",\"option_b\":\"$-3$\",\"option_c\":\"$3$\",\"option_d\":\"$4$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The system has infinitely many solutions when one equation is a multiple of the other: $\\\\frac{a}{4} = \\\\frac{3}{a + 1} = \\\\frac{9}{12}$.\\n\\nFrom $\\\\frac{a}{4} = \\\\frac{3}{4}$, $a = 3$. Check: $\\\\frac{3}{a + 1} = \\\\frac{3}{4}$ as well. With $a = 3$, the equations are $3x + 3y = 9$ and $4x + 4y = 12$, both equivalent to $x + y = 3$.\\n\\nChoice A also makes the lines parallel, since $a(a + 1) = 12$ holds for $a = -4$, but then the equations are $-4x + 3y = 9$ and $4x - 3y = 12$, i.e., $4x - 3y = -9$ and $4x - 3y = 12$: the system has no solution. Choices B and D give non-parallel lines, so the system has exactly one solution.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Tenglamalardan biri ikkinchisining karralisi bo‘lsa, sistema cheksiz ko‘p yechimga ega: $\\\\frac{a}{4} = \\\\frac{3}{a + 1} = \\\\frac{9}{12}$.\\n\\n$\\\\frac{a}{4} = \\\\frac{3}{4}$ dan $a = 3$. Tekshiruv: $\\\\frac{3}{a + 1} = \\\\frac{3}{4}$ ham bajariladi. $a = 3$ da tenglamalar $3x + 3y = 9$ va $4x + 4y = 12$ bo‘ladi, ikkalasi ham $x + y = 3$ ga teng kuchli.\\n\\nA variantda ham chiziqlar parallel, chunki $a = -4$ uchun ham $a(a + 1) = 12$, lekin unda tenglamalar $-4x + 3y = 9$ va $4x - 3y = 12$, ya’ni $4x - 3y = -9$ va $4x - 3y = 12$ bo‘ladi: sistema yechimga ega emas. B va D variantlarda chiziqlar parallel emas, demak sistema aynan bitta yechimga ega.\",\"estimated_seconds\":130,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"systems_linear_equations\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A worker strikes one end of a long steel rail with a hammer. A student with an ear pressed to the other end hears two sounds: one that travels through the steel and one that travels through the air. Assume that sound travels at $5{,}100$ meters per second in steel and $340$ meters per second in air. The student hears the sound through the air $1.4$ seconds after hearing the sound through the steel. What is the length of the rail, in meters?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"510\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Let $d$ be the length in meters and $t$ the time, in seconds, for sound to travel through the steel. Then $d = 5{,}100t$ and $d = 340(t + 1.4)$.\\n\\nSet them equal: $5{,}100t = 340t + 476$, so $4{,}760t = 476$ and $t = 0.1$ second. Then $d = 5{,}100(0.1) = 510$ meters.\\n\\nCheck: through the air, $510 \\\\div 340 = 1.5$ seconds, and $1.5 - 0.1 = 1.4$. A common error is to use only the air speed, $340(1.4) = 476$ meters, which ignores the time the steel sound took.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$d$ — relsning uzunligi (metr), $t$ — tovushning po‘lat orqali o‘tish vaqti (sekund) bo‘lsin. U holda $d = 5{,}100t$ va $d = 340(t + 1.4)$.\\n\\nUlarni tenglaymiz: $5{,}100t = 340t + 476$, demak $4{,}760t = 476$ va $t = 0.1$ sekund. U holda $d = 5{,}100(0.1) = 510$ metr.\\n\\nTekshiruv: havo orqali $510 \\\\div 340 = 1.5$ sekund va $1.5 - 0.1 = 1.4$. Ko‘p uchraydigan xato — faqat havodagi tezlikni olib, $340(1.4) = 476$ metr deb hisoblash; bunda po‘lat orqali o‘tgan tovush vaqti hisobga olinmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":150,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from Mark Twain's 1883 memoir *Life on the Mississippi*. Twain grew up in a small town on the Mississippi River.\\n\\nWhen I was a boy, there was but one permanent ambition among my comrades in our village … on the west bank of the Mississippi River. That was, to be a steamboatman. We had transient ambitions of other sorts, but they were only transient. When a circus came and went, it left us all burning to become clowns; … now and then we had a hope that if we lived and were good, God would permit us to be pirates. These ambitions faded out, each in its turn; but the ambition to be a steamboatman always remained.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"To describe a childhood goal that, unlike the boys' other ambitions, never lost its appeal\",\"option_b\":\"To explain how the narrator eventually became a steamboatman\",\"option_c\":\"To compare the dangers of working on a steamboat with those of performing in a circus\",\"option_d\":\"To criticize the adults of the village for discouraging the boys' dreams\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text names one “permanent ambition,” to be a steamboatman, and contrasts it with “transient” ambitions such as becoming clowns or pirates, which “faded out, each in its turn.” Its purpose is to show that this one goal lasted while the others did not, as choice A says.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because the text never says whether or how the narrator achieved his ambition. Choice C is wrong because the text mentions the circus only as a passing ambition and discusses no dangers. Choice D is wrong because adults and their opinions are not mentioned at all.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda bitta “doimiy orzu” — paroxodchi bo‘lish — tilga olinadi va u masxaraboz yoki qaroqchi bo‘lish kabi “o‘tkinchi” orzularga qarama-qarshi qo‘yiladi; ular “birin-ketin so‘nib ketgan.” Matnning maqsadi — boshqa orzulardan farqli o‘laroq, aynan shu orzu saqlanib qolganini ko‘rsatish. To‘g‘ri javob — A.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: matnda hikoyachi orzusiga erishgani yoki qanday erishgani aytilmaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: sirk faqat o‘tkinchi orzu sifatida tilga olinadi, xavf-xatar haqida gap yo‘q. D noto‘g‘ri: kattalar va ularning fikri umuman tilga olinmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":65,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from Willa Cather's 1913 novel *O Pioneers!* A little boy is crying on a town sidewalk because his kitten is stuck at the top of a pole. He sees his older sister coming toward him.\\n\\nHis sister was a tall, strong girl, and she walked rapidly and resolutely, as if she knew exactly where she was going and what she was going to do next. She wore a man’s long ulster (not as if it were an affliction, but as if it were very comfortable and belonged to her; carried it like a young soldier), and a round plush cap, tied down with a thick veil. She had a serious, thoughtful face, and her clear, deep blue eyes were fixed intently on the distance, without seeming to see anything, as if she were in trouble.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"To explain why the boy's kitten climbed to the top of the pole\",\"option_b\":\"To describe the harsh winter weather in the town\",\"option_c\":\"To introduce a character by emphasizing her strength, confidence, and seriousness\",\"option_d\":\"To show that the sister is embarrassed by the coat she is wearing\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The whole text describes the sister: she is “tall, strong,” walks “resolutely,” wears her coat “like a young soldier,” and has a “serious, thoughtful face.” Its purpose is to introduce her and stress these qualities, as choice C says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the text says nothing about how the kitten got onto the pole. Choice B is wrong because the weather is not described in this text. Choice D is wrong because the narrator says the opposite: she wears the man's coat “not as if it were an affliction, but as if it were very comfortable and belonged to her.”\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Butun matn opani tasvirlaydi: u “baland bo‘yli, baquvvat,” “qat’iyat bilan” yuradi, paltosini “yosh askardek” kiyib yuradi va “jiddiy, o‘ychan yuzli.” Matnning maqsadi — uni tanishtirish va shu xislatlarini ta’kidlash. To‘g‘ri javob — C.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: matnda mushukcha ustunga qanday chiqib qolgani aytilmaydi. B noto‘g‘ri: bu matnda ob-havo tasvirlanmagan. D noto‘g‘ri: hikoyachi buning aksini aytadi — u erkaklar paltosini “azob-uqubatdek emas, balki juda qulay va o‘ziniki bo‘lgandek” kiyib yuradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":65,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Nearly every musical instrument needs a person to play it. __The aeolian harp, however, is played by the wind.__ Its strings are stretched across a long wooden box that is placed in an open window or on a hilltop. As air moves across the strings, they vibrate and produce soft, shifting chords that no human hand controls.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?\",\"option_a\":\"It describes how the aeolian harp is built.\",\"option_b\":\"It argues that instruments played by the wind sound better than those played by people.\",\"option_c\":\"It gives an example that supports the claim made in the first sentence.\",\"option_d\":\"It introduces an exception to the general statement in the first sentence, which the rest of the text explains.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first sentence says nearly every instrument needs a person to play it. The underlined sentence, beginning with “however,” introduces an instrument that does not: the aeolian harp is played by the wind. The rest of the text explains how this works. Choice D describes this function.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the harp's construction is described in the next sentence, not the underlined one. Choice B is wrong because the text never compares the sound of wind-played and human-played instruments. Choice C is wrong because the harp does not support the first sentence's claim; it is an exception to it.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi jumlada deyarli har bir cholg‘uni odam chalishi kerakligi aytiladi. “However” bilan boshlangan tagiga chizilgan jumla bunga mos kelmaydigan cholg‘uni — shamol chaladigan eol arfasini tanishtiradi. Matnning qolgan qismi bu qanday ishlashini tushuntiradi. D javob aynan shu vazifani ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: arfaning tuzilishi tagiga chizilgan jumlada emas, keyingi jumlada tasvirlanadi. B noto‘g‘ri: matnda shamol va odam chaladigan cholg‘ularning ovozi solishtirilmaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: arfa birinchi jumladagi fikrni tasdiqlamaydi, aksincha, undan istisno hisoblanadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from Frederick Douglass's 1845 autobiography *Narrative of the Life of Frederick Douglass, an American Slave*. As a boy, Douglass has overheard his enslaver, Mr. Auld, order his wife to stop teaching Douglass to read, because learning would make Douglass unfit to be a slave.\\n\\nThese words sank deep into my heart, stirred up sentiments within that lay slumbering, and called into existence an entirely new train of thought. It was a new and special revelation, explaining dark and mysterious things, with which my youthful understanding had struggled, but struggled in vain. I now understood what had been to me a most perplexing difficulty—to wit, the white man’s power to enslave the black man. It was a grand achievement, and I prized it highly. From that moment, I understood the pathway from slavery to freedom.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"It describes a painful memory and then explains why the narrator tried to forget it.\",\"option_b\":\"It presents the narrator's emotional reaction to words he overheard and then identifies the insight those words gave him.\",\"option_c\":\"It summarizes Mr. Auld's argument and then offers evidence that the argument was false.\",\"option_d\":\"It contrasts the narrator's early hopes for freedom with the disappointment he felt later.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text first describes how Mr. Auld's words affected Douglass (they “sank deep into my heart” and started “an entirely new train of thought”). It then states what he learned from them: he now understood “the white man’s power to enslave the black man” and “the pathway from slavery to freedom.” Choice B describes this structure.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because Douglass treasures the insight (“I prized it highly”) rather than trying to forget the moment. Choice C is wrong because Douglass does not argue that Mr. Auld was wrong; he says the words revealed a truth to him. Choice D is wrong because the text moves from confusion to understanding, not from hope to disappointment.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matn avval janob Old so‘zlari Duglasga qanday ta’sir qilganini tasvirlaydi (ular “yuragimga chuqur botdi” va “butunlay yangi fikrlar oqimini” uyg‘otdi). So‘ng u bu so‘zlardan nimani anglaganini aytadi: endi u “oq tanlining qora tanlini qul qilish qudrati”ni va “qullikdan ozodlikka olib boruvchi yo‘l”ni tushundi. B javob shu tuzilishni ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: Duglas bu lahzani unutishga urinmaydi, aksincha, anglaganini “juda qadrlagan.” C noto‘g‘ri: Duglas janob Oldni nohaq deb isbotlashga urinmaydi; u bu so‘zlar unga haqiqatni ochib berganini aytadi. D noto‘g‘ri: matn umiddan hafsalasi pir bo‘lishga emas, chalkashlikdan tushunishga qarab boradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from W. E. B. Du Bois's 1903 book *The Souls of Black Folk*.\\n\\nBetween me and the other world there is ever an unasked question: unasked by some through feelings of delicacy; by others through the difficulty of rightly framing it. All, nevertheless, flutter round it. __They approach me in a half-hesitant sort of way, eye me curiously or compassionately, and then, instead of saying directly, How does it feel to be a problem? they say, I know an excellent colored man in my town; or, I fought at Mechanicsville; or, Do not these Southern outrages make your blood boil?__ At these I smile, or am interested, or reduce the boiling to a simmer, as the occasion may require. To the real question, How does it feel to be a problem? I answer seldom a word.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?\",\"option_a\":\"It shows that most people eventually ask the author their real question directly.\",\"option_b\":\"It illustrates, with specific examples, the indirect ways people approach the question mentioned in the first sentence.\",\"option_c\":\"It explains why the author refuses to answer any questions from strangers.\",\"option_d\":\"It introduces a new topic that is unrelated to the unasked question.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first sentence describes an “unasked question,” and the second says people “flutter round it.” The underlined sentence shows what this looks like: “instead of saying directly” what they mean, people offer remarks such as “I know an excellent colored man in my town.” It gives concrete examples of the indirect approach, as choice B says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the underlined sentence stresses that people do not ask the question directly. Choice C is wrong because the author does respond to these remarks (“I smile, or am interested”); what he seldom answers is the unspoken question, and the underlined sentence does not explain his reasons. Choice D is wrong because the examples are directly connected to the unasked question.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi jumlada “so‘ralmagan savol” tasvirlanadi, ikkinchisida odamlar “uning atrofida aylanib yurishi” aytiladi. Tagiga chizilgan jumla bu qanday ko‘rinishini ko‘rsatadi: odamlar o‘z fikrini “to‘g‘ridan-to‘g‘ri aytish o‘rniga” “shahrimizda ajoyib bir qora tanli odamni bilaman” kabi gaplarni aytishadi. U bilvosita yondashuvning aniq misollarini keltiradi — to‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: tagiga chizilgan jumla, aksincha, odamlar savolni to‘g‘ridan-to‘g‘ri bermasligini ta’kidlaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: muallif bu gaplarga javob beradi (“jilmayaman yoki qiziqib qolaman”); u kamdan-kam javob beradigan narsa — aytilmagan savol, va tagiga chizilgan jumla buning sababini tushuntirmaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: misollar so‘ralmagan savol bilan bevosita bog‘liq.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In the 1950s, some scholars argued that the remote islands of the Pacific had been settled mostly by accident, by canoes blown off course in storms. In 1976, however, a crew sailed a traditionally designed double-hulled canoe from Hawaii to Tahiti, a distance of more than 4,000 kilometers, without compasses or charts, finding their way by the stars, the ocean swells, and the flight of birds. The voyage showed that deliberate long-distance navigation without instruments was possible, and many scholars now view the settlement of the Pacific as the result of planned voyages.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"It describes a voyage and then explains why the voyage failed to reach its destination.\",\"option_b\":\"It compares two methods of navigation and argues that one is more accurate than the other.\",\"option_c\":\"It presents a debate among scholars and concludes that the debate cannot be settled.\",\"option_d\":\"It presents an earlier explanation, describes a voyage that challenged it, and notes how scholars' views changed as a result.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text opens with an earlier conclusion (the islands were settled by accident), then describes the 1976 voyage that showed instrument-free navigation was possible, and ends by noting that many scholars now see settlement as planned. Choice D describes this structure.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the voyage succeeded; it is offered as evidence of what was possible. Choice B is wrong because the text does not compare the accuracy of two navigation methods. Choice C is wrong because the text reports that many scholars have changed their view, rather than concluding that the question cannot be settled.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matn avvalgi xulosa bilan boshlanadi (orollarga tasodifan odamlar joylashgan), so‘ng asboblarsiz suzish mumkinligini ko‘rsatgan 1976-yilgi sayohatni tasvirlaydi va ko‘plab olimlar endi orollarga joylashishni rejali sayohatlar natijasi deb bilishini aytib tugaydi. D javob shu tuzilishni ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: sayohat muvaffaqiyatli bo‘lgan va nima mumkinligining dalili sifatida keltirilgan. B noto‘g‘ri: matnda ikki xil yo‘l topish usulining aniqligi solishtirilmaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: matnda masalani hal qilib bo‘lmaydi degan xulosa yo‘q, aksincha, ko‘plab olimlar fikrini o‘zgartirgani aytiladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from the 1872 edition of Charles Darwin's *On the Origin of Species*, a book first published in 1859.\\n\\nWhen on board H.M.S. Beagle, as naturalist, I was much struck with certain facts in the distribution of the organic beings inhabiting South America, and in the geological relations of the present to the past inhabitants of that continent. … On my return home, it occurred to me, in 1837, that something might perhaps be made out on this question by patiently accumulating and reflecting on all sorts of facts which could possibly have any bearing on it. After five years' work I allowed myself to speculate on the subject, and drew up some short notes; these I enlarged in 1844 into a sketch of the conclusions, which then seemed to me probable: from that period to the present day I have steadily pursued the same object. __I hope that I may be excused for entering on these personal details, as I give them to show that I have not been hasty in coming to a decision.__\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?\",\"option_a\":\"It acknowledges that the preceding account is personal and explains that it is meant to show how carefully the author reached his conclusions.\",\"option_b\":\"It apologizes for conclusions that the author now believes he reached too quickly.\",\"option_c\":\"It introduces the facts about South America that the author will discuss in later chapters.\",\"option_d\":\"It suggests that the author's personal experiences are more important than the facts he collected.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Before the underlined sentence, Darwin recounts his own history: years of “patiently accumulating” facts, waiting five years before speculating, and pursuing the subject “steadily” ever since. The underlined sentence admits that these are “personal details” and explains why he included them: “to show that I have not been hasty in coming to a decision.” Choice A captures this function.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because Darwin says the opposite: he was not hasty, and he asks to be excused only for including personal details. Choice C is wrong because the facts about South America are mentioned in the first sentence, not introduced by the underlined one. Choice D is wrong because Darwin presents his personal history as evidence of the care he took in studying facts, not as more important than those facts.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Tagiga chizilgan jumladan oldin Darvin o‘z tarixini hikoya qiladi: yillar davomida faktlarni “sabr bilan to‘plagan,” taxmin qilishdan oldin besh yil kutgan va o‘shandan beri mavzuni “izchil” o‘rgangan. Tagiga chizilgan jumlada u bular “shaxsiy tafsilotlar” ekanini tan oladi va ularni nima uchun keltirganini tushuntiradi: “qaror qabul qilishda shoshilmaganimni ko‘rsatish uchun.” A javob shu vazifani ifodalaydi.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: Darvin buning aksini aytadi — u shoshilmagan, faqat shaxsiy tafsilotlarni keltirgani uchun uzr so‘raydi. C noto‘g‘ri: Janubiy Amerika haqidagi faktlar tagiga chizilgan jumlada emas, birinchi jumlada tilga olingan. D noto‘g‘ri: Darvin shaxsiy tarixini faktlarni qanchalik puxta o‘rganganining dalili sifatida keltiradi, faktlardan muhimroq narsa sifatida emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Restorers of old paintings once routinely removed yellowed varnish from canvases, confident that they were revealing the colors the artist had intended. Some art historians now question that confidence. __Pigments themselves change over the centuries—some blues fade, some reds darken—so a freshly cleaned painting may display colors that no one, not even the artist, ever saw.__ These historians do not argue that cleaning should stop, but they urge restorers to describe their work as an interpretation rather than a return to an original state.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?\",\"option_a\":\"It describes the method restorers use to remove varnish from paintings.\",\"option_b\":\"It presents evidence that cleaning damages paintings and should therefore be stopped.\",\"option_c\":\"It gives a reason for the doubt that the historians express in the previous sentence.\",\"option_d\":\"It explains why restorers originally became confident that cleaning reveals an artist's colors.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The previous sentence says some historians “question that confidence”—the belief that cleaning reveals the artist's intended colors. The underlined sentence explains why: the pigments themselves have changed, so the cleaned colors may not be the original ones. Choice C describes this function.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the sentence is about how pigments change, not about how varnish is removed. Choice B is wrong because the final sentence states that the historians “do not argue that cleaning should stop,” and the underlined sentence says nothing about damage caused by cleaning. Choice D is wrong because the sentence undermines the restorers' confidence rather than explaining where it came from.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Oldingi jumlada ba’zi tarixchilar “bu ishonchga shubha bilan qarashi” aytiladi — ya’ni tozalash rassom nazarda tutgan ranglarni ochib beradi degan ishonchga. Tagiga chizilgan jumla buning sababini tushuntiradi: bo‘yoqlarning o‘zi vaqt o‘tib o‘zgargan, shuning uchun tozalangan ranglar asl ranglar bo‘lmasligi mumkin. C javob shu vazifani ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: jumla lakni qanday olib tashlash haqida emas, bo‘yoqlar qanday o‘zgarishi haqida. B noto‘g‘ri: oxirgi jumlada tarixchilar “tozalashni to‘xtatish kerak deb aytmaydi” deyilgan, tagiga chizilgan jumlada esa tozalash zarari haqida gap yo‘q. D noto‘g‘ri: jumla restavratorlarning ishonchi qayerdan kelganini tushuntirmaydi, aksincha, uni zaiflashtiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Most metals, such as iron, copper, and gold, are solid at room temperature. ______ mercury is a liquid at room temperature; it does not become solid until it is cooled to about −39°C.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"Similarly,\",\"option_b\":\"However,\",\"option_c\":\"For example,\",\"option_d\":\"As a result,\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first sentence says that most metals are solid at room temperature. The second sentence describes mercury, a metal that is an exception because it is liquid at room temperature. “However” introduces a contrast, so choice B is correct.\\n\\n“Similarly” would wrongly suggest that mercury behaves like the other metals. “For example” would present mercury as an example of a solid metal, but mercury is not solid. “As a result” would wrongly suggest that mercury is liquid because other metals are solid.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi gapda aksariyat metallar xona haroratida qattiq ekani aytilgan. Ikkinchi gap esa simob haqida: u xona haroratida suyuq, ya’ni istisno. “However” qarama-qarshilikni bildiradi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\n“Similarly” simob boshqa metallarga o‘xshash degan noto‘g‘ri ma’noni beradi. “For example” simobni qattiq metallarga misol qilib ko‘rsatadi, holbuki u qattiq emas. “As a result” simob boshqa metallar qattiq bo‘lgani uchun suyuq degan noto‘g‘ri sabab-oqibat bog‘lanishini bildiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Not all birds can fly. ______ the ostrich, the largest living bird, is far too heavy to lift itself into the air, though it can run at speeds of more than 70 kilometers per hour.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"For example,\",\"option_b\":\"Nevertheless,\",\"option_c\":\"In contrast,\",\"option_d\":\"Afterward,\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first sentence makes a general claim: some birds cannot fly. The second sentence gives a specific case of such a bird, the ostrich. “For example” introduces an example of a general claim, so choice A is correct.\\n\\n“Nevertheless” signals that what follows happens despite the previous point, but the ostrich supports that point rather than going against it. “In contrast” signals a difference, but the ostrich is consistent with the first sentence. “Afterward” signals a later time, but the sentences do not describe events in sequence.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi gapda umumiy fikr bor: hamma qushlar ham ucha olmaydi. Ikkinchi gap shunday qushning aniq misolini — tuyaqushni keltiradi. “For example” umumiy fikrga misol keltirishni bildiradi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob A.\\n\\n“Nevertheless” keyingi fikr oldingisiga qaramay sodir bo‘lishini bildiradi, lekin tuyaqush oldingi fikrga zid emas, uni tasdiqlaydi. “In contrast” farqni bildiradi, ammo tuyaqush birinchi gapga mos keladi. “Afterward” keyinroq vaqtni bildiradi, gaplarda esa voqealar ketma-ketligi tasvirlanmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"A monarch butterfly begins life as a tiny egg laid on a milkweed plant. After hatching, the caterpillar eats milkweed leaves almost constantly and grows quickly. Next, it hangs upside down and forms a hard-shelled chrysalis, inside which its body is reorganized. ______ an adult butterfly breaks out of the chrysalis, pumps fluid into its crumpled wings, and waits for them to dry before flying away.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"For instance,\",\"option_b\":\"In contrast,\",\"option_c\":\"Similarly,\",\"option_d\":\"Finally,\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text describes the stages of a monarch's life in order: egg, caterpillar, chrysalis. The last sentence describes the final stage, when the adult butterfly emerges. “Finally” introduces the last step in a sequence, so choice D is correct.\\n\\n“For instance” would present the adult's emergence as an example of the chrysalis stage. “In contrast” would signal a difference between stages, not the next one. “Similarly” would suggest that the adult's emergence is like the chrysalis stage rather than the step that follows it.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda monarx kapalagi hayotining bosqichlari tartib bilan tasvirlangan: tuxum, qurt, g‘umbak. Oxirgi gap yakuniy bosqich — voyaga yetgan kapalak chiqishini tasvirlaydi. “Finally” ketma-ketlikning oxirgi bosqichini kiritadi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob D.\\n\\n“For instance” kapalakning chiqishini g‘umbak bosqichiga misol qilib ko‘rsatadi. “In contrast” keyingi bosqichni emas, bosqichlar orasidagi farqni bildiradi. “Similarly” kapalakning chiqishi g‘umbak bosqichiga o‘xshash degan ma’noni beradi, holbuki u undan keyingi bosqich.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The Moon spins once on its axis about every 27 days, almost exactly the time it takes to complete one orbit around Earth. ______ the same side of the Moon always faces Earth, and people on Earth never see most of the Moon's far side.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"Even so,\",\"option_b\":\"For instance,\",\"option_c\":\"As a result,\",\"option_d\":\"Likewise,\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first sentence explains that the Moon's spin and its orbit take about the same amount of time. The second sentence describes what follows from this: the same side always faces Earth. “As a result” introduces an effect of what was just described, so choice C is correct.\\n\\n“Even so” would signal that the same side faces Earth despite the matching periods, but the matching periods are actually the cause. “For instance” would present the second sentence as an example, not a consequence. “Likewise” would suggest that the second sentence describes a similar fact rather than a result of the first.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi gapda Oyning o‘z o‘qi atrofida aylanishi va Yer atrofidagi orbitasi deyarli bir xil vaqt olishi tushuntiriladi. Ikkinchi gap bundan kelib chiqadigan natijani tasvirlaydi: Yerga doim Oyning bir tomoni qaragan bo‘ladi. “As a result” oldingi fikrning natijasini kiritadi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\n“Even so” bir tomon Yerga qaragani davrlar mos kelishiga qaramay sodir bo‘ladi degan ma’noni beradi, holbuki aynan shu moslik sababdir. “For instance” ikkinchi gapni natija emas, misol sifatida ko‘rsatadi. “Likewise” ikkinchi gap birinchining natijasi emas, unga o‘xshash fakt degan ma’noni beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Botanists classify the tomato as a fruit because it develops from the flower of the plant and contains the plant's seeds. ______ in 1893 the US Supreme Court ruled that tomatoes should be treated as vegetables under a tax law on imported produce, reasoning that in everyday speech people called tomatoes vegetables and served them with main courses rather than as dessert.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"Nevertheless,\",\"option_b\":\"Therefore,\",\"option_c\":\"In addition,\",\"option_d\":\"Specifically,\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first sentence explains why botanists consider the tomato a fruit. The second sentence describes a court ruling that treated tomatoes as vegetables anyway. “Nevertheless” introduces something that happens in spite of what came before, so choice A is correct.\\n\\n“Therefore” would wrongly present the court's ruling as a logical result of the botanical classification, when the ruling actually goes against it. “In addition” would present the ruling as further support for the idea that tomatoes are fruits. “Specifically” would introduce a more detailed version of the first sentence, but the ruling reaches a different conclusion.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi gap botaniklar pomidorni nima uchun meva deb hisoblashini tushuntiradi. Ikkinchi gap esa shunga qaramay pomidorni sabzavot deb topgan sud qarorini tasvirlaydi. “Nevertheless” oldingi fikrga qaramay sodir bo‘lgan narsani kiritadi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob A.\\n\\n“Therefore” sud qarorini botanik tasnifning mantiqiy natijasi qilib ko‘rsatadi, holbuki qaror unga zid. “In addition” qarorni pomidor meva degan fikrga qo‘shimcha dalil qilib ko‘rsatadi. “Specifically” birinchi gapni batafsilroq ochib berishni bildiradi, ammo sud qarori boshqa xulosaga keladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":65,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Marie Curie is the only person to have won Nobel Prizes in two different sciences. ______ she shared the 1903 Nobel Prize in Physics for research on radiation, and she alone received the 1911 Nobel Prize in Chemistry for her work on the element radium.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"Still,\",\"option_b\":\"Instead,\",\"option_c\":\"Consequently,\",\"option_d\":\"Specifically,\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first sentence makes a general statement: Curie won Nobel Prizes in two different sciences. The second sentence gives the precise details—which prizes she won, in which years, and for what work. “Specifically” introduces more exact details about a preceding statement, so choice D is correct.\\n\\n“Still” would signal that the second sentence is true despite the first, but the two sentences agree. “Instead” would suggest that the prizes replaced something mentioned earlier. “Consequently” would wrongly present winning the two prizes as a result of being the only person to win them, reversing the actual relationship.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi gap umumiy fikrni bildiradi: Kyuri ikki xil fan bo‘yicha Nobel mukofotini olgan. Ikkinchi gap aniq tafsilotlarni beradi — qaysi mukofotlar, qaysi yillarda va qanday ish uchun. “Specifically” oldingi fikr haqida aniqroq tafsilot kiritadi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob D.\\n\\n“Still” ikkinchi gap birinchisiga qaramay to‘g‘ri degan ma’noni beradi, holbuki ikki gap bir-biriga mos. “Instead” mukofotlar avval aytilgan nimanidir almashtirgan degan ma’noni beradi. “Consequently” ikki mukofotni olishni yagona g‘olib bo‘lishning natijasi qilib ko‘rsatadi — bu munosabatni teskari qiladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In 2006, the International Astronomical Union (IAU) decided that a planet must meet three criteria: it must orbit the Sun, it must have enough mass for its own gravity to pull it into a nearly round shape, and it must have cleared the area around its orbit of other objects. Pluto orbits the Sun and is nearly round. ______ it shares its orbital zone with many other icy objects, so the IAU classified Pluto as a dwarf planet rather than a planet.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"In addition,\",\"option_b\":\"For this reason,\",\"option_c\":\"However,\",\"option_d\":\"In other words,\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Before the blank, the text shows that Pluto meets the first two criteria. After the blank, it shows that Pluto fails the third criterion—it has not cleared its orbital zone—which is why it is not considered a planet. “However” signals this shift from criteria Pluto meets to one it fails, so choice C is correct.\\n\\n“In addition” might seem to fit because the sentence introduces the third criterion, but it would present Pluto's crowded orbit as one more point in Pluto's favor. “For this reason” would wrongly suggest that Pluto shares its orbit because it is round and orbits the Sun. “In other words” would present the sentence as a restatement of the previous one, but it adds new and contrasting information.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Bo‘sh joydan oldin Pluton dastlabki ikki mezonga javob berishi ko‘rsatilgan. Bo‘sh joydan keyin esa u uchinchi mezonga javob bermasligi — o‘z orbitasi atrofini tozalamagani — va shuning uchun sayyora hisoblanmasligi aytilgan. “However” Pluton javob beradigan mezonlardan javob bermaydigan mezonga o‘tishni bildiradi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\n“In addition” to‘g‘ridek ko‘rinishi mumkin, chunki gap uchinchi mezonni kiritadi, lekin u Plutonning gavjum orbitasini uning foydasiga yana bir dalil qilib ko‘rsatadi. “For this reason” Pluton dumaloq bo‘lgani va Quyosh atrofida aylangani uchun orbitasini boshqa jismlar bilan bo‘lishadi degan noto‘g‘ri ma’noni beradi. “In other words” gapni oldingisining qayta ifodasi qilib ko‘rsatadi, holbuki u yangi va qarama-qarshi ma’lumot beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In 1866, Gregor Mendel published the results of his experiments with pea plants, which showed that traits are passed from parents to offspring in predictable patterns. Scientists of his time, ______ paid little attention to his work. Only in 1900, sixteen years after Mendel's death, did three botanists working independently rediscover his findings, which became a foundation of modern genetics.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"therefore,\",\"option_b\":\"however,\",\"option_c\":\"for example,\",\"option_d\":\"in other words,\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first sentence describes findings that, as the last sentence makes clear, were important enough to become a foundation of modern genetics. The middle sentence says that Mendel's contemporaries nonetheless paid little attention to them. “However” signals this contrast between the value of the work and the way it was received, so choice B is correct.\\n\\n“Therefore” would wrongly suggest that scientists ignored the work because it showed that traits follow predictable patterns. “For example” would present the scientists' neglect as an example of something in the first sentence, but the first sentence describes Mendel's findings, not reactions to them. “In other words” would present the middle sentence as a restatement of the first, but it adds new and contrasting information.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi gap natijalarni tasvirlaydi; oxirgi gapdan ko‘rinadiki, ular zamonaviy genetikaning asosiga aylanadigan darajada muhim bo‘lgan. O‘rtadagi gap esa shunga qaramay Mendelning zamondoshlari ularga deyarli e’tibor bermaganini aytadi. “However” ishning qimmati va unga bo‘lgan munosabat orasidagi qarama-qarshilikni bildiradi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\n“Therefore” olimlar ishni belgilar oldindan aytib bo‘ladigan qonuniyat bilan o‘tishini ko‘rsatgani uchun e’tiborsiz qoldirgan degan noto‘g‘ri ma’noni beradi. “For example” olimlarning e’tiborsizligini birinchi gapdagi biror narsaga misol qilib ko‘rsatadi, holbuki birinchi gapda unga bo‘lgan munosabat emas, Mendelning natijalari tasvirlangan. “In other words” o‘rtadagi gapni birinchisining qayta ifodasi qilib ko‘rsatadi, ammo u yangi va qarama-qarshi ma’lumot beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"two_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A scatterplot shows the permanent population of Uzbekistan on January 1 of each year from 2020 to 2025. The line of best fit for the data is $y = 0.73x + 33.85$, where $y$ is the predicted population, in millions, and $x$ is the number of years since 2020. Which of the following is the best interpretation of 0.73 in this context?\",\"option_a\":\"The predicted population increases by about 0.73 million people each year.\",\"option_b\":\"The predicted population on January 1, 2020, is 0.73 million people.\",\"option_c\":\"The predicted population increases by about 0.73% each year.\",\"option_d\":\"The predicted population increases by about 33.85 million people each year.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"In a line $y = mx + b$, the slope $m$ is the change in $y$ for each increase of 1 in $x$. Here $x$ increases by 1 each year and $y$ is in millions of people, so 0.73 means the predicted population increases by about 0.73 million (730,000) people per year. Choice B describes the $y$-intercept, but the intercept is 33.85, not 0.73. Choice C treats the slope as a percent; a linear model adds a constant **amount** each year. Choice D confuses the intercept with the slope.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$y = mx + b$ to‘g‘ri chiziqda $m$ qiyalik — $x$ 1 ga ortganda $y$ ning o‘zgarishi. Bu yerda $x$ har yili 1 ga ortadi, $y$ esa million kishida, demak 0.73 bashorat qilingan aholi har yili taxminan 0.73 million (730,000) kishiga ortishini bildiradi. B javob $y$-kesishmani tasvirlaydi, lekin kesishma 0.73 emas, 33.85. C javob qiyalikni foiz deb olgan; chiziqli model har yili o‘zgarmas **miqdor** qo‘shadi. D javob kesishmani qiyalik bilan adashtirgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"two_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"For each of the 12 months of the year, a scatterplot shows the mean temperature $x$, in degrees Celsius, and the mean precipitation $y$, in millimeters, in Tashkent, Uzbekistan, based on climate data for 1991–2020. The line of best fit for the data is $y = -2.4x + 76$. According to the line of best fit, what is the predicted precipitation, in millimeters, for a month with a mean temperature of 20°C?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"28\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Substitute $x = 20$: $y = -2.4(20) + 76 = -48 + 76 = 28$ millimeters. A common error is to drop the negative sign and get $2.4(20) + 76 = 124$. Enter 28.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$x = 20$ ni qo‘yamiz: $y = -2.4(20) + 76 = -48 + 76 = 28$ millimetr. Ko‘p uchraydigan xato — manfiy ishorani tashlab yuborib, $2.4(20) + 76 = 124$ ni olish. Javob: 28.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"two_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Which of the following best describes the association between a planet's distance from the Sun and its mean temperature for the planets in the table?\",\"passage\":\"According to NASA, the planets' mean distances from the Sun and mean temperatures are as shown.\\n\\n| Planet | Distance from the Sun (millions of km) | Mean temperature (°C) |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Mercury | 57.9 | 167 |\\n| Venus | 108.2 | 464 |\\n| Earth | 149.6 | 15 |\\n| Mars | 228.0 | -65 |\\n| Jupiter | 778.5 | -110 |\\n| Saturn | 1,432.0 | -140 |\\n| Uranus | 2,867.0 | -195 |\\n| Neptune | 4,515.0 | -200 |\",\"option_a\":\"A positive association\",\"option_b\":\"A negative association\",\"option_c\":\"No association\",\"option_d\":\"An association in which the temperature first decreases and then increases\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"As the distance from the Sun increases, the mean temperature generally decreases: from Earth (15°C) outward, every planet is colder than the one before, down to Neptune at −200°C. When greater values of one variable tend to go with lower values of the other, the association is negative. Venus is hotter than Mercury (its thick atmosphere traps heat), but one point that breaks the pattern does not change the overall trend. Choice A would mean that farther planets tend to be hotter. Choice C ignores the clear downward trend, and choice D does not match the data, because the temperatures never rise again after Venus.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Quyoshdan uzoqlik ortgan sari o‘rtacha harorat umuman kamayadi: Yerdan (15°C) boshlab har bir keyingi sayyora oldingisidan sovuqroq, Neptunda −200°C gacha. Bir o‘zgaruvchining katta qiymatlari ikkinchisining kichik qiymatlariga to‘g‘ri kelsa, bog‘liqlik manfiy bo‘ladi. Venera Merkuriydan issiqroq (qalin atmosferasi issiqlikni ushlab qoladi), lekin qoidaga bo‘ysunmaydigan bitta nuqta umumiy tendensiyani o‘zgartirmaydi. A javob uzoqroq sayyoralar issiqroq bo‘lishini anglatardi. C javob aniq kamayish tendensiyasini e’tiborsiz qoldiradi, D javob esa ma’lumotlarga mos emas, chunki Veneradan keyin harorat qayta ko‘tarilmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"two_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"The table shows the permanent population of Uzbekistan on January 1 of each year from 2020 to 2025, where $x$ is the number of years since 2020. Which of the following equations best models the data, where $y$ is the population, in millions?\",\"passage\":\"| Year | $x$ | Population (millions) |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| 2020 | 0 | 33.91 |\\n| 2021 | 1 | 34.56 |\\n| 2022 | 2 | 35.27 |\\n| 2023 | 3 | 36.02 |\\n| 2024 | 4 | 36.80 |\\n| 2025 | 5 | 37.54 |\",\"option_a\":\"$y = 33.85x + 0.73$\",\"option_b\":\"$y = -0.73x + 37.54$\",\"option_c\":\"$y = 0.073x + 33.85$\",\"option_d\":\"$y = 0.73x + 33.85$\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The population increases by roughly the same amount each year (0.65 to 0.78 million), so a linear model with a positive slope fits. From 2020 to 2025 the population grows by $37.54 - 33.91 = 3.63$ million in 5 years, or about $\\\\frac{3.63}{5} \\\\approx 0.73$ million per year, and the value at $x = 0$ is about 33.9. So $y = 0.73x + 33.85$ fits best; for example, it gives $0.73(5) + 33.85 = 37.5$ for 2025. Choice A switches the slope and the intercept and would predict 33.85 million more people every year. Choice B has a negative slope, so it predicts a decreasing population. Choice C's slope is 10 times too small: it predicts only 34.2 million for 2025.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Aholi har yili taxminan bir xil miqdorga (0.65 dan 0.78 milliongacha) ortadi, demak musbat qiyalikli chiziqli model mos keladi. 2020 dan 2025 gacha aholi 5 yilda $37.54 - 33.91 = 3.63$ millionga, ya’ni yiliga taxminan $\\\\frac{3.63}{5} \\\\approx 0.73$ millionga ortgan, $x = 0$ dagi qiymat esa taxminan 33.9. Demak $y = 0.73x + 33.85$ eng yaxshi mos keladi; masalan, 2025 uchun $0.73(5) + 33.85 = 37.5$ ni beradi. A javob qiyalik va kesishmani almashtirgan — u har yili 33.85 million kishi qo‘shilishini bashorat qiladi. B javobda qiyalik manfiy, ya’ni aholi kamayishi bashorat qilinadi. C javobning qiyaligi 10 marta kichik: u 2025 uchun atigi 34.2 millionni beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"two_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"The line of best fit $y = 0.73x + 33.85$ models the permanent population of Uzbekistan, where $y$ is the predicted population, in millions, on January 1 of the year that is $x$ years after 2020. The actual population on January 1, 2024, was 36.80 million. By how many **thousand** people was the actual population greater than the population predicted by the line of best fit for that date?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"30\"],\"explanation_en\":\"January 1, 2024, is 4 years after 2020, so $x = 4$. The predicted population is $0.73(4) + 33.85 = 2.92 + 33.85 = 36.77$ million. The actual population minus the predicted population is $36.80 - 36.77 = 0.03$ million. Since 1 million = 1,000 thousand, $0.03 \\\\text{ million} = 30$ thousand. Using $x = 2024$ or $x = 24$ instead of $x = 4$ gives an impossible prediction. Entering 0.03 gives the answer in millions, not thousands. Enter 30.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"2024 yil 1 yanvar — 2020 dan 4 yil keyin, demak $x = 4$. Bashorat qilingan aholi: $0.73(4) + 33.85 = 2.92 + 33.85 = 36.77$ million. Haqiqiy aholidan bashoratni ayiramiz: $36.80 - 36.77 = 0.03$ million. 1 million = 1,000 ming bo‘lgani uchun $0.03 \\\\text{ million} = 30$ ming. $x = 4$ o‘rniga $x = 2024$ yoki $x = 24$ ni olish mantiqsiz bashorat beradi. 0.03 deb yozish javobni mingda emas, millionda beradi. Javob: 30.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"two_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"For each of the 12 months of the year, a scatterplot shows the mean temperature $x$, in degrees Celsius, and the mean precipitation $y$, in millimeters, in Tashkent, Uzbekistan. The line of best fit for the data is $y = -2.4x + 76$. Which of the following is the best interpretation of $-2.4$ in this context?\",\"option_a\":\"Each month, the precipitation decreases by 2.4 millimeters.\",\"option_b\":\"For a month with a mean temperature of 0°C, the predicted precipitation is 2.4 millimeters.\",\"option_c\":\"For each increase of 1°C in the mean temperature of a month, the predicted precipitation decreases by 2.4 millimeters.\",\"option_d\":\"For each decrease of 1 millimeter in precipitation, the predicted mean temperature increases by 2.4°C.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The slope is the predicted change in $y$ (precipitation, in mm) for each increase of 1 in $x$ (temperature, in °C). A slope of $-2.4$ means that for each 1°C increase in a month's mean temperature, the predicted precipitation is 2.4 mm **less**. Choice A treats $x$ as time, but $x$ is temperature, not the month number. Choice B describes the $y$-intercept, which is 76 mm, not 2.4 mm. Choice D reverses the roles of the variables: the model predicts precipitation from temperature, not temperature from precipitation.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qiyalik — $x$ (harorat, °C) 1 ga ortganda $y$ (yog‘in, mm) ning bashorat qilingan o‘zgarishi. $-2.4$ qiyalik oyning o‘rtacha harorati har 1°C ga ortganda bashorat qilingan yog‘in 2.4 mm ga **kam** bo‘lishini bildiradi. A javob $x$ ni vaqt deb olgan, lekin $x$ — oy raqami emas, harorat. B javob $y$-kesishmani tasvirlaydi, u esa 2.4 mm emas, 76 mm. D javob o‘zgaruvchilarning rolini almashtirgan: model yog‘inni haroratga qarab bashorat qiladi, aksincha emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"two_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"The table shows each planet's mean distance from the Sun, $d$, in astronomical units (AU), where 1 AU is Earth's mean distance from the Sun, and the time it takes the planet to orbit the Sun once, $P$, in Earth years. Which of the following equations best models the relationship between $P$ and $d$?\",\"passage\":\"The table is based on NASA data for the five planets closest to the Sun.\\n\\n| Planet | $d$ (AU) | $P$ (Earth years) |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Mercury | 0.39 | 0.24 |\\n| Venus | 0.72 | 0.62 |\\n| Earth | 1.00 | 1.00 |\\n| Mars | 1.52 | 1.88 |\\n| Jupiter | 5.20 | 11.86 |\",\"option_a\":\"$P = 1.5d$\",\"option_b\":\"$P = d^{1.5}$\",\"option_c\":\"$P = d^2$\",\"option_d\":\"$P = 1.68d - 0.68$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Test each model, especially at Jupiter, where the models differ most. $P = d^{1.5}$ gives $5.20^{1.5} = 5.20\\\\sqrt{5.20} \\\\approx 11.86$, which matches. It also fits the others: $0.39^{1.5} \\\\approx 0.24$, $1.52^{1.5} \\\\approx 1.87$, and at Earth $1^{1.5} = 1$. Choice A gives 7.8 for Jupiter and 1.5 for Earth, which is wrong even at $d = 1$. Choice C gives $5.20^2 \\\\approx 27.0$ for Jupiter, much too large. Choice D, the line through the points for Earth and Mars, gives only about 8.1 for Jupiter and a negative period (about $-0.02$) for Mercury: the data curve upward, so no line fits all of them.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Har bir modelni, ayniqsa modellar eng ko‘p farq qiladigan Yupiterda tekshiramiz. $P = d^{1.5}$: $5.20^{1.5} = 5.20\\\\sqrt{5.20} \\\\approx 11.86$ — mos keladi. Boshqalariga ham mos: $0.39^{1.5} \\\\approx 0.24$, $1.52^{1.5} \\\\approx 1.87$, Yerda esa $1^{1.5} = 1$. A javob Yupiter uchun 7.8, Yer uchun 1.5 ni beradi — hatto $d = 1$ da ham xato. C javob Yupiter uchun $5.20^2 \\\\approx 27.0$ ni beradi — juda katta. D javob (Yer va Mars nuqtalaridan o‘tuvchi to‘g‘ri chiziq) Yupiter uchun atigi 8.1 ga yaqin qiymat, Merkuriy uchun esa manfiy davr (taxminan $-0.02$) beradi: ma’lumotlar yuqoriga egilgan, shuning uchun hech bir to‘g‘ri chiziq hammasiga mos kelmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":130,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"two_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"The line of best fit $y = 0.73x + 33.85$ models the permanent population of Uzbekistan, where $y$ is the predicted population, in millions, on January 1 of the year that is $x$ years after 2020. If the trend continues, in what year will the predicted population on January 1 first be greater than 40 million?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"2029\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Solve $0.73x + 33.85 > 40$: $0.73x > 6.15$, so $x > \\\\frac{6.15}{0.73} \\\\approx 8.42$. Since $x$ counts whole years, the first value that works is $x = 9$. Check: $x = 8$ gives $0.73(8) + 33.85 = 39.69$ million (not greater than 40), and $x = 9$ gives $40.42$ million. The year is $2020 + 9 = 2029$. Rounding 8.42 down to 8 and answering 2028 is a common error. Enter 2029.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$0.73x + 33.85 > 40$ ni yechamiz: $0.73x > 6.15$, demak $x > \\\\frac{6.15}{0.73} \\\\approx 8.42$. $x$ butun yillarni sanagani uchun shartni qanoatlantiradigan birinchi qiymat $x = 9$. Tekshiruv: $x = 8$ da $0.73(8) + 33.85 = 39.69$ million (40 dan katta emas), $x = 9$ da esa $40.42$ million. Yil: $2020 + 9 = 2029$. 8.42 ni 8 gacha yaxlitlab, 2028 deb javob berish — ko‘p uchraydigan xato. Javob: 2029.\",\"estimated_seconds\":110,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from Charlotte Brontë's 1847 novel *Jane Eyre*. The narrator, Jane, is a young girl living with her aunt, Mrs. Reed.\\n\\nThere was no possibility of taking a walk that day. We had been wandering, indeed, in the leafless shrubbery an hour in the morning; but since dinner (Mrs. Reed, when there was no company, dined early) the cold winter wind had brought with it clouds so sombre, and a rain so ______, that further outdoor exercise was now out of the question. I was glad of it: I never liked long walks, especially on chilly afternoons.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"gentle\",\"option_b\":\"penetrating\",\"option_c\":\"welcome\",\"option_d\":\"brief\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The rain, together with the cold wind and gloomy clouds, made outdoor exercise “out of the question,” so it must have been heavy and soaking enough to get through clothing. “Penetrating” (piercing, soaking through) fits this meaning.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because a “gentle” rain would not make a walk impossible. Choice C is wrong because the rain is described alongside a “cold winter wind” and “sombre” clouds as something that ruled out going outside, not as something pleasant. Choice D is wrong because a “brief” rain would not keep the family indoors for the rest of the afternoon.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Yomg‘ir sovuq qishki shamol va qovog‘i soliq bulutlar bilan birga tashqarida sayr qilishni butunlay imkonsiz qilib qo‘ygan (“out of the question”). Demak, yomg‘ir kiyimdan o‘tib ketadigan darajada kuchli va zax bo‘lgan. “Penetrating” (ichiga o‘tib ketadigan, suyak-suyakka o‘tadigan) so‘zi shu ma’noga mos keladi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: “gentle” (mayin) yomg‘ir sayrni imkonsiz qilmaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: yomg‘ir sovuq shamol va “sombre” (qorong‘i, g‘amgin) bulutlar qatorida tilga olinadi, ya’ni u yoqimli narsa sifatida emas, tashqariga chiqishga to‘sqinlik qilgan narsa sifatida tasvirlangan. D noto‘g‘ri: “brief” (qisqa) yomg‘ir oilani butun kun davomida uyda ushlab turmas edi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":65,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from Willa Cather's 1913 novel *O Pioneers!*\\n\\nOne January day, thirty years ago, the little town of Hanover, anchored on a windy Nebraska tableland, was trying not to be blown away. A mist of fine snowflakes was curling and eddying about the cluster of low drab buildings huddled on the gray prairie, under a gray sky. The dwelling-houses were set about haphazard on the tough prairie sod; some of them looked as if they had been moved in overnight, and others as if they were straying off by themselves, headed straight for the open plain. None of them had any appearance of ______, and the howling wind blew under them as well as over them.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"celebration\",\"option_b\":\"disorder\",\"option_c\":\"movement\",\"option_d\":\"permanence\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The houses look as if they “had been moved in overnight” or were “straying off by themselves,” and the wind blows even under them. In other words, none of them looks settled or lasting. “Permanence” (the quality of lasting or staying in place) completes the description logically.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because nothing in the text concerns festivity; the description is about how unsettled the houses look. Choice B is wrong because the houses are “set about haphazard,” so they clearly do look disordered. Choice C is wrong because houses that seem to be “straying off” and “headed straight for the open plain” do give an appearance of movement.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Uylar go‘yo “bir kechada ko‘chirib keltirilgan” yoki “o‘z-o‘zidan dashtga qarab ketib qolayotgan”dek ko‘rinadi, shamol esa hatto ularning tagidan ham esadi. Boshqacha aytganda, birortasi ham mustahkam, uzoq turadigan joyga o‘xshamaydi. “Permanence” (doimiylik, mustahkam turib qolish) so‘zi tasvirni mantiqan to‘ldiradi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: matnda bayram yoki shodlik haqida gap yo‘q, tasvir uylarning beqarorligi haqida. B noto‘g‘ri: uylar “haphazard” (tartibsiz) joylashgan, demak ular tartibsiz ko‘rinadi — bu jumlaga zid. C noto‘g‘ri: “ketib qolayotgan” va “dashtga qarab yo‘l olgan”dek ko‘ringan uylar aynan harakatdagidek taassurot qoldiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":65,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The new footbridge over the canal in the town of Nurobod was designed in lightweight sections so that it could be ______ quickly. Workers put the entire structure together in a single weekend, and the road beside the canal had to be closed for only a few hours.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"admired\",\"option_b\":\"abandoned\",\"option_c\":\"assembled\",\"option_d\":\"measured\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The second sentence explains what happened quickly: workers “put the entire structure together in a single weekend.” A word meaning “put together” is needed, so “assembled” is correct.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the text is about how the bridge was built, not about people's opinions of it. Choice B is wrong because nothing suggests the bridge was left unused. Choice D is wrong because measuring the bridge is not the same as putting it together, which is what the workers did in one weekend.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkinchi jumla nima tez bajarilganini tushuntiradi: ishchilar “butun inshootni bitta dam olish kunlarida yig‘ib qo‘yishgan.” Demak, “yig‘moq” ma’nosidagi so‘z kerak — “assembled” to‘g‘ri javob.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: matn ko‘prikka odamlarning munosabati haqida emas, uning qanday qurilgani haqida. B noto‘g‘ri: ko‘prik tashlab qo‘yilgani haqida hech qanday ishora yo‘q. D noto‘g‘ri: ko‘prikni o‘lchash uni yig‘ish bilan bir xil emas, ishchilar esa aynan uni bir dam olish kunida yig‘ishgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from Mark Twain's 1883 memoir *Life on the Mississippi*. Twain recalls the town on the Mississippi River where he grew up. A “packet” is a steamboat that carries passengers and goods on a regular route.\\n\\nOnce a day a cheap, gaudy packet arrived upward from St. Louis, and another downward from Keokuk. Before these events, the day was glorious with ______; after them, the day was a dead and empty thing. Not only the boys, but the whole village, felt this. After all these years I can picture that old time to myself now, just as it was then: the white town drowsing in the sunshine of a summer's morning; the streets empty, or pretty nearly so; one or two clerks sitting in front of the Water Street stores, with their splint-bottomed chairs tilted back against the wall, chins on breasts, hats slouched over their faces, asleep…\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"expectancy\",\"option_b\":\"regret\",\"option_c\":\"routine\",\"option_d\":\"exhaustion\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The sentence contrasts the time before the boats arrived with the time after: afterward the day was “dead and empty.” Before the arrivals, then, the day must have been full of something positive that the boats' arrival used up. “Expectancy” (eager waiting for something to happen) fits: the day was “glorious” because everyone was looking forward to the boats.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because “regret” is a negative feeling about something past and could not make a day “glorious.” Choice C is wrong because ordinary “routine” would not be called glorious, and it does not explain why the day became empty once the boats had come. Choice D is wrong because “exhaustion” is negative and does not contrast with the “dead and empty” day that followed.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Jumla kemalar kelishidan oldingi va keyingi vaqtni qarama-qarshi qo‘yadi: keyin kun “o‘lik va bo‘m-bo‘sh” bo‘lib qolgan. Demak, kemalar kelishidan oldin kun qandaydir ijobiy narsa bilan to‘la bo‘lgan va kemalar kelishi bilan bu narsa tugagan. “Expectancy” (biror narsani intiqlik bilan kutish) mos keladi: hamma kemalarni kutgani uchun kun “ajoyib” (glorious) bo‘lgan.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: “regret” (afsus) — o‘tgan narsa haqidagi salbiy tuyg‘u, u kunni “ajoyib” qila olmaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: oddiy “routine” (odatiy tartib) ajoyib deb atalmaydi va nima uchun kemalar kelgach kun bo‘shab qolganini tushuntirmaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: “exhaustion” (holdan toyish) salbiy ma’noli va keyingi “o‘lik va bo‘sh” kunga qarama-qarshi qo‘yilmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from Frederick Douglass's 1845 autobiography *Narrative of the Life of Frederick Douglass, an American Slave*.\\n\\nI have no accurate knowledge of my age, never having seen any authentic record containing it. … A want of information concerning my own was a source of unhappiness to me even during childhood. The white children could tell their ages. I could not tell why I ought to be deprived of the same privilege. I was not allowed to make any inquiries of my master concerning it. He deemed all such inquiries on the part of a slave improper and ______, and evidence of a restless spirit.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"harmless\",\"option_b\":\"admirable\",\"option_c\":\"impertinent\",\"option_d\":\"customary\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Douglass was “not allowed” to ask about his age, and his master considered such questions “improper” and a sign of “a restless spirit.” The missing word must be another disapproving term that fits beside “improper.” “Impertinent” (rude, disrespectful, overstepping one's place) is the precise choice.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the master forbade the questions, so he clearly did not think them “harmless.” Choice B is wrong because “admirable” is praise, which contradicts “improper.” Choice D is wrong because “customary” means usual or expected, yet the master forbade these questions, and Douglass notes that enslaved people generally did not know their birthdays.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Duglasga o‘z yoshi haqida so‘rashga “ruxsat berilmagan,” xo‘jayini esa bunday savollarni “improper” (nojo‘ya) va “bezovta ruh”ning belgisi deb hisoblagan. Bo‘sh joyga “improper” yonida turadigan yana bir qoralovchi so‘z kerak. “Impertinent” (beadab, o‘z haddidan oshgan) — aniq tanlov.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: xo‘jayin bu savollarni taqiqlagan, demak ularni “harmless” (zararsiz) deb hisoblamagan. B noto‘g‘ri: “admirable” (tahsinga loyiq) — maqtov, bu “improper” so‘ziga zid. D noto‘g‘ri: “customary” odatiy, kutilgan degani, ammo xo‘jayin bu savollarni taqiqlagan, Duglas esa qullar odatda tug‘ilgan kunini bilmasligini aytadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Botanist Shirin Qodirova expected the seeds of the desert shrub she studies to sprout as soon as they were watered in her laboratory. Instead, the seeds remained ______ for nearly four months, showing no sign of growth until they had been exposed to a series of cold nights. Qodirova suspects that this delay keeps the young plants from sprouting in autumn, only to be killed by winter frost.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"fragile\",\"option_b\":\"abundant\",\"option_c\":\"visible\",\"option_d\":\"dormant\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The seeds showed “no sign of growth” for months until a period of cold nights, even though they had water. A word describing a resting, inactive state is needed. “Dormant” (alive but temporarily inactive) fits precisely.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the text says nothing about the seeds breaking easily; it describes their failure to grow. Choice B is wrong because “abundant” refers to quantity, and the number of seeds is irrelevant to the delay. Choice C is wrong because whether the seeds could be seen has nothing to do with their not sprouting.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Urug‘lar suv berilganiga qaramay, bir necha oy davomida sovuq kechalar o‘tmaguncha “o‘sish belgisini ko‘rsatmagan.” Demak, tinch, faol bo‘lmagan holatni bildiruvchi so‘z kerak. “Dormant” (tirik, lekin vaqtincha harakatsiz, uyqudagi) aynan shu ma’noni beradi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: matnda urug‘larning tez sinishi haqida gap yo‘q, ularning o‘smagani haqida gap boradi. B noto‘g‘ri: “abundant” (mo‘l) miqdorni bildiradi, urug‘lar soni esa kechikishga aloqasi yo‘q. C noto‘g‘ri: urug‘larning ko‘rinishi yoki ko‘rinmasligi ularning unib chiqmaganiga aloqador emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from Jane Austen's 1813 novel *Pride and Prejudice*. The narrator describes Mr. and Mrs. Bennet.\\n\\nMr. Bennet was so odd a mixture of quick parts, sarcastic humour, reserve, and caprice, that the experience of three-and-twenty years had been insufficient to make his wife understand his character. Her mind was less difficult to develope. She was a woman of ______ understanding, little information, and uncertain temper. When she was discontented, she fancied herself nervous. The business of her life was to get her daughters married: its solace was visiting and news.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"mean\",\"option_b\":\"keen\",\"option_c\":\"profound\",\"option_d\":\"mysterious\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Mr. Bennet is too complicated for his wife to understand, but “her mind was less difficult to develope” (that is, to figure out), and she has “little information.” The missing word must describe a limited, unremarkable intelligence. “Mean” is correct here in its older sense of “inferior” or “of low quality” (not “unkind”): she was a woman of limited understanding.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because a “keen” (sharp) understanding contradicts the claim that her mind was simple and that she could not understand her husband. Choice C is wrong for the same reason: “profound” means deep. Choice D is wrong because the text says her mind was “less difficult” to figure out than her husband's, so it was not “mysterious.”\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Janob Bennet xotini tushuna olmaydigan darajada murakkab odam, ammo xonimning “aqlini tushunish ancha oson edi” (“less difficult to develope”), bundan tashqari u “kam ma’lumotli” edi. Demak, bo‘sh joyga cheklangan, oddiy aql-idrokni tasvirlovchi so‘z kerak. “Mean” bu yerda eski ma’nosida — “past, pastroq sifatli” degan ma’noda keladi (“yovuz” ma’nosida emas): u aql-idroki cheklangan ayol edi.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: “keen” (o‘tkir) aql uning ongi oddiy ekani va erini tushuna olmasligiga zid. C ham shu sababli noto‘g‘ri: “profound” — chuqur degani. D noto‘g‘ri: matnda uning ongini tushunish eriga qaraganda “osonroq” ekani aytilgan, demak u “mysterious” (sirli) emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from Henry David Thoreau's 1862 essay “Walking.” Thoreau has been discussing two possible origins of the word “saunterer,” meaning a person who walks at a leisurely pace.\\n\\nSome, however, would derive the word from sans terre without land or a home, which, therefore, in the good sense, will mean, having no particular home, but equally at home everywhere. For this is the secret of successful sauntering. He who sits still in a house all the time may be the greatest vagrant of all; but the saunterer, in the good sense, is no more vagrant than the meandering river, which is all the while ______ seeking the shortest course to the sea.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"idly\",\"option_b\":\"sedulously\",\"option_c\":\"reluctantly\",\"option_d\":\"randomly\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Thoreau argues that the saunterer is “no more vagrant” (aimless) than a river. Although a river “meanders,” it is always heading toward a goal, “the shortest course to the sea.” The missing word must therefore stress purposeful, persistent effort. “Sedulously” (diligently, with steady attention) conveys this.\\n\\nChoices A and D are wrong because “idly” and “randomly” would make the river aimless, which is exactly what Thoreau denies when he says it is no more vagrant than the saunterer and is seeking “the shortest course.” Choice C is wrong because nothing suggests the river is unwilling; the comparison is about direction and purpose, not reluctance.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Toro sayr qiluvchi (saunterer) daryodan ortiq “daydi” (maqsadsiz) emasligini ta’kidlaydi. Daryo garchi ilon izi bo‘lib oqsa ham, doim bir maqsad sari — “dengizga eng qisqa yo‘l” sari intiladi. Demak, bo‘sh joyga maqsadli, tinimsiz harakatni ta’kidlovchi so‘z kerak. “Sedulously” (tirishqoqlik bilan, tinmay, astoydil) shu ma’noni beradi.\\n\\nA va D noto‘g‘ri: “idly” (bekorchilik bilan) va “randomly” (tasodifiy) daryoni maqsadsiz qilib qo‘yadi, Toro esa aynan buni inkor etadi — daryo “eng qisqa yo‘l”ni izlaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: daryo istamay harakat qilayotgani haqida hech qanday ishora yo‘q; taqqoslash yo‘nalish va maqsad haqida, istamaslik haqida emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v3\"]}]" }
